AB  - With the GAVI programme gradually drawing to a close, this UNICEF SBCC evaluation is intended to provide critical programmatic insights into what has worked, what has not worked and key learnings from the sample states included in the evaluation. Similarly, the findings of the evaluation are expected to support strengthening of SBCC cell functioning, and ongoing program initiatives aimed directly at maximizing utilization of public services. Based on the findings of the evaluation, UNICEF aims to advise the MoHFW and other public administrative bodies on the effectiveness and sustainability of institutionalizing SBCC Cells. -----Full Evaluation text----- Authors: PwC: Sangeeta Tikyani, Gitanjali, Srija Basu, Nishigandha Joshi, Rahul Varyan i Evaluation Manager: Mr. Atishay Mathur, Research & Evaluation Specialist, UNICEF Evaluation Consultant: Dr. Nidhi Pasi, Research & Evaluation Specialist, UNICEF Programme Team: Mr. Siddharta Shreshta, Chief SBC, UNICEF India. Ms. Alka Malhotra, SBC Specialist, UNICEF India. Ms. Cheshta Gulati, SBC Consultant, UNICEF India. Evaluation Reference Group (ERG) Members: Catalina Salazar Silva Dr. Vishal Singh, Associate Professor, Communication, SIHFW-Rajasthan Mr.Kailas Baviskar (DDHS IEC, Maharashtra) Participants of Recommendations Workshop: Catalina Salazar Silva Others: Mona Singh, SBC Specialist, Bihar Nishant Shukla, Ministry of Health and Family Welfare Jaishri Jethwaney, Senior Advisor – Communications (ITSU), MOHFW. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 8 3. Executive summary Background Information, Education, and Communication (IEC) is a process of adequately supporting information and education to people to advance healthy behaviors which are influenced by several factors such as the local context, family, and the community – factors that are crucial to an individual’s growth[1] . The concept of Social and Behavior Change Communication (SBCC) which is an evidence-based, consultative process of addressing social attitudes, knowledge and practices has evolved from earlier models of IEC done through identifying, analyzing and providing communities and individuals with relevant information through welldefined strategies. SBCC aims to make changes in knowledge, attitudes, and practices and has emerged as a more comprehensive approach than IEC. While IEC involves use of wide range of channels and material to pass on fact based instructional messages to the individual or community, SBCC acknowledges underlying socio-economic factors that affect individual behavior and, uses robust approach to facilitate improved social behavior and mobilize targeted populations for change. Various countries and international organizations across the globe have begun investing in SBCC activities to empower individuals and communities, and lower structural barriers that hinder people from adopting positive practices and societies from becoming more equitable, inclusive, cohesive and peaceful. [2] Over last two decades, India’s growth has contributed phenomenally to global human development. However, despite making considerable progress over the years, it continues to face challenges with reproductive health, child health and nutrition. [3] SBCC interventions have increasingly been seen as crucial contributor towards addressing social and cultural barriers in health programmes and enhancing positive behavior towards health and wellbeing of oneself and community, as a whole. In this light, UNICEF has been working actively along with Ministry of Health and Family Welfare (MoHFW) and state governments to strengthen the health system institutional structures for SBCC. Behavior change communication has been inculcated as a part of the larger Health Systems Strengthening program approach. In 2013, a national behavior change communication (BCC) strategy for RI was developed with UNICEF’s support. UNICEF also extended its support to GoI towards institutionalization of SBCC Cells and transformed communication focus from IEC to demand generation through behavioral change. In 2014, Global Alliance for Vaccines and Immunization (GAVI) provided support to this by revamping and revitalizing existing IEC/BCC institutions; or setting up new ones within the state institutions. The SBCC Cells were equipped with trained human resources and budgets that directly supported the State Immunization Division and played a significant role in planning and operationalization of communication interventions for flagship interventions such a s Mission Indra Dhanush and new vaccine introductions. Under the GAVI HSS 2 UNICEF is mandated to engage and facilitate the process of SBCC Cells with the sustainability considerations as stated in the programme document, which outlines that the cells must be fully state government owned and operated. Objective: With the GAVI programme gradually drawing to a close, this UNICEF SBCC evaluation is intended to provide critical programmatic insights into what has worked, what has not worked and key learnings from the sample states included in the evaluation. Similarly, the findings of the evaluation are expected to support strengthening of SBCC cell functioning, and ongoing program initiatives aimed directly at maximizing utilization of public services. Based on the findings of the evaluation, UNICEF aims to advise the MoHFW and other public administrative bodies on the effectiveness and sustainability of institutionalizing SBCC Cells. Methodology: PricewaterhouseCoopers India (PwC) was engaged by the UNICEF India Country Office (ICO) to undertake this evaluation study. The geographic scope of the evaluation state specific (six states, namely, Odisha, Madhya Pradesh, West Bengal, Uttar Pradesh, Telangana, and Assam). A purposive sampling approach was used in district selection across sample states. Further, details on the rationale used for district selection has been included in the methodology section of the report (3.4.2). The SBCC Cells were assessed on the OECD DAC criteria to understand > 2 https://www.unicef.org/social-and-behaviour-change > 3 https://www.unicef.org/india/children in india UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 9 the milestones reached by these cells in terms of relevance, effectiveness, efficiency, coherence, and sustainability considerations within the period of evaluation that is 2017-2021. The evaluation followed the health systems strengthening approach which was also a part of the SBCC systems strengthening, and which included SBCC workforce, service delivery, financing, leadership, governance, information, and knowledge management. Gender and equity lens was adopted as cross-cutting themes across all evaluation objectives. The data collection for the evaluation was conducted during November 2022 to January 2023. The evaluation was mainly conducted by the way of qualitative interviews and FGDs. Additionally, certain identified aspects were verified through observations such as availability of IEC material (Flyers, posters, media clips on social media), display of IEC material at the SBCC cell, and health centers etc. Similarly, aspects related to routine practices were assessed through observation and record reviews. Key findings The main findings are described for each evaluation criterion and structured around the key evaluation questions that guided the evaluation. Relevance : The SBCC Cells aligned with the national and state priorities and other programmes, however the degree of alignment varied from model to model. The cells-initiated activities enhanced the reach and adherence of the programmes and interventions at grass root level. Even during the pandemic cells responded pro-actively and developed strategies, designed materials and planned activities to ensure right information is provided to people in efficient and effective manner. Effectiveness : SBCC cells have been only partially successful in achieving their desired goals and objectives. Lack of systematic structure, scarcity of financial and human resources, limited scope of creativity negatively impacted the performance of the cells. Although the cells were established to lead the communication change interventions but majority of them ended up being supportive unit to the state’s IEC activities. Efficiency: SBCC cells across the states (except Telangana) did not have a say in allocation of financial resources for the SBCC activities. This limits the cells’ efficiency in delivery due to lack of adequate funding for monitoring, research and evidence generation and other essential aspects of SBCC. Due to various reasons like vacant posts, retired / transferred personnel, lack of dedicated communication cadre etc. coupled with lack of dedicated financial allocations leads to reduced efficiency of cells to function within planned timelines. Coherence : In most of the states, the cells had good coordination and convergence with state government. Cells are used to consistently provide government support for implementation of communication strategies at state and district level. However, intersectoral convergence was missing due to which cell were not able to achieve its goals and objectives in effective and efficient manner. It was also observed that cells failed to utilize the potential of other development partners and NGOs in creating a difference and initiating social behavioral change. Sustainability : The SBCC cells work is well integrated into the programming of the departments they are housed under. However, they have a considerable scope of scaling up and integration in the public health system of the country and states. The government (at the state and district-level) have been reported to have limited capacity to adequately carry out the activities of the SBCC Cells independently at present due to limited technical knowledge / expertise and dedicated human resource and financial allocations. Collaborative efforts of all stakeholders would enhance the pool of resources and increase the reach of the cells and help in its transition. Conclusio: The SBCC cells program was initiated to help state government departments to transition from IEC to SBCC. Though this transition is not yet complete for any of the six states evaluated in this study, the inception of the cells has got the ball rolling in the right direction. The state and district level officials are now familiar with the term ‘SBCC’ and are aware of its basic conceptual definition and how it differs from IEC. They are also aware of the novel methods being used in SBCC such as miking, interpersonal communication, leveraging key influencers, etc. The SBCC program has contributed to health systems strengthening in these six states by bringing about a change in the way health, ICDS and PRI officials approach and engage with community members. In conclusion, it can be said that the SBCC cells program kickstarted the transition of communications activities from IEC to SBCC . The program's contribution has been significant and in many of the states been the first SBCC intervention initiated. Though the progress has been slow over the past programmatic cycle, it is important to understand that to bring about a change not just in methods but in the approach, understanding and attitude towards communications is a mammoth task. The very principles of SBCC can be applied here to see the slow progress not as a sign of inefficiency and ineffectiveness of the program, but rather as a work in progress status for the cells . The cells require guidance and support for at least one more programmatic cycle whilst they figure out their transition strategy to move from being UNICEF supported to completely government owned. We strongly suggest UNICEF to continue its investment and support in the program during this period. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 10 Recommendations: The recommendations have been formulated based on the findings which were analyzed through primary and secondary data. The recommendations are divided between those which are common to all the cells across all 3 categories and those which are specific to each category, and are formulated specific to the two main stakeholders, i.e., the government and UNICEF. Broadly, the recommendations are focused on addressing the need for establishment of district-level cells in all the six states, continued need of technical assistance and aid in handover process from UNICEF, capacity building and skill development of health officials at the state, district and block level, and the need for increased agency for cells to lead SBCC programming and have a greater say in budget and timeline planning. We also recommend, a similar evaluation to assess the progress of the transition of the state and decide future course of action at the end of the next programmatic cycle of five years. | Recommendation | Priority and Time frame | SBC SS Indicator | Responsible organization | |---|---|---|---| | State should develop a dedicated cell at state level for SBCC activities |High 6 – 12 months|Leadership and Governance|Department under which cell is housed in states| | UNICEF to prioritize key areas for provision of technical expertise |High 6 – 12 months|Workforce and Service Delivery|UNICEF| | Ensuring dedicated financial resources for SBCC cells |High 6 – 12 months|Finance|Department under which cell is housed and UNICEF| | Adopt a systematic approach to human resource management |High 12 – 18 months|Workforce|Department under which cell is housed and UNICEF| | Ensuring monitoring and evaluation of SBCC cells |High 6 – 12 months|Leadership & Governance, Workforce, Service Delivery, Information|Department under which cell is housed and UNICEF| | SBCC interventions should be more explicit and strategically integrated to gender equity |High 6 – 12 months|Across all Indicators|State Government with UNICEF’s support| | UNICEF to develop SOPs for SBCC cells at state and district level |Medium 12 – 18 months|Service Delivery|UNICEF| | Adopt a systematic approach for evidence based SBCC planning |Medium 12 – 24 months|Information|UNICEF| UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 11 | Development of SBCC cell repository |Medium 12 – 24 months|Information|Department under which cell is housed in states| |---|---|---|---| UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 12 5. Methodology Figure 2: Evaluation Criteria Evaluation criteria and questions For this evaluation, we adapted the Organization for Economic Co-operation, and Development Assistance Committee (OECD-DAC) evaluation criteria of relevance, effectiveness, efficiency, coherence, and sustainability and contextualized it to use as a guide for the evaluation. These criteria provided a normative framework which served as basis for evaluation. We developed key evaluation questions (KEQs) to further elaborate each evaluation criteria. Further, a consultative and iterative process was adopted with the ICO and state teams, to re-sequence, club and recategorize the KEQs (Table 1) Table 1: Categorization of key evaluation questions as per evaluation criteria ||| |---|---| | Evaluation Criteria | Key Evaluation Questions (KEQs) | ||• How relevant and appropriate are the SBCC Cells to the flagship programs, and priorities of the national and state governments? • How relevant, appropriate, and aligned is the design of the SBCC Cell to the State's established systems and institutions of service delivery? • To what extent does the SBCC Cell respond to the needs of community members and community representatives (FLWs, trainees etc. ) • How adaptable is the SBCC Cell to evolving concerns/areas of focus/evidence for realignment (including COVID)| | Relevance || ||| ||• To what extent has setting up of SBCC Cells strengthened government systems in bringing about more effective and evidence-based communication initiatives? • To what extent is evidence-based programming effective in identifying and addressing the challenges to demand generation for RI, health, nutrition, and WASH services? • To what extent have the training equipped stakeholders to plan, implement, monitor behavioral change, and demand generation initiatives? • How effective is the communication strategy, material and content developed by the SBCC Cells towards demand generation for development schemes and initiatives? • To what extent have the SBCC Cells improved collaborations, partnerships, and convergence with allied departments at the national and state level/ Developmental agencies/ corporates? • Have the SBCC Cells successfully fulfilled their role as institutions for supporting evidence generation, strategic planning, operationalization, monitoring, analysis, training, documentation, knowledge sharing, including convening partnerships? • How has the institutionalization of SBCC-based programming through the setting up of SBCC Cells strengthened the health system?| | Effectiveness || ||| ||• Did the SBCC Cells efficiently deliver/implement their activities and function within the planned/allocated monetary resources? • Did the SBCC Cells efficiently deliver/implement their activities and function within the planned/allocated human resources and timelines? • Did the SBCC Cells sufficiently deliver/implement their activities and function within the planned/allocated timelines?| | Efficiency || ||| UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 20 ||| |---|---| | Evaluation Criteria | Key Evaluation Questions (KEQs) | ||• To what extent have the different state departments integrated SBCC programming into its planning and implementation of initiatives? • Does the government (at the state and district-level) have the capacity to successfully carry out the activities of the SBCC Cells independently? • Do the Cells and their implementation structures (at the state and district-level) have adequate financial allocations and partnerships to successfully carry out the activities of the SBCC Cells independently? • What are some of the recommendations to further strengthen the functioning of SBCC Cells to improve health systems delivery at the state/district/block/village levels?| | Sustainability || ||| ||• How do the activities/interventions of the SBCC Cells interlink and synergize with the existing/other programme/initiatives by other relevant actors (NGOs and development partners and other sector players)? • How do the SBCC Cells interventions align/synergize/fit with other interventions being carried out by the state for improving health systems?| | Coherence || ||| 5.1. Conceptual framework: Theory of change Based on document review, consultation with UNICEF ICO and UNICEF SBC specialists across the states under focus for this study, a retrospective Theory of Change (ToC) has been developed for the SBCC Cells. The activities of the Cell are categorised under the five pillars of Health System Strengthening including: (1) Leadership and governance; (2) Workforce; (3) Service Delivery; (4) Finance; and (5) Information. The overall intent of the SBCC Cell emerged to provide support for improvement of demand generation and accelerate uptake of government schemes that can positively impact a child’s wellbeing through enhanced and equitable access to government sponsored services/schemes for health, nutrition, education, water, sanitation, and protection. Accordingly, the outcomes for this model are: (1) Sustainable government systems created for SBCC programming; (2) Improved service delivery* and outreach of government programmes; (3) Improved uptake and beneficiary participation towards government sponsored programmes/schemes; and (4) Positive policy environment created for SBCC programming across sectoral development initiatives. To attain this intent, the pathways of change are designed as following: - If the leadership is sensitised on the need for behavioural change, then increased number of programmes will adopt SBCC at the design and result stage - If SBCC Cell is institutionalised under the government set-up then SBCC-based programming will be more sustainable, accountable, and transparent - If SBCC relevant content is integrated in programme planning and capacity development, then beneficiary identification, targeting, coverage and outreach will improve - If IPC capability of functionaries is strengthened, then their convincing power and quality of service delivery will improve - If the desired behaviour is established, then communities will actively participate in monitoring service delivery and thereby demand for improved service quality Overarching assumptions: Availability of a nodal govt department/institutional set up to host the SBCC Cell; adequate resource mobilization by nodal department/institution in supporting competent HR and financial budgets; availability of a network of community mobilizers to support awareness and demand for services. Risks: - Given the vast socio-cultural diversity, inability to identify behavioural triggers based on social norms and cultural practices - If housed within a Directorate, difficulty in convergence and resource pooling - If not housed under a Directorate, difficulty in getting participation from functionaries and officers (under other line departments) at the district and community level - Restricted budget for mix-media integration - Inhibition of shifting from IEC based approach UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 21 - Limited digital literacy amongst service providers and community representatives to adopt/harness technology enabled communication channels (particularly in post COVID era) In collating the information, it was noted that the SBCC Cells under review have distinctive characteristics, which although similar, are not comparable. According to the nature of institution and the thematic area of focus, the states were clubbed under three categories (1) SBCC Cells housed under the health department and based at the state level – Assam; (2) SBCC Cells housed under the health department, based at state and district level - Odisha, Madhya Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh; and (3) SBCC Cells housed outside the Health Department: West Bengal (with district level cells), Telangana (without district level cells). Accordingly, the team had developed (1) an overarching ToC capturing the broad intent and core areas of focus or change; and (2) three supporting ToCs as per the categorization of the states. This section provides an overarching ToC which will be finalized in the next phase (data collection) in detailed consultation with the states and ICO. Figure 2 below highlights the contents of the overarching ToC developed. Figure 2: Overarching Theory of Change ==> picture [496 x 297] intentionally omitted <== 5.2. Evaluation design The evaluation focused on key building blocks in the structure, design, and operation of SBCC Cells. The core themes included nature of workforce, mechanism of service delivery, financing, leadership and design and execution of governance and information and knowledge management. Along with the OECD DAC evaluation criteria, the evaluation also included the domain of gender and social equity. In the detailed evaluation framework (refer to Annexure 1), we have mapped indicators with the KEQs as per all the overall evaluation criteria. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 22 Figure 3: Representative image of evaluation sites 5.3. Sampling design i. Selection of states The assessment was planned across six states in India across the country’s regions: Assam, West Bengal, Odisha, Jharkhand, Telangana and Madhya Pradesh. The states were selected purposively considering UNICEF’s categorization of high burden states (Madhya Pradesh), tribal (Odisha, North-East), good governance (Telangana). Geographical variance in the eastern region (North-East (Assam), East (Odisha, West Bengal), Central (Madhya Pradesh and Jharkhand) and South (Telangana) was ensured, providing a more representative picture of the program. However, Jharkhand, a sample state for the evaluation, was later replaced with Uttar Pradesh. This change was made considering that the SBCC Cell in Jharkhand has been handed over to the government. prior to the evaluation period. Hence, the evaluation of this cell would not enable assessment of UNICEF’s role in supporting the cell during the evaluation period. Evaluation in the states of Odisha, Madhya Pradesh, West Bengal, and Uttar Pradesh was done at two levels as there are two levels of SBCC Cells, at the state and district level. In the remaining two states of, Assam and Telangana, the SBCC Cells only function at the state level, hence the evaluation was carried out at the state level. ==> picture [227 x 270] intentionally omitted <== ii. Selection of districts/blocks We understand that marginalized communities including scheduled castes, scheduled tribes, religious minorities, and persons with disabilities have been historically excluded and may be under-represented/covered/reported across development programmes intervention focus and results. While there have been efforts towards taking into consideration the need for greater intervention focus, and integration of such stakeholders within active planning and implementation of SBCC and related programming, there are further opportunities no ted in this regard. [45] In this context, a stepwise methodology was used to ensure diversity of population in the selection of districts in the sample states. Step 1: Within diversity of population, district level values of percentage of scheduled caste population, percentage of scheduled tribe population, percentage of population of religious minorities and total district level population of Persons with Disability was mapped across the sample states. The Census 2011[46] data was considered for the above-mentioned indicators. Step 2: Two districts per sample state were selected based on values mapped across indicators mentioned above, elaborating the overall diversity of population[47] . One district with a lower than state average value across the selected indicators and another with a higher than state average value across the selected indicators per state was selected. Further, distance from state capital was also considered in the process of district selection. Table 2:Presentation of indicators mapped for selection of sample districts > 45 UNICEF Gender Results Report, 2020 > 46 https://censusindia.gov.in/census.website/ Accessed on September 06th 2022 > 47 It is to be noted that other indicators were considered to support mapping the proposed district selection across sample states. The indicators include availability of year of establishment of district level cells and year of implementation of first activity by SBCC Cell. However, there was limited availability of relevant information on these indicators across the secondary evidence for all sample states. In consultation with UNICEF, it was agreed to propose diversity of population as a potential indicator for mapping the district selection. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 23 ==> picture [494 x 247] intentionally omitted <== Table 3: Evaluation sites |||| |---|---|---| | Region | State | Districts | |||| |North – East|Assam|Dubri, Morigaon*| |Central|Madhya Pradesh|Shivpuri, Guna| |East|Odisha|Puri, Mayurbhanj| |East|West Bengal|South 24 Parganas, Purulia| |North|Uttar Pradesh|Mirzapur, Balarampur| |South|Telangana|Mahbubnagar, Hyderabad| Note: West Karbi, Anglong was one of the proposed evaluation districts in Assam initially. However, we had to change the district to Morigaon due to conflict in the district. The district was chosen as it was next in the list as per the criteria followed for selection of study sites. We followed the steps given below to arrive at sample size for the evaluation Step 1 - Stakeholder mapping: We undertook stakeholder mapping across national, state, district, sub-district, and community level. This exercise's intent was to systematically capture the variant group of stakeholders engaged in supporting and implementing the activities of the Cells to be included in the evaluation. These stakeholders were categorized and clubbed under 16 sub-headings across various levels. Mapping was done vis-à-vis document review and in discussion with the respective UNICEF state /SBC officers and specialists. Step 2 - Selection of sample size in consultation with the respective UNICEF state /SBC officers and specialists. For this evaluation, respondents were selected purposively considering the area of enquiry. The table below provides an overview of the stakeholder categorization and listing. It indicates the representative stakeholders who hold their respective positions across the sample states. It must be noted that these positions were not uniform across the states and variations were noted across the range of stakeholders engaged. With reference to the overall categorization of stakeholders, state specific stakeholder included in the study have been provided in the annexure. Table 4: Distribution of stakeholder interviewed by type UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 24 | Level | # | Stakeholder Category | Type of stakeholders | |---|---|---|---| | National |1.|UNICEF India Country Office|Chief SBC, SBC Specialist| | State | 2. |Nodal Leadership, SBCC Cell|Director IEC Bureau/ DG Directorate of Medical and Health Services/etc.| || 3. |Functionaries of the SBCC Cell|RMNCH+A Officers/ Media officers/ Deputy Media officer/ MEIO/ General Manager IEC (NHM PMU)/ etc.| ||||State communication officer/ State Planning and monitoring officers/ Content development officers/ State IEC Officer| || 4. |UNICEF Consultant (loaned staff)|Technical Consultant| || 5. |Leadership, Nodal government department|Mission Director NHM/ Director General/ Director Dept. of HFS/ Director ICDS/ etc.| || 6. |Collaborating government official|Govt. officials of the nodal govt department including, SEPIO/ IO| || 7. |Allied department' officials|Director or Officers from WCD/ Culture/ PRI/ RD/ SC& ST dept.| || 8. |UNICEF state officials|SBC Specialist| || 9. |Government officials responsible for the setting up of SBCC Cells(if different from current officials)|Ex-officials of Nodal government department/ SBCC Cell head| || 10. |UNICEF officials responsible for the setting up of SBCC Cells (if different from current officials)|Ex-UNICEF state SBC Specialists/ Officers who were in the state when the Cell was established| || 11. |Development agencies with which the SBCC Cell has collaborated|International Development Agencies/ allied UN agencies| ||||National/ state level NGOs/ CSO/ Medical college| | District 2 districts to be covered per state| 12. |Head of District SBCC Cell/ district SBCC set-up|District Magistrate/ CDMO/ CEO Zila Panchayat| || 13. |Officials supporting communication planning at district level|DHEIO/ DPHCO/ DMC/ DMEIO/ DIO/ Key Resource Person (KRP) from collaboration departments| || 14. |SBC Cadre at district level (Master Trainers)|(District level representatives who have been engaged in SBCC related training and dissemination since last 6 months)48| | Block | 15. |SBCC Cadre at sub-district level (Master Trainers)|(BCM/ BEE/ BPHEO) (Representatives who have been engaged in SBCC related training and dissemination since last 6 months)| || 16. |Trainees of SBCC cells|FLWs (ANM/ASHA/AWWs/ASHA Sahyogini/ CSO/ CBO| || 17. |Community Members|Parents and caregivers| Table 5: Sample size covered per state > 48 The relevant MT designation for district level were discussed with UNICEF state SBC officials while the evaluators undertake the coordination related discussions in planning for state consultations as part of the data collection phase. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 25 | # | Name of the state | State level | State level | Divisional Level | Divisional Level | District level | District level | Block Level | Block Level | Total interactions | Total interactions | |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---| |||No of interactions in IR|No of interactions completed|No of interactions in IR|No of interactions completed|No of interactions in IR|No of interactions completed|No of interactions in IR|No of interaction completed|Total interactions mentioned in IR|Total interactions completed on field| | 1 | Uttar Pradesh |7|4|2|1|3|4|8|6|20| 15 | | 2 | Madhya Pradesh |14|11|NA|NA|16|9|5|7|26| 26 | | 3 | Telangan a |9|7|NA|NA|1|1|5|5|15| 13 | | 4 | West Bengal |5|3|NA|NA|12|9|5|7|22| 19 | | 5 | Assam |15|7|NA|NA|5|4|5|8|25| 19 | | 6 | Odisha |14|19|NA|NA|7|6|8|8|30| 33 | | 7 | National level |NA|NA|NA|NA|NA|NA|NA|NA|01| 01 | || Total Interactions proposed in IR and completed on field |||||||||139| 126 | 5.4. Data collection methods The data was collected through qualitative interview guides/ FGD guide. Pilot testing of both the qualitative and FGD guides was conducted to assess the feasibility of data collection tools in Mandar district of Jharkhand in September- October 2022. After the successful completion of the pilot testing, a team consultation was arranged, and the tools were revised based on the observations from the pilot. The revisions included incorporating new areas of inquiry which emerged during the discussion, and feedback received on the questions framed during the pilot testing. The tools were administered by a trained research team, oriented, and trained on the study methodology and technical components of interview/ FGD guides. The study team who conducted the interviews were public health professionals with prior experience of conducting such studies. On field training of the team for conducting the interviews was also carried out. Safety protocols were followed during the data collection to counter the spread of COVID-19 and protect all the individuals associated. To ensure the quality of collected data, certain identified aspects were verified through observations such as availability of IEC material (Flyers, posters, media clips on social media), display of IEC material at the SBCC cell, and health centers etc. And those related to routine practices were assessed through observation and record reviews. Similarly, the team corroborated aspects on capacity building by SBCC cells by observation of training like ToT for master resource person and other capacity building sessions. The questionnaire was administered in Hindi/ English based on respondents’ preference. FGD guide was administered in local languages taking the help of translators. Translators were provided with an orientation on the study methodology and FGD guide beforehand. The team of researchers was trained to administer the questionnaire in the respective languages. The qualitative interviews were audio-recorded after getting the oral consent of the respondents. In case the consent for audio recordings was not obtained, manual notes were taken for the same. On average, the interviews took 30 minutes each. The interview was stopped when information saturation was reached i.e., no new information was emerging from the interaction. Data collection in each state took five days of time, by a team of 2-3 researchers. Data was collected during November 2022 to January 2023. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 26 5.5. Analytical approaches Desk review and secondary data analysis A large volume and a comprehensive number of documents were reviewed in this evaluation including the program documents shared by the state SBCC cells including state case studies, power point presentations and communication material and reports publications by UNICEF including C4D progress reports. Detailed list of The secondary data was subjected to content analysis to get insights on aspects such as structure of SBCC cells, its functional and thematic areas and key challenges in functioning. Qualitative interviews and focused group discussions The evaluation team considered the design of the programme, its organic evolution, its’ state-specific context, variations, and core areas of focus while developing the data analysis approach. Given that this study is primarily qualitative in nature, the data was analysed as follows: Interviews and Focus groups discussion : Coding of the transcriptions was conducted using reflexive thematic analysis. Themes were mapped to various components of the Organization for Economic Co-operation, and Development Assistance Committee (OECD-DAC) evaluation criteria of relevance, effectiveness, efficiency, coherence, and sustainability. Coding framework was shared with the larger study team to form the consensus, and accordingly finalized. The study findings have used illustrative quotations in English where required. Coding and analysis of qualitative data was carried out using Microsoft Excel. Field level Observation: The data for this is drawn from the inputs gathered across the parameters noted in the observation checklist. This information was added under relevant sections of state wise findings Steps followed in analysis of qualitative data- Step 1: Transcription of the recorded interviews /discussions into English were done manually. Particular care was taken during the transcription so that there is no loss of information during the process. To ensure quality of transcriptions, researchers and supervisors undertook random checks by listening to audio recordings. Step 2: Development of coding framework and coding of qualitative data: Coding framework was developed as per the KEQs and SEQs to categorize responses using the information areas covered in the discussion guides. However, this list was augmented based on other codes that emerged from the transcripts during the actual coding process. After carefully reading the transcriptions, information from transcriptions was put into the relevant code Triangulation of qualitative interview data and other secondary information was conducted to synthesize the evaluation findings and draw conclusions. ==> picture [430 x 227] intentionally omitted <== UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 27 5.6. Limitations of the study - The use of quantitative and numerical data points pertaining to health, behavioral and social indicators is limited within the scope of this evaluation. A link of causality and/or attributability cannot be formed between said datapoints and findings of the study. The scope of the study is limited to understanding contribution of SBCC cell in health system strengthening. - We could not cover the target sample as we could not conduct few stakeholder consultations across state, district and block levels owing to unavailability of relevant stakeholders or prior commitments to other engagements or unresponsiveness to communications shared requesting for consultations. However, the response rate for the study was approximately 90 percent, which is acceptable in social science research. - There was limited awareness of SBCC related activities among both trainers, trainees of SBCC and community members across a few sites. Further, the recently transferred recruitments had limited institutional memory on the evolution of the SBCC Cell in the state. Limited relevant insights were received on the nature of work done and challenges faced in doing so and evolution of SBCC cell through the interactions conducted with the above stakeholder groups. - There is a lack of any prior in-depth, comprehensive baseline studies that could offer insights into all dimensions of SBCC programming, that could further be used or referred to during the evaluation. Hence, it was observed that the gaps in covering and strengthening SBCC programmes in certain areas in each state was largely attributed to the absence of on-ground research and profiling based on demographic and socioeconomic status of the populations in the respective settlements. - There was lack of institutional memory within the government related to SBCC cell including relevant documentation which might have resulted in recall bias. The evaluation team tried to include appropriate probes in the questionnaire to aid respondents’ recall of activities. The team obtained relevant documents from the state SBCC team to fulfil the objectives of the evaluation. - As there is no state level SBCC Cell in Uttar Pradesh, the stakeholders mapped at the state level were from different IEC cells across line departments with limited technical context and awareness of SBCC related activities. As a result, the input received from such consultations highlighted limited relevant insights on the same. - In Assam, West Karbi, Anglong was one of the proposed evaluation districts initially. However, we had to change the district to Morigaon due to conflict in the district. - The good practices that we have documented in the report have been compiled by information received from the respondents and we have not validated the same through field observations. 5.7. Ethics and UNEG Standards Data collection team comprised of PwC staff including senior consultant and manager. All members of the team completed a foundational ethics training specifically UNICEF’s course ‘Introduction to Ethics in Evidence Generation’ on Agora before conducting the evaluation. The study team abided by 2021 UNICEF procedure on ethics and Evaluation and UNEG norms and standards for Evaluation. The team involved in primary data collection were oriented on protocols for ensuring compliance to ethics, gender sensitivity and equity principles in data collection and management. The data collection methods employed for the evaluation did not include any interaction with children or adolescents. All stakeholders and evaluation participants identified for consultations and data collection, were duly informed on the study, its intent, how the input received is proposed to be used and limits of confidentiality. Mandatory oral consent was obtained from all the respondents before the interview / discussion, and any queries, concerns, or questions were addressed prior to the interview / discussion. All respondents were informed about the voluntary nature of participation in the study and if they wish to withdraw after the consent, then they are free to do so. Each project team member engaged in the ensured adherence to non-disclosure of any data received or accessed in course of the evaluation. The study team undertook measures to maintain the confidentiality of the respondents. During the process of synthesizing of findings personal identifiers were removed from the database and unique identification codes (UID) were used while analyzing the data. All interview data including audio files of recordings and transcriptions UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 28 were handled as per the research protocols and the project manager ensured that access to data was restricted and shared on a need-to-know and use basis. Protocols for interactions and data collection including on-field conduct, particularly with women, and other vulnerable groups, were defined. Adherence to "do no harm" principle, respect for dignity and diversity, compliance with codes was ensured for the same. Further, team was trained and periodically oriented on the protocols. To maintain the quality of evaluation we ensured following points. - Clarity of roles and reporting lines were well defined. Each person had specific responsibilities and clear job descriptions. Their outputs and deliverables directly contributed to the project’s overall impact. - Each team member had clear objectives at the start of each project year in discussion with the project leadership team. There was a protocol to update the TL (Team Leader) and project manager daily o n field interactions and proceedings. The performance of the core team was assessed on a monthly basis. Training and mentoring were ensured if any member faced challenge in delivering quality results. - The TL and the subject matter experts were engaged at state level interactions. Spot quality control checks were in place to address grievance if any during the evaluation on field. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 29 6. Findings The SBCC Cell Evaluation covered six states namely, Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh, Assam, West Bengal, Odisha, and Telangana. The findings have been triangulated from various sources of data such as secondary literature review, UNICEF state team presentations and primary data collection across all six states. Each state’s Cell structure can be categorised broadly as those which are housed within the health department, those which are outside of the purview of the health department, those which are with state level institutional setup and those with the state as well as district level institutional setup. | Category | State | |---|---| | Category 1 : SBCC cells housed under the health dept. and based at the state level|Assam| | Category 2 : SBCC cells housed under the health department, based at state and district level|Uttar Pradesh, Odisha and Madhya Pradesh| | Category 3 : SBCC cells housed outside the Health Department|West Bengal (with district cells) and Telangana (no district cells)| The findings are presented across these categories and have been mapped against the key evaluation questions across the five OECD-DAC criteria of Relevance, Effectiveness, Efficiency, Sustainability and Coherence. The evaluation includes an overall cross-cutting theme of gender and equity embedded within the adopted methodology and are presented in the findings accordingly. To establish a holistic understanding about the evaluation criteria, each of these have been elaborated below: 6.1. Relevance Relevance as an evaluation parameter aims to analyse the extent to which the intervention objectives and design respond to beneficiaries, global, country, and partner/institution needs, policies, and priorities, and continue to do so if circumstances change. To evaluate the level of relevance for the SBCC program, state level officials, UNICEF consultants (loaned staff) and district level officials of SBCC cells were asked to comment upon the program’s alignment to national and state level flagship programs, usage of established systems of delivery and the cells’ response to community needs. Similarly, to assess the adaptability of SBCC cells to COVID 19 response by assessing modified approach to SBCC activities during the pandemic. KEQ 1: How relevant and appropriate are the SBCC Cells to the flagship programs, and priorities of the national and state governments? Category 1 There is a relevance of the SBCC Cells to the flagship programs particularly as they are under the National Health Mission (NHM) and with the Directorate of Health Services, Family Welfare (DHSFW). The integrated SBCC activities are aimed at addressing health topics and behaviors for improved health and development outcomes. Mission Indradhanush has been a major action-oriented programme of the Cell to strengthen immunization coverage in priority districts which have less than 50 per cent unvaccinated or partially vaccinated children. Mission Tejaswee was aimed at reducing maternal, neonatal and child mortality. Over the years, special immunization drives have targeted children and expectant mothers to increase the immunization coverage to 90 per cent. Although Assam made considerable improvements in Routine Immunization, currently, the Cell’s focus is on urban immunization, as it was found that immunization coverage is lower in urban areas as compared to rural areas. The relevance of RI has helped to have wider reach of demand generation for RI activities. Largely, the Call to Action for Child Survival and Development initiative by the Govt. of India was concurrent with the Reproductive, Maternal, New-born Child Health +Adolescent (RMNCH+A) Strategy for addressing child survival and reduction of Infant Mortality Rate (IMR), and Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR). SBCC communication comprised mass media spots, open book wall painting, folk media, community theatre, rallies by school children, dissemination of IPC (ICDS and health service providers, and short videos and ASHA radio programs on UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 30 Immunization which were some of the key strategies. During Covid-19, the importance of hygiene and sanitation were reiterated through key messages on handwashing, social distancing, etc. District task force meetings were organized for SBCC coordinators across all 33 districts of Assam. The BCC activities have been under the Child Health or Maternal Health are included in their respective sections. The SBCC cell under NHM receive financial support through the State’s PIP, as the NHM PIP is the main source of funding. IEC-related planning has been the aggregate of the budget of IEC under the umbrella of the NHM at state level. Category 2 SBCC cells in category two are housed under the health department and hence work well within the ambit of flagship programmes of this department. The cells act as a facilitator of communication activities for various health interventions and programs. For example, in UP the cell contributes to programs such as Mission Indra Dhanush, Dastak Sanchari and Measles and rubella awareness; in Odisha the cell contributes to programs for routine immunization and RMNCHA+; and in Madhya Pradesh the cell has been able to contribute to programs such as Beti Bachao Beti Padhao. The cells aid in developing strategic plans and materials for integrating SBCC programming in flagship health programs of the state and national level. For example, in UP to increase vaccine coverage under Mission Indra Dhanush, the cell was able to recognise the importance of interpersonal communication (IPC) to increase a trust building process between the community members and frontline health workers. The cell was then able to train the frontline health workers in the skills of IPC which aided the said workers in spreading awareness about the importance of vaccination and reduce vaccine hesitancy. Further, since the cells monetary allocation comes from the respective flagship programs, their capacity to function and develop strategic plans is restricted to the aspects of said programs. Category 3 In the case of category 3, the cells are housed outside of the health department under NIRDPR (CRU) ad WCD (West Bengal cell). Hence, the cells’ functioning does not align with core health flagship programs such as Mission Indra Dhanush; rather the cells’ functioning aligns with supporting health programs related to nutrition, WASH and sanitation. For example, CRU in Telangana has contributed to flagship programs such as SBM, JJM and Poshan Abhiyan by developing communication strategies which focus on integrating SBCC into the planning and programming stages of these programs, providing training to master trainers who are able to further disseminate their learnings at the field level and contextualization of SBCC and IEC material by translating to local languages and changing of social examples and imagery to those which are locally relevant. For the cell in West Bengal, the expected area of focus is ICDS for Nutrition and the four largest schemes viz., Kanyasree, Rupasree, Lakshmir Bhandar and Social Welfare schemes such as old age and widow pension. The cell contributes to these programs by developing differentiated district wise communications plans based on community needs. The expected role of the cell is that of a convergence agency connecting the dots required for communications around the focus areas of WCD such as nutrition. ==> picture [476 x 63] intentionally omitted <== KEQ 2: How relevant, appropriate and aligned is the design of the SBCC Cell to the State’s established systems and institutions of service delivery? Category 1 The Health Communication in Assam is implemented by the District Extension Media Officer (DEMO), (now the state Cold Chain officer under NHM. The other members include DHSFW at the district level, Block Community Mobilizer (BCM), NHM and Health Educator (HE)/Block Extension Educator, DHSFW at the blocks. ASHA and ASHA Supervisors are the main frontline cadre involved in informing communities on available health services, disseminating health messages and assisting in utilization of health services. In addition to immunization, service delivery at the state level have comprised various innovations towards reduction of mortality, cleft-lip, and distribution of iron tablets in school, etc such as through the Rural Health Practitioners (RHP). Furthermore, the state is looking at launching the Boat Ambulance and Clinic concept (a good practice from Bangladesh) as was shared by an SBCC officer participant, the Mobile Medical Unit (MMU), ASHA Radio Program, Sarathi-104 (Health Information Helpline & Complaint Redressal system), etc. However, on ground it was found that accessibility of these in remote and hard-to-reach areas such as the _Chars_ – areas or lands which are surrounded by water, UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 31 were difficult, and still a major concern. The gaps in Reproductive Maternal Newborn Child and Adolescent Heath (RMNCH+A), have been addressed and reduced although services and knowledge and awareness related to more fundamental issues of child/early marriage, teen pregnancy, and school dropout, need to be addressed and tackled for integration of social behavior change, and health concerns related to adolescents and women. This is particularly true of the two districts of Dhubri and Morigaon in the evaluation study where it was found that the aforementioned practices are highly prevalent in LODOR groups, largely in the economically vulnerable and hardto-each areas. These two districts are also the ones which have seen the least intersectoral convergence among the 33 districts during Covid-19. The cells in this category occasionally converge fundamentally in RI work, polio and vaccination, although collaborations with other departments are not yet strengthened, specifically to tackle issues of early marriage and adolescent SRH which need more attention and convergence. Category 2 The cells in category two are highly aligned to the states’ established systems and institutions of service delivery. Since the cells are housed under the health department, they are able to leverage and make use of established systems such as the differentiated cadres of frontline health workers. Further, the cells do not have a dedicated communications cadre and hence comprise of members who are government employees in the positions of health officials, hence leveraging the state’s established human resource cadre within the health system. Even the cells’ activities are planned in accordance with the established programming and timelines of the health department. Hence, the cells’ functioning is not only aligned but can be said to be dependent on the states’ established systems and institutions of service delivery. Further, the cells in this category have positive convergence and collaborations with other relevant departments such as their respective DWCD. This allows for even greater access to the states’ established systems and institutions of service delivery. For example, the cells in this category are able to leverage the Anganwadi system too under the DWCD, in addition to the established systems of the health department. Category 3 The cells in category three have access to the state’s established systems and institutions of service delivery of the respective department they are housed under. For eg, CRU has access to leverage the PRI cadre as it is housed under NIRDPR and the cell in West Bengal has access to the Angwadi system as it is house under DWCD. Further, the cells do not have a dedicated communications cadre and hence comprise of members who are government employees in the positions of ICDS and PRI officials, hence leveraging the state’s established human resource cadre within the state’s established systems. For the cell in West Bengal, even the cells’ activities are planned in accordance with the established programming and timelines of the DWCD. CRU has an added advantage of being housed under a national institute which gives it access to the institutes’ established systems and institutions of service delivery (SIRDs) at the state level too. KEQ 3: To what extent does the SBCC Cell respond to the needs of the community members and community representatives? Category 1 One of the major activities of the Cell’s engagement in responding to the needs of communities and representatives has been in the RMNCH+A coverage, and to increase awareness amongst the mothers and communities about the need of ANC and post-partum health. Although, the key focus and approach has been on the (six) High Priority Districts, there is an overall gap in, and the need to cover all vulnerable and hard -to-reach areas across the state’s 33 districts. It was noted that communication gaps exist in tea garden areas of indigenous populations, and char areas which do not yet have a flow of transport facilities. It was also found that there is a limited capacity of FLHWs to tackle issues of ineffectual communication. Most FLWS in the workforce is currently aging and ae close to retirement, while there is concurrently, a shortage of FLHWs due to a lack of recruitment. The FLHWs need continued and advanced training in improving their interpersonal skills to engage better with communities. A direct impact of this scenario has led to the impact of demand for service utilization. There is a lack of detailed, lucid explanation of locally relevant SBCC material particularly for indigenous populations speaking dialects. Thus, there is a need to create a communication strategy with appropriate and relevant messages for specific populations in specific areas. During Covid-19, the SBCC officers have themselves travelled to sensitive districts for addressing vaccine hesitancy of communities, by self -demonstrating the criticality of vaccine on themselves. Further, SBCC Cells have reached out to religious leaders and other Faith Based Organizations through an orientation, such that the importance of covid-19 vaccination, and immunization, UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 32 and early marriage prevention can be addressed. However, not much has been addressed as wider SBCC activities need to be undertaken with coordinated efforts at the district and block levels which need intervention of SBCC Cells’ officers. Although there is one SBCC coordinator in each district, there is a need for capacity building. At present, IEC materials are the usual brochures and pamphlets which the FLWs and community influencers use as guiding sources for information. The overarching health issues related to mothers and expectant mothers In the last few years have increased mainly with attention to vaccine, RI, measles, etc. However, system strengthening through sufficient budgeting, M&E etc for state-level plans for RI across 33 districts in the state are necessary. For example, IMR/MMR, child marriage, etc are social concerns that are not yet addressed with any preventive measures. Reportedly, there is a case of child (under-age) marriage at 32 percent in Assam. Departments can play a role in better integration and convergence, but there is a limitation and challenge on account of no convening powers. Jingles, banners are not relevant and sufficient as awareness needs to be generated through actual on-groundwork. FLWs need advanced training to be able to be more communicable with communities. Conclusively, the targeted health-related activities and communication have been carried out within the usual and prescribed tasks of the SBCC programmes, but crucial and urgent social concerns of women and adolescent SRH, teen pregnancy, safeguarding rights of girls (early marriage), and school dropout need urgent responsive and preventive measures need to be set to be able to address community needs and generate awareness. ==> picture [476 x 54] intentionally omitted <== Category 2 Since the cells function not only at the state level but also at a district level, they are in regular communication with community members and representatives such as frontline health workers. They are able to provide regular guidance and oversight of dissemination of health messaging at the community level. Though there is a major limitation in the cells ability to respond to the needs of community members and representatives due to resource and budget constraints. It was found that the cells’ approach is top down as they function only on the mandate of the state government and have no flexibility to include findings from formal and informal needs assessments done at the community level. Since the cells are provided with monetary allocation through the budget for each respective program, they are given detailed instructions on how much to spend and are restricted to spend the fund only on items given on a state mandated list of IEC and SBCC materials. ==> picture [476 x 55] intentionally omitted <== Category 3 CRU does not have a mandate to work at the community or implementation level. Hence, it does not directly contribute to responding to the needs of community members and representatives. Though it works closely with allied departments like WCD which have grassroot level presence and utilizes secondary data to analyse and understand the needs of community members while designing the SBCC programme for various programs under different departments. Since the cells in West Bengal functions at a district level, they are in regular communication with community members and representatives such as frontline health workers. They are able to provide regular guidance and oversight of dissemination of health messaging at the community level. Further, the cells are able to contribute to responding to the needs of the community members by developing customised strategies for communications focused on local contexts and the needs of community members and representatives. KEQ 4: How adaptable is the SBCC Cell to evolving concerns/areas of focus/evidence for realignment (including COVID) Category 1 UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 33 During Covid-19, the SBCC cells were active in rolling out vaccines, and most people in the cells were directly or indirectly involved in vaccine roll out programmes. As part of the Covid-19 communication activities, state cells covered populations under the 33 districts, wherein district coordinators visited sessions which were conducted, and gathered information through mobile phones. The district coordinators covered and conducted these interactive sessions to address logistical issues of vaccine accessibility of diverse populations groups. It was noted that the state was the first towards attaining effective vaccine management (EVM). However, as was found that at the state and district level, there was no direct Covid-management of medical activities of doctors; rather it was largely focused on administrative tasks. In terms of adaptability, post-Covid, social media has not had a substantial role, even though it is a pressing need with comprehensive awareness communication material for MR issues, and additionally, there is no information generation or awareness/coverage for RI through social media. Furthermore, no training has been conducted in the last 2 years for ASHA/Aanganwadi workers, while No BRIDGE training has been conducted post virtual training that was conducted during Covid. The lack of training of FLHWs has been a barrier in improving and advancing issues on communication and attitudes related to health both from a micro-level and macro level perspectives. Category 2 As the cells are housed under the health department, and with COVID-19 being a public health issue, the cells were able to contribute to the COVID response for their respective states. The cells were able to conduct online training for frontline health workers when social distancing was recommended during the pandemic. Further, the cells were able to train frontline health workers to use novel means of communication such as telephone and WhatsApp to deliver health messaging to community members while maintaining physical distance. The cells were also able to act as a repository for online IEC and SBCC material which was sent out to the frontline health workers and health officials to be spread amongst community members. The cells have been able to adapt to other evolving concerns too such as being introduced to an increased focus on ODF practices. Category 3 The cells in this category reported to have flexibility to be able to adapt to evolving concerns and areas of focus. For example, CRU was able to contribute to designing and providing technical assistance in developing Covid19 response not just for the states in serves (namely Andhra Pradesh, Telangana and Karnataka) but also for other states such as Sikkim. Largely, at the state level, the cells are able to adapt national level IEC material into regionally contextual material includes translation into local languages and changing of imagery. For West Bengal, at the district level, citizen-centric steps are being taken to have a wider reach and needs of the target beneficiaries. The districts level cells work on issues of institutional delivery, child marriage, adolescent health, malnutrition, menstrual hygiene etc. The cells have been very active during COVID 19 and mobilization during COVID vaccination. The district level cells were able to act as a convergence point for BMOHs and frontline health workers during the pandemic hi. SBCC cell aligns with the needs arising from time to time. Conclusion The cells in all three categories are gradually working towards the pathways of change indicated for the first and fourth longer term outcomes* in the theory of change namely, institutionalization of SBCC programming through sustainable systemic reforms, and positive policy environment created for SBCC programming across sectoral development initiatives. The outputs pertaining to relevance as an evaluation indicator are leadership and officials being trained on SBCC programming reflects the shift from IEC to SBCC and joint programmes being planned and organised with allied sectors and meetings conducted with the same for SBCC. The cells were found to be taking active initiative to train leadership and officials, and in developing joint efforts with other development actors. Although considerable progress is yet to be made. (*Outcomes two and three, namely, improved service delivery and outreach of government programmes, and improved uptake and beneficiary participation towards government sponsored programmes/schemes are beyond the scope of this evaluation.) UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 34 My Village, My Home campaign The Maternal and Child Health Integrated Program (MCHIP) adopted a community-level tool-based programme called My Village, My Home. The objective of this tool is to strengthen service delivery and RI coverage for communities, assess the high drop-out rates and to set up a system of tracking beneficiaries in districts, and to improve the quality of coverage. FLWs use this visual depiction of the immunization status of infants and make use of the community to monitor the immunization coverage of all infants in the locality. The tool is designed in a way that the information on the oldest infant in the community of the respective village is on the bottom row, while that of the younger infants are in the rows upward. The dates of vaccination are written or highlighted in colours in the respective boxes against the names. The advantage of the tool is that it has increased intersectoral collaboration and is a community-friendly tool that can be accessed easily by parents of children, and the ASHA, Anganwadi and ANM workers. 6.2. Effectiveness Effectiveness as an evaluation parameter, helps in analysing the extent to which an initiative or intervention has been able to achieve its goals and objectives. It highlights the intervention’s success in terms of producing the desired effects. It helps in understanding, whether the intervention has structural or implementational limitations. In the course of SBCC cell’s evaluation, effectiveness was measured through, their role as institutions for evidence generation, strategic planning, monitoring, training, documentation and analysis, knowledge sharing, cell's contribution in health system strengthening and effectiveness of strategy, material and content developed by the cell. We gauged the effectiveness of SBCC with respect to above mentioned categories by interviewing state and district level officials of SBCC cells and corroborated the same through interactions with master trainers, FLW, and community members and observation of training and workshops across the field. KEQ 1: To what extent has setting up of SBCC Cells strengthened government systems in bringing about more effective and evidence-based communication initiatives Category 1 The transition from IEC to SBCC although formally occurred between 2014-2015, in practice, the Cells are largely continuing as IEC bureau. Although, an evidence-based communication strategy was designed with support from UNICEF in Assam, there is a lack of evidence generation in terms of regular, updated documentation, particularly in the High Priority aspirational districts, detailed mapping of hard-to-reach areas, Monitoring and Evaluation (M&E) at district and block levels, and prioritizing IPC and evidence-building and documentation in tribal belts (hills), Char areas, and tea estates. While the state has worked effectively in evidence based RMNCH+A and RI strategies, there is a potential to further address LODOR areas through innovative communication material and strategies, and through in-depth studies for evidence generation. There was no reported participatory studies or strategy pertaining to M&E plans. The Special Immunization Drive (SID) for immunization coverage of dropout and left-out children was conducted using household display of color-coded stickers (Red, Yellow and Green) based on Immunization status were pasted on houses. The system of stickers ensured easy identification of houses for immunization coverage using community monitoring. Additionally, the HBNC Voucher System worked well and may be considered an evidence-based communication as it helped mothers and FLHWs to be updated on home visits which were documentary evidence. However, there was no mention or observation of the HBNC Voucher system on field. The SBCC Cell has received good support from UNICEF for work on RI, Training and Communication activities. The Har Ghar Dastak campaign has been an effective programme that reached out to LODOR areas through awareness generation, mobilization, and vaccination activities. To a great extent, it can be observed that the SBCC Cell has strengthened the government system specifically in the health-related development, i.e., Immunization and such, but needs to do more to collaborate with government’s efforts on behavioral change at the ground. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 35 Category 2 The cells in category two have not been able to focus on evidence generation to develop evidence-based communication initiatives or planning. Though, it is important to note that the national and state programs that the cells contribute to are planned and programmed with an evidence-based focus and are able to identify vulnerable sections of the community which require higher efforts to be equitably integrated in the service delivery process. The members of the cells are government health officials who do not have the technical expertise to conduct evidence generation studies and engage in data collection. Further the funding for these cells comes through program budgets which only include enough funds for conducting communications activities such as printing of posters for the said program, and does not take into account other aspects of SBCC such as evidence generation, monitoring, etc. Category 3 The cells participation in primary research is limited. Though within CRU, there was evidence of use of existing secondary data sources (from sources such as NFHS and DHS surveys) for evidence. Further, both cells were able to leverage data collected by WCD’s through its interventions and use it as source of secondary literature. The cells leverage secondary data and evidence generated by line departments such as the WCD who are engaged in implementation activities on field to identify challenges related to demand generation of WASH, child protection and nutrition schemes and services. In this manner, the cells have been able to contribute to developing differentiated communications strategies based on evidence from secondary sources. ==> picture [490 x 79] intentionally omitted <== Research oriented forward looking goals CRU’s (Telangana) main forward-looking goal is to establish itself as a research focused institution. It wishes to focus on primary data collection and drawing in depth behavioral analyses from the data collected. CRU is housed in NIRDPR and is under the leadership of the head of Center of Gender Studies, TISS Hyderabad. Under such a leadership, the cell staff is academically oriented and has a clear understanding of research methodologies. For example, compared to the other state cells, staff of CRU had a distinctly better understanding of the process of evidence generation and the type of resources required to conduct it. KEQ 2: To what extent is evidence-based programming effective in identifying and addressing the challenges to demand generation for RI, health, nutrition, and WASH services? Category 1 At this point, the effectiveness of evidence-based programming in RI, health, nutrition, and WASH services cannot be entirely ascertained for the cell as they have not engaged in evidence-generation activities (as was observed on ground during the evaluation). Category 2 The effectiveness of evidence-based programming and identifying and addressing challenges in routine immunization, health, nutrition, and wash practices cannot be ascertained for the cells in this category as they UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 36 have not been able to engage in evidence generation activities and develop evidence-based programming. (As observed during the evaluation) Category 3 The effectiveness of evidence-based programming and identifying and addressing challenges in routine immunization, health, nutrition, and wash practices cannot be fully ascertained for the cells in this category as they have not been able to engage in primary data collection to generate evidence and develop evidence-based programming. Though through the cells’ success in being able to identify and address the challenges in nutrition and WASH services using secondary sources of evidence suggest a positive effect of evidence-based programming on these processes. It is important to note that since the cells in this category are not housed under the health department, they have not contributed to developing evidence-based programming for identifying and addressing the challenges in RI and health services. (As observed during the evaluation) KEQ 3: To what extent have the training equipped stakeholders to plan, implement, monitor behavioral change, and demand generation initiatives? Category 1 It was gathered from the evaluation, that there are gaps at two levels in the capacity building of the Cells. One is, that there is a lack of vision to design SBCC interventions in terms of innovative design, programmes, and their implementation, and continued newer ways to contextualize and strategize local health communication interventions. Second, that a shortage of (suitably skilled) staff/Cell officers with competencies on account of a lack of organizational capacity within the Cell has hindered the possibilities of conducting and strengthening the capacity building and improve the quality and effectiveness of SBCC work. Vacant positions at key government positions at the State and district level SBCC cells have only stalled capacity strengthening. The Cell’s existing members are only able to execute the tasks assigned and possible within their capacity and purview of their duties on a regular basis. However, capacity building for SBCC officers and FLHWs to increase their skills and knowledge through periodic trainings is lacking at this point. Even though FLHWs have received training on communication intermittently, there is a gap in scaling up their attitudes, knowledge, awareness, and skills to keep pace with the frequent and evolving health schemes and programmes. As it was found, currently, there are no BRIDGE training and people (including trainers) are no familiar with TARANG. FLHWs are not adequately skilled in conducting mid-media events and IPC, or even interact with communities on deeper and wider social and gender and equity concerns. These would require them to gain skill sets and there are gaps in varying degrees across the FLHWs workforce. As was found on the field, processes of feedback sharing, monitoring, and remedying measures to scale up by the Cells are missing. Therefore, as was observed, the Cells are not yet strengthened in providing refresher’s training. ==> picture [483 x 64] intentionally omitted <== Category 2: The cells have been able to carry out training and capacity building to equip stakeholders to plan and implement behavioural change initiatives. Though the effectiveness of the training differs for each of the cells. For example, in Odisha the training included an interactive workshop to develop the capacity of the frontline health workers in methods of SBCC, whilst the training in UP merely was a presentation of the district’s performance across health indicators and was delivered in a lecture mode. Since the cells in this category are housed under the health department, they have easy access to a large cadre of trainees (frontline health workers) who can implement techniques of behaviour change at the field level. Training on monitoring has not happened as the cells in this category have not engaged with monitoring activities. Category 3: CRU conducts training for district level functionaries such as the IEC consultants within SBM on SBCC and demand generation initiatives. The effectiveness of the training translating into demand generation could not be commented on in absence of supporting research. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 37 ==> picture [476 x 63] intentionally omitted <== In West Bengal the SBCC cell staff have conducted training on SBCC principles such as how an individual can change their behavior while creating domino effects in their immediate environment like family, community, school etc. Similar training has been done for frontline workers like ANM, AWW, ASHA, PRI members, SHG groups and have been equipped to deliver key messages. KEQ 4 : How effective is the communication strategy, material and content developed by the SBCC Cells towards demand generation for development schemes and initiatives? Category 1 Most of the health communication strategies and materials for the state are guided/ developed/managed by the IEC/BCC unit related to thematic BCC activities, including a range of communication channels, and designing materials and programmes for vulnerable populations. Key activities carried out by the IEC division are drawn from thematic campaigns of NRHM, including message designing, and material development, and distribution of audio/video and print material/software. In addition to communication, IEC/BCC unit is also responsible for training on BCC/IPC (inter-personal communication). IEC/BCC unit undertakes several activities in the ongoing year as determined in the Annual Action Plans. State Institute of Health and Family Welfare (SIHFW) is entrusted to train in all other subjects/themes except BCC/IPC in the state. The SBCC material must be published in multiple languages considering the cultural and ethnic diversity. Mission Tejaswee on the prevention and control of anemia is an instance of effective communication strategy included awareness generation, advocacy, distribution of IFA tablets to expectant mothers and lactating mothers by ANM. It was found that despite such efforts, there is a lack of funding that is much needed for creating transformative SBCC communication material in minority areas. and LODOR. Conclusively, district level officers and trainers demand communication material for LODOR groups, it was observed that both lack of vision to have wider reach in LODOR combined with insufficient funds has prevented the Cells from disseminating effective communication materials. However, there was no documented evidence demonstrated on lack of fund, during the evaluation. ==> picture [468 x 59] intentionally omitted <== Category 2 The cells themselves do not develop a lot of communication material or content. They rely on the material provided by the health department which includes materials such as posters and banners. Sometimes material and content developed by other development agencies such as UNICEF, CHAI and UNDP are also used as part of dissemination of health messaging. For example, the ‘RI wheel’ developed by CHAI is found to be extremely useful by frontline health workers in explaining to new mothers the due date of the next vaccine for their infant. This can be attributed to the lack of funds and dedicated communications cadre including a designer in each cell. The cells’ inability to develop material can be attributed to the lack of funds and dedicated communications cadre including a designer in each cell. The cells in this category are able to contribute to developing a communication strategy at the district level by giving inputs on how dissemination and implementation at the district should be charted out. Category 3 Most of the IEC material has resulted in appropriate and targeted communication at the district level. For instance, the material sent by the national and state governments is made relevant to local contexts by translating the UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 38 content to local languages and dialects and changing the imagery and social examples to more culturally relevant ones. In the districts, not all SBCC agenda of targeted communication and driving social behaviour change is successful in the district priorities. The evidence-driven communication of SBCC programme often takes a backseat and gradually engagement of issue-based communication design is moving towards an IEC-based approach. Though there is evidence of differentiated district for instance, during COVID, the SBCC cell gave developed a lot of material on how to communicate with communities and designed pamphlets and posters for dissemination. Saas-Bahu Sammelan The Saas Bahu Sammelan is a state health department's initiative (Madhya Pradesh) rolled out under the directive of the National Rural Health Mission (NRHM). The main objective of this initiative has been to create a sense of bonding and understanding between expectant and new mothers and mothers-in-law to address concerns and misconceptions and myths around child-bearing and postpregnancy phases for new mothers and their child. Through regular meetings, awareness campaigns, and ‘munaadis’, the sammelan is conducted at the village level by Anganwadi workers to encourage pregnant and new mothers and their mothers-in-law to gather and participate in discussions on socially relevant topics, to engage in a healthy discussion in a safe space in address and ask and get clarifications on women’s health, childbirth, personal hygiene, etc., in a communitybased environment. A larger objective of this initiative is to protect the girl child. KEQ 5: To what extent have the SBCC Cells improved collaborations, partnerships, and convergence with allied departments at the national and state level/ Developmental agencies/ corporates? Category 1 The state’s SBCC Cells are currently RMNCH+A and RI focused. However, within the SBCC, there is a need for improving coordination between the NHM and DHSFW whereby resources and SBCC activities can be shared and allocated between the health departments where the Cells can have equitable participation in development of the PIPs and pool in resources to IPC than on printing materials in order to make the transition to SBCC more effective, and to strengthen SBCC activities. There is better convergence at the block and district levels which are aligned to WASH and ICDS and engaged in SBCC health communication. Medical college faculties have monitored programmes with the support of UNICEF, although there was no further insight on the same. Although the NGOs work with the support of the NHM in remote and hilly tribal and other socio-economically vulnerable areas, it was found that collaborations and engagements with development agencies and NGOs are limited. As was noted, that boat clinics and community IEC is not yet comprehensively covered. NGOs such as the Voluntary Health Association of India (VHAI) engage in issues such as communicable diseases, Reproductive Maternal Child and Adolescent Health, immunization, food and nutrition, and community participation and ownership through innovative programmes (egs: SALT, KHOJ, Arunoday), the organization does not work very closely with the state Cell. The State cells partner with bilateral and donor agencies such as WHO, UNDP, UNICEF, and JSI. Additionally, the NHM BCC Cell collaborates with a few NGOs and CBOs for communication outreach activities. The DHSFW has had a partnership with the Community Medicine Department of Assam Medical College. Category 2 The cells are housed under the health department which already has a large network of collaborations and partnerships in place which the cells can leverage to their benefit. Though the cells have been able to develop convergence with allied departments and development agencies to carry out implementation of SBCC based programming, there is potential to further strengthen convergence with allied departments which is often restricted by the mutual exclusivity of programs within these departments. The cells also have partnerships with NGOs with whom they collaborate for more effective implementation at the community level as NGOs usually have field level UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 39 presence. In UP, there was a one-off incidence of a local sugar factory contributing to the health department’s Covid-19 response through its CSR funds. Category 3 The cells in this category are housed under the NIRDPR and WCD. Both departments already have a large network of collaborations and partnerships in place which the cells can leverage to their benefit. Though the cells have been able to develop convergence with allied departments such as WCD for CRU and Department of Panchayats & Rural Development for the cell in West Bengal, and other development agencies to carry out implementation of SBCC based programming, there is potential to further strengthen convergence with other allied departments which is often restricted by the mutual exclusivity of programs within these departments. There is evidence of engagement of the cells with CSOs/NGOs during the pandemic at an implementation level but beyond the pandemic, there has been limited standing partnerships. CRU has an added advantage of being housed under a national institute which gives it access to the institutes’ established systems and institutions of service delivery (SIRDs) at the state level too. KEQ 6: Have the SBCC Cells successfully fulfilled their role as institutions for supporting evidence generation, strategic planning, operationalization, monitoring, analysis, training, documentation, knowledge sharing, including convening partnerships? || Role | Category 1 | Category 2 | Category 3 | |---|---|---|---|---| || Evidence Generation |Evidence generation has not been conducted by the cells in category one.|Evidence generation has not been conducted by the cells in category two.|Evidence-driven communication design of the SBCC program| |||||Budget cycle of SBCC| |||||affects the work wherein| || Strategic Planning |The cell functions within the mandates and the allocated budget provided by the national and state health and family welfare department. This is a barrier to the cells’ capacity to develop dedicated strategic plans and implement them.|Strategic planning for communications is majorly driven by the programmatic planning for each respective program. The approach is still top down in nature and hence, plans implemented on the field are unable to take into account needs of the community.| quarterly releases are delayed by months and it affects the planning and programming for West Bengal. CRU is able to develop strategic plans in communications for various| |||||departments that seek its| |||||technical expertise.| |||||No impact evaluation or| |||||monitoring has been| ||||States are not engaged in|conducted for the activities| |||Lack of monitoring due to|monitoring impact due to a lack of a|of the CRU.| || Monitoring |shortage of human resource and financial constraints|dedicated communication cadre and limited funds as it is labour and| In WB, the SBCC cell is| ||||expertise intensive process.|understaffed to be able to| |||||carry out any monitoring| |||||activities.| || Training |There has been no active training or materials designed for FLWs. BRIDGE training has not been conducted for more than tow years.|Cells have conducted capacity development and training workshops for health officials and frontline health workers|No training has been conducted in SBCC cell of West Bengal since 2020. Additionally, inappropriate budget affects training of existing staff| UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 40 ||||Odisha: Evidence of behaviour|| |---|---|---|---|---| || Documenta tion and analysis |No apparent documentation and analysis of the SBCC activities beyond the general administrative documentation|analysis conducted at the block level by studying community members’ health seeking activities and social contexts. In MP and Up there is lack of documentation and no evidence of|No apparent documentation and analysis of the SBCC activities beyond the general administrative documentation| ||||analysis.|| || Knowledge sharing | No evidence of knowledge sharing was allied, collaborating departments and development agencies / NGOs was found in the state,|Knowledge sharing has not been priority though some evidence of knowledge sharing through collaborations with other developments and agencies on health and related attributes.|Knowledge sharing is a part of both the cells in this category. It is done through provision of technical expertise to various departments| KEQ 7: How has the institutionalization of SBCC-based programming through the setting up of SBCC Cells strengthened the health system? | HSS building blocks | Category 1 | Category 2 | Category 3 | |---|---|---|---| | Leadership and Governance | The state leadership has been partially successful to capture the requirements of the communities in Assam, and this was evident through their contribution towards RI enhancement and Covid 19 management. However, at district level, due to scarcity of human resource and dependence on government health authorities for implementation, SBCC activities have not been covered widely and effectively. Additionally, the cell had limited scope of accountability and governance as elements of monitoring and evaluation have been limited in their structure.|The Cell being housed under health department, the leadership of state and district public health system were involved in the functioning of the cell. However, the degree of involvement varied from low in UP to high in MP and very high in Orissa. Cell Leadership played very active role in strategy designing and content development at state level. It was supported by effective and efficient implementation of these strategies at the district level by the district level leadership|CRU’s leadership was actively involved in day-to-day functioning of the cell. It also plays a vital role in financial and strategic aspects of the functioning of the cell. Since there is only one state level cell, the leadership and governance structure is centralized. In WB, ownership and leadership of SBCC activities at district level from the government’s side has kept the cell relevant and active. Cell workers, both at the district and block levels are well recognized by the relevant leadership who ensure that all stakeholders are working within a collaborative network. Further, the cell is housed within the Zilla Parishad under the leadership of the ADM and ICDS project officer.| | Workforce |Consultants deployed by UNICEF have been actively involved in bridging the gap in communication related interventions in the state. The field visits, training and interactions of the cell’s human resource with FLWs played a major role in community mobilization and enhanced coverage of the government interventions. However, limited exposure to hard-to-reach areas|The workforce engaged in SBCC activities in category two are health officers by designation who already have roles and responsibilities assigned to them. Their capacity to focus on communications activity is limited as priority is given to the governance and programmatic aspects of their professional role. Further, even at the field level, FHWs reported a similar dual burden of programmatic and|It is the only category, where in the workforce of the cell does not consist of health officials. The workforce in Telangana consists of direct members of the cell including the designer, and members of PRI for field level implementation. With NIRD&PR being a national level institute, the cell has access to all its state level counterparts (SIRD&PR across states). In West Bengal, district or block administration personnel| UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 41 ||continues to be limited in certain regions of the state.|communications activities. Hence, the need for a dedicated communications cadre at all three levels was strongly felt for better functioning of the cells in respect to human resource utilization|manage the activities of the cell. cell workers, both at the district and block levels work within a collaborative network.| |---|---|---|---| | Service Delivery |The Cell has been primarily focusing upon increasing the RI coverage in the state, and over the years due to its consistent efforts, intervention has gained momentum and vaccination coverage has enhanced across the state. However, the cell has not been able to strengthen the service delivery of promotive, preventive and curative measures towards good health. Collaborative efforts of ANMs, and Anganwadis and ASHA has enhanced the coverage of SBCC activities but the shortage of FLWs and the need for scaling up their training and skill sets is needed for wider community interactions|SBCC cells in category two are housed under the health department and hence work well within the ambit of flagship programs of this department. The cells act as a facilitator of communication activities for various health interventions and programs and develop strategic plans to bring about social change through communications. Though, the service delivery of the cells in category two is limited by a lack of adequate funding and absence of a dedicated communications cadre|CRU's service delivery is limited to provision of technical expertise to other departments and training of master trainers. It develops comprehensive strategic communications plans as per the need of the said departments. Further, CRU translates and contextualises IEC material sent by the central government. The roles and responsibilities of the cell in West Bengal is to act as a facilitator of communication activities for various interventions and programs under DWCD. Its targets for service delivery are dependent on the programmatic needs of the said interventions| | Finance |The strategies of cell were inclined towards RI and hence, financial resources provided for the promotion of immunization interventions were being extensively utilized by the cell. The budget is managed through collaborative efforts of DMO, DCM, DEMO, etc. all work together on PIPs. Cell and system strengthening can be achieved through sufficient budgeting and availability of independent financial resources for the cell. There is thinning of budgets at the time of national review of the state PIPs, which leads to communication activities being placed under innovative schemes within their respective budget heads. There is a single-nodal agency where funds come in and which are then distributed from the source to other departments.|The cells in category two receive their monetary allocation from the respective program for which they have to carry out communications activity; meaning there is no dedicated budget for the cells. The cells are not allowed to use the funds as they seem fit to carry out SBCC activities rather the department gives out program wise mandates on which activities to spend how much on. The monitoring of expenditure of this fund is done through checking whether the expenditure target was reached for each item suggested by the department. Hence, the cell works strictly within these guidelines.|The cell in Telangana has adequate budget for SBCC activities and has a say in the yearly budget planning. However, it was mentioned that during Covid-19 response, it has not been able to utilize the available budget and the relevant funds have been returned to UNICEF. The allocated budget for the cell in West Bengal are drawn from existing IEC budgets of the respective departments like WCD, SBM, NHM and a certain section is funded by UNICEF. The budget is spent on certain activities like miking, nuked natak, folk arts etc.| | Information | The cell is aligned with health department of the state government and hence, cell’s priorities and sources of information are inclined towards national and state objectives. The cell utilizes the information and material provided by the health department. Local|The cells rely on the material provided by the health department which includes materials such as posters and banners to spread awareness and information relating to positive social and health practices amongst community members. Sometimes material|Various departments and programmes such as WCD and SBM have approached CRU for technical support in strategizing and developing communication materials. CRU aids in customizing communication materials of the central government by translating them| UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 42 |||contextual issues are highlighted in the communication strategies of the cell as flow of information largely follows top – down approach. However, much more socio-cultural contexts, language, and dialects must be assessed|and content developed by other development agencies such as UNICEF, CHAI and UNDP are also used as part of dissemination of health messaging. This can be attributed to the lack of funds and dedicated communications cadre including a designer in each cell|in local languages and adding context specific images and examples. In West Bengal, the cells are given need-based pamphlets by DWCD to distribute in district and block. Also, UNICEF’s materials for communication like posters, pamphlets, key messages are used in health information dissemination.| |---|---|---|---|---| Conclusion The cells across all three categories were found to be lacking in three out of the four short term outputs mentioned in the theory of change. The cells were not successfully able to establish systems and processes to design, implement and monitor SBCC plans/programs and use evidence for programme planning hence leading to limited evidence, documentation, and completed studies on SBCC. Though it was found that effective dissemination for SBCC program evidence through workshops, media, etc was being taken place. Hence, it can be concluded that within the evaluation criterion of effectiveness, the cells have a major scope of improvement to achieve the first and fourth longer term outcomes in the theory of change namely, institutionalization of SBCC programming through sustainable systemic reforms, and positive policy environment created for SBCC programming across sectoral development initiatives. 6.3. Efficiency Efficiency measures the extent of resource utilization in the optimum, economical and timely manner. It provides justification about resources utilized in the achievement of intervention’s goals. Evaluation of efficiency of an intervention includes critical evaluation of timeliness, economic and operational efficiency. Throughout the SBCC cell evaluation, programme was examined through cell’s functioning within allocated resources and within planned timelines. The efficiency of the cell was analysed through insights received by interviewing state, UNICEF consultants (loaned staff) and district level officials of SBCC cells. KEQ 1: Did the SBCC cells efficiently deliver / implement their activities and function within the planned/ allocated monetary resources? Category 1 This has been partially achieved. SBCC activities use funds from the Single Nodal Agency (SNA) and do not have dedicated funds presently, and thus Child Health or Maternal Health are included in their respective sections. As it was found that the SBCC Cell is not duly consulted during the PIP formulation, thus impacting communication activities vis-á-vis the budget . Although budget for FY 2020–2021 increased in Assam, it was mainly allocated for Anemia Free India strategy, a progamme that is conducted through track and act for improved planning and allocations which is at 48 percent. Budgets have increased due better coverage of IFA supplementation and use of ‘Hemoglobin’ meters . It was difficult to ascertain from the evaluation if the budget was utilized anywhere other than RMNCH+A. ==> picture [476 x 63] intentionally omitted <== Category 2 UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 43 The cells in category two receive their monetary allocation from the respective program for which they have to carry out communications activity; meaning there is no dedicated budget for the cells. Since the state governments are still in the process of transitioning from IEC to SBCC, their understanding of estimated expenditure on communications activities is still guided by an IEC inventory of printing posters and banners with the recent inclusion of activities such as wall painting and mega phoning. ==> picture [476 x 63] intentionally omitted <== For example, in Odisha even though the budget increased from INR 2 crores in 2011 to INR 200 crores in 2022, the number of programs and initiatives being run by the cell also increased and thus ensured that the deficit was still maintained. Hence, the cells’ efficiency in delivery is limited by inadequate funding which does not account for many aspects of SBCC such as monitoring, research and evidence generation. Further, the cells are not allowed to use the funds as they seem fit to carry out SBCC activities rather the department gives out a detailed instruction on which activities to spend how much on. The monitoring of expenditure of this fund is done through checking whether the expenditure target was reached for each item suggested by the department. Hence the cell must work strictly within these guidelines. Category 3 The cell in West Bengal has inadequate financial allocation to efficiently deliver SBCC activities. For example, in the district of Purulia, only 20 schools could be selected for the WASH in school initiative due to limited budget availability. Further, inconsistencies in the budget cycle of SBCC affects the functioning of the cell wherein quarterly releases are delayed by months which affects the planning and programming. Though the SBCC consultant and budget available from UNICEF is a resource which the district cells are able to leverage in the functioning of the cell. The cell in Telangana is able to deliver efficiently within the allocated monetary resources as it has a say in the annual budget planning which is done vis-à-vis its annual action plan. Hence, the cell is able to advocate for the budget it would require to efficiently carry out its activities for the year. However, it was mentioned that during Covid-19 response, it has not been able to utilise the available budget and the relevant funds have been returned to UNICEF. KEQ 2: Did the SBCC Cells efficiently deliver/implement their activities and function within the planned/allocated human resources? Category 1 This has been partially achieved, as there is shortage of FLWs at the district and block levels, specifically in remote and hard to reach areas, and additionally, the FLWs lack adequate and advance training in building their interpersonal skills. With a larger segment of FLWs in the aging category, and no new recruitment of FLWs currently, there is a wide gap in practicing the skill after training. This also has had an impact on the coverage of immunization which has not reached a hundred percent due to limited outreach and inaccessibility issues. The Cells although short-staffed, have been able to deliver the SBCC activities in a timely manner without delays, particularly prescribed tasks which are delivered by the Cell on a regular and periodic basis, and which are well within the scope of the duties of SBCC officers. Category 2 The cells’ allocation of human resources depends on state to state as the structure of each cell is different and other external factors such as vacancy in positions of health officials in this state also affects this allocation. For example, in Odisha there was only one resource person in the block level cells, whilst in UP there is an issue of vacancy in positions of health officials at district and block level. The capacity to focus on communications activity by these health officials at block, district and state levels is limited as priority is given to the governance and programmatic aspects of their professional role. The need for a dedicated communications cadre at all three levels was strongly felt for better functioning of the cells in respect to human resource utilization. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 44 Category 3 The cells can efficiently deliver within the allocated human resources. CRU does not have a mandate to implement the communications strategies it has developed. For the processes of developing strategies and contextualizing IEC and SBCC material, it has adequate human resources and can deliver well within the allocated human resources. For example, having an allocated designer within CRU has proved effective in quickly turning around campaign material at a low cost as compared to hiring designers for every campaign. In West Bengal, effective convergence of block level SBCC staff with frontline workers and Panchayat workers has allowed the cell to deliver efficiently within the allocated human resources. Empowered frontline health workers for a robust health system ==> picture [74 x 65] intentionally omitted <== Eruptions of laughter and loud chatter welcome you into Balrampur Anganwadi centre. Frontline health workers such as anganwadi workers, ASHA workers and ANMs have congregated in the centre for an interaction with the evaluators. The workers are confident, well informed and experienced in bringing about social behavioural change through health messaging and communications. Details of the myriad of health interventions they contribute to are at their fingertips. Their engagement is active, and they voice opinions and suggestions that they have already shared with their superiors. There is a lot of focus on empowering frontline health workers in Uttar Pradesh. The frontline health workers are equipped with the latest SBCC material available in the state, such as health educational videos (which are readily accessible in their own personal mobiles), pamphlets, posters and banners on topics such as COVID protocols, hand washing, routine immunization, etc. They also undergo regular training in communications and understanding health indicators in their local context. Most importantly, they are in direct line of communication with their superiors, which includes not just the ANMs but also other health officials such as the chief medical officer and health education officers. This sort of open communication has led to better access to resources for the frontline health workers and has harboured a sense of empowerment in the workers who feel that their voices and opinions. KEQ 3: Did the SBCC Cells sufficiently deliver/implement their activities and function within the planned/allocated timelines? Category 1 Even though FLHWs have received training on communication intermittently, there is gap in scaling up their attitudes, knowledge, awareness, and skills to keep pace with the frequent and evolving health schemes and programmes. This also has had an impact on the coverage of immunization which has not reached a hundred percent due to limited outreach and inaccessibility issues. While the Cell works under tight timelines, particularly when there are new SBCC campaigns on RMNCH+A to be delivered or awareness to be shared. During Covid19 and at other times of immunization activities, the Cell has worked beyond its capacity to be able to largely meet the allocated timelines. Category 2 Since the cells are housed under the health department, they are mandated to carry out the communications activities for various health intervention and programs. The cells work on a challenging timeline as they must manage to fulfil their role in long term programs while also organizing various days celebrated by the government to bring about increased awareness regarding health practices and increase of uptake of health services such as Poshan Pakhwada, Swachh Bharat Mission day, international yoga day and world immunization week. This affects the efficiency of the cells’ delivery as it results in reduced time and capacity of the cell to engage in other aspects of SBCC such as evidence generation, monitoring impact and conducting research on behaviour change. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 45 Category 3 In West Bengal, the budget cycle of SBCC affects the cell’s efficient delivery within planned timelines. Due to delays in quarterly releases by months, the cell is unable to carry out its activities in a timely manner. These delays affect the planning and programming of the cell and implementation of its activities. For CRU, whilst they are able to more or less deliver efficiently within the plan the timelines, the cell does work on a challenging timeline. The dual responsibility of organizing various days, like, SBM day, celebrated by the government along with regular SBCC activities, act as barriers for functioning within allocated timelines . Conclusion The short term output goals mentioned in the theory change which fall within the ambit of evaluating efficiency are namely availability of a pool of skilled functionaries in effective SBCC programming, monitoring and implementation and increased allocation and utilization of budget/resources for SBCC programming. Across the cells in all three categories, with the exception of the cell in Telangana (CRU), it was found that there is a need for a skilled and dedicated cadre of functionaries with expertise in communications. Further, the same five cells (with CRU as exception) also exhibited a need for increased budget to ensure efficient delivery of SBCC activities. This indicates that the cells have a major scope of improvement to achieve the first and fourth longer term outcomes in the theory of change, namely, institutionalization of SBCC programming through sustainable systemic reforms, and a positive policy environment created for SBCC programming across sectoral development initiatives. Har Ghar Dastak campaign The Har Ghar Dastak Abhiyan launched in 2021 is aimed at vaccinating and covering eligible populations groups (and those in hard-to-reach areas) for the first, second and precaution doses through house-to-house and door-to-door campaigns. The campaign is aimed at old age homes, schools and colleges, including the out-of-school children, and tribal rural communities and workers in brick kilns, etc. The campaign was relaunched as “Har Ghar Dastak campaign 2.0” to accelerate the coverage of COVID19 vaccination across States and districts, widely undertaken by NGOs. It is undertaken by utilizing the local influencers and community leaders to enhance the awareness among communities to take vaccine doses. 6.4. Sustainability Evaluation of sustainability of the intervention involves exploring institutional, financial, social, and environmental capacity and capability. It provides crucial insights into the perpetuation or estimated perpetuation of the intervention’s positive impacts in shorter and longer duration. During the evaluation of SBCC cells programme, probability of integration of SBCC programming in state department planning, government’s capacity to carry out SBCC activities independently. Additionally, adequacy of the financial allocations and partnerships to carry out the activities of the SBCC Cells was also evaluated. The sustainability of the cell was evaluated through insights received by interviewing state and district level officials of SBCC cells. It was corroborated through interactions with UNICEF SBCC specialists at national and state level. KEQ 1. To what extent have the different state departments integrated SBCC programming into its planning and implementation of initiatives? Category 1 Assam was one among the first states to have established an SBCC cell in 2007 brought under the NRHM for converging all health communications at on place. Under the DHSFW, the Cell focuses on communication related UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 46 to RI to address and eliminate the surrounding misconceptions that have so far constrained the state from attaining full immunization coverage. In this regard, the departments have integrated SBCC programming into its planning and implementation of SBCC initiatives, as it has comprehensively addressed a few critical healthrelated areas within the same programme such as flagship initiatives such as Mission Indradhanush and Mission Tejaswee, RMNCH+A, WASH, and MCH, etc., some key socially relevant issues related to child and adolescent SRH and women’s SRH have not been integrated within the SBCC programmes. However, state departments need further integrate SBCC programming into its planning and implementation more holistically with focus and emphasis on behavior change. Category 2 The transition from IC to SBCC has not fully happened, and has happened to varying degrees, for the cells in category 2. Planning, programming, and implementation of initiatives take into account communications activities only to the extent of IEC. For example, in UP, communications activities are mostly limited to printing of posters and banners. Even in Odisha, which is the best performing sell out of the three in this category, the integration of SBCC programming in planning and implementation of initiatives is restricted by factors such as budget. For example, for the implementation of SBCC activities within a program, a district is only given 2000 rupees to conduct the activities. With the budget being so small, majority of it gets spent on the tried and tested methods of IEC such as printing posters and banners, and almost no budget is left for conduction of SBCC methods such as nukad natak and IPC. Though it is important to note that since the cells in category two are housed under the health department in the state government and their work is in alignment with state and national level programs, this allows for a certain level of integration of SBCC programming in the state’s planning and implementation of initiatives, including allocation of funds for SBCC activities from program budget and designating of human resource for communications from within the workforce of the department. Amongst the three cells in this category, the integration of SBCC programming in the states planning and implementation of initiatives was lowest in UP. This can be attributed to the lack of an active cell at the state level which would otherwise play the role of advocating for integration of SBCC in state programming. Category 3 States’ shift from IEC to SBCC process has been only partially fulfilled. Cells have not been able to bring the SBCC agenda to the forefront and hence, SBCC programming in state department planning has not been fully integrated. In West Bengal, the visibility of the block volunteers at the ground level and regular interactions and updates on SBCC Cell’s activities have widely helped in integration and collaboration, as the cell’s members work with officials from AWW and CDPO related to the WCD, and also with the BMOH and MNB coordinators. The SBCC cell’s engagement with District Social Welfare officers rather than with Zilla Parishad has impacted integration to a certain extent. CRU in Telangana has been able to establish itself as a one stop shop for support and technical guidance provision on communications activities and through its work, has been able to help departments understand the importance of SBCC. Hence, various state departments reach out to the cell for support to develop communications strategy for programs such as SBM and JJM out of their own understanding of the significance of having a communications strategy for their respective programs. Hence, it can be said that CRU has been able to contribute to the integration of SBCC programming in the state department planning. KEQ 2. Does the government (at the state and district-level) have the capacity to successfully carry out the activities of the SBCC Cells independently? Category 1 The findings indicate that there is potential for the government to be able to successfully carry out the activities of the SBCC cells independently. The cells housed under the health department, are working on attaining comprehensive coverage of RI across rural and urban areas. However, the state’s capacity building will include increasing the workforce strength of FLWs at the district, and down to the block level. The SBCC cells need a more socially aware and SBCC-oriented (not only IEC-oriented) personnel, inclusion in the programme budget for mainstreaming SBCC and gender equity, and leadership from senior government officials and other government resources. Only if the cells can actively endeavor to build capacity of the SBCC for officials within the departments, it can accelerate the integration of SBCC into its programming and carry out the activities. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 47 However, greater accountability, coordination and convergence with SBCC-orientation and streamlining budget allocations can enable the government to take the SBCC activities forward. At this point, it was observed that the government both at the state and district level can independently carry out the commonplace activities in so far as the health and communication of SBCC mandates are concerned. Category 2 For cells in category two, the findings indicate that there is potential for the government to be able to successfully carry out the activities of the SBCC cells independently. These cells are housed under the health department, which gives them a few advantages such as access to a large cadre of health workers positioned down to the block level, inclusion in the program budget, governance and leadership from senior government officials, and other government resources. With the cells actively advocating for and building capacity in SBCC for officials within the health department, the department has been able to develop its capacity to integrate SBCC into its programming and be able to successfully implement it at the field level. Though in UP, there has been a lack of ownership and accountability from the government’s side, especially at the state level, due to which the potential for the government to be able to successfully carry out the activities of the SBCC cells independently is not fulfilled and has remained limited. ==> picture [476 x 63] intentionally omitted <== Category 3 In both states, the government (at the state and district-level) have been reported to have limited capacity to adequately carry out the activities of the SBCC Cells independently. They have limited technical knowledge / expertise. Though in West Bengal, the cells work very closely with district administration and are flexible to adopt to changes. The block volunteers and district coordinator are selected through district recruitment processes. And any requests from district administration for IEC support is well received by the department. In CRU’s case, its financial allocation comes solely from UNICEF. Hence, the government presently has limited opportunity to support adequate financial allocations and partnerships to successfully carry out activities independently. Though each department and programme have a budget for IEC/SBCC activities, this only covers the cost of development of material. KEQ 3. Do the Cells and their implementation structures (at the state and district-level) have adequate financial allocations and partnerships to successfully carry out the activities of the SBCC Cells independently? Category 1 The existing partnerships as was observed, are not yet strengthened for better communication strategies. ; Additionally, a lack of convergence with other relevant stakeholders for collaboration, implementation, and sustainability of SBCC strategies is currently not robust in the state. The SBCC cell under NHM receive financial support through the State’s PIP, as the NHM PIP is the main source of funding. IEC-related planning has been the aggregate of the budget of IEC under the umbrella of the NHM at the state level. Moreover, the funding for these cells comes through program budgets which are largely channelized for RI communications activities. The budget is managed through collaborative efforts of DMO, DCM, DEMO, etc. all work together on PIPs. Cell and system strengthening can be achieved through sufficient budgeting and availability of independent financial resources for the cell. There is thinning of budgets at the time of national review of the state PIPs, which leads to communication activities being placed under innovative schemes within their respective budget heads. There is a single-nodal agency where funds come in and which are then distributed from the source to other departments. streamlining budget allocations can enable the government to take the SBCC activities forward. Community engagement although supported strongly by community influencers, it is largely lacking due to shortage of human resources, coordinated and convergent efforts between departments, lack of intersectoral convergence, and community engagement are factors that can scale up and sustain through a more empowered community-based interventions and community-led initiatives with support from the state and district cells. There was a consensus among district officials that the Cell has not disseminated adequate funds to carry forward SBCC activities in UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 48 areas and among communities where urgently needed. However, whether it is a paucity of funds or a lack of optimal usage of funds at appropriate contexts, was difficult to ascertain from the evaluation. Partnerships too are limited and most definitely have not been utilized to the fullest. Category 2 The funds for the cells in category two are allocated from within the budget of the health department. In most cases, the budget for SBCC/IECC activities is accounted for from the program budget itself. All three cells within this category spoke about experiencing limitations in working within the allocated funds. The cells also faced an issue in the way the fund expenditure is being monitored, which is through checking whether the cells have reached their spending target as allocated by the health department at the state level. This method was pointed out to be rigid and resulting in a top-down approach which did not consider the difference in the local contextual reality across the state. The cells have adequate partnerships with other development agencies and other departments through the collaborations of the health department. Development agencies such as CHAI, JHPIEGO and UNDP also develop communications material for various aspects of health programming. Such material contributes to the larger objective to reach out to as many members of society especially those from vulnerable populations. Category 3 The cell in West Bengal has inadequate financial allocation to effectively carry out SBCC activities. Delays in the budget cycle of SBCC affects the functioning of the cell wherein quarterly releases are delayed by months which affects the planning and programming. There is an instance where due to limited budget availability, the cell in Purulia had to choose only 20 schools for WASH in School initiative in an entire district. Though the SBCC consultant and budget available from UNICEF is a resource which the district cells are able to leverage in the functioning of the cell. The cell in Telangana has adequate financial allocations as it has a say in the annual budget planning which is funded wholly by UNICEF. However, it was mentioned that during Covid-19 response, it has not been able to utilize the available budget and the relevant funds have been returned to UNICEF. Though, it is important to note that since the budget is funded completely by UNICEF, the sustainability of this source it's limited till the time UNICEF continues to extend this support. The department under which the cell is housed (NIRD PR), does not allocate any part of the funding for the cell which has been noted to be an important indicator of sustainability. In view of the lack of funding from the state department under which the cell is housed, increases the dependency on UNICEF supported resources and budget. SBCC cells need to develop and/or strengthen partnerships with the department of health (in both states), WCD (in Telangana) and Panchayati Raj (in West Bengal) department for implementation of SBCC activities. It would allow increased leveraging of available IEC/ SBCC budgets with allied departments. Additionally, NGOs should be involved in implementation of SBCC activities . Conclusion Against sustainability as an evaluation criterion, there are initiatives being taken for achievement of all three outputs mentioned in the theory of change, namely, training of leadership and officials on SBCC programming reflecting the shift from IEC to SBCC, availability of structured plan for interactions with community members,and field functionaries, and availability of relevant job aids, tools, AV materials, prototypes, etc for SBCC. While progress is being made in each of these, they are yet to be fully achieved. In the case of Odisha as an example, government ownership is high and hence the cells are able to actively advocate for and build capacity in SBCC for officials within the health department, integrate SBCC into its programming, and be able to successfully implement it at the field level. Hence, with the achievement of these outputs, the cells will be able to contribute to the moving towards the longer term outcomes namely, institutionalization of SBCC programming through sustainable systemic reforms, and positive policy environment created for SBCC programming across sectoral development initiatives. 6.5. Coherence Evaluation of Coherence of the intervention helps in gauging the degree of support, coordination or undermining, hinderance witnessed with other interventions and policies of the stakeholders. In current interdependent and interrelated world, it becomes essential to explore the impact of internal and external stakeholders, their interventions and policies, to ensure effective and efficient results. Here, evaluation of SBCC cells programme involved examining coherence based on partnerships and integration of SBCC in state programming. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 49 We evaluated interlinkage of SBCC cells with other development partners and its alinement with interventions being carried out by state for improving health systems by interviewing state, district level officials of SBCC cells and consultants of various development partners and NGOs. KEQ 1. How do the activities/interventions of the SBCC Cells interlink and synergize with the existing/other programme/initiatives by other relevant actors (NGOs and development partners and other sector players)? Category 1 Partnerships with NGOs as was found have proved effective during Covid-19. However, the NGOs on a regular basis are not actively involved, nor exchange any knowledge or work on SBCC activities. This is despite the fact that NGOs are also working closely with communities on RI programmes, as do the SBCC Cells. However, at this point, there is no concrete linkage of NGOs with the SBCC cells especially related to more focus -driven and pointed work on women’s health, gender and equity. NGOs such as the Voluntary Health Association of India (VHAI) engage in issues such as communicable diseases, Reproductive Maternal Child and Adolescent Health, immunization, food and nutrition, and community participation and ownership through innovative programmes (egs: SALT, KHOJ, Arunoday), the organization does not work very closely with the state Cell. However, they too do not specifically work on gender and equity as a distinct or special attention aspect of their work. It was noted that NGOs are open to collaborating with the state cells to work on the ground level with communities (and FLWs) for wider outreach of SBCC and look forward to convergence. At the state level, there is no mapping of NGO partners is not yet carried out at the local levels, even as it has a limited number of partners and unskilled resources. Most importantly, the state faces challenges in reaching or directly working with communities in sensitive areas or hard-to-reach areas. In case of RI activities, greater institutional support will be further needed. Although the NGOs work with the support of the NHM in remote and hilly tribal and other socio-economically vulnerable areas, it was found that collaborations and engagements with development agencies and NGOs are limited. As was noted, that boat clinics and community IEC is not yet comprehensively covered and is only in its nascent stages (boat clinic concept is one of the good practices from Bangladesh which Assam intends to replicate in the state’s char areas and LODOR). The State cells partner with bilateral and donor agencies such as WHO, UNDP, UNICEF, and JSI. Additionally, the NHM BCC Cell collaborates with a few NGOs and CBOs for communication outreach activities. The DHSFW has had a partnership with the Community Medicine Department of Assam Medical College. ==> picture [476 x 50] intentionally omitted <== Category 2 The cells engage with various development actors through the collaborations that the health departments have such as with NGOs, International Development Agencies like UNDP, CHAI, BMGF, etc. to ensure that they delve into specific areas for e.g., youth mobilization, immunization, etc. comprehensively. There are collaborative efforts between the cells and these development agencies to develop new material and seek technical expertise. The implementation aspect of the programs is carried out through government mechanisms already in place. The development agencies leverage these resources and mechanisms to aid and enhance the government work. Category 3 Though CRU mostly works in the area of WASH, and hence has convergence with Swachh Bharat Mission and Jal Jeevan Mission. It also has interlinkage with other departments such as WCD and has contributed to programs such as Poshan Abhiyan. CRU does not have any ongoing interlinkage with other development actors and NGOs. Though during Covid-19 pandemic, they did leverage collaborations with NGOs for on file dissemination of Covid protocols. In West Bengal, regarding interdepartmental convergence, the cell collaborates with the health and sanitation departments, and with district Information and Culture officer. The cell does not have any ongoing interlinkage with other development actors and NGOs in the state, despite the presence of multiple such development actors working in the field of health and other related fields such as nutrition. Since there is no convergence or collaboration amongst these actors including with the cell, there is duplication of effort on multiple fronts as each actor is working in silos . UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 50 KEQ 2. How do the SBCC Cells interventions align/synergize/fit with other interventions being carried out by the state for improving health systems? Category 1 Overall, the SBCC Cell is striving to align to its interventions in health objectives. Programmes such as Mission Indradhanush and Mission Tejaswee, and Anemia Free India have been aligned to the objectives of immunization coverage strengthening in priority districts. This has also been largely possible due to the structure of the cell being housed in the NHM as well as the DHSFW. The Health Communication in Assam is implemented by the District Extension Media Officer (DEMO), (now the state Cold Chain officer under NHM. The other members include the DHSFW at the district level, Block Community Mobilizer (BCM), NHM and Health Educator (HE)/Block Extension Educator, DHSFW at the blocks. ASHA and ASHA Supervisors are the main frontline cadre involved in informing communities on available health services, disseminating health messages and assisting in utilization of health services. Service delivery at the state level have comprised various innovations towards reduction of mortality, such as through the Rural Health Practitioners (RHP), Boat Ambulance and Clinic (still in the discussion phases and was not heard of from participants in the study, except for one SBCC officer and participant), Mobile Medical Unit (MMU), ASHA Radio Program, Sarathi-104 (Health Information Helpline & Complaint Redressal system), etc. However, on ground it was found that accessibility of these in remote and hard-to-reach areas such as Chars, were difficult, and still a major concern. The gaps have been addressed and reduced in Reproductive Maternal Newborn Child and Adolescent Heath (RMNCH+A), although services and knowledge and awareness by the health facilities and their utilization need a further scale up. However, health systems need to assess and address the existing communication gaps in priority districts and aspirational districts, in LODOR areas, tea gardens, Char areas, and minority settlements which have precarious, cross-cutting health and social behavior change issues such as early marriage, teenage pregnancy, and school dropout holistically and meticulously. These are factors which are also entrenched in mindsets, ignorance, and poverty of the communities. Category 2 The cells in category two are highly aligned to the interventions being carried out by the state for improving health systems. Since the cells are housed under the health department, they are essential parts of the programming and implementation plan of various interventions brought about at state and national level. For example, the cells have contributed to spreading inclusive messaging on various state and national level health priorities such as Mission Indra Dhanush, Dastak Sanchari, Measles and rubella awareness, RI, postnatal care, home based care for young and new-born children and institutional delivery. Since the cells’ functioning occurs within the workings of the health department, the cells can leverage the department’s key resources such as frontline health workers who act as the last mile connection with vulnerable populations on field. Further, the cells can enhance the functioning of the health department through introduction and advocacy for novel and inclusive methods of communication which lead to social behavior change and contribute to a significant development from the earlier practice of mere distribution of IEC material. Category 3 Since the cell are not housed within the health department, they have limited alignment to core health programs. Though, they contribute to health systems strengthening by developing communications strategy for programs supporting health initiatives such as nutrition and sanitation programs. For example, CRU in Telangana has contributed to flagship programs such as SBM, JJM and Poshan Abhiyan. For the cell in West Bengal, the expected area of focus is ICDS for Nutrition and the four largest schemes viz., Kanyasree, Rupasree, Lakshmir Bhandar and Social Welfare schemes such as old age and widow pension. Conclusion For the program to have high coherence to the allied policy space and stakeholders, the theory of change suggests achievement of two outputs, namely, joint programmes planned and organised with allied sectors and meetings conducted with the same for SBCC, and availability of a network of CSOs/CBOs engaged in demand generation and behaviour change activities. It was found that the cells in Telangana, Assam and West Bengal have not been able to develop a steady network of CSOs/CBOs/NGOs due to which they have not been successful in initiating many joint ventures with allied sector actors. Whilst, in Odisha, U.P. and M.P., it was found that cells are able to leverage the network of other development actors and engage in joint planned programmes. Hence, it can be said for Odisha, U.P. and M.P. that they are closer to achieving the long term outcome goals UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 51 mentioned in the theory of change namely, institutionalization of SBCC programming through sustainable systemic reforms, and positive policy environment created for SBCC programming across sectoral development initiatives. SBCC programming with effective leadership The ownership and leadership of SBCC activities at district level from the government’s side in West Bengal has kept the SBCC cell relevant and active. The SBCC cell workers both at the district and block levels are well recognized by the relevant leadership who ensure that all stakeholders are working within a collaborative network. Further, the cell is housed within the Zilla Parishad under the leadership of the ADM and ICDS project officer. Receiving guidance and leadership from senior officials of two departments results in effective convergence at the district level. Convergence also exists between block level SBCC staff and frontline workers. The block volunteers of SBCC cell work very closely with Panchayat workers, ANM, ASHA and AWW at the village/GP level. Further, availability of a senior SBC specialist from UNICEF (loaned staff)- The presence of a specialized staff is found to give technical guidance in leading conversations with district administration, forging partnerships (with NSS, with private partners during COVID to leverage masks, sanitizers etc.) and leveraging available IEC budgets with allied departments (like funds for COVID IEC available with BMOH 6.6. Gender and Equity From the six evaluated states, it was found that although gender and equity are cross-cutting and a critical component of the SBCC, in practice, the Cells in the states have not yet mainstreamed gender equity. Neither are the Cells gender-cognizant while designing SBCC activities, as was noted under Relevance and Effectiveness across all the categories. Although, the SBCC progammes have principles of gender and equity in theory, at present, the gender and equity as a dimension and intersectional pillar of the SBCC is skewed across the states both in thought and practice. For instance, the evaluation in Telangana finds that gender and equity is institutionalized within the ambit of academics; whereas in other states, gender and equity are far from academic institutionalization; in fact, they are not even well-embedded within the SBCC practice. Most of the state cells have overlooked the gender and equity dimension, whereas, in some state level (and even district level cells), officials who were cognizant of the need for deeper enquiries and assessment of gender issues, simply do not have the necessary human resources (neither in workforce strength), nor mindset or evolving attitudes), and neither a dedicated (gender-responsive) budget. We gather that this gap has created a larger caveat at the district and block level where FLWs interactions with communities is narrow, and restricted to the prescribed SBCC activities. The SBCC Cells have given attention to WASH services and RMNCH+A , and hygiene and sanitation, nutrition, and women’s health, but have not attended to more fundamental issues of child protection, safeguarding of the rights of adolescent girls, and women. . In this context, the Rashtriya Kishor Swastha Abhijan and ARSH (national programmes for adolescent SRH and holistic development) are missing in any of the states during the consultations). Relevance Under relevance, it was found that across the 3 categories covering six states, Gender and equity are largely peripheral to most of the SBCC interventions. Nutrition and child survival with the support of community mobilisation have been focus areas, but not as such gender-centric or gender-aware. There is no advocacy at the state and district level on issues of child marriage and adolescent SRH, although the health department is overwhelmed at the number of cases of teen pregnancy and early (child) marriage being reported. Parallelly, in some states, male child preference continues to be prevalent among communities in categories 1 and 2. In the states in category 3, as the cells are housed in departments which are other than the health department, the UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 52 programmes do not entirely include or focus on gender and equity in its programmatic planning. Whereas, in West Bengal, the state cell housed under WCD, have gender-focused interventions but do not have any specific programmes on equity for vulnerable groups such as tribal populations, people with disabilities, and other poor and marginal communities. Effectiveness Under effectiveness in category 1, programmes like Mission Tejaswee are anemia-related for women, girls and new mothers, these are only part of women’s health which do not include a gender-inclusive dialogue on the household dynamics of food and nutrition, or women’s and girls’ need for timely eating or eating ‘well’. In category 2, it was found that programmes such as the Adolescent Empowerment Programme (AEP) was implemented across 16 districts under the leadership of District Collectors and the Department of Women and Child Development (DWCD). The states do not yet have adequate and (gender-cognizant) field functionaries to focus on adolescent empowerment, particularly as there is a lack of gender-targeted interventions coupled with lack of literacy in parts of the regions. In Category 3, although there is a lack of stakeholder interventions, and the demand and need of the states is based on contextual requirements of diverse vulnerable populations. Although, West Bengal has ICDS for Nutrition and Social Welfare schemes such as old age and widow pension, there is no specific programmes that are centered on gender and equity. In Mayurbhanj district in Odisha, the Cell has an equity-focused approach wherein they focus on IPC to develop trust building relationships with community members. There is a lack of stakeholder interventions, and the demand and need of the states is based on contextual requirements of diverse vulnerable populations. Efficiency The overall shortage of resources in Category 1as we gather, has not affected the efficiency of the SBCC objectives, but has largely hindered a gender-transformative approach to address and reduce gender gaps, such as social practices of early marriage or school dropouts. In category 2, there is no specific budget allocation for gender and equity, as the Cell is only part of programmatic planning. Hence, despite a programmatic focus on vulnerable including women and children, there are no specific interventions for the same. We gather that prevalent gender disparities will continue to perpetuate without timely SBCC intervention. In categ ory 3, gender and equity in Telangana is constrained due to lack of separate monetary allocation which also impedes the staff from envisioning gender and equity-focused programmes. In West Bengal, although gender is identified as a key dimension of the Cell’s activities, equity-based planning is missing, and resources are used only partially mainly on other SBCC activities. Sustainability and Coherence We gather from the study that Sustainability and Coherence are far from being attainable at this point, as we have in the aforementioned sections, concluded that gender and equity are the weak and missing links as was found under relevance and effectiveness. In this direction, largely the advantage of the states in Category 3 is that the programmatic focus is on women and children, which can serve as a good motivation for the state cell to have inclusion in its SBCC agenda – tribals, urban poor, and LODOR in its future activities. Reaching vulnerable groups through house-to-house surveys, a mix of communication approaches, including base line studies which are critical for equity, gender and social inclusion. With no gender-responsive budget but for states like West Bengal, advocacy and establishment of the state adolescent cell is of no added value if there is SBCC activity on equity and inclusion to assess the outcome of an adolescent cell on girls and women in tribal and economically vulnerable communities. Conclusively, gender equity must be holistically as well as granularly integrated into the SBCC strategy to make health-related communication more effective, and to trigger a dialogue around equity and gender relations and roles. From budgeting to mainstreaming, gender and equity needs to be entrenched within the SBCC objectives more firmly, to increase the accessibility of SBCC interventions to large numbers of under-served and marginalized populations of women, girls and even men and adolescent youth. Conclusion Gender and inclusion focused integrated SBCC plans/programmes as an output has not been fully achieved by any of the cells across all three categories. Since, public health is the larger ambit within which the SBCC program functions, there is a programmatic focus on aspects of gender inclusion and equity such as maternal health and increasing health information access for LODOR communities. But, no specific focus was found on gender and equity while planning communications activities relating to SBCC programming. Hence, there is limited success of integrating a gender and equity approach in achievement of the longer term outputs namely, institutionalization UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 53 of SBCC programming through sustainable systemic reforms, and positive policy environment created for SBCC programming across sectoral development initiatives. Equity focused SBCC programming In Odisha SBCC programming is being done in a manner which focuses on equity and local contexts. For example, a large percentage of the population in Odisha are tribals. Hence SBCC programming is done in such a manner that it considers various socioeconomic realities of such vulnerable communities such as religious beliefs, social mores, difference in language and dialect and imagery that they associate with. This results in having to develop SBCC material which is slightly different in each district. The SBCC cells are responsible for this process of making the material relevant in the local context. For example, Mayurbhanj has a tribal population of more than 50% of its total population. The frontline health workers in this district have been given extensive training on how to reach out to hard to reach and vulnerable communities. Jaya Sahu, an ASHA worker, tells us how one community in the area assigned to her had no uptake of immunization services when she started out 10 years ago. This community had various misbeliefs regarding the effects of immunization and hence were weary towards it. With the introduction of SBCC methods, Jaya Understood the importance of strengthening interpersonal communication with the members of this community and to have an empathetic ear when they shared their fears regarding vaccination. She was able to build relationships of trust with these members and has managed to get 100% immunization within the said community. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 54 Conclusion 7. The SBCC cells program was initiated to help state government departments to transition from IEC to SBCC. Though this transition is not yet complete for any of the six states evaluated in this study, the inception of the cells has got the ball rolling in the right direction. The state and district level officials are now familiar with the term ‘SBCC’ and are aware of its basic conceptual definition and how it differs from IEC. They are also aware of the novel methods being used in SBCC such as miking, interpersonal communication, leveraging key influencers, etc. The SBCC program has contributed to health systems strengthening in these six states by bringing about a change in the way health, ICDS and PRI officials approach and engage with community members. It can be said that the program is operating in alignment with all four desired outcomes mentioned in the theory of change . Evidence suggests that the cells are contributing and moving towards outcomes one and four which are creation of sustainable government systems for SBCC programming and creation of positive policy environment for SBCC programming across sectoral development initiatives, respectively by achieving, to varying degree, some of the shorter term output goals such as having leadership and officials trained on SBCC methods, and establishing systems and processes to design and implement SBCC programs. The scope of this evaluation does not include assessing outcome two and three which are namely improved demand and outreach of government programs and improved uptake and community participation towards government sponsored programs/schemes. It was found that the model of cell in Telangana (CRU) under category 3 was functioning in a manner most efficient to integrate SBCC programming in state planning and implementation of initiatives. As opposed to all the other cells, CRU has managed to establish itself as one stop shop for all communications activities in the state which allows the direction of pursuit for CRU to be such that the departments approach the cell for support out of their own understanding of the significance of having a communications strategy for their respective programs. In this way, CRU has been able to market itself as a center of technical expertise in communications which supports various departments in developing communications strategies. Further, it was found that CRU’s mandate to only operate at the state level acts as an advantage as its resources can be focused on strategizing, whilst implementation remains within the ambit of the state departments. This also ensures that the cell is able to function effectively in the role of providing technical expertise on communication within a smaller pool of human resources and budget allocation. It is important to note that though CRU’s model works best, it is still heavily dependent on UNICEF for its budget allocation. For the sustainability of the cell, CRU should advocate with NIRDPR for fund allocation from within the department’s budget. The SBCC cells’ functioning was found to have high relevance and alignment with the national and state priorities by contributing to building the communications strategy for flagship programs of departments under which they are housed. The cells have been seen to be flexible and adaptable to evolving needs in the community such as the incidence of Covid-19 pandemic. Further, to varying degrees, the cells have been able to leverage existing systems and institutions of service delivery such as conduction of on field implementation through the cadres of frontline health workers; and have been able to have the presence and guidance of senior government officials in creating the leadership and governance structure of the cell. But, due to various limitations such as inadequate finance , challenging timelines, a workforce which does not specialize in communications or behavior change and lack of inter-departmental convergence, the effectiveness and efficiency of the service delivery by the cell has been negatively affected. The cells have been only partially successful in fulfilling their role as institutions of evidence generation, monitoring and evaluation, documentation and analysis. Although the cells were established to bring about a change in the way communications is planned, programmed and implemented, a majority of them ended up playing the role of a supportive unit to the state’s IEC activities. The aspects that the cells are working to successfully deliver in are training and capacity building, strategic planning, knowledge sharing and increasing awareness of community members by disseminating health information and positive social practices. Most of the cells have been able to foster an environment of convergence and collaboration with relevant departments and with other development actors such as IDAs, NGOs and CSOs. Though it is important to note that the need for even higher convergence (especially intersectoral convergence such as a collaboration between health and education department) was noted from the findings across all six states. Through these collaborations, the cells are able to have a higher contribution to integrating SBCC into state programming. Hence, it can be concluded that the cells have coherence with institutions and interventions of health and related fields, but not intersectoral. The cells have a considerable scope of scaling up and being fully integrated in the public health system of the country. However, to ensure the program’s sustainability , government ownership and accountability of the cells UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 55 is of utmost importance. The first step in this process would be for the government to have a complete shift from IEC to SBCC in its attitude and approach. Following which, the government must conduct regular capacity development workshops and training in communications and behavior change methods for its officials who also play the role of communications officers. This would improve the quality of SBCC material being developed, lead to better planning and programming resulting in more technically sound communications strategies. It was found that although gender and equity are cross-cutting and a critical component of the SBCC, in practice, the Cells in the states have not yet mainstreamed gender and equity, and neither are there any gender focused programme beyond a basic programmatic focus on gender within health programs. For example, focus on gender in programs such as Poshan Abhiyan and RMNCHA+. A gender-transformative approach is thus a critical requirement to address social norms, practices in diverse social and economic contexts by the state and district cells. Though, through the programmatic focus on gender and equity within health programs, vulnerable populations such as young others from lower socio-economic strata and those in LODOR communities are included in the state programming. In conclusion, it can be said that the SBCC cells program kickstarted the transition of communications activities from IEC to SBCC . The program's contribution has been significant and in many of the states been the first SBCC intervention initiated. Though the progress has been slow over the past programmatic cycle, it is important to understand that to bring about a change not just in methods but in the approach, understanding and attitude towards communications is a mammoth task. The very principles of SBCC can be applied here to see the slow progress not as a sign of inefficiency and ineffectiveness of the program, but rather as a work in progress status for the cells . The cells require guidance and support for at least one more programmatic cycle whilst they figure out their transition strategy to move from being UNICEF supported to completely government owned. We strongly suggest UNICEF to continue its investment and support in the program during this period. Following are a few recommendations to increase the relevance, effectiveness, efficiency, sustainability, and coherence of the SBCC cells and to streamline the goals, efforts and resources being invested in the program. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 56 8. Policy implications and recommendations The objective of this evaluation exercise was to understand the SBCC Cells’ performance towards maximizing utilization of allocated resources and augmenting the results of the health systems strengthening initiatives and other cross sectoral themes of social and health determinants at the state and district level. A qualitative research design was adopted for the same. Findings from this study have provided insights into the status of the existing SBCC cells and identified certain areas for improvement in its functioning. The suggestive recommendations for strengthening the functioning of SBCC cells are based on the findings from study, field observations, consultations with stakeholders. Confidence in the generalizability of the findings and recommendations is enhanced by the comprehensive strategy and design used for the selection of the states and arena of stakeholders interviewed as a part of this evaluation . Recommendation 1: | Strengthen existing state level cells |Responsible Organization: Department under which cell is housed in states| |---|---| |Priority: High|SBC SS indicator: Leadership and Governance| |Time frame: 6 – 12 months|Recommendation for category: I, II and III| Rationale: As noted under relevance evaluation criteria (Relevance KEQs), among all the evaluated states, presence of an active state SBCC cell ensured high relevance of SBCC activities to flagship programs, and priorities of the national and state governments. It also enabled collaboration among departments, development partners and NGOs for various SBCC activities. However, the state governments have not taken full ownership of the cells. In states like UP and West Bengal, the state level cells are defunct and SBCC activities were being conducted mostly at the district level. Hence, it is recommended that government should strengthen the state level SBCC cells for better implementation of SBCC activities in the state and advocacy for adoption of SBCC approach in state government programming. The state SBCC cell would act as a one stop solution for all the SBCC activities in the state and converge the impact of district level cells. Action points for State: - ➢ Government should strengthen the state level SBCC cells by setting a clear mandate for its roles and responsibilities vis-à-vis the structure of the state department under which the cell is housed. - ➢ The state cell should enable communication capacity building for health workforce and provide technical assistance to department it is housed under and other allied departments. - ➢ The state cell must assign, but not be limited to, a nodal officer (preferably a BCC expert), a graphic designer, a M&E expert, a communication/ language expert for contextualization of SBCC material to local languages and cultural preference and imagery. - ➢ The state cell should coordinate with the district level officials and FLWs and conduct SBCC activities effectively and efficiently at district and block levels. Recommendation 2: | Prioritize key areas for provision of technical expertise |Responsible Organization: UNICEF| |---|---| |Priority: High|SBC SS indicator: Workforce and service delivery| |Time frame: 6 – 12 months|Recommendation for category: I, II and III| UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 57 Rationale: The evaluation found that district level engagement by UNICEF in form of district level loaned consultant was limited to contribution towards capacity building and SBCC repository management. Their role at the district level can be taken over by a health / PRI official, already placed at the district. However, states are yet to make the transition from IEC to SBCC. In view of this, UNICEF should continue its assistance to the states, but with focused engagement. To ensure focused and effective delivery of SBCC, we strongly recommend that UNICEF should concentrate efforts and resources at the state level only through the continued placement of an UNICEF consultant. As evident in states such as Odisha, Telangana and Madhya Pradesh, the presence of a state level consultant who is able to advocate and provide technical expertise at the state level. In the case of Odisha and Madhya Pradesh, a cascade effect of the contribution of the state consultant resulting in increased effectiveness of service delivery was observed even at the district level. Action points for UNICEF: - ➢ Technical expertise – Support from UNICEF can be instrumental to reduce the gap that currently exists within SBCC programming, particularly related to gender disparities, prejudices, and ill-practices of early marriage, empowerment of girls and safeguarding sexual and reproductive health. UNICEF should continue to extend its support in SBCC activities across the cell through provision of technical expertise. To do so, UNCEF must continue to place a consultant in the state level cells in all six states whose main responsibility would be to set and implement the transition plan for at least one more programmatic cycle of five years. The consultant must have domain expertise in social behavior change and communication. - ➢ During the next programmatic cycle, whilst the cells have the guidance of the UNICEF loaned SBCC expert, the focus of the cell should be to develop a clear transition strategy. This strategy should be developed to cover, but not be limited to, aspects of financial resource management human resource management, methods of evidence generation, monitoring and evaluation framework, community engagement and inclusion of gender and equity lens in SBCC programming (expanded in Recommendation 3). This would enable a smooth transition from SBCC activities being UNICEF supported to becoming fully owned by the state government. At the end of the next programmatic cycle of five years, a similar evaluation should be conducted to assess the progress of the transition of the state and decide future course of action including the decision to continue stationing of the UNICEF consultant at the state level cell. Recommendation 3: | Develop SOPs for SBCC cells at state and district level |Responsible Organization: UNICEF| |---|---| |Priority: Medium|SBC SS indicator: Service Delivery| |Time frame: 12 - 18 months|Recommendation for category: I, II and III| Rationale: Since the state departments under which the cell is housed have not fully transitioned from IEC to SBCC, they require technical support to effectively deliver SBCC activities (Sustainability KEQ 2). As suggested earlier, an UNICEF SBCC consultant who is a technical expert in SBCC programming should continue to provide support for another programmatic cycle of five years. In addition to the consultant, whose engagement is for a fixed period of time, the development of principle guiding documents in the form of clearly laid out SOPs would ensure a continued support and source of knowledge of methods of SBCC for the said departments. Hence, UNICEF is recommended to develop SOPs on various aspects of the functioning of cell to effectively deliver SBCC activities. Action point for UNICEF: - ➢ Development of SOPs - UNICEF should develop SOPs for the SBCC cells at state and district level which will work as principle guiding documents for state department communications officers to effectively carry out SBCC activities after the withdrawal of UNICEF support post the next programmatic cycle. The SOPs should cover, but not be limited to, the following topics- UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 58 1. Financial resource management 2. Human resource management 3. Methods of evidence generation 4. Monitoring and evaluation framework 5. Community engagement 6. Inclusion of gender and equity lens in SBCC programming Recommendation 4: | Adopt a systematic approach to human resource management |Responsible Organization: Department under which cell is housed (primary role) and UNICEF (secondary role)| |---|---| |Priority: High|SBC SS indicator: Workforce| |Time frame: 12-18 months|Recommendation for category: I, II and III| Rationale: - It was highlighted that there was shortfall in availability of human resources (Relevance KEQ 2 and 3, except Telangana) corresponding to required positions due to vacancies at all three levels (state, district and block level). This hinders effective service delivery of SBCC activities and in engagement with community members. - Further, it was also found that the capacity of human resources at all three levels in cells of category 1 and 2 to focus on communications activities is limited as priority is given to the governance and programmatic aspects of their professional role. (Efficiency KEQ 2) - The human resources at all three levels (state, district and block level) across the six states were at differing levels of competency in regard to knowledge and skills relating to communications and social behavior change. There is a need to ensure uniform level of training and capacity development across all six states to ensure effective service delivery of SBCC activities. Action points for State: - ➢ The respective state government departments under which the cell is housed should increase advertising of vacant positions at state, district and block level and ensure active hiring to reduce vacancy of said positions. - ➢ To ensure that the human resources at all three levels in cells of categories 1 and 2 give equal priority to communications activities and programmatic aspects of their professional role, the state government departments under which the cell is housed should clearly highlight responsibilities related to communications in the job description advertisement. This would decrease the likelihood of these human resources feeling that communications activities are additional responsibilities as compared to programmatic aspects being primary. - ➢ Competency mapping - The respective state government departments under which the cell is housed across all six states should conduct a competency mapping exercise in which the current competencies of those human resource who are engaged in communications activities should be mapped against a cadre wise preselected list of required communications related competencies. This mapping would act as a needs assessment exercise from which the level of capacity development required can be determined for human resource who are engaged in communications activities within the state. - ➢ Capacity development - The delivery of the capacity development modules and its regular refresher should be taken up by the respective state government departments under which the cell is housed. The conduction should be done by a communications expert hired by the state department. This person should also be trained in workshopping and facilitation to ensure an engaging delivery. This facilitator should work in tandem with the UNICEF consultant placed at the state level. Action points for UNICEF: - ➢ Development of capacity development framework - UNICEF has the advantage of having subject and technical expertise in methods of SBCC as compared to the state departments which have not fully UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 59 transitioned from IEC to SBCC. Hence, UNICEF should develop a capacity development framework for the state departments who in turn would be responsible of the conduction and delivery of the same. The framework should provide cadre wise lists of desired knowledge and skills to effectively delivery SBCC activities and meaningfully engage in SBCC programming. Further, the framework should indicate the level of capacity development required as per level of presence/absence of the desired knowledge and skills as assessed in the workforce. - ➢ Development of training modules - Basis the capacity development framework; UNICEF should develop training modules which would be differentiated for cadres of human resource engaged in communications activities. Further, the modules can be differentiated on the basis of presence of desired knowledge and skills assessed in the workforce. Recommendation 5: Adopt a systematic approach for evidence Responsible Organization: UNICEF based SBCC planning Priority: Medium SBC SS indicator: Information Time frame: 12 - 24 months Recommendation for category: I, II and III Rationale: - As noted in the effectiveness KEQ 1, the cells in category I and II, and III (WB) have not been able to focus on evidence generation to develop evidence-based communication initiatives due to lack of training and mentorship in required methodologies and HR constraints. The evaluation found instances of evidence-based planning in category III (Telangana) where the communication strategies developed by the CRU are based on secondary data sources. - There is a need for planning, designing and implementation of the SBCC strategies, taking due account of local culture, practices and sensibilities - As noted in the effectiveness KEQ 7.6, the cell in category I, no evidence of knowledge sharing with allied, collaborating departments and development agencies / NGOs was found in the state. Similarly, across other categories knowledge sharing was not seen as the priority and was need based as in case of COVID -19 pandemic. - Similarly, it was found during the evaluation, effectiveness KEQ 7.5, there was little focus on documentation and analysis, and monitoring and evaluation of the SBCC activities beyond the general administrative documentation. This affects SBCC activities due to which strategic thinking and decision making regarding in SBCC programming are negatively affected. Action points for UNICEF: Evidence Generation - ➢ UNICEF through its state level consultant with domain expertise in social behavior change and communication should provide technical expertise in designing appropriate implementation mechanisms for SBCC design and maintain social and behavioral sustainability by planning SBCC by consideration of changing social and behavioral norms and ensuring the needs of varied communities making it gender responsive and equity focused. - ➢ To design socially relevant implementation mechanisms for SBCC activities, the state level consultant should work in close collaboration with district level officials through monthly meetings. The district levels officials should provide field level insights on the needs of community members and community representatives. - ➢ Similarly, identification of key stakeholder groups (primary – those who are directly addressed, secondarythose who influence primary stakeholders and tertiary- directly or indirectly help or hinder the behaviors of other stakeholders) is crucial for SBCC planning. UNICEF consultant should work in coherence with the district officials to get insights about the social norms and needs of the community and in identification of key stakeholders UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 60 - ➢ Pre-testing of SBCC material – UNICEF through its state level consultant in collaboration with the department the cell is housed under should conduct the pre-testing of SBCC material to know if the intended audience understands the key messages before finalizing the SBCC material. UNICEF should develop a comprehensive guide for pretesting based on the measures – A) Comprehension – clarity of content, B) Attractiveness C) Acceptance – to the community based on socio-cultural norms, D) Involvement E) Call to action*. Based on the five criteria a tool to be developed for testing of SBCC material through FLWs in the districts where UNICEF has presence. - ➢ Pre-testing (data collection) can be carried out by FLWs during the community meetings like VHND. The inputs from the community should be conveyed by the district to the state cell. Finally, by incorporation of the inputs, SBCC material needs to be modified. Knowledge Management - ➢ UNICEF to institutionalize knowledge management (documentation of good practices, learnings, knowledge sharing) into the overall SBCC planning. - ➢ Good practices and lessons learned can be shared across the SBCC cells by publication of knowledge products on yearly basis. Documentation of case studies of successes and challenges and creation of documentation of good practices facilitate knowledge sharing among the state cells. - ➢ The SBCC alliance can facilitate an annual conclave for innovative practices in SBCC with participation from MOHFW, state SBCC cells, development partners and other relevant stakeholders which will act as a platform for knowledge sharing and cross learning. Recommendation 6: | Ensuring dedicated financial resources for SBCC cells |Responsible Organization: Department under which cell is housed in states and UNICEF| |---|---| |Priority: High|SBC SS indicator: Finance| |Time frame: 6 – 12 months|Recommendation for category: I, II and III| Rationale: During the evaluation, under evaluation criteria of efficiency KEQ 1, it was noted that: - ➢ state cell of Assam (category 1) had sufficient funds for certain activities like, RI, a state priority, whereas there were limited financial resources for other SBCC activities beyond RI. - ➢ Although, states under category 2 were housed health department, but the state, and district cells had to depend upon financial resources provided by various national programs who collaborated with the cell for SBCC activities. Cell’s efficiency curtailed due to non-availability of earmarked funding for SBCC activities. - ➢ CRU (state cell for Telangana) was able to make efficient and effective contribution towards SBCC activities of the state because it was involved in financial planning for the cell. The annual budget for CRU was based upon the strategic plan developed by CRU for the upcoming year, which led to optimum utilization of allocated monetary resources. - ➢ Additionally, under evaluation criteria of relevance KEQ 3, it was highlighted in all three categories that there is a major limitation in the state cell’s ability to respond to the needs of community members and representatives due to resource and budget constraints. - ➢ Evaluation findings suggest that SBCC cells require independent financial resources for efficient functioning. Efficiency and effectiveness in state cells of states belonging to category 1 and 2 was affected by financial resource constraints as well. Whereas CRU’s financial management system has positively contributed towards efficient functioning of the cell. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 61 - ➢ It is proposed that the state cell should be given autonomy in deciding its financial resource requirement and utilization mechanism. State governments should be shouldered with the responsibility of providing desired financial resources to cell. Action points for State: - ➢ State governments should provide cells with opportunities to have an increased agency for conducting independent financial planning and spending the budget on SBCC activities. - ➢ States should streamline financial management of the SBCC cells and invite annual planning from the state SBCC cell (in consultation with district cells) and include them in the overall departmental budget of the state to ensure sustainable availability of funds for SBCC activities. Action point for UNICEF: - ➢ UNICEF should advocate for provisioning of financial resources for SBCC activities in national programs and state interventions. Recommendation 7: | Development of SBCC cell repository |Responsible Organization: Department under which cell is housed in states| |---|---| |Priority: Medium|SBC SS indicator: Information| |Time frame: 6 – 12 months|Recommendation for category: I, II and III| Rationale: - ➢ It was found that it was easier for human resources engaged in communications activities to implement IEC and SBCC activities when they had access to readily available SBCC material (Effectiveness KEQ 4, Category 2). - ➢ For example, In UP, communication officials were able to reach out to the district SBCC cell of Balarampur, when in need to access any communication material across aspects of health messaging. The cell had a large reserve of various communication material (posters, pamphlets, flipbooks, videos, etc.) developed by center, state and other development partners. Another example is the district level cells in Mayurbhanj and Puri (Category 2) which served a similar role of a repository for all communications material developed for the district. - ➢ The repository should include material which has been contextualized to ensure higher relevance of the material to local needs and norms. For example, in Telangana (Category 3), the CRU adapts national level IEC material into regionally contextual material which includes translation into local languages and changing of imagery. Action points for State: - ➢ One stop repository of SBCC material: The state SBCC cell should be developed as a one stop repository of SBCC material for accessing and use by programme officials and frontline health workers. At the district level the repository should be physical, whilst at the state level, the repository should be physical and online to ensure greater accessibility to district level officials and frontline health workers. - ➢ The repository should contain SBCC material including, but not limited to leaflets, posters, banners, written content on various common topics of public health, flipbooks, charts, designs for wall painting, audio-visual material and other campaign material which are arranged under thematic rubrics. The knowledge resources should be available in English, Hindi and regional languages. - ➢ The state instated graphic designer and translator should focus on contextualization of SBCC material to local languages and dialects, culturally relevant imagery and social norms. This will enrich quality of the material stored in the repository. UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 62 Recommendation 8: | Ensuring monitoring and evaluation of SBCC cells |Responsible Organization: Department under which cell is housed in states and UNICEF| |---|---| |Priority: High|SBC SS indicator: Leadership and Governance, Workforce, Service Delivery and Information| |Time frame: 6 – 12 months|Recommendation for category: I, II and III| Rationale: During the evaluation, under evaluation criteria of effectiveness KEQ 6, it was noted that: - Across the categories, monitoring and evaluation of the SBCC cell’s activities was not conducted because there was no mandate and framework for M&E. The cells also lacked either financial resources or human resources (in terms of both people as well as required skill set) to conduct M&E activities. - Additionally, inconsistency in effectiveness of the cell in regard to training, documentation etc. could also have been reduced, if there had been a monitoring and evaluation mandate in the programming of the cells. - A system for monitoring and evaluation of SBCC activities is critical to ensure regular information flow on performance of activities, deviation of the cell from objectives and overall performance. It is recommended that UNICEF should advocate and provide technical support to states for investing in development of monitoring and evaluation framework. Action points for State: - ➢ The state SBCC cell should include monitoring and evaluation, as crucial pillar of the SBCC cell for the state. For which the state should instate a domain expert for M and E who can guide other officials for ensuring regular monitoring of SBCC activities in the state. - ➢ The state should conduct periodic monitoring and evaluation of the activities of SBCC activities through field visits, community observation etc. - ➢ The state should invest in leveraging technology (dashboard, apps etc.) to monitor SBCC activities. Action points for UNICEF: - ➢ UNICEF should design a sustainable monitoring and evaluation framework involving input and process indicators, like availability of SBCC strategy, percentage of fund utilization, number of training, trainee/trainer feedback etc., for states to follow and adopt in long run. - ➢ UNICEF should also act an independent evaluator of the performance of the cells across the states. - ➢ UNICEF should train and build capacity of the state officials to conduct monitoring and evaluation activities independently. Recommendation 9: | Gender and Equity - SBCC interventions should be more explicit and strategically integrated to gender equity|Responsible Organization: Respective State Governments with UNICEF’s support| |---|---| |Priority: High|SBC SS indicator: Across all Indicators| |Time frame: 6 – 12 months|Recommendations for Category: I, II, and III| Rationale: UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 63 - From the six evaluated states, it was found that although gender and equity are cross-cutting and a critical component of the SBCC, in practice, the Cells in the states have not yet mainstreamed gender equity, and neither are they gender-cognizant while designing SBCC activities, as was noted under relevance and effectiveness across all categories. WASH services and RMNCH+A has given attention to hygiene and sanitation, nutrition and women’s health. - However, SBCC activities are largely limited to comprehensive health strategy under RMNCH+A with priority intervention for nutrition, IFA supplementation, and so on. Wide gaps persist in fundamental areas of adolescent sexual and reproductive health (SRH), information and counseling, and in early marriage, teen pregnancy, school dropout of girls, and even male child preference in some states. In this context, the Rashtriya Kishor Swastha Abhijan and ARSH (national programmes for adolescent sexual and reproductive health (SRH) and holistic development) were not featured, neither mentioned by the states during the consultations). - As was found across the six evaluated states, there are no (in-depth) gender equity integrated SBCC activities for health for women and men, and adolescent boys and girls. There are no participatory studies related to sexual and reproductive health. Therefore, SBCC activities should emphasize the importance of health priorities but more comprehensively that intersects with related fundamental social issues, central to gender and equity. - Furthermore, it was found that the departments are not yet gender-focused, and largely do not have the necessary skills or level of knowledge to initiate gender and equity programmes, except for Odisha which has a relatively better equitable approach to SBCC planning. - The evaluation further found that a comprehensive Gender and Equity approach was missing at the state level SBCC agenda, while no specific gender-focused programmes are part of the district cell’s SBCC activities. A gender-transformative approach is thus a critical requirement to address social norms, practices in diverse social and economic contexts by the state and district cells, and assessing areas that need genderbased intervention, such as those in LODOR and other vulnerable communities. In view of this, UNICEF should support to the states in creating an enabling environment that is gender responsive on priority within its next programmatic cycle/ as a part of its transition strategy. Presence of a ‘Gender and Equity aware’ program staff will enable assessment of contexts and consciously address existing gaps, gender constraints, and so on within SBCC. - We gather that UNICEF’s support and interventions will be imperative both at state and district levels to initiate a dialogue and consolidate the SBCC agenda on mainstreaming Gender and Equity more thematically within the Cells. To ensure relevant, focused and effective delivery of the SBCC objectives, UNICEF should support state and district cells on research-based inputs on gender responsiveness and inclusivity and create the initial dialogue between states and districts for a Gender Equality Continuum. | Gender and Equity | Action points for State | Action points for UNICEF | |---|---|---| ||• States should design more gender|| ||responsive SBCC programs and|• UNICEF should engage with states in| ||enhance dialogue of men and women|order to give impetus to the| | Integrate and mainstream gender into the SBCC agenda |to ensure their active participation in discussions on family planning, mindsets, and gender roles •Conduction of needs assessmentwith inputs fromFLWs on prevalent social and|assessment • UNICEF should extend support to each state in the research inputs with frameworks, while the Cells can conduct the needs assessments based| ||SRH-related issues|on socio-economic and cultural| ||• Development of differentiated district-|contexts in their respective states| ||level plans based on needs of|| ||communities is a critical need.|| | Social Mobilization Committee (or Group) for gender- inclusive and gender-responsive |• States to Increase gender-inclusive and gender-responsive participatory studies as a core aspect for SBCC Cells for improved social behaviour outcomes|• UNICEF should design the methodologies and framework, and extend support by sharing inputs on the method of such studies for optimal| UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 64 | participatory |• States must undertake the|impact on the SBCC’s gender and| |---|---|---| | studies |responsibility of conducting the studies|equity outcomes| ||and allocating human and financial|• UNICEF should support states to| ||resources|create a social mobilization group that| |||is built on socio-cultural and economic| ||• A Social Mobilization Group for each state is crucial for initiating the study, as the state does not have a coordinating team for carrying out such|contexts of population groups, and settlements, need-specific gender- responsive issues| ||studies, and moreover, this can|| ||eventually increase gender equitable|| ||demand generation related activities.|| ||• States must Leverage partnerships with|| ||key (established) partners and|| | Identify and map |stakeholders focused on gender|• UNICEF should support the mapping| | stakeholders for |equality NGOs and development|exercise and appraise identified| | focus-driven gender |agencies must be involved with the|stakeholders.| | and equity partnerships within SBCC |state and district cells • States should identify relevant stakeholders both geographically and|• It may be useful for states to receive feedback from UNICEF on the mapping and the stakeholders identified| ||thematically to gauge the most relevant|| ||organizations|| | Design communication materials that are |• States to Increase effectiveness and inclusivity through communication on gender sensitization for adolescent youth, men and women health and hygiene, SRH|• UNICEF must support (at least in the initial phase) to help states with a blueprint for the communication materials that must be gender- cognizant and emphasizes on the need for equity (as this requires a carefully| | gender-sensitive to |• Mainstream health information|planned, gender-sensitive and gender-| | mainstream gender |communication to a more nuanced|inclusive agenda that UNICEF can| | equity |communication-strategy that will address issues taboos around gender-|provide) • UNICEF can appraise the| ||related topics|communication materials and share their inputs with the states| | Leverage UNICEF’s capacity development framework for capacity building of the States |• Respective departments under which the Cells are housed, must leverage UNICEF’s capacity development framework, to strengthen capacity • States would be responsible for the conduction and delivery of the SBCC activities • These will progressively expedite the gender and equity dimension in the|• Augment UNICEF staff capacity on gender and other equality and inclusion-related concepts and programming strategies • UNICEF having the advantage of expertise in both social issues and technical expertise, should support states in formulating the research inputs and guide in buildingcapacity, as this requires thorough research and| ||transition from IEC to SBCC|extensive baseline studies| ||• A refresher’s course needed for the Cell, trainers, and FLWs officers|• UNICEF should appraise (at least in the initial phase) the refresher’s course as this would determine the quality and| UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 65 degree of Gender and Equity integration within SBCC ||• States can conduct SBCC through oral|| |---|---|---| ||presentations, and interactive plenary|| ||sessions for SBCC Cells on the issues (but not limited to):|• UNICEF to support the Cells in orienting SBCC officers, trainers and| | Training of SBCC officers and FLWs in Gender sensitization and gender responsiveness |• Social status, gender roles, and role of men and women in household decisions, employment, education and age during marriage, childcare, family planning and health, access to employment, education and healthcare • Attitudes of adolescent youths, men,|FLWs on the issues mentioned (under actions for state) • UNICEF should support states by sharing their inputs (or designing) the oral presentations that will eventually be taken over by state cells to be carried forward • UNICEF must also support actively in| ||and women toward Gender based|the interactive plenary sessions during| ||Violence (GBV) (dominance, power|the initial phase that state cells would| ||dynamics, etc)|eventually carry out| ||• Status of women and girls in physically|| ||work household responsibilitiesand|| ||reproduction|| UNICEF SBCC Cells Programme Evaluation for 2017-21 PwC 66
UR  - https://www.unicef.org/evaluation/reports#/detail/19842
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
T1  - Evaluation of Scaling up Anticipatory Action and Shock Responsive Social Protection  in ASEAN
PY  - 2024
ST  - Beijie Lai
AU  - Beijie Lai
KW  - country:EAPRO, Thailand
CY  - EAPRO, Thailand
PP  - EAPRO, Thailand
KW  - region:EAPR
KW  - geros:Satisfactory
KW  - language:English
KW  - category:Evaluation
ID  - 20323
T2  - 20323
AB  - Climate risks, including climate change and disasters, significantly threaten the achievement of SDGs in Southeast Asia, especially concerning poverty alleviation by 2030. Vulnerable communities face recurring shocks that jeopardize children's well-being, highlighting the urgent need for risk-informed social protection. Traditional short-term humanitarian approaches are insufficient, necessitating a shift towards coherent climate risk management to tackle the root causes of vulnerability. This evaluation aims to assess the project "Scaling up Anticipatory Action and Shock Responsive Social Protection" from July 2022 to March 2024, focusing on its implementation in Indonesia and Cambodia. By examining its relevance, effectiveness, and sustainability, the evaluation seeks to identify challenges, successes, and lessons learned to enhance future programs. The findings will inform stakeholders, including UNICEF and national governments, to develop integrated strategies for disaster risk management and social protection, ultimately benefiting vulnerable populations in the region. -----Full Evaluation text----- |RDI|Resilience Development Initiative| |---|---| |RIMES|Regional Integrated Multi-Hazard Early Warning Systems| |SDGs|Sustainable Development Goals| |SPRI|Social Policy Research Institute| |SRSP|Shock-Responsive Social Protection| |TOR|Terms of Reference| |ToT|Training of Trainers| |UN|United Nations| |UNDRR|United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction| |UNEG|United Nations Evaluation Group| |UNICEF|United Nations Children’s Fund| |UNICEF EAPRO|UNICEF East Asia and Pacific Regional Office| |WASH|Water, Sanitation and Hygiene| |WB|World Bank| |WFP|World Food Programme| |WMO|World Meteorology Organization| Page | viii EXECUTIVE SUMMARY Background Climate risk – including climate change – and disasters pose a major threat to achieving the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) related to ending poverty in Southeast Asia by 2030. Climate risk and disasters are a growing menace to families living in communities vulnerable to shocks and facing emergency situations, with potentially devastating impacts on children and their childhoods. These alarming trends underscore the urgency of addressing these challenges through risk-informed social protection to help respond to a swiftly changing landscape in Southeast Asia. As most shocks in the region are broadly predictable, recurrent and/or protracted, a ‘business as usual’ approach is no longer an option. It has become apparent that short-term humanitarian strategies, funding cycles and programmes are neither intended nor able to address climate-induced crises and the interplay of shocks with poverty and chronic vulnerability. It is therefore necessary to promote coherent climate risk management approaches that address the underlying causes of vulnerability and poverty in Southeast Asia. These approaches must also increase the capacity of people in the region to cope and adapt to climate shocks in the short and long term. A paradigm shift towards early action based on forecast and social policy instruments such as social protection to prevent/reduce losses and impacts, respond more effectively and enhance resilience while enabling access to financial resources, has taken place in recent years. Anticipatory Action (AA) has been piloted in the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) region by international development and humanitarian partners to promote this shift. To ensure that no one is left behind, countries in Southeast Asia are striving to achieve commitments framed within the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, the Paris Agreement, the Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction, and the Regional Action Plan on the Implementation of the Declaration on Strengthening Social Protection in ASEAN. Social protection is a critical tool to protect people in Southeast Asia. A social protection system that is designed to better anticipate and respond to shocks, in coordination with other sectors such as disaster risk management (DRM), can help children and their families to better prepare for and cope with the impacts of stresses and shocks. At the same time, using social protection for shock response has been increasingly prioritized in the development and humanitarian agenda in Southeast Asia. Between 2017 and 2021, European Civil Protection and Humanitarian Aid Operations (ECHO) funded a consortium of United Nations agencies (FAO, UNICEF, WFP and UNDRR) and the International Federation of Red Cross and Red Crescent Societies (IFRC) to provide capacity-building for ASEAN Member States to design and implement shock-responsive social protection (SRSP) and AA. The vision was that these would become new approaches in the implementation of the ASEAN Agreement on Disaster Management and Emergency Response (AADMER) and its work programme; hence the development of country roadmaps to set up SRSP systems in Cambodia and the Philippines and policy recommendations in Viet Nam, as well as the ASEAN Guidelines on Disaster Responsive Social Protection to Increase Resilience. Page | ix From 2022 to 2024, UNICEF, WFP and the Finnish Red Cross (FinnRC) continued their efforts to strengthen regional disaster preparedness through a new iteration of the initiative ‘Scaling up Anticipatory Action and Shock Responsive Social Protection to proactively manage the risks and impacts of climate shocks and disasters in ASEAN’. The project aimed at continued capacity-building of ASEAN Member States and regional cooperation to further advance the roll-out of the ASEAN Guidelines on Disaster Responsive Social Protection to Increase Resilience, and country roadmaps. The objective was to establish SRSP systems in select ASEAN Member States (such as Cambodia and Indonesia) as an effective and sustainable mechanism to deliver emergency response, focusing particularly on the most vulnerable populations, and to the extent of possible, anticipate shocks. Evaluation objectives, scope and framework The objective of this evaluation is to assess the project ‘Scaling up Anticipatory Action and Shock Responsive Social Protection to proactively manage the risks and impacts of climate shocks and disasters in ASEAN’. The evaluation covers the entire project implementation period, including the original 18 months and the three-month non-cost extension (July 2022 to March 2024). Its geographical scope is the ASEAN region, with a special focus on Indonesia and Cambodia at the national level. The evaluation covers a number of summative, process and formative components, and considers the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development/Development Assistance Committee (OECD/DAC) evaluation criteria (relevance, effectiveness, efficiency, coherence, impact, and sustainability) to identify challenges, successes and lessons learned for future reference and improvement. [1] In regard to the thematic scope, given the limited project period and the very short period left for the assessment, the evaluation focuses on the activities themselves rather than on the expected results of the activities. The main interventions were intended to enhance institutional changes, but a project period of less than two years cannot be expected to produce solid structural or institutional changes. Also, project level objectives and outcomes were not assessed during this evaluation. It is anticipated that both the evaluation process and results will contribute to a body of evidence and learning about the implementation of programmes related to SRSP and AA. This includes identification of the enablers and barriers to delivering such programmes in countries in East Asia and the Pacific and how these have impacted on UNICEF and partners’ performance and ability to institutionalize SRSP and AA. The evaluation and results will generate practical solutions and recommendations that will help UNICEF – in its work with governments and other partners – to advance this agenda, which will ultimately ensure that vulnerable populations in Southeast Asia benefit from SRSP programmes that have improved links to early warning systems, AA, risk financing mechanisms and multidimensional vulnerabilities. The prospective users of the evaluation are UNICEF, the European Union (EU) and other key stakeholders, including national governments, international and regional partners (WFP/FinnRC/FAO) and civil society organizations. It is expected that the evaluation will be used by these stakeholders to work closely with national governments to develop longer-term integrated SRSP and DRM strategies to facilitate and enhance effectiveness in responding to climate change-related disasters. This also embraces advocacy for longer-term funding commitments, including through public financial management (PFM) systems, and working towards investing in strengthened social protection systems as a core element of DRM, while promoting a comprehensive SRSP approach that addresses the entire DRM cycle, enhancing readiness, resilience and recovery efforts. > 1 Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development, _Applying Evaluation Criteria Thoughtfully_ , OECD Publishing, Paris, 2021, https://doi.org/10.1787/543e84ed-en. Page | x Methodology The evaluation followed a participatory and utilization-focused approach, using mainly qualitative methods. It involved all concerned stakeholders, including government partners, project partners and service providers. Key to guiding the process was an evaluation matrix containing the main questions the evaluation sought to answer and – through systematic secondary questions – providing a practical, sequenced approach to gathering the data to enable each main question to be answered. The matrix also highlighted which of the evaluation criteria were most relevant in addressing each main question. Data was gathered from three primary sources: 1. a desk review of project-related documents; 2. 30 key informant interviews (KIIs) with nearly all identified stakeholders; 3. evaluation surveys conducted after two ASEAN initiatives (a social policy forum in Bali and a workshop in Bangkok). The evaluation covered a number of summative, process and formative components, specifically: - (i) relevance of the project design; - (ii) efficiency and effectiveness of implementation encompassing partnerships, operational arrangements, resource utilization, and so forth; - (iii) achievement of results with a focus on the capacity-building of implementing countries (Cambodia and Indonesia) and the broader ASEAN region; - (iv) overall quality of the project deliverables (such as accuracy and completeness); - (v) whether the project was implemented as planned and identification of the enablers and barriers to its delivery; - (vi) documenting what worked, what did not and lessons learned; - (vii) provision of recommendations for ASEAN countries and humanitarian/development partners for further scaling up and sustaining the approaches underpinned by the project. The evaluation aimed for triangulation and complementarity between methods. Evidence was triangulated within methods wherever appropriate (comparing the perspectives of different stakeholders interviewed). The findings were validated through a workshop with representatives from implementing partners at both regional and national levels. Certain limitations were found during the evaluation; most of them had been anticipated and were mitigated. The evaluation team did not encounter challenges to its independence or impartiality or any conflict of interest during the evaluation exercise. Throughout the process and in close consultation with UNICEF and all relevant stakeholders, the evaluation team ensured adherence to all ethical considerations, in line with the UNICEF Procedure for Ethical Standards in Research, Evaluation, Data Collection and Analysis, and the United Nations Evaluation Group’s Ethical Guidelines. Key findings The project's overall design was deemed adequate by nearly all international and government partners. It effectively aligned with the needs and priorities of stakeholders, focusing on early warning systems and their integration with SRSP, which are crucial for disaster-prone contexts and climate change adaptation. During implementation, the project adapted to governmental interests and data availability, ensuring continued relevance and responsiveness to evolving conditions. Page | xi Most activities were executed on time and government partners acknowledged significant benefits from the project , particularly in enhancing their understanding of both AA and the underlying forecasting and early warning systems. They also recognized the necessary actions required to integrate these elements into ongoing disaster management efforts. International partners valued the cooperation fostered by the project and emphasized the need for continued and increased collaboration. Workshops and conferences were highlighted as valuable platforms for sharing practices from other countries, networking and gaining deeper insights into disaster management and its relation to social protection. - In Cambodia, the development of climate vulnerability and risk indicators for beneficiary targeting and the surpassing of the Disaster Risk Reduction (DRR) information, education and communication outreach goal were noted as significant successes. The AA plan and operational manual for the Family Package are expected to be completed soon. - Indonesia benefited from capacity-building activities on adaptive social protection (ASP) and SRSP as well as the revision of the Disaster Risk Financing Insurance Strategy. On the other hand, the integration of information into the Disaster Mitigation Information System of the Indonesian Ministry of Social Affairs (e-SIMBA) platform was more difficult to implement due to the lack of a smooth communication channel at higher policy levels with the Ministry of Social Affairs (MoSA). - At the regional level , all intended deliverables are expected to be completed by the endproject deadline. The Regional Social Policy Forum in Bali in November 2023 and the May 2024 SRSP workshop in Bangkok were successful, based on the feedback received from the organizers and participants; significant progress has been made in enhancing forecasting capabilities, disaster risk financing (DRF), capacity-building and regional knowledge sharing. Progress was made in the forecasting study at the national level, but its applications at the regional level encountered several challenges. The risk financing study was held back due to delays in accessing ministries and obtaining relevant financial data. Furthermore, the project has stimulated and reinforced policy initiatives that will leave an imprint long after the project ends. In Cambodia, the launch and subsequent implementation of the SRSP framework marks a significant milestone in DRM and social protection integration. Collaborative efforts between the Government and WFP are under way to develop operational plans, scalability frameworks and tools tailored to flood-related emergencies. Pilot programmes for both stand-alone emergency cash transfers and anticipatory cash under the SRSP scalability framework are poised to enhance disaster response capabilities. Moreover, the commitment to piloting the AA protocol that was developed (to include forecast monitoring), coupled with capacity-building initiatives, underscores a strategic approach towards forecast monitoring and timely warning dissemination. Meanwhile, in Indonesia, capacity-building endeavours alongside improvements in integrated socioeconomic registration reflect a concerted effort to strengthen ASP at the provincial and village levels. Additionally, regional collaboration, as evidenced by initiatives in Malaysia, China, Thailand and Viet Nam, highlights a shared commitment to bolstering social protection systems and promoting resilience across Southeast Asia. However, the ambitious project objectives were constrained by the limited timeline and several coordination challenges. Ministries tended to operate in silos, and data management issues hindered efficient coordination. Stakeholder scepticism towards new technology-based approaches and skills gaps in disaster forecasting and social protection areas, particularly in Cambodia, posed additional challenges. A major issue was the lack of effective coordination between government institutions and between the several layers of government and international organizations, resulting in poorly integrated donor contributions and reduced impact. Page | xii The integration of social protection and AA presents significant conceptual and pragmatic challenges. AA operates within a well-defined period of the DRM cycle, demanding swift responses to imminent threats. AA can be linked to social protection instruments, but their integration faces challenges due to the rapid response required. In general, successful integration also depends on the degree to which AA is integrated into the larger social protection system. The urgency of AA complicates the alignment of specific social protection instruments, which typically entail longer preparation and implementation periods, into a cohesive SRSP system. Overcoming these obstacles necessitates improved coordination, robust data management and heightened political commitment to effectively integrate AA initiatives into a comprehensive system of SRSP. Conclusions Conclusion 1: Relevance and strategic alignment The project's objectives and interventions were highly relevant, aligning closely with regional and national needs and priorities. However, the overly ambitious scope led to challenges in defining clear, measurable objectives, impacting verifiability. While the project fostered significant benefits and enhanced stakeholder understanding, legislative constraints and forecast reliability concerns remain. Capacity-building and knowledge-sharing were pivotal, but continued efforts are needed to ensure the project's relevance and impact amid evolving risks and policies. Refining objectives and aligning them with measurable outcomes are essential for sustained success. Conclusion 2: Effectiveness in achieving results The SRSP and AA approaches had the potential to advance the overall strategic objectives if more time and resources had been allocated to the project. Many of the output targets were met as per the project deliverables. Although impactful results and valuable knowledge products were produced, ongoing challenges remain. Inefficiencies stemmed from the absence of a smooth communication channel with higher policy levels, leading to communication gaps. Additionally, there were limitations on data accessibility. The project also required more realistic planning and sustained support beyond the ECHO framework, elements that are essential for overcoming current operational and technological hurdles. Conclusion 3: Enhanced efficiency and coherence through multi-stakeholder collaboration The project enhanced efficiency and coherence by cultivating a collaborative environment among diverse stakeholders. Specific success stories included regional workshops and knowledge-sharing events, which contributed to a more unified approach across borders. The partnership between UNICEF and WFP was strengthened under the SRSP framework. There was, however, less than optimal overall coordination with governments at different levels. This misalignment caused governments to struggle with integrating donor contributions to the project and other sources into their mid-term and long-term plans, leading to confusion, poorly coordinated assistance, and reduced impact. Challenges also arose from ministries working in silos, insufficient communication between regional and national levels, and bureaucratic procedures straining resources. Clearer accountability and better alignment between donor and government perspectives are needed to enhance overall coordination and effectiveness. Conclusion 4: Sustained commitment There is a tangible commitment from governmental bodies and implementing partners to continue scaling up SRSP and AA, underpinned by national budget allocations and political will. Nevertheless, sustaining these efforts requires overcoming challenges in Cambodia and Indonesia. In Cambodia, establishing clear leadership, integrating data systems for real-time Page | xiii updates, and maintaining consistent communication among stakeholders are necessary. In Indonesia, despite strong political will and budget support, further engagement with ministries is needed to sustain progress. At the regional level, effective dissemination and regular updates of guidance documents, along with integrating AA systems into government frameworks, are crucial for long-term sustainability. Conclusion 5: Impact and systemic integration The interventions generated systemic changes that promote the rights and well-being of vulnerable populations in Cambodia and Indonesia, with ripple effects anticipated across the ASEAN region. Although immediate humanitarian impacts remain nascent, the groundwork established through this project enhanced preparedness and response mechanisms. The incorporation of advanced methodologies and the fostering of cross-border knowledge exchanges signal a progressive trajectory towards more resilient and inclusive policies. Stakeholders' positive reception and the initial strides in policy formulation indicate fertile ground for future developments, emphasizing the necessity for sustained investment and collaborative efforts to fully realize the potential benefits of AA and SRSP integration. Lessons learned 1. Strategic and long-term planning for climate change: To enhance the effectiveness and efficiency of future projects, it is important to reconsider how SRSP and DRM can be better integrated. As climate change disasters become more frequent and devastating, policy reactions will be more effective and efficient if they are part of a strategic exercise rather than ad-hoc responses to crises. Successful mitigation and management of and recovery from disasters depend on long-term investments and preparatory actions. Future projects should be based on strategic and long-term planning, with countries developing mid-term and longterm strategies for dealing with the social consequences of climate change-related disasters. This approach also facilitates easier donor coordination and encourages donors to pledge financing for specific parts of a comprehensive plan. 2. Longer timelines and flexible and cooperative project design and implementation: Longer timelines and/or long-term funding commitments from donors would boost project effectiveness, especially if actions require institutional reforms from governments. This argues for a broader conceptualization of the interaction between climate change response policy and SRSP. Even with a focus on short-term actions, as in AA using social protection instruments, slightly longer project cycles would enhance effectiveness and efficiency. Breaking down silos between ministries and international assistance branches, allowing for more integration between humanitarian interventions and longer-term (social) development initiatives and adopting a coaching approach rather than prescriptive exchanges would be beneficial. For instance, establishing working groups can ensure stakeholder alignment and coordination, as well as secure government buy-in. Moreover, prioritizing educational campaigns and capacitybuilding can foster familiarity and confidence in new approaches. Lastly, pilot testing serves as a vital step in building evidence and refining approaches before full-scale implementation, minimizing risks and maximizing impact. 3. Centralized data management and coordination: Centralized data management emerges as a critical component, streamlining information flow across ministries and countries to enable real-time updates and enhanced coordination. A standard civil registry containing basic information on all residents stands out as an essential component for comprehensive data management, offering a reliable foundation for social registries, especially in the aftermath of disasters when accuracy is paramount. Additionally, establishing coordination mechanisms, such as technical working groups, fosters collaboration during implementation, providing a platform for ongoing dialogue and feedback among stakeholders. Clear leadership Page | xiv and coordination mechanisms are imperative, spanning ministries and institutions, including national disaster coordinators, to maintain direction and coherence throughout the project life cycle. Targeted training initiatives play a pivotal role in addressing specific skills gaps, particularly in forecasting capabilities and strengthening social protection systems, thereby bolstering project effectiveness. Lastly, emphasizing capacity-building and local ownership ensures the sustainability of project outcomes, empowering communities and institutions to continue the project's legacy beyond its completion. Integrating these elements into project planning and execution is essential for achieving meaningful and lasting results. 4. Broader role of social protection in the full DRM cycle: SRSP is much more than shortterm, one-off cash benefits. Social protection is inherently a tool for social risk management and cannot be reduced solely to cash transfers within AA. Social protection has a broader role to play in the entire DRM continuum; it can, however, be integrated into AA. Integrating social protection into the AA framework requires investment in social protection long before a disaster is predicted. This includes developing robust registries and efficient disbursement mechanisms and securing earmarked funding. A clear link with PFM needs to be established to make it easier and more cost-effective for politicians to connect AA with financing long-term strategies. A forward-thinking approach is essential, where a well-designed social protection system not only responds to crises but also influences every aspect of the DRM cycle – from readiness and anticipation to recovery, resilience-building and risk reduction. Looking ahead, it is imperative to extend the connection between AA and SRSP to encompass the full DRM cycle. By embracing a comprehensive range of SRSP measures and integrating them into DRM strategies, future initiatives can achieve greater impact and ensure long-term social protection. Recommendations Recommendation 1 : Continue to work with governments to develop mid-term and long-term integrated SRSP and DRM strategies. Ensure these strategies are part of a comprehensive plan for better donor coordination. UNICEF regional and country offices, together with other partners, should continue to advocate for and support governments to develop mid-term and long-term integrated SRSP and DRM strategies to facilitate and enhance effectiveness in responding to climate changerelated disasters. This is key to accommodate the necessary institutional reforms and foster a coordinated approach that integrates SRSP and AA with long-term strategies. _Responsible entity : UNICEF, working with national governments and other partners._ _Priorities: High_ Recommendation 2 : Advocate for longer-term funding commitments to allow for institutional reforms and capacity-building, including linking AA with financing of long-term strategies through PFM systems. UNICEF should continue to advocate with governments and donors to consider longer project timelines and secure longer-term funding commitments by negotiating agreements. _Responsible entity : UNICEF, working with national governments, other partners and donors._ _Priorities: Medium_ Page | xv Recommendation 3 : Invest in strengthening social protection systems as a core element of DRM, while promoting a comprehensive SRSP approach that addresses the entire DRM cycle, enhancing readiness, resilience and recovery efforts. Governments, in collaboration with UNICEF and other partners, should be supported to invest in strengthening social protection systems as a key component of DRM. This investment should promote a comprehensive SRSP approach that integrates all phases of the DRM cycle – prevention, preparedness, response, and recovery. Specific actions include building infrastructure for faster response, conducting regular risk assessments and scaling up social protection programmes during crises. _ Responsible entity : Governments, in collaboration with UNICEF and other development partners_ _Priorities: High_ Recommendation 4 : Implement pilot projects to refine strategies and ensure stakeholder alignment before full implementation. To ensure clear and measurable objectives, governments and international organizations should conduct pilot projects before full-scale implementation. Initiate small, controlled pilot projects to test feasibility and refine methodologies. Use these pilots to collect practical insights, adjust strategies based on real-world feedback and secure commitment from stakeholders. This preparatory phase is essential for the successful roll-out of larger initiatives. _ Responsible entity: Governments, in collaboration with UNICEF and other partners._ _Priorities: Medium_ Recommendation 5 : Establish cross-ministerial working groups to break down silos and encourage collaboration between humanitarian and development sectors. To address the lack of coordination during project implementation, governments, with support from UNICEF and other partners, should continue to advocate for and support the establishment of cross-ministerial working groups. This should include developing mechanisms for cross-sectoral collaboration dedicated to integrating SRSP and DRM, and facilitating coordination with other stakeholders, including donors. _ Responsible entity: Governments, with support from UNICEF and other partners._ _Priorities: High_ Recommendation 6 : Support the development of a standardized civil registry for accurate data management, particularly after disasters. Governments, with technical support from UNICEF and other international organizations, should develop a standardized civil registry system to enhance data management and disaster response, especially after disasters. Actions should include: creating a centralized, reliable database for real-time data collection and post-disaster response; and establishing protocols for regular updates and data audits to ensure accuracy. _ Responsible entity : Governments, with technical support from UNICEF and other international organizations_ _Priorities: Medium_ Page | xvi Recommendation 7 : Provide targeted training to fill specific skills gaps, particularly in forecasting and social protection systems. UNICEF and other partners should support integrated and targeted training to address specific skills gaps in disaster forecasting and social protection systems. This includes designing tailored training programmes that focus on technical skills such as data management and analysis, forecasting models and the implementation of social protection measures. Training sessions should be localized, empowering community-level personnel to respond effectively in disaster situations. Additionally, continuous capacity-building initiatives should be established to ensure skills are kept up to date. _ Responsible entity : UNICEF, working with other partners._ _Priorities: High_ Recommendation 8 _:_ Support the development of coherent results frameworks that include clear, measurable and verifiable results. UNICEF and other partners should work with governments to better articulate results for new projects and develop results frameworks that clearly detail the intervention logic, strategic goals and the relationship between programme components. Furthermore, these should include indicators that are SMART (specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and timebound). By developing such frameworks with all key stakeholders, embedding them in all planning documents and regularly updating them to reflect evolving contexts, the programme will enhance understanding and communication. Additionally, UNICEF should advocate for robust monitoring and reporting systems to systematically report on progress. _Responsible entity : UNICEF, working with national governments and other partners._ _Priorities: High_ Page | xvii ==> picture [473 x 664] intentionally omitted <== © UNICEF/UNI617017/Piojo Page | xviii 1 Project description 1.1 Project objectives The overall objective of the project was to expand SRSP programmes in ASEAN Member States to benefit vulnerable populations and proactively manage risks arising from climate shocks and disasters. More specifically, the aim was to achieve the strategic objective through strengthening the links to early warning systems, implementing AA, establishing risk financing mechanisms and addressing multidimensional vulnerabilities. The following section describes the original design of the project. Changes made to the original design will be discussed in the evaluation process. The strategic objective and the indicators for achievement are as follows: Strategic objective : Vulnerable populations in Southeast Asia benefit from enhanced SRSP programmes with improved links to early warning systems, AA, risk financing mechanisms and multidimensional vulnerabilities. Two indicators will reflect the achievement of the strategic objective: 1. At the end of the project, two ASEAN Member States have recorded a 15 per cent reduction in the expected number of affected people. 2. At the end of the project, at least two ASEAN Member States have their social protection systems connected to climate risk-informed tools as well as the mechanisms to target beneficiaries before, during and after climate shocks through social protection systems. As indicated in the introduction, the expected results of the strategic objective are three-fold: - Result 1: Social protection and AA systems are strengthened and interlinked to address the impacts of climate hazards on the most vulnerable populations in Cambodia. - Result 2: The Government of Indonesia has systems, tools, knowledge and financial mechanisms to mitigate the impact of climate hazards and disasters through SRSP. - Result 3: ASEAN Member States benefit from harmonized approaches and make use of regional tools to design and implement integrated AA and SRSP programmes. Each of these expected results has associated indicators designed to monitor its achievement. These indicators are associated with activities, which are elaborated in the upcoming sections. 1.2 Project description and design This project builds upon two previous iterations of the initiative begun in 2018: - i. The first phase, from 2017 to 2019, was led by the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (FAO) in collaboration with the International Labour Organization (ILO), UNICEF and WFP. Its objective was to institutionalize AA and SRSP. Key achievements were the different country roadmaps and the development of the ASEAN Guidelines on Disaster Responsive Social Protection to Increase Resilience. - ii. The second phase was led by a wider consortium of United Nations agencies until 2021. That phase focused on ongoing capacity-building among ASEAN Member States and regional collaboration to advance AA and implement the ASEAN guidelines to increase resilience and country roadmaps developed in the initial phase, within selected ASEAN countries. - iii. The third phase ran from 2022 to 2023 with a three-month extension to 2024. The main collaborators in this phase were UNICEF, WFP and FinnRC. The ‘Scaling up Anticipatory Action and Shock Responsive Social Protection to proactively manage the risks and impacts of climate shocks and disasters in ASEAN’ incorporated the key take-aways from previous phases. As such, this phase aimed to bridge the existing Page | 3 gap between AA and SRSP to leverage their respective benefits. Key activities were implemented in Cambodia and Indonesia as well as on a regional scale, involving stakeholders from across ASEAN. 1.3 Beneficiaries The beneficiaries of this project were selected based on the same principles as in the previous phases. In ASEAN Member States, indirect beneficiaries included poor and vulnerable populations living in high-risk areas with severely affected livelihoods and limited social protection. Specific attention was to be given to women from vulnerable households, including poor, near-poor and female-headed households. Additionally, priority was to be given to older persons, children, youth, people with disabilities and ethnic minority households. Direct beneficiaries were to include: (i) Local and national authorities in social welfare, DRR, health and related sectors – to benefit from capacity-building; and (ii) United Nations agencies, NGOs and other stakeholders – to benefit from technical methodologies, tools, training materials and collaborative efforts with ASEAN Member States and the ASEAN Secretariat (ASEC). At the regional level, the project aimed to enhance the capacity of ASEAN sector bodies[8] and ASEC in disaster management, social welfare, health, finance and related areas. 1.4 Project management and coordination structure The main agencies involved in the project were UNICEF, WFP and FinnRC. Coordination was sustained with FAO, other humanitarian partners, national and local authorities as well as development actors, including the World Bank and Asian Development Bank (ADB), and programmes. Regionally, the programme involved agencies that are part of the Inter-Agency Standing Committee (IASC) and collaborated closely with ASEAN partners through ASEC, aligning activities with ASEAN's humanitarian agenda. Coordination with national and local authorities at the regional level was also facilitated through collaboration with ASEC. Finally, the development actors involved in coordination were the World Bank, ADB, think tanks, research institutes and other United Nations agencies. At the country level, coordination with humanitarian partners occurred through existing coordination mechanisms like the Humanitarian Forum in Cambodia and national Cash Working Groups. In terms of national and local authorities, the project closely engaged with ministries or agencies related to DRM and social protection authorities. The development actors engaged regionally also played a role in Cambodia and Indonesia separately. 1.5 Activities 1.5.1 Activities in Cambodia In Cambodia, activities focused on strengthening systems-building within the National Committee for Disaster Management (NCDM) and Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation (MoSVY) to integrate the Government’s SRSP frameworks and AA with risk monitoring tools. Under Result 1 , as defined above, three activities were delineated. > 8 ASEAN Committee on Disaster Management, Senior Officials Meeting on Social Welfare and Development, ASEAN Senior Officials Meeting on Health and Development, finance and others. Page | 4 _1.1. Develop the AA plan and integrate into DRM and SRSP systems_ This activity consisted of developing and testing a framework for triggering AA in response to flood hazards in Cambodia. This involved updating flood forecast thresholds and integrating them with AA to mitigate flood impacts. The goal was to integrate this plan into Cambodia's DRM and enhance impact-based forecasting capacities for relevant institutions and actors. Further execution of the AA plan was led by the NCDM and was expected to be finalized by June 2024. The SRSP framework was developed by the General Secretariat, National Social Protection Council (GS-NSPC) in collaboration with the MoSVY and the National Social Assistance Fund (NSAF). WFP was responsible for the outputs related to this activity. _1.2. Support development and institutionalization of the shock-responsive/adaptive Family Package._ This activity introduced risk-adjusted procedures for existing cash transfer programmes in Cambodia, building on the SRSP framework to enhance programme design and operational models. It consisted of a Family Package of core cash transfer programmes to replace the COVID-19 cash transfer programme, targeting vulnerable groups such as pregnant women with young children, children in school, people with disabilities, and the elderly. An operations manual was designed for the implementation of the Family Package and was expected to be delivered by Quarter 4 2024. UNICEF was responsible for the outputs related to this activity. _1.3. Support development and update of the vulnerability and risk monitoring system tools._ The primary purpose of the Risk and Vulnerability framework is to monitor and track beneficiaries of the Family Package to gain a deeper understanding of their situation, which in turn will improve service design and facilitate referrals. As the family package has not yet been finalized, the risk and vulnerability tool has not thus far been integrated into the package The framework also monitors risk factors such as changes in household composition or outcomes of beneficiaries. Additionally, the framework supports SRSP by identifying key indicators and associated risk factors that influence household vulnerability to poverty and social exclusion. UNICEF is responsible for the outputs related to this activity. 1.5.2 Activities in Indonesia In Indonesia, the activities aimed to enhance government capacity to make timely, riskinformed decisions addressing multidimensional vulnerabilities and to develop financing models for SRSP. Under Result 2 , as defined above, six activities were initially expected. _2.1. Provide technical assistance to strengthen the interoperability and connectivity of_ _e-SIMBA with other risk information systems._ The e-SIMBA platform is used to monitor the status of disaster preparedness and response activities as well as social protection support in Indonesia. On the request of MoSA, WFP provided technical assistance to strengthen e-SIMBA to provide a more comprehensive picture of potential disaster risks and help forecast impacts on the most vulnerable communities. The aimed-for result is better decision-making regarding anticipatory and shockresponsive social protection (ASP/SRSP) and AA programmes. WFP provided the manual while a Training of Trainers (ToT) workshop was conducted at the national level and three such workshops were carried out at the province level to 192 participants. Page | 5 _2.2. Strengthen sustainable capacities at national and subnational levels (province and district levels) to improve the knowledge and skills of relevant government officials on ASP/SRSP and AA._ On the request of Indonesia’s Ministry of Planning (BAPPENAS), WFP and UNICEF were responsible for developing training materials on ASP/SRSP, undertaking a training of national ministries and selected provincial governments involved in ASP/SRSP implementation, and establishing a core team of 10 trainers on e-SIMBA. UNICEF, with the support of BAPPENAS, supervised the delivery of both the materials and the training, while WFP led the creation of the core team of trainers. The implementation of four activities originally intended proved to be challenging. In order to address the challenges, the remaining activities were reorganized: initial activities 2.3[9] and 2.4[10] were consolidated under 2.2 as a training provided by BAPPENAS. Similarly, activities 2.5[11] and 2.6[12] were merged into a revised activity (2.5) under the helm of UNICEF. _2.5. Inform the revision of the Disaster Risk Financing Insurance Strategy to integrate an emergency social protection financing strategy._ This last activity included a public engagement event on DRF at the ASEAN 2023 summit chaired by Indonesia. A report and policy brief were developed to inform the revision of the Disaster Risk Financing Insurance Strategy to integrate an emergency social protection financing strategy. 1.5.3 Activities in the ASEAN region At the regional level, the consortium agencies have been leveraging lessons from the pilot countries and have worked on a series of complementary projects to equip ASEAN Member States with the tools and knowledge to integrate, design, implement and monitor AA and SRSP programmes effectively. Under Result 3 , as defined above, three activities were expected. _3.1. Ensure quality forecast products and impact-based analysis platforms at regional and ASEAN Member State level._ This activity was aimed at enhancing disaster management among ASEAN Member States. It focused on improving risk information, forecasting and early warning systems as outlined in the ASEAN Framework for Disaster Management. The activity included a mapping exercise, a training and a guidance note on seasonal and sub-seasonal forecasts. FinnRC led this activity with support from WFP and technical support from Regional Integrated Multi-Hazard Early Warning Systems (RIMES) and the Finish Meteorological Institute (FMI). The mapping exercise reported on the access to and availability and usage of seasonal to sub-seasonal forecasts in Cambodia and Indonesia. The training was related to Seasonal to sub-seasonal forecast translation. It was organized in March 2024 in Phnom Penh for staff from the National Meteorological and Hydrological Services (NMHS) and representatives from > 9 (2.3) Provide technical support to the Ministry of Social Affairs (MoSA) and the Ministry of Villages, Development of Disadvantaged Regions, and Transmigration (MoV) to develop Ministerial Regulations on SRSP/ASP in line with the Presidential Regulations on Social Protection Reform. > 10 (2.4) Provide technical support to the provincial governments of Yogyakarta, East Nusa Tenggara and West Nusa Tenggara to develop and disseminate Provincial Regulations and action plans for the implementation of SRSP/ASP in line with the Presidential Regulations on Social Protection Reform. > 11 (2.5) Develop and disseminate a financial model and a report on disaster risk financing for SRSP/ASP at the national level. > 12 (2.6) Develop and disseminate financial models and a report with a focus on potential local/community-level financing for three selected provinces. Page | 6 sectoral agencies (such as disaster risk reduction and management, and agriculture) from Cambodia and Indonesia. The guidance note provides practical guidance on accessing and interpreting seasonal and sub-seasonal forecasts and is based on evidence from Indonesia and Cambodia but is intended to be applicable/usable by any country in the region. _3.2. Develop and disseminate technical guidance on risk financing options for AA and SRSP for ASEAN countries._ The purpose of this activity was to develop guidelines and an ex ante framework for reconfiguring risk financing instruments in anticipation or in the wake of a disaster in ASEAN and to improve risk-informed PFM processes in ASEAN countries. The activity should establish an evidence base supporting increased investment in anticipatory and shockresponsive approaches through social protection systems. It should also inform policymakers on the design and prioritization of Disaster Risk Financing (DRF) instruments. The deliverables for this activity have not yet been completed and information on their progress is scarce. After consultations with ASEAN, it was decided to link the work on this component to the PFM work that UNICEF and WFP are undertaking in the Philippines. Despite some internal delays, this part of the intervention was completed before the project’s end. _3.3. Promoting South-South cooperation between ASEAN and other regions on how to best link AA and SRSP._ The Regional Social Policy Forum East Asia and Pacific was organized in November 2023 in Bali, Indonesia, to exchange knowledge and learning on social protection systems, share best practices, address common challenges and explore effective policies to strengthen AA and SRSP systems in Southeast Asia and the wider region. More specifically, the Forum included sessions to inform policymaking and contribute to development visions in the ASEAN region. Selected experts presented background papers, facilitated sessions and contributed to a final conference report. Stakeholders included ministry representatives from regional countries, policy experts and representatives from organizations including UNICEF, the World Bank, FinnRC, WFP and ILO. This activity resulted in two reports and two working papers: one conference report,[13] one report on SRSP,[14] a working paper on evidence, knowledge and data on child-sensitive and ASP in East Asia and the Pacific, and another working paper on management information systems for social protection in the East Asia-Pacific region. UNICEF organized the Forum in collaboration with the Indonesian Government with contributions from all project partners. A workshop was also organized in March 2024 bringing together the Asia Pacific AA Technical Working Group, governments from the region and development and humanitarian partners. The workshop highlighted how social protection contributes to climate risk management in Southeast Asia and sought to increase senior government officials' understanding of the role of social protection in DRR, DRM and climate change adaptation and mitigation. It also helped officials to identify opportunities to enhance linkages across policies and programmes to protect children from the economic impacts of climate shocks in the region. All three organizations were involved in this activity and facilitated the workshop. 1.6 Framework The project was guided by a logical framework that outlines the goals, activities and expected outcomes. The detailed framework is shown in Table 1. > 13 United Nation’s Children Fund, _Regional Social Policy Forum East Asia and Pacific_ , UNICEF East Asia and Pacific Regional Office, Bangkok, 2023. > 14 United Nation’s Children Fund, _Shock Responsive Social Protection: What we know so far, a stocktaking from East Asia and the Pacific_ , UNICEF East Asia and Pacific Regional Office, Bangkok, 2023. Page | 7 Table 1. Logical framework used to guide project implementation | Outcome | Vulnerable populations in Southeast Asia benefit from enhanced SRSP programmes with improved links to early warning systems, AA, risk financing mechanisms and multidimensional vulnerabilities. | Vulnerable populations in Southeast Asia benefit from enhanced SRSP programmes with improved links to early warning systems, AA, risk financing mechanisms and multidimensional vulnerabilities. | Vulnerable populations in Southeast Asia benefit from enhanced SRSP programmes with improved links to early warning systems, AA, risk financing mechanisms and multidimensional vulnerabilities. | |---|---|---|---| | _Outcome_ _indicators_ |• Default Key Outcome Indicator (KOI): At the end of the project, two ASEAN Member States have recorded a 15 per cent reduction in the expected number of affected people. _[Description and measure: Affected people is the ‘number of vulnerable children and their families unable to financially cope with the_ _impact of a shock and meet their basic needs’. ‘Expected’ is understood as the projection (or modelling) that will inform the coverage of the_ _government’s social protection response in the intervention areas of the project-province, district and community. ]_ • At the end of the project, at least two ASEAN Member States have their social protection systems connected to climate risk-informed tools as well as the mechanisms to target beneficiaries before, during and after climate shocks through social protection systems. _[Description and measure: The two selected countries will operationalize this indicator through contributing to one or several building_ _blocks of Disaster Responsive Social Protection as defined in the ASEAN Guidelines for Disaster Responsive Social Protection, namely: i)_ _improved risk-informed targeting, ii) vulnerability assessments, iii) availability of financial models for disaster risk financing, and iv)_ _connection to climate data for better decision-making related to disasters. ]_||| | Results | Cambodia | Indonesia | Regional | || R.1. Social protection and AA systems are strengthened and interlinked to address the impacts of climate hazards on the most vulnerable populations in Cambodia. | R.2. Government of Indonesia has systems, tools, knowledge and financial mechanisms to mitigate the impact of climate hazards and disasters through SRSP. | R.3. ASEAN Member States benefit from harmonized approaches and make use of regional tools to design and implement integrated AA and SRSP programmes. | | Outcome Indicators |• Default Key Result Indicator (KRI) / DRR/Information, communication and public awareness; Number of people reached through information, education and communication on DRR (baseline 0, target 1,000 government officials at national, district and commune levels as well as community members from five intervention districts). •AA plan developed and linkages with DRM and social protection systems established.|• Default KRI /DRR/Hazard, risk analysis and early warning; Number of people covered by a functional early warning system (baseline 0, target 14 million in three provinces) • Default KRI /DRR/Information, communication and public awareness; Number of people reached through information, education and communication on DRR (baseline 0, target 200 government officials at national and subnational levels).|•At the end of the project, ASEAN Member States have access to at least two new regional tools to enhance SRSP and AA programme design and implementation. •At the end of the project, at least five new knowledge products, studies and joint events developed to support ASEAN Member States and ASEAN on advancing AA and SRPS.| Page | 8 ||•The shock responsiveness of the core cash transfer programmes under the Family Package is built into its design and links to the DRM systems established. •Climate vulnerability and risk indicators for beneficiary targeting are designed and tested to be included in social protection response programmes.|•34 million beneficiaries from MoSA and MoV social protection programmes will benefit from two Ministerial Regulations on SRSP/ASP. •14 million people from three provinces will benefit from provincial regulations and action plans on SRSP/ASP. •A finalized and disseminated report and the revised underlying financial models for SRSP/ASP at the national and subnational levels, aligned with risk- informed PFM in the country (Indonesia Disaster Risk Financing & Insurance).|| |---|---|---|---| | Activities | 1.1. Develop and integrate AA plan in DRM and SRSP systems. 1.2. Strengthen the shock responsiveness of the core cash transfer programmes under the Family Package and link to the DRM system. 1.3. Support development and update of the vulnerability and risk monitoring system tools.| 2.1. Provide technical assistance to strengthen the interoperability and connectivity of e-SIMBA with other risk information systems for enhanced decision- making informing ASP/SRPS programmes serving populations most vulnerable to climate and disaster risks. 2.2. Strengthen sustainable capacities at national and subnational levels (province and district level) to improve the knowledge and skills of relevant government officials on ASP/SRSP and AA. 2.3. Provide technical support to MoSA and MoV to develop Ministerial Regulations on SRSP/ASP in line with the Presidential Regulations on Social Protection Reform. 2.4. Provide technical support to the provincial governments of Yogyakarta, East Nusa Tenggara and West Nusa Tenggara to develop provincial regulations and action plans for the implementation of SRSP/ASP in line with the Presidential Regulations on Social Protection Reform.| 3.1. Ensure quality forecast products and impact-based analysis platforms at regional and ASEAN Member State levels to inform risk assessment and improvement of seasonal and sub- seasonal forecasts in conjunction with short-range weather forecasts for DRR, AA and SRSP at country level. 3.2. Develop and disseminate a technical guidance on risk financing options for AA and SRSP for ASEAN countries. 3.3. Promote South-South cooperation between ASEAN and other regions on how to best link AA and SRSP.| Page | 9 ||| 2.5. Develop and disseminate a financial model and a report on disaster risk financing for SRSP/ASP at the national level. 2.6. Develop and disseminate financial models and a report with a focus on potential local/community-level financing for three selected provinces.|| |---|---|---|---| | Problem statement |●AA and SRSP are still being developed in silos and thus neither approach has been able to reach scale and sustainability yet. ●AA tends to have short outreach and SRSP programmes are not yet operational to respond to the impact of climate shocks. ●AA and SRSP are not integrated into national and subnational DRM systems. ●The selected ASEAN countries have limited data, tools, knowledge or the financial means to implement AA and SRSP to proactively manage climate risks and reduce humanitarian needs. ●ASEAN regional tools and technical guidance and standardization for AA and SRSP is still an ongoing process.||| Page | 10 ==> picture [462 x 646] intentionally omitted <== © UNICEF/UNI641159/Do Khuong Duy Page | 11 2 Methodology 2.1 Evaluation objectives and scope The objective of this evaluation was to conduct a final assessment of the project ‘Scaling up Anticipatory Action and Shock Responsive Social Protection to proactively manage the risks and impacts of climate shocks and disasters in ASEAN’. The evaluation covers the entire project implementation period, including the original 18 months and the three-months non-cost extension (July 2022 to March 2024). The geographical scope of the evaluation is the ASEAN region, with a special focus on Indonesia and Cambodia at the national level. In regard to the thematic scope, given the limited project period, the evaluation focuses on assessment of the activities themselves rather than on the expected results. The main activities were intended to enhance institutional changes, but a project period of less than two years cannot be expected to produce solid structural or institutional changes. Also, project-level objectives and outcomes were not assessed during this evaluation. After ethical clearance, the time left for the evaluation and report writing was four weeks; studying institutional changes or the longer-term results of activities was impossible in that short period. The evaluation followed a participatory and utilization-focused approach, using mainly qualitative methods. It involved all concerned stakeholders, including government partners, project partners and service providers. An evaluation matrix was used as a guided. It contains the main questions the evaluation sought to answer and – through systematic secondary questions – provided a practical, sequenced approach to gathering the data to enable each main evaluation question to be answered. It also highlighted which of the evaluation criteria were most relevant in addressing each main evaluation question. Data was gathered from three primary sources: 1. A desk review of project-related documents; 2. 30 KIIs with nearly all identified stakeholders; and 3. Evaluation surveys conducted after two ASEAN initiatives (workshop in Bangkok and Regional Social Policy Forum in Bali). The project activities evaluated are detailed in section 1.5. The evaluation also covers some summative, process and formative components, specifically: - (i) Relevance of the project design; - (ii) Efficiency and effectiveness of implementation encompassing partnerships, operational arrangements, resource utilization, and so forth; - (iii) Achievement of results with a focus on capacity-building of implementing countries (that is, Cambodia and Indonesia) and from the broader ASEAN region; - (iv) Overall quality of the project deliverables (such as accuracy and completeness); - (v) Whether the project was implemented as planned and identification of the enablers and barriers to implementation; - (vi) Documenting what worked, what did not and lessons learned; - (vii) Providing recommendations for ASEAN countries and humanitarian/development partners, for further scaling up and sustaining the approaches underpinned by the project. The evaluation aimed for triangulation and complementarity between methods. Evidence was triangulated within methods wherever appropriate (comparing the perspectives of different stakeholders interviewed). The findings were validated through a workshop with representatives from implementing partners at both regional and national levels. Page | 12 2.2 Evaluation use It is anticipated that both the evaluation process and the results will contribute to a body of evidence and learning about implementation of programmes related to SRSP and AA. This includes identification of the enablers and barriers to delivering such programmes in countries in East Asia and the Pacific and how these have affected the ability of UNICEF and partners to institutionalize SRSP and AA. The evaluation contains practical solutions and recommendations for UNICEF’s work with governments and other partners to advance this agenda – ultimately to ensure that vulnerable populations in Southeast Asia benefit from SRSP programmes that have improved links to early warning systems, AA, risk financing mechanisms and multidimensional vulnerabilities. The prospective users of the evaluation are UNICEF, the EU and other key stakeholders Table 2. Evaluation users |Evaluation users|Evaluation uses| |---|---| |International and regional partners (including UNICEF, WFP, FinnRC and FAO)|To inform and adjust where required their country and regional programme strategies for the upcoming period by better understanding the contributions of the project, as well as to deepen their understanding of best practices in linking AA and SRSP.Additionally, the project aims to support regional efforts to standardize AA and SRSP work through the AA Regional Technical Working Group and the AA framework, endorsed by ASEAN; thus the evaluation findings also support the standardization of multi-agency SRSP work at regional level.| |Donors (DG-ECHO)|To better articulate the outcomes and impacts of their intervention and inform future interventions.| |Regional organizations (ASEC) and national governments|To better define the terms of collaboration with development partners with respect to the attainment of the relevant goals set in their national development plans, and to be informed of best practices in SRSP and AA.| |NGOs/civil society organizations (Resilience Development Initiative, and so forth)|To mainstream (into their day-to-day practices) the good practices identified during the evaluation and address the weaknesses highlighted during the analysis.| 2.3 Evaluation criteria Following the methodological guidelines set out in the ToR, the evaluation team developed an evaluation framework according to the evaluation criteria developed by the OECD/DAC (relevance, effectiveness, efficiency, coherence, impact and sustainability). Relevance refers to the extent to which the project is suited to the priorities and policies of the target group, recipient and donor. Effectiveness is a measure of the extent to which the project attains its objectives. Efficiency measures the outputs (qualitative and quantitative) in relation to the inputs. Coherence considers the compatibility of the intervention with other interventions in a country, sector or institution. Impact refers to the positive and negative changes produced by a development intervention, directly or indirectly, intended or unintended. And finally, sustainability is concerned with measuring whether the benefits of the project are likely to continue after donor funding has been withdrawn. [15] > 15 Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development, _Applying Evaluation Criteria Thoughtfully_ , OECD Publishing, Paris, 2021, https://doi.org/10.1787/543e84ed-en. Page | 13 Nonetheless, after preliminary consultations with key engaged partners,[16] it was noted that the main focus of the evaluation should be on identifying lessons learned, best practices, what worked and what did not work, the processes, challenges and enablers, together with a thorough understanding of the context and other underlying questions. A request to delve deeper into the project's significance, timeline and potential long-term impact and sustainability was also noted. Additionally, there was a call to examine the appropriateness of assessing the integration of AA and SRSP, given the limited timeline. 2.4 Evaluation questions The evaluation was guided by the following set of Evaluation Questions: Table 3. Key Evaluation Questions | Evaluation criteria | Key evaluation questions | |---|---| |Relevance| Key Evaluation Question: _To what extent have the intervention’s objectives and_ _design responded to the needs, policies and priorities of beneficiaries, and global,_ _regional and country stakeholders? Will this continue to be the case if circumstances_ _change?_ Sub-questions : 1. To what extent does the project design align with the priorities and needs of key regional and national-level stakeholders (and beneficiaries), including any changes made during implementation? 2. To what extent did the SRSP and AA approaches in disaster preparedness and building resilience align with the context and existing regional and national policies, frameworks and strategies? 3. Are the objectives of the intervention adequately defined, realistic and feasible, and are the results verifiable? 4. How can the SRSP and AA approaches adapt to changing circumstances, such as evolving disaster risks or policy priorities, to maintain their relevance over time? 5. To what extent is the link between the integration of AA and SRSP appropriate and useful?| |Effectiveness| Key Evaluation Question :_To what extent has the intervention achieved its_ _objectives and expected_ _results?_ Sub-questions : 1. How effective have the SRSP approaches been in achieving the results outlined in the results framework? What about AA? 2. What variations were identified in the quality of implementation in the focus countries and what are the factors influencing these variations (for example management, financial aspects, regulatory aspects, implementation modifications or deviation from plans)? What were the implementation challenges in the focus countries? 3. How have the timeline and time frame of the project impacted the results?| |Efficiency| Key Evaluation Question :_How well are resources being used?_ Sub-questions : 1. What processes and actions were enabled by the project that led to increased efficiencies of programming in the focus countries? What were the gaps, if any?| 16 UNICEF EAPRO and FinnRC. Page | 14 ||2. To what extent were the resources (human, technical, financial) allocated efficiently? 3. To what extent have the project’activities been delivered in a timely manner?| |---|---| |Coherence| Key Evaluation Question :_To what extent was the intervention compatible with_ _other interventions in the countries and ASEAN region?_ Sub-questions : 1. To what extent has the SRSP initiative promoted complementarity, harmonization and coordination by UNICEF, WFP and FinnRC to maximize the achievement of results? 2. How has the project contributed to strengthening the understanding across these different technical areas?| |Sustainability| Key Evaluation Question :_To what extent are the net benefits of the intervention_ _likely to continue?_ Sub-questions : 1. To what extent do capacities, budgets and political will exist to sustain and scale up this work? How can partner organizations work better internally to further advance and enhance sustainability of SRSP and AA (not only related to social protection but also to other social sectors like water, sanitation and hygiene, and education? 2. What are the main challenges and opportunities for ensuring the continued outcomes of the SRSP interventions beyond the current funding? 3. What are the lessons learned that can be applied? What are the critical next steps or recommendations moving forward, at country and regional levels? For United Nations and development/humanitarian partners?| The full evaluation matrix can be found in Annex 1 of this report. 2.5 Evaluation approach The evaluation team used a participatory approach aimed at involving stakeholders across national and subnational levels, including government officials, members of ASEC/sector bodies, project partners and United Nations partners. The approach was designed to explore diverse avenues towards AA, covering institutional frameworks and financing mechanisms. By fostering inclusive and iterative dialogues throughout the evaluation process, stakeholders could actively contribute insights and perspectives, thereby facilitating a comprehensive understanding of scenarios for effective AA. Given the short duration of the project, the evaluation team did not engage with the ‘indirect beneficiaries of the project’ – that is, the vulnerable populations at decentralized levels in either of the two countries. Therefore, the evaluation team did not assess the extent to which the implementation of the SRSP intervention benefited these populations. 2.6 Evaluation process The evaluation process resulted in a final evaluation report containing strategic recommendations at regional and country level. The participatory approach was structured into iterative stages of discussion with key stakeholders, as per Figure 1: Page | 15 Figure 1. Participatory approach stages ==> picture [455 x 152] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- Define evaluation Data collection and Preliminary findings Evaluation report matrix analysis • Documentary •Qualitative and •Workshop 1: •Final evaluation review quantitative data Validation report •Preliminary collection at the workshop on discussions with country level preliminary findings project partners •KIIs, surveys and country specific (UNICEF, WFP, findings FinnRC) •Draft evaluation •A first stage of report discussions to identify key questions and evaluation matrix. •Inception report ----- End of picture text ----- (a) Defining an evaluation matrix 1. A first stage of the evaluation involved a thorough review of the available project documentation, including the project proposal, description of activities, project logframe, interim reports, financial reports, monitoring and evaluation (M&E) documentation, and output of activities. 2. Initial interviews were held with project coordinators from UNICEF and FinnRC to discuss expectations of the evaluation. A meeting with WFP was not secured at this initial stage. 3. Evaluation questions and a matrix were drafted based on the documentary review and preliminary interviews and reviewed by UNICEF and other stakeholders based on the modality agreed upon in consultation with UNICEF. 4. During the inception phase, a full list of key stakeholders was jointly defined with UNICEF EAPRO to ensure a diversity of representation. 5. The evaluation questions, the matrix, the list of key stakeholders and data collection tools were summarized in the inception report for approval by UNICEF and other stakeholders. 6. Feedback was incorporated into a final inception report. (b) Data collection and analysis - An extensive second stage of the initial cycle involved the collection of qualitative and quantitative data at the country level (in Cambodia and Indonesia) and at the regional level. This included KIIs and the analysis of the results of evaluation surveys conducted after two ASEAN-wide initiatives (the Regional Social Policy Forum in Bali and workshop in Bangkok). [17] - • By triangulating data collected through multiple methods, such as a desk review, KIIs and surveys, the validity and reliability of the findings were enhanced and potential biases mitigated. (c) Preliminary results and validation - Workshop 1: A validation workshop on the preliminary findings and country-specific findings was held to discuss the main observations, share experiences and build a coherent narrative on lessons learned. - Based on the validation workshop the final draft of the evaluation report was formulated. (d) Evaluation report - The feedback from project coordinators at UNICEF, WFP and FinnRC was taken into account for the final evaluation report. > 17 Data collection methods are explained in the next section. Page | 16 Figure 2. Diagram of the evaluation process ==> picture [454 x 246] intentionally omitted <== 2.7 Data collection methods The evaluation followed a mixed-method approach, including a desk review and semi-structured interviews. This section briefly describes the contribution of each method to the overall process. 2.7.1 Document review A comprehensive desk review encompassing project documentation and other pertinent materials was conducted. The review included descriptions of the project and activities, annual donor reports, mid-year and end-year reviews, financial reports, M&E documentation, and the description and output of implemented activities. Additionally, key government documents relevant to the project were reviewed. Additional sources pertaining to contextualizing crises and the early warning systems developed by the relevant countries along with documents concerning regional and national regulations, policies, frameworks and strategies complemented the desk review. 2.7.2 Key informant interviews During the inception phase, a list of potential KIIs was drawn up, representing a diverse array of stakeholders. The list encompassed donors, implementing partners, national institutions and NGOs actively engaged in social protection and climate-related emergency initiatives and other emergencies. Between 6 May and 31 May, 30 KIIs were conducted. The list of interviews that the evaluation team conducted is summarized in Table 4, while the complete guidelines used during the KIIs are provided in Annex 3. Questions in the interview protocols were tailored depending on the specific stakeholder and the nature of their involvement in the project. Despite repeated attempts, the evaluation team was not able to realize interviews with representatives of ASEC, IFRC and WFP Rome at the regional level; MoWRAM in Cambodia; and the Indonesian Red Cross Society and MoSA in Indonesia. Page | 17 Table 4. List of agencies included in KIIs for the project evaluation | Location | Category | Included | Not included | |---|---|---|---| | Region |Regional partners|FAO regional Office|ASEAN Secretariat, IFRC| ||Service providers|FMI, RIMES, EPRI|| ||Project partners|UNICEF, FinnRC, WFP Regional office, WMO|WFP Rome| ||Donor|DG-ECHO|| | Cambodia |Government partners|MoSVY, GN-NSPC, NCDM, NSAF|MoWRAM| ||Service providers|EPRI|| ||Project partners|WFP, UNICEF, FinnRC|| | Indonesia |Government partners|MoF, Bappenas, MoV, BMKG|MoSA| ||Service providers|RDI|| ||Project partners|WFP, UNICEF, FinnRC|Indonesia Red Cross (PMI)| | Philippines |Project partners|WFP|| 2.7.3 Online surveys To supplement the KIIs, evaluation surveys were conducted to assess the experiences of participants who attended the following project events: - Regional Social Policy Forum East Asia and Pacific (activity 3.3) - Regional-SRSP Workshop (activity 3.4) An anonymous online survey in English was distributed post-conference in both cases. The questionnaire results for each activity are provided in Annex 4. 2.7.4 Validation workshops Two validation workshops were carried out: - Preliminary results validation workshop in Bangkok (hybrid): This workshop presented initial outcomes derived from document reviews and KIIs at the regional and country levels. The goal of the gathering was twofold: first, to disseminate the preliminary findings and second, to solicit feedback for potential adjustments. Bringing together various stakeholders enhanced the outcomes, enabling the identification of shared challenges, existing gaps and opportunities for enhancement and collaboration. - Final dissemination workshop (online) : The objective of the final workshop was to present the final results to the donor. Page | 18 2.8 Limitations and Mitigation Strategies Certain limitations were found during the evaluation; most of them had been anticipated and were mitigated. The evaluation team did not encounter challenges to its independence or impartiality or any conflict of interest during the evaluation exercise. Throughout the evaluation and in close consultation with UNICEF and all relevant stakeholders, the evaluation team ensured adherence to all ethical considerations, in line with the UNICEF Procedure for Ethical Standards in Research, Evaluation, Data Collection and Analysis, and the United Nations Evaluation Group’s Ethical Guidelines. The following are some of the methodological constraints, along with the evaluation team’s mitigation measures: - Responding to all evaluation questions: - The project logframe revealed that the pitching of the results was overly ambitious and not possible to achieve during the short project timelines – the overall goal of the project was: “ _Vulnerable populations in Southeast Asia benefit from enhanced shock-responsive social protection programmes with improved links to early warning systems, anticipatory action, risk financing mechanisms and multidimensional vulnerabilities”_ . Furthermore, while objective indicators were outlined within the logframe, they were not consistently well defined. For example, the first objective indicator seeks a "15 per cent reduction in the expected number of affected people" by the project's conclusion, yet the method for quantifying this reduction remains ambiguous. As a result, the evaluation team could not systematically assess several evaluation questions, including: _How effective have the SRSP approaches been in achieving the results outlined in the results framework?_ While the findings of the evaluation show that a number of output indicators were met or partially met, one of the most important findings is that the design of the project, the pitching of the results framework, and the lack of monitoring data did not permit a detailed and consistent examination of achievement of the overall objective and expected outcomes. The evaluation team therefore focused on the evaluation questions that could realistically be responded to, given the available data and time. - Vulnerable populations and communities not included in data collection: Related to the above, the evaluation approach did not include engagement with and participation of the ‘indirect beneficiaries’ in data collection – defined by the project as poor and vulnerable populations living in high-risk areas with severely affected livelihoods and limited social protection, especially women from vulnerable households (including poor, near-poor and female-headed households), older persons, children, youth, people with disabilities, and ethnic minority households. Therefore, the evaluation team did not assess the extent to which the implementation of the SRSP intervention benefited these populations. Only a segment of ‘direct beneficiaries’ was included in the main data collection: national government and relevant departments in Cambodia and Indonesia, a few United Nations agencies and NGOs, who were expected to benefit from technical methodologies, tools, training materials and collaborative efforts with ASEAN countries; and ASEAN sector bodies and ASEC, expected to benefit from capacity development in disaster management, social welfare, health, finance and related areas. - Time allocated for the evaluation: The evaluation team had less than three months to conduct the assessment and deliver the final evaluation report, before conclusion of the project. As a result of this very short time frame, the scope of the evaluation had to be discussed and narrowed down to ensure that it focused on key evaluation questions that the team could realistically respond to. The team prioritized tasks and focused on the data collection phases that would answer most evaluation questions (the desk Page | 19 review and KIIs of UNICEF staff and partners, including government partners). Beneficiaries, including populations expected to benefit from the project, were not involved in the evaluation. The evaluation was also supported by a number of team members who could assist when necessary. - Challenges in data verification: Verifying information from key informants was challenging due to the limited availability of secondary data and the potential for biased responses from respondents actively engaged in the work being subjected to the evaluation. Data triangulation across multiple sources (KIIs with UNICEF and external stakeholders, online surveys and a systematic document review) was employed to effectively overcome the data verification challenges despite the limited capacity for independent verification. - Difficulties in scheduling KIIs: The time allocated for the field work was very tight, and the evaluation team was proactive in reaching out and facilitating online and in-person interviews depending on stakeholders’ availability. The team also participated in two regional workshops where they were able to meet and interview many stakeholders. Wherever necessary, the evaluation team relied on stakeholders to contact their networks to secure time slots for the interviews. • Aligning the diverse perspectives of involved parties: Many of the key stakeholders were included as part of the KIIs. However, for this size of evaluation, this number was still relatively small (30). With varied perspectives of how the project performed, the evaluation team employed a number of mitigation measures to help counter any risk of bias to the overall interpretation of results. They convened a validation workshop with key stakeholders to discuss the findings and receive initial comments, while the draft report was also shared widely for comments, which were incorporated into the final report. 2.9 Ethical Clearance The evaluation took into consideration key aspects related to human rights, gender and equity. At the same time, it was based on a participatory approach that included and engaged stakeholders. Consequently, the evaluation followed UNICEF and United Nations guidance, including but not limited to the UNEG Ethical Guidelines for Evaluation (2020); UNEG Norms and Standards for Evaluation (2016); UNEG, Integrating Human Rights and Gender Equality in Evaluation (2014); UNEG Code of Conduct for Evaluation in the UN System (2008); and the UNICEF Procedure for Ethical Standards and Research, Evaluation, Data Collection and Analysis (2021). Furthermore, the evaluation team abided by the European Union General Data Protection (EU 2016/679) and ASEAN Framework on Personal Data Protection guidelines for collection and use of data. The evaluation team made every effort to ensure the safety of the informants participating in the research as well as their anonymity. The data collected for this evaluation did not directly involve participants whose personal agency is limited, nor did we anticipate any harms or risks to participants, the broader community or the environment due to this evaluation. The views and statements of individual key informants are anonymized, and all inputs from stakeholders remain confidential to the evaluation team. Quotes used in the report from interviews or from survey responses are unattributed. The evaluation team ensured that all data provided by individuals was based on informed consent and is protected. The instruments for data collection were further reviewed and approved by the Health Media Lab Institutional Review Board (see Annex 5). Page | 20 ==> picture [467 x 654] intentionally omitted <== © UNICEF/UNI473436/Chair Page | 21 3 Key findings by evaluation questions This section presents the findings against the key evaluation questions. The findings were guided by the questions and criteria from the evaluation matrix, which is included in Annex 1, and are organized by the evaluation criteria: relevance, effectiveness, efficiency, coherence, and sustainability. 3.1 Relevance _Assess the extent to which the project objectives, intervention logic, strategy and approaches respond to the needs, policies and priorities of the beneficiaries and stakeholders, and will continue to do so if changes are encountered. [18]_ KEQ1: To what extent have the intervention’s objectives and design responded to the needs, policies and priorities of beneficiaries and global, regional and country stakeholders? Will they continue to do so if circumstances change? The SRSP and AA approaches generally aligned well with the priorities and needs of regional and national stakeholders and with existing regional and national policies. The project successfully addressed a critical gap between emergency response and social protection systems, which is highly relevant to the needs of vulnerable populations. However, the project faced challenges in defining clear and measurable objectives. 3.1.1 To what extent does the design of the project align with the priorities and needs of key regional and national-level stakeholders (and beneficiaries), including any changes made during implementation? The project's overall design was judged to be adequate by nearly all international and government partners. Based on evidence gathered during the KIIs, the majority of stakeholders believe the project aligned well with government needs and priorities and the needs of vulnerable populations. It addressed a critical gap between emergency response and social protection systems, which stakeholders recognized as highly relevant. Project activities were designed to meet stakeholder needs by integrating emergency and social protection actors, focusing on early warning systems and their potential links with SRSP, which are crucial for disaster-prone contexts and climate change adaptation. During implementation, the project adapted to governmental interests and data availability, ensuring continued relevance and responsiveness to evolving conditions. Government partners acknowledged significant benefits from the project, particularly in enhancing their understanding of both AA and the underlying forecasting and early warning systems. They also acknowledged the related actions needed to anchor SRSP in ongoing disaster management efforts. International partners valued the cooperation fostered by the project and emphasized the need for continued and increased collaboration. Additionally, workshop and conference events were highlighted by most informants as valuable platforms for sharing practices from other countries, networking and gaining deeper insights into disaster management and its relation to social protection. > 18 Organisation for Economic Co-Operation and Development, Development Assistance Committee: ‘Understanding the Six Criteria: Definitions, elements for analysis and key challenges’, www.oecd-ilibrary.org/sites/543e84eden/1/3/4/index.html?itemId=/content/publication/543e84eden&_csp_=535d2f2a848b7727d35502d7f36e4885&itemIGO=oecd&itemContentType=book. Page | 22 In Cambodia, the first activity’s focus on developing and implementing an AA protocol and templates, raising awareness and establishing policy frameworks aligned well with national priorities. The second activity aiming to link the Family Package with SRSP systems was seen as a significant aspect of the project's relevance. This linkage is crucial for providing continuous support to poor households during and after crises, addressing both immediate relief and long-term resilience. The last activity, the development of the Vulnerability and Risk monitoring tool, was also seen as relevant, strengthening the delivery channels of social assistance programmes. In Indonesia, the materials and trainings on ASP/SRSP were found to be relevant and useful by government institutions such as BAPPENAS and MoV. The latter found these activities especially relevant since they were perceived as filling a knowledge gap in subnational levels of ASP/SRSP, in which MoV plays a big role. Similarly, the regional activities were viewed as relevant and well aligned with stakeholder needs. Several stakeholders emphasized the importance of knowledge-sharing events, best practice exchanges and capacity-building. 3.1.2 To what extent did the SRSP and AA approaches in disaster preparedness and building resilience align with the context and existing regional and national policies, frameworks and strategies? In general, implementing partners, government stakeholders and service providers agreed that the SRSP and AA approaches were well aligned with national policies and the context in Cambodia and Indonesia, which are highly vulnerable to climate change. However, in both countries the Government has reservations regarding the adoption of AA, primarily due to concerns about reliability of forecasts. In Cambodia, legislation currently restricts the disbursement of funds until a disaster occurs. Nevertheless, it is noteworthy that the implementing partners observed that the Government of Cambodia has actively participated in discussions surrounding AA, indicating a proactive approach towards addressing these challenges and fostering improvements in disaster preparedness and response. In Indonesia, the activities related to strengthening e-SIMBA’s interoperability and connectivity with other risk information systems were seen as relevant by the stakeholders interviewed. There is a broad consensus on the alignment of the different activities within the overall policy frameworks of SRSP, DRF and governmental priorities and needs. The only exception concerned the provision of technical support to MoSA and MoV to develop provincial regulations on SRSP/ASP – an activity that had to be dropped as the regulations at the national level had still not been put in place. In terms of activities related to informing the revision of the Disaster Risk Financing Insurance Strategy, the materials produced were seen as highly relevant and very much aligned with the policy context of Indonesia. Government stakeholders stated that it was a positive next step, and that such materials would inform upcoming changes to the Strategy. Moreover, both implementing partners and service providers in Indonesia indicated that the Indonesian Government has strong political will to continue strengthening DRF. At the regional level, in regard to ensuring quality forecast products and impact-based analysis platforms, it was perceived that collaboration with meteorological institutes and sectoral agencies ensured that outputs were not only relevant but also valuable at the country level, and well aligned with both regional and national initiatives. The second activity focusing on linking social protection to risk-informed PFM was seen as highly relevant, particularly in Cambodia, where there is a strong commitment to studying this area. In contrast, Indonesian ministries were less interested in the findings, likely due to multiple finance strategy initiatives, including one from the World Bank and national efforts in this sphere. Country offices viewed this as a regionally led project imposed upon them. Page | 23 3.1.3 Are the objectives of the intervention adequately defined, realistic and feasible, and are the results verifiable? An examination of the M&E documentation highlighted some of the challenges pertaining to the clarity and measurability of the intervention's objectives. Although a logframe exists (see the framework in section 1.6), the project lacked a clear theory of change. Furthermore, while objective indicators are outlined within the logframe, they are not consistently well defined. For example, the first objective indicator seeks a "15 per cent reduction in the expected number of affected people" by the of the project, yet the method for quantifying this reduction remains ambiguous. Moreover, this indicator necessitates a revision for Indonesia due to changes in activities, a step that should have been promptly addressed as soon as activities were revised, rather than waiting until after their completion. Another challenge is that though quantifiable, the outcome activities are vague in assessing the quality of the delivered product. For example, an outcome indicator for Result 2 states that 14 million people from three provinces will benefit from the provincial regulations and action plans on SRSP. However, the quality or impact of these regulations and plans remains unspecified. Similarly, indicators such as ASEAN Member States have access to at least two new regional tools to enhance SRSP and AA programme design and implementation (Result 3) lack clarity on how the effectiveness or utility of these tools will be assessed. Finally, implementing partners at the regional level mentioned challenges due to the donor's narrow focus, strict definitions and default indicators. For example, the default indicators primarily focused on humanitarian impact, in contrast with the project’s broader aim of strengthening the link between AA and SRSP and enhancing capacity-building. This misalignment created difficulties in effectively measuring and communicating the project's success and broader impact. 3.1.4 How can the SRSP and AA approaches adapt to changing circumstances, such as evolving disaster risks or policy priorities, to maintain their relevance over time? Adaptation to evolving disaster risks and policy priorities is crucial for maintaining the relevance of SRSP and AA approaches over time. In interviews, stakeholders emphasized the importance of capacity-building and knowledge-sharing events to enhance readiness for new challenges. In implementing countries, hesitancy remains regarding AA implementation due to the cost and uncertainty associated with forecasts, highlighting the need for ongoing capacity development in this area. 3.1.5 To what extent is the integration of AA and SRSP appropriate and useful? Integrating AA and SRSP emerged as a vital strategy for proactively addressing and responding to shocks, particularly in disaster-prone regions like Southeast Asia. Traditionally, social protection has been primarily linked to population vulnerability, while AA focuses on disaster risk. The urgent need to link social protection to AA became evident during the COVID-19 pandemic, highlighting the necessity for timely disaster responses. However, despite its significance, the implementing partners interviewed raised concerns about the ambitious nature of this integration within the project's timeline. Page | 24 3.2 Effectiveness _Assess the extent to which the project achieved, or is expected to achieve, its objectives and results, and factors affecting implementation and results (positively and negatively). [19]_ KEQ 2: To what extent has the intervention achieved its objectives and results? The SRSP and AA approaches have shown effectiveness in meeting many of their objectives and results, producing meaningful knowledge and tools for stakeholders. However, the project’s ambitious scope, coupled with limited time and resources, hindered full achievement of targets and delayed several deliverables. Continuous efforts in capacitybuilding, enhanced coordination and extended timelines are essential for the sustained effectiveness and impact of such interventions. 3.2.1 How effective have the SRSP approaches been in achieving the objectives and results outlined in the results framework? What about AA? Nearly all stakeholders mentioned that the project objectives were overly ambitious, given the current capacities and resources available. The project focused heavily on delivering knowledge products, such as reports and guidelines, and producing agenda-setting government documents such as concept notes on SRSP and DRM. According to the tracking of progress indicators (Annex 2, Table 7), the overall strategic objectives were partially achieved,[20] and most targets for results indicators were met. In Annex 2 it can be observed that most of the deliverables were delivered as foreseen in the project. Some of the deliverables are not yet finalized but their completion is expected in the weeks and months to come. The delays experienced for some deliverables were mainly due to administrative hiccoughs, especially at the start of the project; to the limited duration of the project extension; and to accessibility problems related particularly to the public finance data needed for one or two outputs. In the case of Indonesia, some project activities were redefined after new consultation rounds with the Government, and some project elements were dropped. Nevertheless, fewer than half of the intended knowledge products were finalized. For Result 1, two out of four targets were met (see Annex 2, Table 8), although the AA plan and the operation manual for the Family Package are expected to be delivered in the future, after which all targets will be met. Only two out of five knowledge products are currently finalized (see Annex 2, Table 11). For Result 2, the indicators and targets were revised after changes in the programme due to unforeseen challenges. Multiple activities were dropped, including 2.1, 2.3, 2.4 and 2.6. For activity 2.1, e-SIMBA was successfully linked with four functional risk information systems, but due to the challenges in accessing MoSA’s unified database for social protection (DTKS) data, the integration of this dataset into e-SIMBA was hindered, as was data-sharing through a mobile application. Activities 2.3 and 2.4 were dropped because MoSA was not interested in collaborating with any external partners on issues related to ASP. On the other hand, the relevance of or need for some interventions changed, for example, the Ministry of Finance does not require an emergency financing model while the Disaster Risk Financing and Insurance Strategy is currently being rolled out. Therefore, activities 2.5 and 2.6 were combined under the new heading for 2.5. The targets for the training of additional officials, including by MoV, were achieved. The report and policy brief to inform the revision of the Disaster Risk Financing Insurance Strategy to integrate an emergency social protection financing strategy were finalized and disseminated. This means that by the project end date, three out of five targets were achieved (see Annex 2, Table 9). Interviews with 19 Ibid. 20 Indicator 1 for Indonesia needs to be revised. Page | 25 government officials also confirmed that the activities related to producing materials and carrying out trainings on ASP/SRSP were found to be helpful in terms of generating meaningful knowledge for ministries in a short period of time and with little prior knowledge, at least for MoV. Additionally, three out of five knowledge products were finalized by the project end date (see Annex 2, Table 11). Finally, for Result 3, two out of two targets were achieved (Annex 2, Table 10), but only three out of 10 knowledge products were finalized by the end date (Annex 2, Table 11). Also, an additional activity was organized that was not originally planned for (a regional SRSP workshop). Yet, although targets were met, interviewed service providers for the regional activities expressed concerns about the national rather than regional focus, with limited implementation at the regional level. Overall, while progress towards strategic objectives and results was tracked and achieved in some instances, revisions to indicators and targets were necessary due to unforeseen challenges. Stakeholder challenges hindered the successful implementation of provincial regulations and action plans for SRSP/ASP in Indonesia. Additionally, certain deliverables faced delays and require subsequent follow-up and dissemination post the official conclusion of the project. Despite some critical observations made above, the evaluation team wants to reaffirm and stress that the project realized a good many of its intentions and stimulated and reinforced policy initiatives that will leave an imprint long after the project is finished. Referring to the specific regional and country sections above and to Annex 2, the evaluation team summarizes the project achievements as follows: - _ Cambodia _ : Climate vulnerability and risk indicators for beneficiary targeting were developed and the DRR information, education and communication outreach goal was surpassed. The AA plan and operational manual for the Family Package are expected to be completed soon. The country successfully launched a policy framework for SRSP leading to improved awareness of SRSP and enhanced government collaboration, particularly between emergency response and social protection actors. - _ Indonesia _ : Capacity-building activities on SRSP/ASP were conducted and the revision of the Disaster Risk Financing Insurance Strategy was implemented. On the other hand, the activities related to e-SIMBA were more difficult to implement due to communication channel issues at higher policy levels with MoSA. - _ Region _ : Despite delays, all intended deliverables are expected to be completed. The Regional Social Policy Forum in Bali and the SRSP workshop in Bangkok were successful, while significant progress was made in enhancing forecasting capabilities, DRF, capacity-building and regional knowledge- sharing. Progress was made in the forecasting study at the national level, but its application at the regional level encountered several challenges. Although it faced delays, the risk financing study was delivered by the end of the project. Connecting AA triggers and cash assistance to government social protection systems proved overly ambitious, given government reluctance to allocate funds based on forecasts. This lack of understanding and interest from governments slowed progress. 3.2.2 What variations were identified in the quality of implementation in the focus countries and what were the factors influencing these variations (e.g., management, financial aspects, regulatory aspects, implementation modifications or deviation from plans)? What were the challenges of implementation in the focus countries? Nearly all the stakeholders interviewed mentioned that the project’s timeline and budget were too limited to achieve its ambitious objectives. The project's implementation was hindered by several challenges, primarily due to ministries working in silos and a lack of leadership and coordination among key stakeholders. For example, there was not a sufficient degree of communication and collaboration between institutions, such as disaster response agencies and SRSP bodies. Page | 26 Also, some informants indicated communication shortcomings regarding the overall project objectives, progress reports and results, which contributed to a lack of clarity and understanding about the project, especially among government stakeholders. In Cambodia, interviewed service providers mentioned that government officials’ unfamiliarity with new technology-based approaches (for example forecast-based AA versus traditional ex post responses) led to initial scepticism, particularly at the provincial level. Furthermore, government partners reported being inadequately informed about the ECHO project's overall objectives, strategy, goals and reports. Government partners were unclear about which activities were part of the project and how they were funded, while there was also insufficient consultation before the project started. These communication gaps contributed to implementation challenges. Furthermore, KIIs with service providers revealed that real-time data monitoring and data management were hampered by underdeveloped information technology (IT) systems and insufficient technical knowledge among government partners, which in turn led to challenges in coordination and efficient data-sharing across ministries. Additionally, the policy environment posed obstacles, such as delays caused by political factors like elections. Moreover, securing long-term funding is a challenge. While there are multiple funding sources available for SRSP, there is no long-term support available for AA, beyond ECHO. Lastly, project partners pointed to complex and unclear reporting requirements from the EU that led to multiple adjustments and delays. In Indonesia, certain activities had to be reconfigured due to different challenges. For instance, the initially foreseen activity on providing technical support to MoSA and MoV to develop provincial regulations on SRSP/ASP had to be dropped since the regulations at the national level were still not in place. Several stakeholders highlighted the unrealistic time frame of certain activities such as this one, since they depended on external factors beyond the control of the implementing team. However, the activities related to producing materials and carrying out trainings on ASP/SRSP were found to be efficient in terms of generating meaningful knowledge for ministries in a short period of time. Activities related to informing the revision of the Disaster Risk Financing Insurance Strategy were implemented smoothly, with only slight delays due to different political events taking place in Indonesia at that time. Implementation was easier also due to pre-existing frameworks. Moreover, service providers pointed out that existing, well-developed relationships with the different ministries made it easier to access and engage with them. Thus, building relationships of trust was described as a key success factor for this activity. In contrast, the activities related to e-SIMBA were more difficult to implement due to lack of a smooth communication channel at higher policy levels with MoSA. Collaboration between implementing partners and MoSA would often take place only at the technical level, leading to bottlenecks when issues of a more political nature arose, like those related to data accessibility and shareability. Hence, e-SIMBA was not implemented to its full potential. At the regional level, in terms of the forecasting study, challenges arose in bringing together sectoral and technical agencies and facilitating information-sharing. While Indonesia has established mechanisms like the Climate Outlook Forum for sectoral and technical agencies to convene, similar structures were lacking at the subnational level. In Cambodia, greater linkages between sectors are needed to ensure all agencies obtain access to information. Furthermore, downscaling information from the national level to subnational and district levels posed a significant challenge, as decisions ultimately rested at the subnational level. Moreover, skills gaps in forecasting, especially in Cambodia, highlighted the need for more basic training to address deficiencies in this area. These matters made it difficult to deliver something regionally applicable, although the lessons from these cases were relevant for the less developed meteorological agencies in Cambodia, leaving less to be gained for the more experienced stakeholders in Indonesia. Page | 27 For the risk financing study, delays were experienced in accessing ministries and obtaining relevant financial data, particularly in Indonesia, where difficulties in accessing government data were compounded by strained relationships between government agencies. The political economy of AA, notably pre-financing and cash disbursement, presented an important hurdle. Connecting AA triggers and cash assistance to government social protection systems proved overly ambitious, given government reluctance to allocate funds based on forecasts. This lack of understanding and interest from governments slowed progress. 3.2.3 How did the timeline and time frame of the project impact the results? Nearly all stakeholders expressed concerns about the project's limited timeline, deeming it insufficient for achieving substantial, long-lasting changes in system strengthening. Project documentation and interviews with implementing partners revealed that there were delays in starting the project, reducing the available time even further. Moreover, although a 6-12 month extension was initially discussed, only a three-month extension was granted. Service providers and implementing partners mentioned that the limited project duration made it challenging to bring together sectoral and technical agencies and facilitate information-sharing. More time was needed to build relationships and generate real change. Also, forum and workshop planning suffered due to the short timeline. Ensuring attendance at the Bali forum was challenging, and lastminute changes could not be communicated effectively. 3.3 Efficiency Assess the extent to which the intervention delivers, or is likely to deliver, results in an economic and timely manner. KEQ3: How well are resources being used? The project enhanced efficiency through fostering collaboration among diverse stakeholders, strengthening alignment with regional and national initiatives. Regional workshops and knowledge-sharing events contributed to best practices and examples, while improved use of forecast products and linking social protection plans to risk financing promoted resource efficiency. However, efficiency was hindered by limited coordination among ministries, insufficient communication between national and regional levels and bureaucratic challenges. Despite these issues, most activities were completed on schedule, with some delays and necessary adaptations to changing circumstances. 3.3.1 What processes and actions were enabled by the project that led to increased efficiencies in programming in the focus countries? What were the gaps, if any? Due to limited time the evaluation did not collect direct evidence on increased efficiencies in programming in the focus countries. However, based on the KIIs, we can conclude that the project fostered collaboration among multiple stakeholders, including governments, think tanks and academics. This cross-regional learning and cooperation improved the coherence of programming efforts and ensured that activities were aligned with both regional and national initiatives. Regional workshops and knowledge-sharing events facilitated the exchange of best practices and lessons learned. These events helped to build a more cohesive approach across different countries, enhancing the efficiency of project implementation through shared experiences and strategies. Additionally, the project improved the interpretation and use of forecast products, and enabled collaboration with meteorological institutes and sectoral agencies to streamline information dissemination and use, crucial for DRR and social protection. Finally, by linking social protection plans to risk financing, the project promoted more efficient use of resources. Page | 28 3.3.2 To what extent were the resources (human, technical, financial) allocated efficiently? For a more efficient allocation of resources, the evaluation finds that it would have been useful to strengthen linkages between different actions not only at the national level but also with regional-level actions to further the understanding that such interventions belonged to the same project – thus also optimizing certain processes. For example, stakeholders pointed out that certain activities, such as the materials/trainings produced on DRF by two different service providers (one at the national level and the other at the regional level), could have benefited from better communication to prevent effort duplication. In this context, implementing partners at the national level observed that if there had been more communication between UNICEF regional and national levels, certain parts of the national study could have informed the regional study, which faced delays due to challenges with accessing certain governmental stakeholders. Efficiency was also hindered by ministries working in silos, without clear coordination. For example, in Cambodia, insufficient communication and collaboration between institutions such as disaster response agencies and SRSP bodies hindered smooth collaboration. In Indonesia, there were difficulties in liaising with MoSA, which never provided implementing partners or other ministries with requested data, leading to changes in activities and delays. An interview with MoSA could have provided useful information to understand the setbacks that prevented it from sharing key data for the activity, but it was not possible to obtain a KII with this ministry. In contrast, activities related to informing the revision of the Disaster Risk Financing Insurance Strategy were implemented smoothly because of pre-existing frameworks, strong relationships with the different ministries and well-informed national stakeholders. Additionally, certain implementing partners also highlighted that working with donors such as DG ECHO involved heavy bureaucratic procedures, which required more human resources and time. 3.3.3 To what extent have the project activities been delivered in a timely manner? Overall, the project’s objectives were ambitious given the limited timeline. Almost all informants reported that the project duration was insufficient to achieve its goals fully. Initial reluctance from governments and scepticism from stakeholders due to unfamiliarity with new technology-based approaches further complicated implementation. The indicators for measuring success were often deemed inadequate and, in some cases, irrelevant. The implementation phase was generally judged as highly satisfactory by most informants. Despite the short project duration, most activities were executed relatively well and on time; they were also considered to have been well organized. A detailed examination of M&E documentation (see Annex 2) reveals that while most activities were completed as scheduled, delays were encountered in some areas, notably in the development of AA plans and the integration of technical guidance on risk financing options. Additionally, certain activities in Indonesia were dropped entirely due to changing circumstances or lack of stakeholder interest. In Cambodia (Result 1), most activities were completed as scheduled, with some minor delays for the production and dissemination of the AA plan and operational manual of the Shock Responsive Family Package. For Indonesia (Result 2), multiple activities were dropped, as mentioned before, but the remaining activities were completed on time. At the regional level (Result 3), an additional activity was organized that was not originally planned for: organization of a regional training on SRSP for government counterparts. Page | 29 3.4 Coherence Assess the compatibility of the intervention with other interventions at the regional and country level, particularly others implemented through UNICEF’s support, those by other United Nations agencies, as well as coordination and harmonization with government and other sector players, institutions and programmes to add value while avoiding duplication of effort. KEQ 4: To what extent was the intervention compatible with other interventions in the countries and ASEAN region? The ECHO SRSP initiative successfully promoted complementarity, harmonization and coordination among UNICEF and key partners, enhancing results. Moreover , it catalysed collaboration among international organizations, governments and meteorological agencies, strengthening the partnership between UNICEF and WFP within the SRSP framework. The project's collaborative nature effectively integrated AA and SRSP approaches and fostered understanding across technical areas through workshops and discussions, although donor coordination requires improvement. 3.4.1 To what extent has the SRSP initiative promoted complementarity, harmonization and coordination by UNICEF and other key partners to maximize the achievement of results? The ECHO project brought together multiple stakeholders, including international organizations, governments, think tanks, meteorological agencies and academics. Implementing partners confirmed that the project served as a catalyst for enhanced cooperation between UNICEF and WFP, facilitating improved coordination and fostering stronger working relationships within the SRSP framework. While the partnership between WFP and UNICEF was strengthened, leading to better coordination and network opportunities, there was a crucial need for clearer internal accountability and delineated reporting lines within joint programmes. A notable issue raised was the limited overall coordination with government at different levels. Government partners often cited insufficient efforts to enhance donor coordination. Project design and budgets were driven more by internal donor and organizer procedures rather than by the needs of the governments. Because of this, governments struggled to integrate donor contributions from this project and other sources into their mid-term and long-term plans, leading to confusion, poorly coordinated assistance and reduced impact. In addition to the international partners involved in this project (UNICEF, WFP, FinnRC and ECHO-EU), multiple other organizations, including the World Bank, ILO, Oxfam and GIZ, are working on related studies and projects in Indonesia and Cambodia. In general, international and donor organizations are informed about each other’s initiatives, but it is challenging for governments to keep abreast of the linkages between the various related projects – each with its own timeline, objectives and focus. Implementing partners in Cambodia observed that significant strides were made in fostering collaboration at the governmental level, particularly between ministries and entities such as NCDM, GS-NSPC and NSAF. The establishment in Cambodia of a national social protection council during project implementation, including members such as the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology (MoWRAM) and NCDM, underscores the progress. Nevertheless, there remains room for improvement in coordination and information-sharing, predominantly due to data management challenges and IT limitations. Each ministry maintains its own goals and safeguards its data, resulting in the absence of a unified registry and hindering effective data-sharing and coordination. Page | 30 3.4.2 What has worked well to encourage the integration of the AA and SRSP approaches, within and beyond the social policy/humanitarian/DRR community? There was strong agreement among all interviewed stakeholders that the collaborative nature of the ECHO project, bringing together multiple stakeholders, was essential to raise awareness and establish vital connections between AA and SRSP. Implementing partners in Cambodia pointed to effective collaboration within development partner groups (beyond the ECHO project), encompassing organizations like WFP, GIZ, UNICEF, ILO, FAO and World Vision. Quarterly coordination meetings and activity mapping for social assistance initiatives showcase efforts to identify overlaps and synergies for collective action. This made it easier to coordinate with relevant partners to encourage the integration of AA and SRSP approaches. On the other hand, coordination among donors remains more informal and scattered. Moreover, there is a need for better alignment between donor and government views on AA and SRSP, including more flexible definitions of what constitutes social protection – for instance, recognizing crop insurance as a form of social protection. A coherent approach that combines policy development with practical implementation was emphasized by implementing partners to ensure that initiatives are both well designed and actionable. 3.4.3 How has the project contributed to strengthening the understanding across different technical areas? As previously mentioned, the ECHO project brought together multiple stakeholders, including stakeholders from different technical areas, fostering cross-regional but also cross-technical learning. Successful partnerships were noted in workshops and discussions, such as those in Bali and Bangkok, where countries shared experiences and learned from each other. The majority of workshop participants found the workshops helpful and will likely apply the learnings in their day-to-day work (see Annex 4). Multiple stakeholders emphasized the imperative of additional capacity-building through regional dialogues and training sessions to further enhance coherence and effectiveness in collaborative endeavours. 3.5 Orientation towards impact Assess the extent to which the interventions implemented during the project drove progress towards, or supported achievement of, the agreed outcomes, including influencing policy frameworks in ways that advanced the AA-SRSP agenda. KEQ 5: What difference do the interventions make? The SRSP interventions have influenced policy frameworks in Cambodia and Indonesia, promoting the rights and well-being of vulnerable populations. While direct humanitarian impacts are challenging to measure, the project has strengthened systems and capacities for integrating AA and SRSP approaches. Page | 31 3.5.1 To what extent did the SRSP interventions influence policy frameworks in the focus countries and at regional level in ways that promote the rights and well-being of vulnerable populations? As stated previously, the project objectives focused on systems strengthening and capacity-building for integrating AA and SRSP approaches. It is therefore challenging to demonstrate direct humanitarian impacts, although interventions contributed to enhancing preparedness and response. However, while trying to assess the orientation towards impact, including follow-up actions of the ECHO project, the following can be deduced. _Cambodia_ - The SRSP framework funded by the ECHO project was officially launched by the Cambodian Government and is now at the implementation stage. WFP is working with the Government to develop its operational plan, including the drafting of an SRSP systems strengthening/roadmap, a scalability framework and a business process tool for floods. A specific/stand-alone emergency cash transfer has been designed and will be piloted, including its upscaling to a multi-hazard approach. - The AA workplan has been further elaborated and will be piloted in collaboration with NCDM, the General Directorate of Agriculture (Ministry of Agriculture and Fisheries), MoWRAM and subnational authorities. - The Shock Responsive Family Package Operational Manual has been further elaborated to include a risk and vulnerability assessment tool. Capacity-building activities are planned at national and subnational level. - The new Social Assistance Family Package Cash Plus programme is intended to be aligned with the shock-responsive Family Package. The programme was designed for greater adaptability to unexpected events and more effective alignment with contemporary disaster risk and climate change management interventions. - The Government is committed to piloting the AA protocol that was developed during the project, in Pursat Province for the 2025 monsoon season. It is planned to include capacity-building activities for MoWRAM focused on forecast monitoring, detection and production of timely warning bulletins. - As an intended addition to the ex post emergency cash transfer programme, WFP is working with the Government (GS-NSPC) to include anticipatory cash as an option under the SRSP scalability framework. The anticipatory cash (built into the AA protocol supported under ECHO) and the ex post cash programmes are planned to be piloted in 2025. _Indonesia_ - The Government continues to improve the capacity of provincial governments to implement ASP. This year, BAPPENAS will provide training on ASP for government officials in central Java. - • MoV will support two provincial governments to train village leaders to make their villages climate and disaster-responsive, following the Government’s regulations and guidelines. - The Government continues to strengthen the integrated socioeconomic registration system by devising a recertification mechanism and improving data-sharing and collaboration, efficiency, data quality, user experience, security and privacy. - The Ministry of Finance continues to strengthen the capacity of provincial governments to enhance their capacity for evidence-based budgeting for social protection, including Lumbung Sosial (a place to store disaster logistics, especially in disaster-prone areas, to meet the basic needs of disaster-affected populations]. - UNICEF will reinforce its efforts to implement the recommendations of the study on integrating emergency social protection financing into the Disaster Risk Financing and Insurance Strategy. Page | 32 _Region_ - A number of countries benefited from the interaction and learning at the Regional Social Policy Forum held in Bali and started to organize follow-up activities, including the following: - Malaysia started preparations for a new iteration of the Forum, scheduled for 22–23 October 2024 in Kuching, Sarawak. It is being organized by UNICEF, the Social Wellbeing Research Center at the Universiti Malaya in collaboration with the Ministry of Women, Early Childhood and Community Wellbeing Development. - The Chinese Ministry of Civil Affairs (responsible for developing national social assistance policies) is taking initiatives to increase investment in children in aging societies in the framework of an integrated, climate-adaptive and shock-responsive social protection system and risk-informed financing. - Within the Thai delegation, the newly appointed director of the Child Support Grant Operation Centre was incentivized during the Forum in Bali to further develop her contacts with the Budget Bureau, reiterating the importance and the urgency for Thailand of investing in children and ongoing engagement of priority investments in human capital. - UNICEF, WFP and FinnRC benefited by working together on this project and plan to enhance coordination between their respective activities in AA and SRSP. - Thanks to ECHO’s investment in the region, Viet Nam is committed to boosting government capacity to use evidence to strengthen the financial efficiency of SRSP. This would increase access to cash transfers for vulnerable populations in Viet Nam, especially families with children affected by climate change and extreme weather events. This work builds on (i) the feasibility analysis on the delivery of cash assistance for children in emergencies in Viet Nam commissioned in 2022 as part of phase II of the ECHO-supported UN project ‘Developing Options for RiskInformed and Shock-Responsive Social Protection’, and (ii) the more recent efforts undertaken on risk-informed PFM for SRSP in Southeast Asia. This new initiative is part of the second phase of the EU-UNICEF Public Finance Facility for South and Southeast Asia. 3.5.2 What, if any, are the systematic changes observed as a result of the project? The impact of the different activities has yet to be measured as these were implemented very recently (in 2023). In this sense, efforts to date have yet to materialize into formal programmes or confirmed action plans. However, stakeholders agree that the project has spearheaded progress in designing SRSP systems and fostering collaborations. In Cambodia, a notable advancement is the incorporation of NCDM into the GS-NSPC and relevant working groups on emergency response, marking a substantial move towards unified emergency response strategies. Additionally, the successful launch of a policy framework for SRSP was often mentioned in the KIIs. The subsequent operational plan is still being designed but is on track, and both have led to positive outcomes, including improved awareness of SRSP and enhanced government collaboration in implementing the SRSP framework and programme designs, particularly between emergency response and social protection actors. In Indonesia, governmental stakeholders have already stated that the findings from the study on integrating ASP in DRF are intended to be implemented, and the recommendations could be reflected in the upcoming Adaptive Social Protection Framework. Thus, these activities have led to increased awareness and capacity for policymaking around ASP and DRF. Regarding the impact of strengthening the interoperability of e-SIMBA, it has not been as strong as expected due to the aforementioned challenges. Page | 33 At the regional level, involved stakeholders acknowledged enhanced capacity for forecast translation and application at both national and regional levels. Notably, Cambodia adopted some of Indonesia's best practices for climate forecasts, showcasing successful, cross-regional learning. Involved stakeholders also praised the PFM study as a valuable initiative, facilitating a better understanding of funding for social protection. Continued support and investment in PFM are hoped for. The guidance document on forecast translation and DRF is anticipated to serve as a valuable tool for the ASEAN region. Partnerships formed during workshops and discussions, such as those held in Bali and Bangkok, facilitated knowledge exchange and learning. The enthusiasm that was shown for receiving information immediately following the Regional Social Policy Forum underscored the value stakeholders placed on the materials provided. The majority of participants at these events found them helpful and indicated they will likely apply what they had learned from them in their day-to-day work (see Annex 4). To conclude, discussions on integrating AA with SRSP were initiated, albeit on a limited scale, underlining the need for continued donor support to expand and sustain these efforts. 3.6 Sustainability Assess the extent to which the results achieved through the intervention continue or are likely to continue. KEQ 6: To what extent are the net benefits of the intervention likely to continue? The sustainability of interventions in Cambodia and Indonesia shows promise due to the demonstrated interest and political will of implementing partners and government stakeholders in the two countries. Strengthening relationships with key ministries is vital to sustaining efforts, as is integrating developed systems into existing government frameworks and ensuring ongoing ownership by working groups. Challenges persist in data system integration and maintaining long-term funding. Moving forward, focusing on evidence-based - pilots, enhancing coordination mechanisms and regular capacity building efforts are critical. 3.6.1 To what extent do capacities, budgets and political will exist to sustain and scaleup this work? How can partner organizations work better internally to further advance and enhance sustainability of SRSP and AA (not only related to social protection but also to other social sectors like water, sanitation and hygiene, and education)? It is evident from the KIIs that there is interest and political will among implementing partners and government partners in Cambodia and Indonesia to sustain and scale up SRSP and AA. Indonesia is willing to dedicate national resources to these areas. In this sense, even if ASP is a fairly new concept, Indonesia is one of the most advanced countries in the region in terms of SRSP, DRM and DRF, meaning that the basis for and the promise of further actions in those sectors already exists. However, implementing partners pointed out the need to secure more funding from the development sector for activities in ASP/SRSP, DRM and DRF. Implementing partners indicated that the ambition of this particular project was greater than what could feasibly be achieved with the planned activities and dedicated budget. Thus, in order to ensure sustainability, there is a need for better alignment between planning and budgeting in order to set realistic goals. Furthermore, several stakeholders also mentioned the need for continued efforts to build strong relationships with key ministries in order to ensure the sustainability of certain activities, which requires open lines of communication with specific government ministries. Page | 34 3.6.2 What continue to be the main challenges and opportunities for ensuring the continued outcomes of the SRSP interventions beyond the current funding? An ongoing challenge to sustain efforts in the future is integrating developed systems into existing government frameworks and cementing ownership by working groups to ensure ongoing responsibility and engagement. Opportunities lie in conducting evidence-based pilots to demonstrate the value of AA and secure ongoing funding and government buy-in. In Cambodia, one of the main challenges is the lack of formal programmes or confirmed action plans despite significant strides in the launch of a policy framework for SRSP. Clear leadership and coordination mechanisms are necessary, as is the integration of data systems across ministries for real-time updates. A notable advancement is the incorporation of NCDM into the GS-NSPC and relevant working groups on emergency response, marking a substantial move towards unified emergency response strategies. In Indonesia, securing adequate funding from the development sector for ASP/SRSP, DRM and DRF activities remains a challenge. Building and maintaining strong relationships with key ministries is crucial for sustaining activities. Nevertheless, the strong political will and national budget commitment to these sectors provide a solid foundation for sustainability. 3.6.3 What are the lessons learned that can be applied? What are the critical next steps or recommendations moving forward, at country and regional level, for UN and development/humanitarian partners? In Cambodia, lessons learned include, firstly, establishing clear leadership and coordination mechanisms to drive both AA and SRSP initiatives forward effectively. Secondly, it is important to integrate data systems across ministries, particularly to ensure real-time updates, with a specific focus on IDPoor data (the Identification of Poor Households Programme under the Ministry of Planning). Such integration will enhance the efficiency and accuracy of decision-making processes. Third, maintaining regular communication among all stakeholders is imperative to ensure alignment and programme effectiveness. Fourth, continuous capacity-building efforts at the local level should also be prioritized to empower communities and officials alike. Fifth, designing and institutionalizing PFM systems for SRSP will ensure transparent and accountable use of resources. Finally, in the long term, the creation of a registry inclusive of all households is necessary to facilitate more efficient and inclusive emergency responses. This registry will serve as a foundational tool for targeted interventions and resource allocation during crises. For Indonesia, the key lesson learned in this context is the importance of building strong relationships with key ministries to ensure the sustainability of specific activities. Regionally, continuous engagement through forums and technical working groups is essential for improved coordination and sustainability. Outputs like the guidance documents should be effectively disseminated, regularly updated and owned by regional working groups to ensure ongoing responsibility and engagement. Integrating AA systems into existing government frameworks will ensure their long-term maintenance and effective utilization. United Nations and development/humanitarian partners should focus on strengthening coordination mechanisms and integrated data systems for the sustained integration of AA and SRSP. Next steps include conducting evidence-based pilots to demonstrate the value of AA, securing ongoing funding support, and gathering evidence for their scale-up and integration into broader programmes. Multidonor initiatives can enhance efficiency and effectiveness by providing a coordinated approach to sustaining financial resources. While not the only means of support, they are a valuable method for ensuring coordinated donor contributions. Finally, increasing the visibility of the EU's contributions is recommended, as in many countries it is often unclear that support is coming from the EU due to the involvement of numerous partners. Page | 35 ==> picture [474 x 664] intentionally omitted <== © UNICEF/UNI468879/Al Asad Page | 36 4 Conclusions Provide a summary analysis of the main findings of the evaluation and a general interpretation of their meaning. This section is divided into two parts. First, general conclusions about the project evaluated are provided that derive from the multiple criteria. Second, specific conclusions are clustered under the evaluation criteria and referenced to the most relevant evaluation questions. _Overall conclusions_ The project ‘Scaling up Anticipatory Action and Shock Responsive Social Protection to proactively manage the risks and impacts of climate shocks and disasters in ASEAN’ improved emergency response and social protection systems in the ASEAN region, achieving significant milestones in Cambodia and Indonesia. International and government partners broadly concurred on its adequacy, pinpointing its relevance and alignment with stakeholder needs. Stakeholders acknowledged the significant benefits derived from the project, particularly in enhancing their understanding of AA and the underlying forecasting and early warning systems. However, the project faced challenges due to its ambitious objectives, limited timelines, coordination issues, initial stakeholder scepticism towards technology-based approaches, lack of measurable indicators, siloed operations within ministries, and data management issues. Skills gaps in disaster forecasting and social protection areas posed additional hurdles. Despite these obstacles, the project succeeded in stimulating policy initiatives that are poised to leave a lasting impact long after its completion. _Specific conclusions_ Conclusion 1: Relevance and strategic alignment In terms of design, the integration of social protection and AA presents significant conceptual and pragmatic challenges. AA operates within a well-defined period of the DRM cycle, demanding swift responses to imminent threats. While AA can include social protection instruments, their integration faces challenges due to the rapid response required. In general, successful integration also depends on the degree to which AA is integrated into the larger social protection system. The urgency of AA complicates the alignment of specific social protection instruments, which entails longer preparation and implementation periods, into a cohesive SRSP system. Overcoming these obstacles necessitates improved coordination, robust data management and heightened political commitment to effectively ensure consistent and comprehensive integration. The project's objectives and interventions were highly relevant, aligning closely with regional and national needs and priorities. However, the overly ambitious scope led to challenges in defining clear, measurable objectives, impacting verifiability. While the project fostered significant benefits and enhanced stakeholder understanding, legislative constraints and forecast reliability concerns remain. Capacity-building and knowledge-sharing were pivotal, but continued efforts are needed to ensure the project's relevance and impact amid evolving risks and policies. Refining objectives and aligning them with measurable outcomes are essential for sustained success. Page | 37 Conclusion 2: Effectiveness in achieving results The SRSP and AA approaches had the potential to advance the overall strategic objectives if more time and resources had been allocated to the project. Many of the output targets were met as per the project deliverables. Although impactful results and valuable knowledge products were produced, ongoing challenges remain. The absence of a smooth communication channel with higher policy levels (in Indonesia) led to communication gaps and was a shortcoming in terms of effectiveness. So too were limitations on data accessibility. More realistic planning and sustained support beyond the ECHO framework are essential for overcoming current operational and technological hurdles and boosting effectiveness. Conclusion 3: Enhanced efficiency and coherence through multi-stakeholder collaboration The project enhanced efficiency and coherence by cultivating a collaborative environment among diverse stakeholders. Specific success stories included regional workshops and knowledge-sharing events, which contributed to a more unified approach across borders. The partnership between UNICEF and WFP was strengthened under the SRSP framework. However, there was less than optimal overall coordination with governments at different levels. Because of this, governments struggled to integrate donor contributions from the project and other sources into their mid-term and long-term plans, leading to confusion, poorly coordinated assistance and reduced impact. Challenges also arose from ministries working in silos, insufficient communication between regional and national levels, and bureaucratic procedures straining resources. There also needs to be clearer accountability and better alignment between donor and government perspectives to enhance overall coordination and efficiency. Conclusion 4: Sustained commitment There is a tangible commitment from governmental bodies and implementing partners to continue scaling up SRSP and AA, underpinned by national budget allocations and political will. Nevertheless, sustaining these efforts requires overcoming challenges in Cambodia and Indonesia. In Cambodia, establishing clear leadership, integrating data systems for real-time updates and maintaining consistent communication among stakeholders are needed. In Indonesia, despite strong political will and budget support, further engagement with ministries is required to sustain progress. At the regional level, effective dissemination and regular updates of guidance documents, along with integrating AA systems into government frameworks, are crucial for long-term sustainability. Conclusion 5: Impact and systemic integration The interventions have led to systemic changes that promote the rights and well-being of vulnerable populations in Cambodia and Indonesia, with ripple effects anticipated across the ASEAN region. Although immediate humanitarian impacts remain nascent, the groundwork established through this project enhanced preparedness and response mechanisms. The incorporation of advanced methodologies and the fostering of cross-border knowledge exchanges signal a progressive trajectory towards more resilient and inclusive policies. Stakeholders' positive reception and the initial strides in policy formulation indicate fertile ground for future developments, emphasizing the necessity for sustained investment and collaborative efforts to fully realize the potential benefits of AA and SRSP integration. Page | 38 ==> picture [474 x 663] intentionally omitted <== © UNICEF/UNI642209/Nong Viet Linh Page | 39 5 Lessons learned Document good practices and identify lessons learned about what works and what does not work from project implementation to enhance project design, performance and outcomes. 5.1 Lessons learned on project design _ The need for strategic and long-term planning: _ To enhance the effectiveness and efficiency of future projects, it is important to reconsider how SRSP and DRM can be better integrated. As climate change-related disasters become more frequent and devastating, policy reactions will be more effective and efficient if they are part of a strategic exercise rather than ad-hoc responses to crises. Successful mitigation of, resilience against and recovery from disasters depend on long-term investments and preparatory actions. Future projects should be based on strategic and long-term planning, with countries developing mid-term and long-term strategies for dealing with the social consequences of climate change-related disasters. Assisting governments in developing integrated SRSP and DRM plans is essential when strategic plans are absent or vague. This approach also facilitates easier donor coordination and encourages donors to pledge financing for specific parts of a comprehensive plan. Integrated SRSP and DRM plans could take the form of a new planning document called ‘Climate Risk Assessment and Social response Plan’ (CRASP). In these plans, governments could be incentivized to systematically explore the options and scenarios at their disposal to respond to climate risks in their country. Donor activities could contribute to implementing the plan to enhance the complementarity between the initiatives. _ The need for flexible and cooperative project design: _ Allowing for longer timelines in project design would increase the effectiveness of the initiative, especially if actions require governmentdriven institutional reforms. This argues for a broader conceptualization of the interaction between climate change response policy and SRSP. Yet even if the focus is on short-term actions as is the case for AA using social protection instruments, slightly longer project cycles would enhance effectiveness and efficiency. Serious AA efforts should adopt an anticipatory approach that integrates time for investments to effect institutional changes in social protection prior to forecasted disasters. Pragmatically breaking down silos between ministries and between branches of the international assistance community would also be helpful. It is also preferable that once a basic level of understanding between all partners/countries has been achieved, coaching rather than prescriptive conferences or exchanges would tend to be more successful. 5.2 Lessons learned on implementation Centralized data management emerges as a critical component, streamlining information flows across ministries and countries to enable real-time updates and enhanced coordination. A standard civil registry containing basic information on all residents is an essential component for comprehensive data management, offering a reliable foundation for social registries, especially in the aftermath of disasters when accuracy is paramount. Additionally, establishing coordination mechanisms, such as technical working groups, fosters collaboration during implementation, providing a platform for ongoing dialogue and feedback among stakeholders. Clear leadership and coordination mechanisms are imperative, spanning ministries and institutions, including national disaster coordinators, to maintain direction and coherence throughout the project lifecycle. Page | 40 Targeted training initiatives play a pivotal role in addressing specific skills gaps, particularly in forecasting capabilities and strengthening social protection systems, thereby bolstering project effectiveness. Also, emphasizing capacity-building and local ownership ensures the sustainability of project outcomes, empowering communities and institutions to continue the project's legacy beyond its completion. Integrating these elements into project planning and execution is essential for achieving meaningful and lasting results. Finally, conducting pilot testing of the project to build evidence and refine approaches before full-scale implementation is undertaken is key to learning lessons and fine-tuning the project design to enhance its chances of success. 5.3 Lessons learned on the broader role of social protection in the full DRM cycle SRSP is about much more than short-term one-off cash benefits. Social protection is inherently a tool for social risk management and cannot be reduced solely to cash transfers within AA. Social protection has a broader role in the entire DRM continuum; it can, however, be integrated into AA. Such Integration requires investments in social protection long before a disaster is predicted. This includes developing robust registries and efficient disbursement mechanisms and securing earmarked funding. A clear link with PFM needs to be established to make it easier and more costeffective for politicians to connect AA with financing long-term strategies. A forward-thinking approach is essential, where a well-designed social protection system not only responds to crises but also influences every aspect of the DRM cycle – from readiness and anticipation to recovery, resiliencebuilding and risk reduction. Looking ahead, it is imperative to extend the connection between AA and SRSP to encompass the full DRM cycle. By embracing a comprehensive range of SRSP measures and integrating them into DRM strategies, future initiatives can achieve greater impact and ensure long-term social protection. Page | 41 ==> picture [465 x 652] intentionally omitted <== © UNICEF/UNI642139/Do Khuong Duy Page | 42 6 Recommendations Provide a set of forward-looking, strategic and actionable recommendations on how UNICEF and key stakeholders can most effectively enhance AA-SRSP programming and build on UNICEF’s comparative advantage as a key partner to governments. While noting that the current project has concluded, the evaluation team understands that UNICEF is continuing to work with country offices in East Asia and the Pacific to advance SRSP and AA. The evaluation therefore provides recommendations for ongoing support and future projects in these two areas. Strategic planning, flexible project design and enhanced coordination emerge as critical factors for success, underscoring the importance of long-term investments. Additionally, the integration of social protection and AA presents conceptual and pragmatic challenges, highlighting the need for improved coordination and robust data management. Future AA–SRSP projects may have a much broader impact if they are clearly linked to the full DRM cycle and a longer-term social protection strategy. By embracing these recommendations, future initiatives can achieve greater impact and ensure long-term resilience in the ASEAN region and beyond. Preliminary recommendations were drafted based on an analysis of the main findings. These recommendations were further modified on the basis of feedback to the report from the reference group members. The recommendations are organized as follows: 6.1 Engage in strategic and long-term planning for social protection systems Recommendation 1 : Continue to work with governments to develop mid-term and long-term integrated SRSP and DRM strategies. Ensure that these strategies are part of a comprehensive plan for better donor coordination. UNICEF regional and country offices, together with other partners, should continue to advocate for and support governments to develop mid-term and long-term integrated SRSP and DRM strategies to facilitate and enhance effectiveness in responding to climate change-related disasters. This is key to accommodate the necessary institutional reforms and foster a coordinated approach that integrates SRSP and AA with long-term strategies. _Responsible entity : UNICEF, working with national governments and other partners._ _Priorities: High_ Recommendation 2 : Advocate for longer-term funding commitments to allow for institutional reforms and capacity-building, including a focus on linking AA with the financing of long-term strategies through PFM systems. UNICEF should continue to advocate with governments and donors to consider longer project timelines and secure longer-term funding commitments by negotiating agreements. At the same time, UNICEF and other partners should support governments to link AA with long-term strategies through PFM systems. This includes developing PFM systems specifically for SRSP and establishing clear guidelines for resource allocation in anticipation of disasters; ensuring the transparent and accountable use of funds by implementing robust monitoring and reporting mechanisms; and establishing contingency funding pools within the PFM system that can be rapidly deployed during emergencies, ensuring efficient and timely disaster response. _Responsible entity : UNICEF, working with national governments, other partners and donors._ _Priorities: Medium_ Page | 43 Recommendation 3 : Invest in strengthening social protection systems as a core element of DRM, while promoting a comprehensive SRSP approach that addresses the entire DRM cycle, enhancing readiness, resilience and recovery efforts. Governments, in collaboration with UNICEF and other partners, should be supported to invest in strengthening social protection systems as a key component of DRM. This investment should promote a comprehensive SRSP approach that integrates all phases of the DRM cycle – prevention, preparedness, response and recovery. Specific actions include building infrastructure for faster response, conducting regular risk assessments and scaling up social protection programmes during crises. _ Responsible entity : Governments, in collaboration with UNICEF and other international organizations._ _Priorities: High_ Recommendation 4 : Implement pilot projects to refine strategies and ensure stakeholder alignment before full implementation. To ensure clear and measurable objectives, governments and international organizations should conduct pilot projects before full-scale implementation. Initiate small, controlled pilot projects to test feasibility and refine methodologies. Use these pilots to collect practical insights, adjust strategies based on real-world feedback and secure commitment from stakeholders. This preparatory phase is essential for the successful roll-out of larger initiatives. _ Responsible entity: Governments, in collaboration with UNICEF and other international organizations._ _Priorities: Medium_ - 6.2 Support project management, coordination, capacity-building and communications Recommendation 5 : Establish cross-ministerial working groups to break down silos and encourage collaboration between the humanitarian and development sectors. To address the lack of coordination during project implementation, governments, with support from UNICEF and other partners, should continue to advocate for and support the establishment of crossministerial working groups and develop mechanisms for cross-sectoral collaboration dedicated to integrating SRSP and DRM, and facilitating coordination with other stakeholders, including donors. These groups should meet regularly to exchange information and address challenges across the humanitarian and development sectors. Breaking down silos between governmental bodies will create a more holistic approach to DRM and social protection, ultimately improving the efficiency and effectiveness of these efforts. Such working groups can be supported to develop a roadmap for policy integration, identifying key actions and timelines for implementation, and assigning clear roles and responsibilities to ensure accountability. The working groups could be responsible for setting up regular meetings to align goals, share progress and address challenges in real time as well as developing a shared platform for exchanging data and best practices to foster cohesive service delivery. _Responsible entity: Governments, with support from UNICEF and other partners._ _Priorities: High_ Page | 44 Recommendation 6 : Support the development of a standardized civil registry for accurate data management, particularly after disasters. Governments, with technical support from UNICEF and other international organizations, should develop a standardized civil registry system to enhance data management and disaster response, especially after disasters. Actions include creating a centralized, reliable database for real-time data collection and post-disaster response. Establish protocols for regular updates and data audits to ensure accuracy. Governments should also implement training programmes for registry staff to maintain system reliability and responsiveness, especially in post-disaster contexts. _Responsible entity : Governments, with technical support from UNICEF and other international organizations_ _Priorities: Medium_ Recommendation 7 : Provide targeted training to fill specific skills gaps, particularly in forecasting and social protection systems. UNICEF and other partners should support integrated and targeted training to address specific skills gaps in disaster forecasting and social protection systems. This includes designing tailored training programmes that focus on technical skills such as data management and analysis, forecasting models, and the implementation of social protection measures. Training sessions should be localized, empowering community-level personnel to respond effectively in disaster situations. Additionally, continuous capacity-building initiatives should be established to ensure skills are kept up to date. _Responsible entity : UNICEF, working with other partners._ _Priorities: High_ Recommendation 8 _:_ Support the development of coherent results frameworks that include clear, measurable, and verifiable results. UNICEF and other partners should work with governments to better articulate results for new projects and develop results frameworks that clearly set forth the intervention logic, strategic goals and the relationship between programme components – these should also include indicators that are SMART. By developing these frameworks with all key stakeholders, embedding them in all planning documents and regularly updating them to reflect evolving contexts, the programme will enhance understanding and communication. Additionally, UNICEF should advocate for robust monitoring and reporting systems so as to systematically report on progress. _Responsible entity : UNICEF, working with national governments and other partners._ _Priorities: High_ Page | 45
