AB  - The overall objective of this evaluation is to conduct a more in-depth analysis of the effectiveness of the different project components and strategies, as well as lessons learned and recommendations to inform future project implementation. The findings, lessons learned and recommendations from this evaluation will be used to inform the implementation of the future phase of the project. -----Full Evaluation text----- _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ EXECUTIVE SUMMARY 1. INTRODUCTION This summative and formative evaluation is carried out as part of the KOICA-UNICEF Project (PKU, 2021-2024) called “fight against child deprivation through resilience in the eastern region of Cameroon”. With a budget of USD 7,996,000, this project aims to improve access to basic social services (education, child protection, WASH) for the most vulnerable people (Central African refugees, host communities) in the communes of Garoua Boulai, Mandjou, Bétaré-oya, Kentzou, Ouli and Ketté in the Eastern region of Cameroon. This project is implemented using a multi-sectoral approach in order to respond to the multiple deprivations of children and accelerate the achievement of the SDGs, in particular SDG 4.1, SDG 4.2, SDG 5.2, SDG 5.3, SDG 6.1, SDG 6.2, SDG 16.2 and SDG 16.9. This evaluation was commissioned and carried out thanks to the technical and financial support of UNICEF with the support of the Government of Cameroon through the Ministry of Economy, Planning and Territorial Development (MINEPAT). This evaluation, conducted from October to December 2024, provided for in the project monitoring-evaluation plan, is undertaken in order to promote accountability and learning about its implementation. It aims to assess the achievements of the said project, draw lessons learned and formulate useful recommendations to inform strategic and operational decision-making in order to improve the second phase of the PKU. 2. PURPOSE, USE, USERS AND OBJECTIVES OF THE EVALUATION This evaluation had two main purposes. The first is formative (learning) with a view to serving not only to inform project implementation strategies in the coming years, but also to highlight some potential corrective actions in the future. The second is summative (accountability) and aims to report to donors as well as rights holders (beneficiaries: municipalities, communities and children) the level of achievement of the PKU results. The main users of this evaluation are: the Government of Cameroon, development partners such as KOICA, UNICEF and other Agencies of the United Nations System (UNSA) as well as implementing NGOs and local communities/bodies served. Specifically, the evaluation aims for seven (7) objectives, namely: (i) Taking stock of the achievements of the implementation of the PKU; (ii) Determine the coherence, effectiveness of implementation strategies, efficiency, impact, sustainability of the achievements of the KOICA project, its mode of operationalization for the delivery of services adapted to children and their communities; (iii) Explain the theory of change that guided the implementation of the project; (iv) Identify lessons learned on what worked and what did not work, including unexpected results (positive and negative); (v) Estimate the contribution of the project in terms of reducing gender gaps, equity and improving the situation of the most vulnerable children; (vi) Identify avenues for behavioral and social change in the areas of child protection, girls’ education and WASH; and, (vii) Formulate key recommendations (strategic and operational) on how to improve the implementation and strengthening the performance of the PKU. 3. SCOPE OF THE EVALUATION The thematic scope of this evaluation primarily concerned the four (4) strategic objectives of the project linked to the 3 sectors of intervention, namely: (i) Ensuring quality education for successful learning; (ii) Ensure inclusive education for disadvantaged groups; (iii) Improve health through reliable water supply and sanitation services; (iv) Establish or strengthen a protective environment for all children through the availability of prevention mechanisms against violence, abuse and neglect. The geographical scope of primary data collection (quantitative and qualitative) covered the central (Yaoundé), regional (Bertoua) and departmental (Lom Djerem and Kadey) levels. Finally, the chronological scope covered the temporal field from January 2021 to December 2024. 4. EVALUATION CRITERIA AND QUESTIONS In accordance with the terms of reference, the evaluation focused on five (5) main criteria of the United Nations Evaluation Group (UNEG) and the Development Assistance Committee of the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (DAC/OECD) namely: (i) coherence, (ii) effectiveness, (iii) efficiency, (iv) impact and (v) sustainability. In addition to these, two other cross-cutting criteria were also taken into account, namely gender equality, human rights and equity as well as accountability). Thus, all these 7 criteria were addressed by 19 evaluation sub-questions developed through an evaluation matrix (Appendix A.8). 5. EVALUATION METHODOLOGY The evaluation adopted the contribution analysis approach based on the theory of change reviewed by the evaluation team as well as that of the so-called “Most Significant Change” (CPS) approach which aims to capture change and understand the effects of the PKU. As a result, the evaluation mobilized seven (7) main data collection methods carried out from November 11 to December 13, namely: _documentary review_ with 53 documents consulted including in particular the basic (2021) and final (2024) PKU surveys carried out by the INS; _semi-structured individual interviews_ carried out in face-to-face and virtual mode at the central (Yaoundé), regional (Bertoua) and departmental level (Lom Djerem and Kadey) with 32 key informants (including 9 women) out of the 34 interviews planned (i.e. 94% participation rate); 12 _life stories_ carried out out of the 12 expected from rights holders (beneficiaries) in the 3 sectors of intervention of the project; 16 sessions of _Frame/Evaluation H_ out of the 16 planned with groups of 6 to 8 children Page | viii _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ (girls and boys separately) under 12 years old; _an online questionnaire_ for which a total of 70[2] people (93% participation) out of the 75 targeted respondents fully responded to the Online Perception Survey (EPEL); 40[3] _Focus Group Discussion (FGD)_ carried out on all 40 planned and 12 _structured site observations_ carried out on the 12 planned in the PKU service offering sectors (Classrooms and tables benches, WASH facilities, Birth registration). Also, it should be noted that the participation of stakeholders in these focus groups and in Cadre H sessions was done on the basis of purposive sampling and integrating the gender and human rights approach. Thus, 269 women and 5 people living with disabilities participated in the interviews, FGD and Cadre H carried out. Two analysis approaches are applied for this evaluation: the qualitative approach (content/comparative analysis via the Excel software package) and the quantitative approach (descriptive statistical analyzes with SPSS software). Finally, the overall analysis of the results was carried out on the basis of empirical findings subjected to a triangulation process resulting from a cross-comparison of at least three collection methods/data sources among all those used for this evaluation. 6. LIMITS OF THE EVALUATION AND MITIGATION MEASURES IMPLEMENTED Limitations (such as not to taint the legitimacy of the results obtained) numbering two (2) for which mitigation measures were applied should be highlighted at the end of this evaluation. The first limitation relates to the unavailability of focal points and certain resource persons identified for interviews during the period allocated to data collection. As a result, respondents were notified well in advance and, when face-to-face appointments could not be accommodated, they were rescheduled and conducted by telephone or virtually. Finally, the second limitation relates to the difficulties of finding the implementation actors and the beneficiaries of the project at all levels (decentralized and community). This is due to the time elapsed between the implementation period and the evaluation period as well as the high mobility of all stakeholders regardless of their type. As such, the contribution of UNICEF and certain project stakeholders made it possible to obtain the completeness of the expected targets in order to achieve quality data collection. 7. MAIN FINDINGS AND CONCLUSIONS BY EVALUATION CRITERION The conclusions are based on the responses to the 7 evaluation criteria assigned to this research, including: 1. Concerning coherence, the evaluation established effective coherence and complementarity of PKU interventions with the components resulting from the UNICEF-Cameroon Country Program Document (CPD, 2022-2026) and the interventions of other partners including the Agencies of the United Nations System (ASNU). Thus, the EPEL made explicit (98.5% of participants affirmed this to a large extent), this coherence and complementarity when the documentary review of the CPD (2022-2026) also confirmed it. Indeed, the evaluation noted that the PKU's interventions were, on the one hand, consistent with the vision of the CPD (2022-2026) which stipulates _ "ensure that every child in Cameroon, girls and boys, including adolescents, survives, thrives, learns, is protected and develops their full potential, thus contributing to the growth of human capital and social well-being_ _". _ And on the other hand, an alignment of the 3 sectors of intervention (Education, protection and WASH) of the PKU on the Achievements of the CPD (2022-2026) was observed. Also, the evaluation noted that the PKU's interventions were complementary and consistent with those of the partners working in the targeted municipalities, namely the NRC, HURDA and the Red Cross for protection[4] and Care Intl, NRC, AIDER for EHA[5]. Moreover, this coherence and complementarity of the PKU's interventions with the interventions of other partners made it possible to avoid duplication by setting up a consultation/coordination platform/framework at the level of the 6 PKU municipalities. However, it appears necessary to strengthen the financial resource allocation of this PKU coordination body at the municipal level with a view to optimal functioning because, although the meetings were regular, they lacked substantial funding (stakeholder per diems) to take place. To remedy this, municipalities through Communal Development Plans (PCD) could plan and provide these resources. In addition, the EPEL data (57.6% to a large extent), the individual interviews, the FGDs and the observations carried out highlighted a strong appreciation of the capacity of the PKU to contribute through its strategies (intersectorality, community engagement, partnerships) implemented to: (i) synergy, collaboration and complementarities between its different components; (ii) and to offer a package of services to the targets. 2. The analysis of the effectiveness of the evaluated project, understood through the Results/Products indicators, the analysis revealed satisfactory performance (≥80% achievement of the set objective) for Result 3 in relation to the _number of children and adolescents benefiting from quality psychosocial and child protection services_ as well as for all Products in the child protection, education and child protection sector. EHA. It is important to specify that strengthening the capacities of social workers through > 2 This survey was carried out among 3 representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, 3 CTD actors, 25 OBC actors and 3 ASNU actors as well as 36 indirect rights holders made up of representatives of beneficiaries (men and women from host and refugee communities). > 3 Including 8 FGD (20%) for members of OBCs, 8 FGD (20%) with women, 8 FGD (20%) with men, 8 FGD (20%) with male Adolescents and young people aged 12 to 17 and 8 FGD (20%) with female Adolescents and young people aged 12 to 17. In total, 320 people were affected by FGD, including 160 (50%) males and 160 (50%) females. > 4 Birth registration and referral of GBV cases 5 Development of water points, revitalization of the municipal water fund and establishment of an efficient water management system Page | ix _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ the project through MINAS (regional and central) having been seen as contributing to the satisfactory performance of the SSP Component, it would be appropriate to strengthen the production of evidence relating to it. The results of the final quantitative PKU survey reinforce this trend provided by the evaluation results. The protection sector recorded the greatest improvements in indicators (15% for the decline in girls aged 15-19 married before the age of 15 and 44% for the registration of refugee children under the age of 5 in the civil registry), followed by education (33% improvement in the (adjusted) net secondary school attendance rate) and finally WASH which recorded one of the smallest improvements (4.8% for the use of a source improved drinking water). Also, the evaluation highlighted several factors (internal and external to UNICEF) explaining the non-achievement of the Results/Products of the evaluated project. These factors also constitute possible avenues of action for greater effectiveness for possible future interventions. They are linked to: (i) gaps or challenges in the timely provision of financial and/or material resources; (ii) the weakness of the results framework of the evaluated project and the reporting of results indicators; (iii) missed opportunities to establish strategic partnerships with certain key stakeholders (MINEPIA; MINEFOP; MINJEC) which would have been useful for monitoring beneficiaries and sustaining the project's achievements; (iv) the absence of decentralization of financial management of administrations (Regional Delegation of Water and Energy); and, (v) the absence of financial support for municipal volunteers by Town Halls despite commitments previously made for their remuneration. 3. As part of the analysis of the efficiency of the PKU, the evaluation reveals low levels of budgetary execution of the project of the order of 46%, 51%, 56% and 90% respectively from 2021 to 2024 despite strong budgetary mobilization (100%) having made it possible to make available the entire USD 7,996,000 expected for the implementation of the PKU. This weak budget execution observed between 2021 and 2023 is thus justified according to the stakeholders by: (i) the slowness of the contractualization procedures of the NGOs in the consortium; (ii) the late provision of financial resources for IGAs to OBCs (RECOPE) and to cash transfer beneficiary households with a view to combating early marriages and keeping girls in school; (iii) fairly long delays in processing partners' financial documents. Also, the evaluation highlighted several management factors that contributed to improving the efficiency of the project, including: (i) the intersectorality and interoperability of implementation strategies; (ii) positioning of the project in regional civil status coordination platforms; (iii) the relaxation of financial management procedures by UNICEF; (iv) implementation of community mechanisms. Although it could not be quantified, the evaluation noted the implementation of strategies, in this case intersectorality and partnership, as favorable to the reduction of project execution costs. Finally, the evaluation concluded that there was a favorable/adequate efficiency ratio between the performance achieved for Results/Products, the resources allocated/used for the WASH, Education sectors and for all PKU Products. 4. The analysis of the sustainability of the evaluated project reveals a mixed assessment of the project's capacity to establish partnerships to achieve the expected results and to guarantee the sustainability of the achievements. Thus, 50% of the EPEL stakeholders surveyed believe this fact to a large extent while 50% of the other stakeholders are of this opinion but to a small extent. The reasons put forward for implementing the KOICA project evaluated as contributing to the creation of the most appropriate partnerships with a view to achieving the expected results and guaranteeing the sustainability of the achievements are among others: (i) the establishment of the NGO Consortium; (ii) the establishment of platforms for consultation/coordination of interventions both within the municipalities and centrally in the Eastern region; (iii) the establishment of official partnership agreements between UNICEF and 2 town halls (Garoua Boulai and Mandjou) covered by the PKU as well as public-private partnerships through NGOs (IMC in this case) as part of the professional training of beneficiaries at the local level; and, (iv) the development of a joint work plan in 2023 with the ILO. On the other hand, other participants in the interviews and FGDs carried out saw in the implementation of the project a missed opportunity to establish strategic partnerships that could guarantee the achievement of the expected results. Indeed, on the basis of its operating mode based on implementation at the municipal level of interventions, the evaluation did not perceive the involvement of actors at the departmental level in certain implementation areas on the one hand as well as it was not possible to highlight strategic partnerships with certain key actors (MINEPIA; MINEFOP; MINJEC) which would have been useful for the sustainability of the project's achievements and the monitoring of beneficiaries (i) of professional training, (ii) radio broadcast listening clubs for adolescents. Finally, the evaluation identified several factors that favored the support of populations and other institutional stakeholders (sectoral administrations, NGOs) in the visions advocated by the evaluated project for improved access to child protection, education and WASH services. These factors of support of the populations and sustainability are among others: (i) the relevance of the response provided by the interventions of the evaluated project to the needs of the populations; (ii) support from local/municipal authorities in charge of the 3 Sectors of intervention as well as that of community leaders in the intervention areas; (iii) financial support to households receiving money transfers as well as RECOPEs, community leaders and municipal volunteers through support for financing viable IGAs; (iv) strengthening and anchoring project interventions in community structures perceived as essential for their sustainability; and, (vi) the organization of the “child-friendly village competition”. 5. As part of the impact, the evaluation noted the contribution of the evaluated project to creating lasting changes in social norms (including those of gender) and behavioral determinants through the strengthening of community systems (their establishment and strengthening of the technical/financial capacities of CBOs) and institutional (strengthening of technical capacities, establishment of institutional mechanisms and creation of a new service specifically in charge of water management). Thus, these Page | x _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ Changes are understood through the perceptions and attitudes of beneficiary communities in relation to the expected objectives of the PKU and are observed in all sectors of PKU intervention. Thus, referring to EPEL data, the evaluation reveals that _“the promotion and respect of the rights of children and adolescents”_ in the child protection sector is the area of ​​intervention where the most significant lasting changes have been observed to a large extent (59.4%). Then follow, in decreasing order of importance, the areas relating to _“access to quality education for children and adolescents”_ (47%), _“registration and availability of a birth certificate for children”_ (46%) and _“access to psychosocial and child protection services”_ (45%). On the other hand, access to drinking water and adequate sanitation facilities in the WASH sector is the area where the lowest significant sustainable change induced by the implementation of project interventions is reported (37.7% to a small extent), especially since this sector only covered 15% of the target localities, i.e. 39 localities (including 9 for new works and 30 for rehabilitation) and therefore barely 20% of the 254 villages in terms of infrastructure. However, the evaluation noted the persistence of the limiting factors identified at the level of the effectiveness criterion which hardly facilitate the expected impacts relating to sustainable changes in social norms (including gender norms), behavioral determinants and institutional systems for the project intervention sectors. 6. The evaluation resulted, according to the EPEL, in the finding of a more pronounced integration to a large extent of the principle of gender equality compared to the principles of human rights and equity (67.2%; 60.9% and 57.8% respectively) both in the design and in the implementation, monitoring and reporting of the project. However, the evaluation notes that taking into account the disability situation under the principle of human rights deserves to be strengthened both during design (no mention was made for the indicators) and for reporting (no mention is made in the project reports). The evaluation establishes according to the EPEL to a large extent (62.5%) that the project succeeded in identifying and eliminating the cultural, social and economic barriers which prevent children (girls and boys) and women from accessing the services available to them in their communities. This observation is established through six (6) types of project mechanisms linked to SBC activities within the communities served, which are: (i) the implementation of joint interventions involving community leaders, health personnel, parents, children, teachers and OBCs (RECOPE; COSIFE; AVUE); (ii) the use of cash transfers as a means of reducing poverty among vulnerable households; (iii) the empowerment of adolescent girls through their placement in vocational training centers to learn a trade; (iv) the establishment of adolescent clubs; (v) the establishment of the Child Rights Violations Reporting Mechanism (MSVDE); (vi) the establishment of the birth registration mechanism in localities through RECOPE. However, it is established by the evaluation that awareness raising around these mechanisms thus put in place should increasingly be carried by community radios or during mass events (16 days of activism, Children's Day). 7. Finally, with regard to the criterion of accountability towards the populations, the evaluation highlighted the perception to a large extent (63.1%) according to the EPEL stakeholders of the participation of all members of the community during all phases of the project. And this through: (i) consultation of leaders and representatives of host and refugee communities for the approval of project priorities and results frameworks during consultation meetings organized in each department (Lom and Djerem and Kadey); (ii) the establishment of OBCs during the implementation and monitoring phases of the project; (iii) the involvement of communities during consultative missions of institutional stakeholders; (iv) the existence of information panels displayed on the infrastructures from the construction stage to the inauguration stage and even beyond so that the populations have perfect information about these infrastructures. In addition, the evaluation noted the existence and effectiveness of community feedback mechanisms specifically with regard to cash transfer and child protection. Thus, this mechanism was put in place to ensure that the cash transfers used within households were actually used within the expected framework. With regard to child protection, the evaluation establishes that the OBCs (RECOPE, COSIFE) have actively contributed to this mechanism through the establishment of the mechanism for reporting violations of children's rights. However, three (03) main reasons are put forward due to the accessibility perceived to an average extent (50.8%) to feedback mechanisms/responses to concerns, namely: (i) lack of knowledge of this mechanism by the populations; (ii) the communication channel relating to the use of the telephone to use it; (iii) the response time considered long enough to receive responses to the concerns raised. 8. LESSONS LEARNED Four (04) strategic-level lessons are learned at the end of this evaluation, namely: (i) the strong contribution of the strategies implemented to achieving the expected results; (ii) the strong involvement of institutional actors and town halls at all stages which made it possible to obtain the expected results of the PKU; (iii) the consideration of innovations by the PKU (the _Club Tech_ relating to the integration of technology and fun applications in children's teaching with a view to making learning more captivating and effective; the adaptation of WASH works and services to climate change through the prioritization of solar energy for water pumping and the adoption of the child-friendly village approach); and, (iv) the strong contribution of the UNICEF-Ube strategic partnership to the PKU evaluation process which can also serve as a channel for disseminating the results obtained and influence the implementation of the second phase of the project. As for the operational level, four (04) lessons learned should be considered and relate to: (i) the establishment of functional community bodies within the framework of multisectoral interventions, a guarantee of Page | xi _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ achieving the principle of social and behavioral change sought within the communities served and the sustainability of the project's achievements; (ii) the need to streamline financial procedures depending on the context of PKU implementation in order to ensure timely provision of financial resources; (iii) the need to extend decentralized financial management to all decentralized administrations; and, (iv) the strong capitalization of the interventions of other partners (ASNU, Town Halls; Private) in the project areas through the establishment of strategic partnerships. 9. RECOMMENDATIONS Ten (10) in number, they are presented and classified by level of responsibility (strategic and operational): ❖ At the strategic level R1. Plan during the validation of PCDs, an allocation of substantial financial resources to institutionalize the holding of meetings to coordinate project interventions (including the PKU) at the municipal level with partners in view of the role and importance of this type of body in guaranteeing the appropriation of acquired knowledge on the one hand, and with the aim of guaranteeing its optimal functioning on the other hand. _Recipients: Town halls; UNICEF, KOICA and implementing NGOs._ R2. Strengthen knowledge management and the production of robust evidence, specifically to strengthen the capacities of social workers carried out thanks to the PKU through MINAS (regional and central) with a view to understanding in its entirety the contribution of this strengthening provided to the “_psychosocial support and child protection services_” component. _Recipients: MINAS; DRAS; UNICEF and implementing NGOs._ R3. Further strengthen the capacities of all PKU stakeholders on community engagement and behavioral change to guarantee integrated protection of children in connection with the fight against child marriage, early pregnancies, and other forms of violence against children, while integrating into these actions the principle of human rights in relation to the consideration of children with disabilities. _Recipients: MINAS/DRAS; MINEDUB/DREB; MINING/DREE; UNICEF; KOICA and implementing NGOs and communities served._ R4. Expand for greater efficiency and sustainability of project interventions as well as sustainability of achievements through better contribution from state structures, strategic partnerships and/or strengthening coordination with other Ministries such as those in charge of vocational training, agriculture and livestock as well as youth as part of local monitoring and support for beneficiaries. _Recipients: MINEFOP; MINJEC; MINEPIA; UNICEF; KOICA and NGOs._ R5. Guarantee the financial support of municipal volunteers by Town Halls through their consideration during the validation of PCDs, so that they continue to ensure the implementation of interventions. This initiative would not only guarantee the perpetuation of the project's achievements but also their sustainability. _Recipients: Town halls; UNICEF, KOICA and NGOs._ R6. Strengthen the coverage of the PKU in terms of infrastructure in the WASH sectors with a view to promoting their access to related interventions and consequently increasing the desired impact in terms of social and behavioral change within the communities served. _Recipients: MINEE; Town halls; UNICEF, KOICA and NGOs._ R7. Strengthen knowledge on children's rights and the merits of promoting these rights through training activities, mass awareness-raising, community dialogue, engagement of local authorities and use U-reports and the various other platforms that exist and are accessible for this purpose. _Recipients: MINAS/DRAS; MINEDUB/DREB; MINING/DREE; UNICEF; KOICA and implementing NGOs and communities served._ ❖ At the operational level R8. Remedy the dysfunctions observed which hinder the effectiveness and efficiency of KOICA project interventions by implementing 3 types of corrective actions linked to: (i) Gaps/challenges in terms of timely provision of financial and/or material resources (means of travel for NGOs/municipal volunteers); (ii) Relatively long delays in processing financial documents as well as non-compliance with deadlines for liquidating allocated funds; and, (iii) The low number of staff at the social center level (CESO) of the DRAS does not facilitate the processing of case management arising from the mechanism for reporting violations of children's rights at the level of this entity. _Recipients: MINAS; DRAS; UNICEF and NGOs._ R9. Highlight on community radios or during mass events (16 days of activism, children's day) actions in the communities served regarding social and behavioral change with the aim of their acceptance of the positive perception of the improvement of child protection systems, (including issues related to early child marriage, early pregnancies in schools, child labor, the preference of boys in matters of schooling/declaration of births and lack of awareness of respect for the rights of the child). _Recipients: UNICEF; NGOs and communities._ R10. Carry out communication actions towards the populations of the areas served with a view to compensating for their poor accessibility to feedback mechanisms and responses to concerns related to access to the interventions implemented. These actions will be linked to awareness raising on: (i) the promotion of knowledge of this mechanism by the populations; (ii) the existence and use of communication channels other than the telephone to make use of it; (iii) the need to be patient in order to receive adequate responses to the concerns raised. _Recipients: UNICEF; NGOs and communities served._ Page | xi _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ CHAPTER 2: PURPOSE OF THE EVALUATION | _Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024)_ CHAPTER 2: PURPOSE OF THE EVALUATION | _Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024)_ CHAPTER 2: PURPOSE OF THE EVALUATION | _Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024)_ CHAPTER 2: PURPOSE OF THE EVALUATION | |---|---|---| | Table 2.1: Overview of the purpose of the evaluation||| | Project title |Project to address child deprivation through resilience|| | Country |Cameroon|| | Donors |Korea International Cooperation Agency (KOICA)|| | Total budget |USD 7,996,000|| | Duration |2021 to 2024|| | Overall and specific objective | Overall objective - Improve access to protection, education and water, sanitation and hygiene services for children affected by the Central African crisis in the Eastern region. - Strengthen community resilience systems and mechanisms to identify and support vulnerable children. Strategic objectives: - Quality education for successful learning; - Inclusive education for disadvantaged groups and ; - Improve health through reliable water supply and sanitation services; - Provide a protective space for all children through the availability of violence prevention mechanisms and appropriate responses.|| | Results |- Result 1: The rights of children and adolescents are promoted and better respected. - Result 2: By 2024, children and adolescents are registered and have a birth certificate. - Result 3: By 2024, children and adolescents have access to quality psychosocial and child protection services. - Result 4: By 2024, children and adolescents have access to drinking water and adequate sanitation facilities. - Results 5: By 2024, children and adolescents have access to quality education.|| | Holders of rights (Expected beneficiaries) | Expected direct beneficiaries numbering 50,000 per year (nearly 200,000 during the 4 years of the duration of the project). The expected indirect beneficiaries are made up of the population of the 6 communes, including parents, teachers, principals, religious/local leaders and local organizations, including refugees (over 430,000 people).|| | Geographic coverage | Region: East; Departments: Lom and Djerem and Kadey Communes: Garoua Bouali, Mandjou, Bétaré-Oya, Kentzou, Ouli and Ketté.|| | partners (institutional, CSO, OBC, Private sector) of implementation |▪ The central and decentralized services of the sectoral ministries (Protection, Education, EHA); ▪ The town halls of the areas served; ▪ The NGO Consortium ADRA-ASAD-ADELPA, IMC ; ▪ The OBCs (RECOPE, COSIFE, Village Savings and Credit Associations (AVEC) and Village Water User Associations (AVUE)).|| |||Source: UNICEF-KOICA project concept note| 2.1 Presentation of the PKU 8. Children in the Eastern region are seriously affected by the limited availability of basic social services. They are deprived of many of their basic rights, namely education, health, protective environment, water, sanitation and social protection. In addition, some communes in the region are continually exposed to security threats caused by the activities of non-state armed groups operating on the border between Cameroon and the Central African Republic. 9. The project reflects KOICA's mission to ensure children's right to education/child protection and water and sanitation for all by strengthening the education, child protection and water, sanitation and hygiene sectors to build the resilience of systems and communities in the Eastern region. The project addresses the following strategic objectives: Quality education for learning success, inclusive education for disadvantaged groups, improved health through reliable water and sanitation services and protective space for all children. 10. It is in this logic that the UNICEF Cameroon country office, thanks to funding (USD 7,996,000) from the cooperation of the Government of South Korea (KOICA), is committed to contributing to improving access to protection, education and water, sanitation and hygiene services for these children affected by the Central African crisis in the Eastern region of Cameroon. The collaboration between KOICA and UNICEF in the Eastern region aims to address the root causes of the main deprivations listed above and contribute to the improvement of living conditions in the targeted municipalities. Interventions also focus on strengthening community resilience systems and mechanisms to identify and support vulnerable children. 2.2 Importance of PKU Page | 3 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 11. The PKU (2021-2024) is of capital importance for various actors involved in its implementation. So: - ❖ This project is important insofar as it contributes to the achievement of its mission of ensuring the realization of the rights of children, in particular the most vulnerable on the one hand, and the SDGs on the other hand specifically through: - ✓ Education is about contributing to the achievement of SDG 4.1: _“Girls and boys must complete a full cycle of free, equitable and quality primary and secondary education”,_ and SDG 4.2 _“Girls and boys must have access to quality early childhood development”;_ - ✓ Protection: ensuring that case management of vulnerable conflict-affected children, girls and boys, helps them return to school (SDG 16.2 _“Violence and exploitation of children”_) and supporting girls victims of gender-based violence, including child marriage (in line with SDG 5.2 _“Violence against all women and girls”_ and the SDG 5.3 _“Child marriage and female genital mutilation/child exploitation”_). Also, with this project, UNICEF also sought to strengthen the capacity of civil registrars, health workers, traditional and religious leaders to play their role in birth registration, in order to prevent children from being left behind (SDG 16.9 _“Birth Registration”_); - ✓ WASH: the project aimed to improve access to drinking water and quality sanitation services (SDG 6.1 _“Equitable access to drinking water at an affordable price”_ and SDG 6.2 _“Adequate and equitable sanitation and hygiene for all and end of open defecation”_). - ❖ _ For the government: _ The interventions of the evaluated project prove to be important insofar as they are in line with the National Development Strategy (SND 30) in particular pillar 4 on Human Capital and national priorities. Furthermore, the project's interventions align in each sector with the priorities of the municipal development plans. - ❖ _ For the lessor/Donor: _ The importance of the KOICA project for the Lessor/Donor is revealed in its desire to contribute to improving the quality of life for children and adolescents in the Eastern Cameroon region through this project, aiming to achieve strategic alignment between humanitarian assistance and development and therefore the promotion of peace through community resilience. - ❖ _ For communities (children, families, etc.): _ In a context where host communities and refugees, in particular children and adolescents, have limited access to social services, this project, which focused on 6 municipalities in the Eastern region, is part of the perspective of raising the level of social indicators. Indeed, children living in these municipalities are seriously deprived of social services. - ❖ _ For the municipalities: _ The project is aligned with the priorities of the municipalities described in the PCD. It also contributes to strengthening municipal leadership through consultation frameworks at the municipal level. 2.3 PKU results framework 12. The results framework (Annex A.2) summarizes all the expected results and outputs of the project as well as the main results indicators, the activities to be implemented and the parties responsible for each activity. It presents five (05) results indicators for the three intervention sectors of the evaluated project. There are a total of nine (09) product indicators for the child protection (06), WASH (01) and education (02) sectors. 2.4 PKU implementation strategy 13. The project adopted a global approach to implementation with of course specificities in each sector. Thus, the implementation was based on partnerships with Civil Society Organizations (CSOs), decentralized government structures, town halls and community radio stations. These elements were at the heart of the implementation of the Project through a community approach leading to change in social behavior and a solid partnership with municipalities and support from sectoral ministries. 14. Specifically, the project implementation strategy was based on several axes including: - ❖ In the context of child protection, for _birth registration_, the strategy was based on bringing services closer to the population through the establishment of civil status posts in health facilities responsible for registering births occurring in health facilities and community birth registration mechanisms responsible for registering births in the community. Concerning the _fight against child marriage,_ the strategies deployed relate to the establishment of a community mechanism for prevention, detection and management of cases, the provision of monetary transfers to families with girls at risk, professional training for Page | 4 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ girls at risk with starter kit, the development of training modules on the fight against child marriage, training of adolescent girls on life skills and SBC activities in support of the fight against child marriage. In addition, regarding the _promotion of children's rights and protection mechanisms,_ community mechanisms for the protection of children have been established (RECOPE, Clup Ado, etc.). Finally, as part of the _protection of children against violence_, community protection mechanisms have been put in place, the SSP as well as the positive parenting program. - ❖ As part of the EHA, it is a question of providing a sustainable improvement in access to drinking water and sanitation services in communities and in schools through the construction of Mini Solar Water Supply, the rehabilitation of boreholes and the establishment of a sustainable management mechanism. Also, a sustainable management mechanism for WASH works has been put in place through the Operational Unit for the Supply of Drinking Water Services. - ❖ In the context of Education, the promotion of education for all children was discussed through the establishment, training and monitoring of community structures for monitoring registrations and attendance of children of school age. Campaigns to promote _"Back & Stay in School"_ were organized. Also, there was also discussion of increasing the reception capacities of schools through the construction of inclusive classrooms and improving the quality of education through the deployment of the educational supervision model, the construction of inclusive latrines and the training of teachers and the strengthening of school governance. 15. In addition to these strategies, the implementation of the PKU was based on a strategic communication and social mobilization plan broken down from two angles, namely ensuring the visibility of the project and carrying out social mobilization activities for change in social behavior. Table A.3 in the Appendix presents for each sector, the key interventions that were carried out as well as the implementing actors and the respective localities of these interventions. 2.5 PKU theory of change 16. The overall approach adopted for this evaluation being based on contribution analysis, a Theory of Change (ToC) was therefore developed by the evaluation team following the documentary review and preliminary interviews carried out with stakeholders during the inception report. The evaluation team set out to compare this ToC thus developed with the opinions collected during the interviews carried out while also taking into account the implementation of the interventions of the evaluated project. This, in order to guarantee its compliance and thus arrive at a final ToC (Appendix A.4) taking into account all the contributions of the stakeholders. 2.6 Geographic coverage of the PKU 17. The main targets of the PKU are the municipalities most affected by the Central African crisis in the departments of Lom and Djérem and Kadey, namely Garoua-Boulai, Mandjou and Bétaré-Oya and Kentzou, Ouli and Ketté respectively. 2.7 Roles and responsibilities of PKU stakeholders 18. The project is implemented by the UNICEF Bertoua area office team in collaboration with other stakeholders from the child protection, water, sanitation and education sectors, such as local and central services, Mayors, NGOs and community organizations. UNICEF collaborates with UN agencies (UNHCR, UNFPA, ILO) and other technical partners such as bilateral and multilateral cooperation agencies (KOICA, AFD among others). UNICEF Yaoundé supports the zone office in the areas of advocacy, technical support, planning, coordination, monitoring and evaluation. 19. Under this project, government institutions and their branches provide multi-purpose technical support to municipalities and civil society organizations for the implementation of interventions. Details of the stakeholders involved and the roles assigned are presented in the table in Annex A.5. 2.8 PKU budget and implementation partners 20. Over the period 2021-2024, the overall amount of KOICA funding for UNICEF intervention in the Eastern Cameroonian region to contribute to improving access to protection, education and water, sanitation and hygiene services for children affected by the Central African crisis amounted to USD 7,996,000 or 4,397,800. FCFA. Table 5 presents the distribution of this budget envelope during the project implementation period. 21. Based on the documentation provided and used to date, only financial information on the NGOs ADRA and IMC is available and shows a budget allocation of 1,035,899,009 FCFA and 186,370,634 FCFA respectively for the implementation of project activities in their geographical area of ​​competence (Appendix A.6). Page | 5 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ CHAPTER 3: PURPOSE, USERS AND USES OF THE EVALUATION 3.1 Purpose 22. This evaluation has two main goals: accountability and learning. Regarding accountability, this evaluation will report on the results (expected or not) that were achieved by the KOICA project, with donors (vertical accountability) as well as beneficiaries (municipalities, communities and children). As for learning, this evaluation will not only serve to inform project implementation strategies in the years to come, but also highlight some potential corrective actions that need to be examined further in the future. 3.2 Users and uses of the evaluation 23. This evaluation is expected to generate recommendations that will help UNICEF program staff and other stakeholders (see Annex A.5) adapt the implementation of the components of the new phase of the project. Table 3.1 summarizes the main users and intended uses of this assessment. | Table 3.1: Users and uses of evaluation| Table 3.1: Users and uses of evaluation| |---|---| | Evaluation users | Use of the evaluation (how the findings and recommendations will be used) | | UNICEF Cameroon |• Improve the development of implementation strategies for the new phase of the project but also highlight some potential corrective measures that need to be examined further in the future; • Identify strategies that have a high impact on rights holders; • Inform the development of the Next UNICEF Country Programming Document; • Capitalize on the acquired from the project by strengthening the quality, effectiveness, efficiency, impact and sustainability of interventions; • Strengthen their advocacy strategy with technical partners, territorial and decentralized administrations in favor of child protection, particularly the most vulnerable.| | Donor |• Generate independent evidence of the contributions of investments in achieving the strategic objectives of building resilience in Cameroon; • Inform KOICA's decisions to continue supporting UNICEF in Cameroon.| | Government Regional Delegations of MINEPAT, MINEDUC, MINPROFF, etc.) |• Orient strategic choices in favor of children, particularly the most vulnerable • Identify strategies that have a high impact on individuals and communities • Capitalize lessons learned and integrate good practices; • Share lessons learned and best practices with other projects/programs ; • Advocate for appropriate investments to ensure quality programs for children.| | Rights holders whose targets are direct (children, adolescents, youth, families and communities) and indirect (parents, teachers, principals, religious/local leaders and local organizations) community. • Collect suggestions from rights holders to help improve the new phase of the project and other UNICEF Cameroon programs in order to meet the expectations of the community; • Disseminate the findings, conclusions and recommendations of this evaluation in order to promote the participation and involvement of rights holders in program interventions by adopting/replicating operational approaches proven to be the most effective by evaluation.| | SNU agencies and NGO implementing partners |• Integrate (into their daily practices) the good practices identified during the evaluation and remedy the weaknesses identified in the implementation of the interventions; • Develop new intervention strategies; • Become familiar with the approaches estimated to be effective by the evaluation and introduce them more systematically into the interventions.| Page | 6 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ CHAPTER 4: OBJECTIVES, SCOPE, CRITERIA AND QUESTIONS/SUB-QUESTIONS OF THE EVALUATION 4.1 Evaluation objectives 24. The overall objective of this evaluation is to conduct a more in-depth analysis of the effectiveness of the different components, strategies, as well as lessons learned and recommendations to inform future implementation. The evaluation is summative in the sense that it expects an objective and critical evaluation of the program impact, achievements and successes of the project. 25. Specifically, the evaluation of this project aims at the following objectives: 1. Take stock of the achievements made in the implementation of KOICA from 2021 to 2024 (compared to expected results); 2. Determine the coherence, effectiveness of implementation strategies, efficiency, impact, sustainability of the achievements of the KOICA project, its mode of operationalization for the delivery of services adapted to children and their communities; 3. Explain the theory of change that guided the implementation of the project; 4. Identify lessons learned about what worked and what did not work including unexpected results (positive and negative); 5. Estimate the contribution of the project in terms of reducing gender gaps, equity and improving the situation of the most vulnerable children; 6. Identify avenues for behavioral and social change in the areas of child protection, WASH, girls’ education and social cohesion, WASH; 7. Formulate key recommendations (strategic and operational) as part of a continuous learning process on how to improve implementation and strengthen project performance. 4.2 Scope of the evaluation 4.2.1 Thematic scope 26. The evaluation covers the entire KOICA project with particular attention to the activities implemented in the following areas: (i) access to quality education for children through increasing the reception capacities of schools, strengthening the capacities of teachers, distribution of school and learning materials, promotion of learning through innovation and support for educational supervision; (ii) access to water and sanitation in schools and communities through the construction and rehabilitation of water and sanitation infrastructure and the strengthening of water service management mechanisms in municipalities; (iii) birth registration by bringing birth registration services closer to the population (the establishment of advanced civil registration posts in health facilities and community birth registration mechanisms); (iv) the fight against child marriage through the education and commitment of stakeholders, the training of workers and young girls on this theme and the establishment of community mechanisms for prevention and response in the event of a case; (v) child protection through capacity building of child protection actors and the establishment of community child protection mechanisms. 4.2.2 Geographic scope 27. The documentary study which was carried out as part of this evaluation covered all project activities mainly in the 6 project intervention communes, namely Garoua Boulai, Bétaré-Oya and Mandjou in the Lom department and Djérem and Ketté, Kentzou and Ouli in the Kadey department. As part of the data collection, 4 primary data collection sites were taken into account, namely Garoua Boulai and Bétaré-Oya in the Lom department and Djérem as well as Kétté and Kentzou in the Kadey department. Figure 1.1 below provides an overview of the geographical coverage of the project. 4.2.3 Chronological scope 28. The evaluation covered all activities implemented between January 2021 and June 2024 in the 6 municipalities of intervention of the evaluated project as required by the ToR. However, the timeline scope of the evaluation has been extended to December 2024 depending on the level of data availability provided to the evaluation team. 4.3 Evaluation criteria 29. In order to achieve the aim and objectives of the evaluation and respond to the expected uses, in accordance with the ToR, the evaluation focused on five (05) of the United Nations Evaluation Group (UNEG) and Development Assistance Committee of the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development OECD/DAC: (i) coherence, (ii) effectiveness, (iii) efficiency, (iv) impact and (v) sustainability (see Figure 3). In addition to these criteria, two additional criteria relating to gender, human rights and equity considerations as well as accountability to affected populations were taken into account as part of this evaluation. Also, taking into account the themes covered by the evaluated project, particular emphasis was placed on the integrated child protection approach. Page | 7 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ ==> picture [470 x 503] intentionally omitted <== Figure 4.1: Geographic coverage of the KOICA-UNICEF project 4.4 Evaluation questions and sub-questions 30. It is essential to note that the Evaluation Questions (EQ) and the Sub-Evaluation Questions (SQE) were developed based on the ToC of the KOICA project in the East, the evaluability analysis and preliminary consultations with key people in the implementation of the evaluation. These questions were discussed and validated in conjunction with the managers of this evaluation during the start-up phase. 31. In total, 7 evaluation questions as well as 19 related evaluation sub-questions were addressed (Annex A.7). Finally, it is noted that each QE/SQE has associated indicators, as well as the data collection method, the sources of information and the analysis method. This set constitutes the evaluation matrix (Appendix A.8). Page | 8 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ CHAPTER 5: EVALUATION METHODOLOGY AND PROCESS 5.1 Evaluation approaches and methodological innovations 32. The evaluation methodology followed a mixed approach combining qualitative (primary sources) and quantitative (secondary sources) methods and data. Qualitative and quantitative data were triangulated and results validated in coordination with project stakeholders. The methodological innovations implemented during this evaluation are included in this box below. Box 5.1: _Methodological innovations implemented as part of the PKU evaluation_ 1. The involvement of students (through the MoU between UNICEF and Ube) and emerging evaluators in this process through their capacity building in evaluation and PSEA, as well as their participation in results presentation meetings to prepare them to contribute to the report dissemination activities constitute a practice specific to this evaluation that can serve as a good practice to be promoted; 2. The use of technologies relating to online surveys through the EPEL carried out with evaluation stakeholders and which made it possible to quantify the results in relation to the evaluation sub-questions is the subject of this evaluation exercise. 33. One of the approaches adopted for this evaluation is linked to contribution analysis, based on the Theory of Change (Appendix A.4). It consisted of seeking consolidated evidence relating to the expected results/products of the PKU and the contribution of the project in obtaining said results/products. It also consisted of seeking evidence as to the expected effects of the project and the cause and effect links between the products and the observed effects. This made it possible to establish to what extent the evaluated project made (or not) a contribution to the products and effects observed. The implementation of this contribution approach for the qualitative component of the evaluation was done specifically based on all the evaluation criteria during (a) individual interviews with key informants and NGOs/CSOs (Appendix A.9.2.1 and A.9.2.2 respectively), (b) focus groups with representatives of CBOs (Appendix A.9.2.3) and representatives of men, women and children aged 12-17 (Appendix A.9.2.4) and (c) Cadre H sessions with children under 12 years old (Appendix A.9.2.5). From the qualitative aspect, this contribution approach was also understood based on statistical analyzes of data relating to the Online Perception Survey (EPEL) (Appendix A.9.1). 34. The evaluation also used the “Most Significant Change” (CPS) approach, which consists of identifying the changes obtained during the implementation of a program. As such, it made it possible to become aware of certain non-predetermined results achieved at the end of the implementation. This participatory form of approach also has the advantage of offering a better understanding of the experiences of participants in the KOICA project as well as a sharing of experiences through the identification of significant changes. It thus made it possible to highlight different interpretations of the reality relating to the contribution of the project in the lives of the rights holders (beneficiaries) to this initiative while taking into account the intersectionality in the choice of participants (in particular for children) in this approach. The implementation of this approach was specifically based on _effectiveness criteria_ during (a) individual interviews with key informants (Appendix A.9.2.1 and A.9.2.2 respectively), (b) FGDs with CBO representatives (Appendix A.9.2.3) and representatives of men, women and children aged 12-17 (Appendix A.9.2.4) and (c) training sessions. Frame H with children under 12 years old (Appendix A.9.2.5). Finally, in terms of the qualitative component, this CPS approach is implemented using the EPEL tool (Appendix A.9.1) for the _effectiveness criterion_ at the level of question C2 of section C as well as at the level of the _impact criterion_ in section F (questions F1 and F2). 35. The design and conduct of the evaluation mission were carried out using a participatory and inclusive approach wherever possible. Thus, the sampling of key informants sought to be as complete and representative as possible within the limits of the information available and the time allocated to the data collection phase. In total, 3 national evaluation experts as well as 10 data collection agents made available by the University of Bertoua were under the leadership of the international consultant/Team Leader during the data collection phase. In addition, the restitution of the preliminary analysis facilitated exchange and constructive analysis between the evaluation team and the main stakeholders[8]. Furthermore, the rights holders (beneficiaries[9]) of the interventions were involved in the evaluation process when the focus groups were carried out. Finally, as part of the management of the evaluation, the process benefited from the support of an Evaluation Reference Group (ERG) of which DREPAT was the lead and which thus made it possible to guarantee the quality assurance of the evaluation. 5.2 Data collection methods and tools 36. This evaluation was based on a mixed research approach (qualitative and quantitative) which mobilized: > 8 Held on December 10 with the GRE. > 9 Obligation bearers and rights holders (OBC (RECOPE, COSIFE) and beneficiaries (men and women and children aged 12-17 from host and refugee communities). Page | 9 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ ❖ Quantitative aspect 37. As part of the EPEL, an online questionnaire was made available to two types of participants: (i) those from government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, ASNUs and NGOs/OBCs and (ii) those representing OBCs and beneficiaries (men and women from host and refugee communities). This questionnaire (Appendix A.9.1.1) was configured on Open Data Kit (ODK) and the link was shared with the various stakeholders to facilitate the collection of quantitative data. The response rate for this component was considered acceptable with an overall participation rate of 93% (70 participants out of the 75 projected) or 29% (5 participants out of the 17 projected) for the stakeholders of the interviews carried out and 105% (61 forms submitted out of the 58 projected) with regard to representatives of CBOs/beneficiaries. The data obtained were subject to statistical analysis in addition to the qualitative analyzes resulting from the interviews. ❖ Qualitative aspect 38. The qualitative data (secondary and primary) for this evaluation were produced from: - ✓ Documentary review through the use of (i) programmatic documents relating to the design, implementation, monitoring and coordination of the evaluated project, (ii) policy, program, strategy and/or study/evaluation documents or any other document related to the thematic areas of the said system produced by the strategic partners (Government; ASNU; CSO/NGO). It also made it possible to analyze the tools of the evaluated Project, as well as the way in which the evaluated Project took into account the Gender, Equity and Human Rights aspects during its design and implementation. The documentary research carried out from around fifty (58) available documents (Appendix A.10), constituted one of the sources of information to answer the evaluation questions. This documentation includes in particular the basic and final surveys of the INS PKU. - ✓ Semi-structured individual interviews with key informants at the central (Yaoundé) and decentralized (Eastern Region) level, conducted with key actors and stakeholders of PKU interventions in order to collect in-depth information and data from the interventions, implementation and/or coordination partners (Government type actors/national institutions/sectoral administrations, CSO/OBC and ASNU). These interviews were conducted using a guide (_Cf._ Appendix A.9.2.1 and A.9.2.2) with the aim of collecting evidence relating to the interventions, the expected results, the factors that may interact and the challenges encountered. This method also made it possible to obtain data on the cross-cutting aspects (Gender, Human Rights and Equity) of the PKU. The 32 semi-structured individual interviews (including 9, or 28% with women) carried out in total on the 34 potentials identified (i.e., 94% participation rate) are contained in the list of people interviewed presented in Appendix A.11. These individual interviews were carried out on the basis of a sampling of key informants and the selection of these key informants was carried out on the basis of the mapping of the stakeholders of the program evaluated and by prioritizing the representativeness and importance of the role played by each of these actors. Taking into account the availability of stakeholders, the interviews combined both face-to-face and virtual mode, through social media channels which ensure the confidentiality of conversations (teams, WhatsApp, Zoom). - ✓ Focus Group Discussion (FGD) carried out using a group discussion guide (Appendix A.9.2.3 and A.9.2.4) in face-to-face mode with representatives of OBCs (RECOPE, COSIFE) and beneficiaries (men, women and children aged 12-17 from host and refugee communities). This method made it possible to collect evidence relating to the needs met/not met by the interventions, the effectiveness of the interventions, the impact and sustainability of project achievements in the communities in an interactive manner. This method also made it possible to introduce discussions on aspects of gender equality, human rights and equity. All 40 projected FGDs were carried out and concerned 8 FGDs (20%) for members of OBCs, 8 FGDs (20%) with women, 8 FGDs (20%) with women, 8 FGDs (20%) with male Adolescents and young people aged 12 to 17 and 8 FGDs (20%) with Adolescents and young people aged 12 to 17 female. In total, 320 people were affected by FGD, including 160 (50%) males and 160 (50%) females. - ✓ Evaluation/Frame H with direct beneficiaries (female and male children under 12 years old, both refugees and those living in host communities) of the communities in relation to PKU interventions in the project's target communes. This participatory method used, using an evaluation guide H (Appendix A.9.2.5) with groups of 6 to 8 children (separate girls and boys) of the same age range, made it possible to introduce discussions on aspects of gender equality, human rights and equity. All 16 sessions of planned H sessions were carried out and involved 8 sessions with children from host communities (including 4 for girls and 4 for boys) and 8 sessions with children from refugee communities (including 4 with girls and 4 with boys). - ✓ Structured observations of sites in the service offering sectors (classrooms and tables, benches, WASH facilities, birth registration) dedicated to children within the framework of the PKU, in relation to the delivery points of these services where certain interventions relating to the PKU were delivered. These observations made it possible to inquire about the quality of services and the conditions under which PKU beneficiaries use the interventions implemented by Page | 10 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ - this experience within their community. This method, implemented using the observation guide (Appendices A.9.2.6), made it possible to collect additional evidence relating to sustainability as well as aspects of gender equality, human rights and equity. Specifically, during the field trips, the site observations that will be organized will be with the aim of identifying the achievements occurring during the PKU, the quality of services and the different service delivery sites in the areas of Education, WASH and protection. All 12 sites in the 4 target municipalities of the evaluated project planned during data collection were actually visited during this evaluation. - ✓ Collection of life stories from representatives of CBOs, children under 12 years of age who benefit from interventions and representatives of beneficiaries (men, women and children aged 12-17 from host and refugee communities) in the context of the _Most Significant Change_ (CSP) in order to justify why they think that one change is more remarkable than another in the context of the implementation of the PKU. The tool used (Appendix A.9.2.7) ensured that all of the 12 life stories expected in the 4 target municipalities of the project evaluated during data collection were actually carried out during this evaluation in the 3 sectors (WASH, education, and child protection). 5.3 Evaluation stakeholder sampling strategy 39. The evaluation followed the purposive sampling strategy articulated around the UNEG ethical guidelines for evaluation (2020)[10]. This sampling process integrated human rights, gender and equity in a cross-cutting manner in accordance with the Code of Conduct[11] and UNEG guidelines on integrating human rights, people living with disabilities, vulnerable populations and gender equality into assessments[12]. In addition, to take into account the UN SWAP criteria on Gender Equality and Women's Empowerment (GEEW), the sampling strategy (Annex A.12) sought as much as possible to balance gender for the different categories of informants, including the most difficult to reach (vulnerable). Indeed, while it was planned that at least 50% of participants in the different collection methods would be women/girls wherever possible, we ultimately managed to reach 49% of this segment of the population. Together with municipal volunteers and implementing NGO actors, the team managed to identify and involve 3 stakeholders living with disabilities during data collection. 40. The mapping of implementing partners made it possible to identify different categories, including: - Actors such as national institutions/deconcentrated or decentralized sectoral administrations: Regional Delegation of the Economy, Planning and Territorial Development (DREPAT), Regional Delegation of Social Affairs (DRAS), Regional Delegation of Basic Education (DREB), Regional Delegation of Water and Energy (DREE), Regional Delegation for the Promotion of Women and Family (DRPROFF), Regional Directorate of the National Office of Civil Status of the East (BUNEC), Regional Agency of the National Institute of Statistics of the East (INS-East), the Decentralized Territorial Communities (CTD) of the localities of intervention; - Community-Based Organizations (CBOs): Community Child Protection Networks (RECOPE), Student Registration and Attendance Monitoring Committees (COSIFE), School Management Committees (COGES); - ASNU: UNICEF; UNHCR, ILO, WFP; UNFPA as well as NGOs/CSOs: ADRA-ASAD-ADELPA Consortium, IMC; - The rights holders made up of direct and indirect beneficiaries of the interventions implemented by the Project. 41. Given the qualitative nature of the data collection activities during field visits, a purposive sampling strategy presented in Appendix A.12 was recommended. This approach favored taking into account the expectations expressed by the various stakeholders consulted during the start-up phase (participatory approach) and made it possible to cover all types of existing stakeholders in the KOICA project (2021-2024) as part of the data collection. 42. It should be remembered that the size of the sample of rights holders (beneficiaries) with whom the FGDs were carried out as well as that of the H Evaluations and life stories was determined for convenience, that is to say according to their availability and accessibility at the time of the field visits. Likewise, in relation to the choice of people to be interviewed, it was notably carried out at the level of rights holders (expected beneficiaries) also using the so-called “snowball” method[13]. This purposive sampling method ensured that women and vulnerable categories were taken into account, including people living with disabilities (four in number) who had to take part in the FGDs. > 10 UNEG (2020) _UNEG Ethical Guidelines for Evaluation._ Available at: https://www.alnap.org/system/files/content/resource/files/summary/UNEG_Ethical_Guidelines_for_Évaluation_2020.pdf. (Consulted on 02/01/2023) > 11 http://www.unÉvaluation.org/document/detail/100 > 12 http://www.uneval.org/document/detail/980 > 13 According to the criteria, we identify a first respondent who, after interview, recommends another respondent who he believes is able to provide us with more information, and so on. Page | 11 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 43. To carry out the quantitative component of the evaluation specifically for the EPEL, a minimum sample of 75 participants was envisaged, made up of 17 participants who could take part in individual interviews relating to key informants and at least 58 representatives of the participants in the 40 FGDs projected for rights holders (representatives of OBCs and representatives of beneficiaries (men, women and children aged 12-17 from host and refugee communities). As such, the survey technique chosen was that of a stratified sample survey with two stages of sampling among these stakeholders. All of these stakeholders for the quantitative component constituted a stratum within which at the 1st level all the individuals identified according to their intervention at the level of the KOICA project (2021-2024) evaluated were considered. evaluation, i.e. 23% for individual interview stakeholders as well as 77% for community rights holder type stakeholders. Finally, the profile of the 70 participants made up of 34 men (49%) and 36 women (51%) for this quantitative part of the evaluation is as follows: Figure 5.1: Profile of participants in the quantitative component linked to the online questionnaire of the PKU evaluation ==> picture [469 x 124] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- Refugee 18 25.7 Host community 18 25.7 COSIFE 11 15.7 RECOPE 14 20.0 UNICEF 3 4.3 BUNEC 1 1.4 CTD 3 4.3 DDEB 2 2.9 0 5 10 15 20 25 30 Valid percentage Frequency ----- End of picture text ----- Source: Online survey data as of 12/13/2024 44. In total, all the data collection methods used for this evaluation concerned 550 people, including 269 (49%) females and 281 (51%) males according to the table below. Table 5.1: Summary of participants according to the data collection methods implemented as part of the PKU evaluation | Methods | Number of participants | Number of participants | Number of participants | Number of participants | Number of participants | Number of participants | Participants living with a disability | |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---| || Female Sex || Male gender || Total ||| || Number | % | Number | % | Number | % || |Semi-structured individual interviews|9|28|23|72|32|5.8|-| |Focus Group Discussion (FGD)|160|50|160|50|320|58.2|5| |Assessment/Framework H|64|50|64|50|128|23.3|-| |Online Perception Survey (EPEL)|36|51|34|49|70|12.7|-| | Total | 269 | 49 | 281 | 51 | 550 | 100 | 5 | Source: Online survey data as of 12/13/2024 5.4 Analysis and data quality assurance 45. Data analysis was carried out using qualitative (content analysis) and quantitative (descriptive statistics) techniques. The consulting team implemented a sequential exploratory triangulation approach as defined by Creswell in 2003[14]. The team first collected and analyzed mainly qualitative data (individual/group interviews and group discussions). Secondly, the team carried out a descriptive analysis of the quantitative primary data from this evaluation as well as providing a descriptive analysis at the level of the effectiveness criterion concerning the expected results of the implementation of the evaluated project. 46. The qualitative data collected were the subject of a thematic analysis in relation to the opinions collected from the participants developed using MS Excel software to identify the main findings and conclusions on the basis of comparison and/or contrasts according to the data collected. 47. Specifically for quantitative analysis, 3 types were used as part of this evaluation depending on the data source that made it possible to obtain the type of quantitative data. - Thus, specifically for the effectiveness criterion, the analysis relating to the performance of the PKU Results/Products indicators is based on a rating system with a qualitative scale with 2 values: a “Satisfactory” threshold, if at least > 14 Creswell, J.W. (2003). Research design: Qualitative, quantitative, and mixed methods approaches (2nd ed.). Thousand Oaks, CA: Sage Page | 12 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ - 80% of the objective in terms of assigned performance is achieved and an “Unsatisfactory” threshold (if the assigned performance achieved is less than 80%). Thus, the analysis of the quantitative performance for each of the Results/Products indicators of the PKU is made on the basis of the calculation of a Completion Rate (TA) according to the formula below: - Completion Rate (TA) = (Achievements-Baseline) x 100/ (Target-Baseline) - ❑ The TA indicates the progress made in achieving the planned target at the end of implementation; - ❑ The complement to 100 of the TA indicates the gap to be filled to achieve the planned target; - ❑ Beyond 100, the TA indicates the excess achieved relative to the planned target in terms of implementation. Table 5.2: Performance measurement relating to the effectiveness criterion of the KOICA project (2021-2024) Rating system Indicators Results/Products (1-n) Sufficient Performance (If TA>80%) Insufficient Performance (If TA<80%) - As for the primary data obtained by the Online Perception Survey (EPEL) from key informants made up of government actors/national institutions/sectoral administrations, CSOs/OBCs and ASNUs as well as from representatives of OBCs and beneficiaries (men and women from host and refugee communities), they were the subject of descriptive statistical analyzes using SPSS software; - The analysis of cost-efficiency and return on investment made on the basis of quantitative data from the documentary review relating to the financial resources mobilized and used, was also carried out as part of this evaluation. Thus, from the examination of the favorable/adequate efficiency ratio between the resources mobilized and used and the results obtained at the end of the implementation of the evaluated project, the ideal situation summarizing the state for the Results/Products of the said Project was thus determined from one of these 4 following cases: - ✓ Either we will have mobilized as much as possible, spent as much as possible and obtained a satisfactory level of performance (_Situation 1_) relating to the achievement of Results/Products; - ✓ Either we will have mobilized as much as possible, spent as little as possible for a satisfactory level of performance (_Situation 2_) relating to the achievement of Results/Products; - ✓ Either we will have mobilized as little as possible, spent as much as possible and obtained a satisfactory level of performance (_Situation 3_) relating to the achievement of Results/Products; - ✓ Either we will have mobilized as little as possible, spent as little as possible and also obtained a satisfactory level of performance (_Situation 4_) relating to the achievement of Results/Products; Conversely, a situation reflecting an unfavorable/inadequate efficiency ratio would reflect the situation where either: - ✓ We will have mobilized as little as possible, spent as much as possible and obtained an unsatisfactory level of performance (Situation 5) relating to the achievement of Results/Products; - ✓ We will have mobilized as little as possible, spent as little as possible and obtained an unsatisfactory level of performance (Situation 6) relating to the achievement of Results/Products; - ✓ We will have mobilized as much as possible, spent as little/as possible for an unsatisfactory level of performance (Situation 7) relating to the achievement of Results/Products; - ✓ We will have mobilized as much as possible, spent as much as possible and obtained an unsatisfactory level of performance (Situation 8) relating to the achievement of Results/Products. 48. Finally, the overall analysis of the data collected was carried out on the basis of findings subject to a triangulation process, that is to say by a cross-comparison of the 6 different data collection methods and by cross-checking the information obtained from the different sources. The triangulation of recurring points of view made it possible to validate the main findings from which the main conclusions were drawn and the recommendations proposed. 49. As part of the management of the evaluation, the process benefited from the support of an ERM, thus guaranteeing the quality assurance of the evaluation with which the team of consultants worked. Quality checks were included at all stages of the process through internal and external reviews of deliverables. As such, quality assurance on mission products was ensured at several levels. Internally, the team of consultants relied on its expertise and experience of similar missions to implement appropriate methods, collect quality data, then conduct robust analyses. This is done through a 3-level quality control mechanism, (i) during the preparation of the evaluation mission, (ii) during the data collection phase as well as during (iii) the processing, data analysis and reporting phase (Appendix A.13). Externally, through a dynamic system for reviewing and validating evaluation products, the GRE opted for participatory validation sessions for each of the deliverables emanating from the evaluation team during the evaluation process with a view to obtaining quality products. 5.5 Ethical considerations and evaluation principles Page | 13 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 50. The entire evaluation process and methodology was structured around the evaluation standards and rules of the United Nations Evaluation Group (UNEG)[15] and was guided by good evaluation practices. The evaluation team also relied on the guidelines established by the Organization for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD-DAC) Development Assistance Committee (OECD) Quality Standards for Development Evaluation[16], the Guide for Ethical Research Involving Children[17], as well as UNICEF GEROS[18] relating to ethical standards, data collection and analysis in evaluations. Moreover, in application of the MoU between the University of Bertoua and UNICEF, the students of this institution to serve as data collection staff benefited from capacity building in evaluation and the PSEA from November 5 to 9, 2024. 51. Thus, in accordance with the applicable laws regarding the protection of stakeholders, the evaluation team took care to ensure the anonymity/confidentiality of the participants and also ensured that the data were protected and used for agreed purposes. 52. Particularly for this evaluation, it was determined during the start-up phase with all stakeholders that the involvement of children[19] and adolescents in data collection would be considered under certain conditions. The evaluation team applied a certain number of considerations, including the principle of _“do no harm”_, before involving them in discussion groups or H evaluation sessions. For the participants in the various interviews, FGD and Evaluation/Frame H, the team requested and obtained their consent (Appendix A.14). 53. Finally, the evaluation team ensured compliance with the ethical principles and standards defined by the UNEG, indicated below, by carrying out the actions defined below: - Anonymity and confidentiality: The evaluation respected the rights of those providing information, guaranteeing their anonymity and confidentiality. - Accountability: The entire team confirmed the results presented in the report and any disagreements were mentioned. The report informs of any conflicts or differences of opinion that have arisen between the consultants and the stakeholders of the evaluated program regarding the conclusions and/or recommendations of the evaluation. - Integrity: The evaluation highlighted issues that are not specifically mentioned in the ToR, in order to carry out a complete analysis of the evaluated program. - Independence: The evaluation team ensured that it remained independent of the program evaluated and that none of its members were involved in its implementation or in any other phase. - Incidents: When problems arose during fieldwork or at any other time during the evaluation, they were reported immediately to the evaluation manager. As such, the existence of such problems could in no way be invoked to justify the inability to achieve the results expected by UNICEF in this mandate. - Credibility: The evaluation team ensured the accuracy of the information collected during the preparation of the reports and affirmed, thanks to the quality control mechanisms deployed, the credibility of the information presented in the final report. - Conflict of interest: The members of the evaluation team were not involved in any stage (design, implementation, monitoring/evaluation) of the implementation of the evaluated program. As such, no conflict of interest was highlighted between the evaluation team and the institutions and communities involved in the evaluation. - Report submission deadlines: reports were submitted within the agreed deadlines, and the evaluation team ensured that all deliverables (reports) provided met the standards stipulated in the mandate. The evaluation's application of the UNICEF Ethical Evidence Generation Guiding Principles for Ethical Standards for Research, Evaluation and Data Collection and Analysis is presented in Annex A.15. 5.6 Limitations and constraints of the evaluation 54. The three (3) limitations and constraints of the evaluation that occurred during implementation as well as the mitigation strategies are presented in the table in Appendix A.16. > 15 UNEG (2017). Evaluation standards and rules. http://www.uneval.org/document/detail/1914 > 16 OECD-CAD (2010). Quality standards for development evaluation, https://www.oecd.org/dac/evaluation/dcdndep/44920384.pdf > 17 - Ethical Research Involving Children (2019). Ethical Guidance. https://childethics.com/ethical guidance/ > 18 https://www.unicef.org/evaluation/global-evaluation-reports-oversight-system-geros > 19 - https://www.unicef.org/supply/files/ATTACHMENT_IV UNICEF_Procedure_for_Ethical_Standards.PDF Page | 14 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ CHAPTER 6: EVALUATION FINDINGS 6.1 Consistency Evaluation criterion question EQ1: To what extent did the KOICA project prove to be adequate in its implementation mechanisms and did it create synergies between implementing parties? Sub-question to the evaluation criterion 1.1. To what extent does the KOICA project ensure the coherence and complementarity of interventions with the other components of the country program and the interventions of other partners including the ASNU? 1.2. To what extent did the different strategies adopted (intersectorality, community engagement, partnerships) in the implementation of the project promote synergy, collaboration and complementarities between the different components of the KOICA project (Protection, Education, WASH) and offer a package of services to the different target groups? ==> picture [318 x 151] intentionally omitted <== Image 6.1: _Training from May 11 to 13, 2022 for facilitators from community nursery schools in the communes of Ketté, Kentzou, Mandjou, Ouli, Betaré Oya and Garoua Boulai on animation and creativity techniques with a view to child protection_ Source: _Photo taken from the KOICA donor report, May 2023_ The triangulation of analyzes relating to the consistency criterion is based on 4 types of data sources, namely: 1. The documentary review covering the documents (Project Document, UNICEF Country Program) and the annual implementation reports of the evaluated UNICEF/NGO project between 2021 and 2024; 2. Semi-structured interviews in the 4 target municipalities with representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated program, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project; 3. The Online Perception Survey (EPEL) among representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated project, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project as well as among indirect rights holders; 4. Focus Group Discussion (FGD) with representatives of CBOs and rights holders (men, women and children aged 12-17) from the communities served by the project (host communities and refugees). 6.1.1 Coherence and complementarity of PKU interventions with other components of the country program and the interventions of other partners including the ASNU 55. The evaluation notes that PKU interventions are consistent with the vision of the country program which consists of _ “ensuring that every child in Cameroon – girls and boys, including adolescents – survives, thrives, learns, is protected and develops their full potential, thus contributing to the growth of human capital and social well-being_ _”. _ Indeed, the in-depth examination of the UNICEF-Cameroon Country Program Document[20] (CPD, 2022-2026) highlights the alignment and > 20 Achievements set out in the UNICEF-Cameroon Country Program Document: > 1. By 2026, more people, particularly young people, women and socially and economically vulnerable groups, including refugees and displaced people, benefit equitably from increased opportunities, in a green, diversified, transformative, resilient and inclusive economy, creating decent jobs in productive sectors. > 2.1. By 2026, more people by age group, particularly the most vulnerable, including refugees and displaced people, use quality basic social services equitably and sustainably to realize their full human potential and increase their social and economic well-being. > 2.2. By 2026, gaps in key socio-economic indicators are reduced, reflecting greater gender equality and progress in the empowerment of youth, women and girls, and other vulnerable groups, including in humanitarian contexts. > 3. By 2026, young people, women, the most vulnerable groups and people living with disabilities, including refugees and displaced people, actively contribute to the effectiveness of policies and the performance of public institutions at the national, regional and municipal levels, and fully enjoy their rights. Page | 15 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ complementarity of the interventions of the evaluated project with those of the UNICEF country program in connection with the fight against child deprivation, these interventions converge and are highlighted in Appendix A.17. 56. Also, the evaluation noted that the PKU interventions evaluated were consistent and complementary with those of numerous partners working in the Eastern region in particular and in Cameroon in general. Thus, the table in Appendix A.18 below lists for each PKU intervention sector, the main consistent and complementary interventions implemented by other partners. Analysis of this table shows that PKU’s interventions are consistent and complementary with those of other partners in the following sectors: - Protection: Consistency with two interventions including birth registration and referral of GBV cases with NGOs such as NRC, HURDA and the Red Cross; - EHA: Adequacy with 3 interventions including: (i) the development of water points, (ii) the revitalization of the municipal water fund and (iii) the establishment of an efficient water management system. 57. It should be noted that these interventions are coherent and complementary to the extent that to avoid duplication in the chain of intervention, a consultation platform/framework which brings together the different partners has been set up in the Town Halls. This platform, which is coordinated by the Mayors of these municipalities, constitutes a place of exchange where all strategic actors and partners meet once a quarter. Moreover, it should be noted that this consultation mechanism thus set up allowed, for example, in the municipality of Ouli that one of the two blocks of latrines planned to be installed there was moved to another school due to a similar intervention by the National Participatory Development Program (PNDP). An action which thus made it possible to limit duplication in the case of similar interventions implemented by entities other than those of the KOICA project evaluated. 58. Finally, this coherence and complementarity of the interventions of the KOICA project with the other components of the country program and the interventions of other partners including the ASNU was perceived through the EPEL. Thus, the stakeholders who took part estimate to a large extent (98.5%) this coherence and complementarity of the activities implemented by the PKU. 59. Regarding coherence and complementarity with ASNU interventions, it was perceptible to the extent that the PKU enabled UNICEF and the ILO[21] to develop a joint work plan in 2023 on the following aspects: (i) _Decentralization:_ with a view to strengthening the leadership of Town Halls in the management of consultation spaces within municipalities; (ii) _Community mobilization and awareness raising:_ by strengthening the capacities of community mobilization actors such as CBOs, village development committees, community protection agents, community health workers as well as the introduction of messages on ILO themes during awareness raising activities conducted by UNICEF; (iii) _Economic empowerment:_ by the ILO supporting RECOPE members in the identification of beneficiaries for entrepreneurship training; (iv) _Social protection:_ by capitalizing on maps of social protection services developed by the two agencies, in order to strengthen the referral circuit within the municipalities; (v) _GBV:_ by establishing a reporting system and strengthening psychosocial support for children and people who are survivors of GBV and victims of child labor and by integrating positive parenting into financial education, particularly on the child's priorities; (vi) _Community complaints and feedback mechanisms:_ through the joint organization of awareness sessions for beneficiaries and community dialogue with leaders. 6.1.2 Strong contribution of the different strategies adopted in the implementation of the project to promoting synergy, collaboration and complementarities between the different sectors/components of the PKU and to offering a package of services to the different target groups 60. Based on the EPEL data as well as those from individual interviews, FGDs and observations carried out, the evaluation highlighted a strong appreciation of the project's capacity to contribute through its implemented strategies to: (i) synergy, collaboration and complementarities between its different components (Protection, Education, WASH/WASH); (ii) and to offer a package of services to different target groups. 61. Thus, with regard to the EPEL data, they corroborate this observation to the extent that nearly 6 out of 10 respondents (57.6%) affirm to a large extent that the strategies implemented had to promote synergies, collaboration and complementarity between the different components of the project evaluated (Graph 6.1). > 4. By 2026, people in different agro-ecological zones, including youth, women and socially vulnerable groups, live in healthier environments, sustainably manage environmental resources, including biodiversity, and are more resilient to disasters and climate change. > 21 The agency is implementing the ILO-KOICA Project: Empowerment of women through capacity building of entrepreneurs in the agropastoral value chains (corn, cassava, chicken), social protection, fight against HIV-AIDS and GBV, in 16 communes in the East and Adamaoua. Page | 16 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ Figure 6.1: Extent to which the strategies promoted synergies, collaboration and complementarities between the different components (protection, education, WASH) of the project ==> picture [344 x 130] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- To a large extent 57.6 To an average extent 34.8 To a small extent 7.6 0.0 10.0 20.0 30.0 40.0 50.0 60.0 70.0 ----- End of picture text ----- Source: Data from the online survey (EPEL) as of 11/12/2024 62. Also, on the basis of the interviews carried out (individual interviews and FGD), the evaluation established that the different strategies implemented promoted synergy, collaboration and complementarity between the different sectors/components of the KOICA project and offered a package of services to the different target groups. 63. Indeed, intersectorality seen as a sign of synergy, collaboration and complementarity and a guarantee of offering service packages was perceptible during this evaluation through the actions implemented in particular: 1. The development of strategies and modules for integrated mobilization with the education and WASH sectors around the accountability/empowerment of children (in school and outside of school) on issues of education, entrepreneurship/volunteering, sanitation, hygiene including menstrual hygiene; 2. The development and implementation of an integrated strategy (education-protection-social policy) to address issues of violence in schools, issuance of birth certificates for school children and child marriage which have an impact on the retention of children in school. Thus, community protection mechanisms through RECOPE and COSIFE have, as part of their actions, integrated key messages from education and WASH during their communication and social mobilization; 3. The establishment of a platform (DREB/DRAS) for the development and validation of the teacher's guide for teaching the rights and duties of children in primary school; 4. The development and validation of training modules to combat child marriage under the leadership of the DRPROFF in concert with the DRAS among others; 5. Provision of integrated technical and financial support from community coordination bodies to support and implement action plans/communication plans; 6. Strong and real collaboration between the WASH and Education section as part of the integration of the complete package (drilling, latrine) in the schools chosen for the construction of classrooms. Moreover, a module on “_EHA in school_” was integrated into teacher training when hygiene practices in schools were reinforced; 7. The implementation of communication actions for change, community mobilization and awareness raising carried out in close collaboration between the SBC section and the 3 sectors of the project for greater impact in the interventions; 8. The development and testing of a practical guide on children's rights produced in collaboration with the Ministry of Basic Education and the Ministry of Social Affairs, in order to help primary school teachers give children knowledge about their rights (and how to assert them). 64. Moreover, this intersectorality, in the opinion of all, has favored synergy, collaboration and complementarity between the interventions implemented and the components of the PKU. To be convinced of this, the arguments of these interlocutors given below prove to be very appropriate: _ “The intersectorality of the implementation of the KOICA project interventions was a reality to such an extent that even external stakeholders no longer made specific use of any section in charge of a component but rather referred to the entire UNICEF team as the synergy, collaboration and complementarity of the teams mobilized by the institution was operational within the framework of the implementation of the project [22]_ _”. _ 22 Individual Interview, UNICEF, Bertoua, Eastern Region Page | 17 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ _ “During the implementation of the KOICA project, as an education actor, I had to work with both the WASH section and the Education section of UNICEF. Our work/coordination sessions were often joint rather than specific [23]_ _”. _ 65. Concerning community engagement, it was understood within the framework of the evaluated project, the establishment within the target communities of the PKU, of Community Based Organizations (CBO) with a view not only to guarantee effectiveness, but also to ensure the sustainability of the implemented interventions. These OBCs are made up of RECOPE, COSIFE, Village Savings and Credit Associations (AVEC) and Village Water User Associations (AVUE). Moreover, the documentary exploitation made it possible to note that community engagement turned out to be the most promising strategy insofar as most of the results come from the mobilization of community leaders, even if it deserves to be strengthened. Indeed, the evaluation noted that this community engagement strategy made it possible to boost birth registrations, systems for reporting cases of child abuse and, in turn, an increase in school demand. It was also noted during this evaluation exercise that the community mechanisms of CBOs strongly contributed to promoting the synergy and collaboration of the stakeholders of the interventions linked to the components of the KOICA project (2021-2024). Thus, for example, the education awareness campaigns carried out by COSIFE have generated the commitment of parents to send their children to school. _ “The success of this project is based on the fact that it integrated young people into community mechanisms for monitoring registrations and student attendance. These young people voluntarily help the program to raise awareness among parents and students about the importance of education and especially that of young girls [24]_ _”. _ 66. Finally, with regard to partnerships within the framework of the PKU, it was implemented through: (i) the establishment of the consortium constituted by the NGOs/CSOs ADRA-ADELPA-ASAD having various skills but who decided to pool their specialized expertise in terms of implementation on themes which were not necessarily theirs at the start of the project implementation. A strategy which allowed these NGOs to offer in their area of ​​competence all the services to the different target groups according to the 3 sectors of intervention of the PKU; (ii) the partnership with the ASNU (Development of a joint work plan in 2023 with the ILO to address questions of synergy, collaboration and complementarity between the two agencies implementing 2 distinct projects under KOICA financing in the 6 municipalities concerned by the UNICEF-KOICA project; Coordination around questions of assistance to refugees with the UNHCR); (ii) the partnership with the 6 municipalities in the intervention zones which enabled the Mayors of these localities to take the lead in steering the project at the municipal level; and finally, (iv) partnership with sectoral administrations to ensure technical compliance and compliance with regulations in this area. _ “The partnership with the municipalities was very decisive in the context of the interventions implemented by the project, for example, their involvement allowed for example a tax exemption over a certain period for the beneficiaries of professional training who have settled [25]_ _”. _ _ “Coordination at the local level by Mayors has enabled more and more of them to know more about our skills as NGOs, which has ensured better visibility as well as better knowledge of our activities at the local level [26]_ _”. _ Preliminary conclusion 67. Coherence (QE1): The evaluation notes that PKU interventions are consistent with the vision of the country program which consists of _ “ensuring that every child in Cameroon – girls and boys, including adolescents – survives, thrives, learns, is protected and develops their full potential, thus contributing to the growth of human capital and social well-being” _. Furthermore, the interventions of the evaluated project were complementary with those of numerous partners working in the target areas, particularly in the sectors of: (i) protection (birth registration and referral of GBV cases with NGOs such as NRC, HURDA and the Red Cross), and (ii) WASH (development of water points, revitalization of the municipal water fund and establishment of an efficient water management system by NGOs such as Care Intl, NRC, HELP and Solidarité Intl). Moreover, to a large extent (98.5%), the EPEL has established this coherence and complementarity of PKU interventions with the Components of the country program and the interventions of other partners including the ASNU (Para 55-59). Based on EPEL data, individual interviews, FGDs and observations carried out, the evaluation highlighted a strong appreciation of the project's capacity to contribute through its implemented strategies to: (i) synergy, collaboration and complementarities between its different components (Protection, Education, WASH); (ii) and to offer a package of services to different target groups. Thus, the EPEL corroborates this observation to the extent that 57.6% affirm it to a great extent (Para 60 66). > 23 Individual Interview, DREB, Bertoua, Eastern Region > 24 FGD, OBC, COSIFE of Mbal, Municipality of Garoua-Boulai > 25 Individual interview, IMC, implementing NGO, Eastern Region > 26 Individual Interview, ADELPARA, implementing NGO, Eastern Region Page | 18 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 6.2 Effectiveness Evaluation criterion question EQ2: To what extent were the expected results of the KOICA project achieved and did they contribute to progress towards the strategic outcomes sought by its implementation? Sub-question to the evaluation criterion - 2.1. To what extent were the planned results achieved for all target groups equally? - 2.2. To what extent did the factors (internal and external to UNICEF) favor the achievement or non-achievement of the expected results or not? 2.3. To what extent has the implementation of the KOICA project made it possible to reduce the weaknesses of deconcentrated/decentralized structures in reaching the most vulnerable sections (refugee population, people living with disabilities and those from deprived social sectors) of the population, especially children? - 2.4. To what extent were project monitoring tools used by UNICEF as well as government authorities to track results consistently and inform programming? ==> picture [186 x 128] intentionally omitted <== Image 6.2: _Donation of motorcycles to civil status secretaries and municipal volunteers_ ==> picture [136 x 123] intentionally omitted <== ==> picture [162 x 121] intentionally omitted <== Image 6.3: _Construction of new ones_ Image 6.4: _RECOPE member and the village chief recording the new toilets for girls in Ounsounou, births in Ketté Commune of Ouli_ Source: Photos taken from the KOICA donor report, May 2023 In order to ensure the triangulation of analyzes relating to the effectiveness criterion, 5 types of data sources are used, namely: 1. The documentary review covering the documents (Project Document, UNICEF Country Program) and the annual implementation reports of the evaluated UNICEF/NGO project between 2021 and 2024; 2. Semi-structured interviews in the 4 target municipalities with representatives of the structures - government/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated program, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project; 3. The Online Perception Survey among representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated project, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project as well as indirect rights holders; 4. Focus Group Discussion (FGD) with representatives of CBOs and rights holders (men, women and children aged 12-17) from the communities served by the project (host communities and refugees); 5. The H evaluation among children (girls and boys) under 12 years old living in the project’s target communities. 68. It is useful to re-specify (_Cf._ Chapter 5, paragraph 47) that for the effectiveness criterion, the analysis relating to the performance of the PKU Results/Product indicators is based on a rating system with a qualitative scale with 2 values: a "Satisfactory" threshold, if at least 80% of the objective in terms of assigned performance is achieved and an "Unsatisfactory" threshold (if the assigned performance achieved is lower at 80%). As a reminder, this analysis of the performance of the PKU Results/Product indicators is based on the Completion Rate (TA) which indicates the progress made in achieving the planned target at the end of implementation. 69. Also, it should be noted that for the analyzes which will follow in this section, the overall performance for each of the Results/Products of the evaluated project is obtained by making the ratio of the number of Results/Products indicators judged to have satisfactory performance to the total number of Results/Products indicators concerned. If this ratio is less than 80%, then the overall performance of the Results/Outputs will be judged “insufficient/unsatisfactory” and otherwise, the overall performance is judged “sufficient/satisfactory”. 6.2.1 Achievement of PKU Planned Results/Outputs _6.2.1.1 Level of achievement of PKU Results_ 70. The analysis of the project results indicators was carried out on the basis of updating the information contained in the results framework of the evaluated project. On this basis, the evaluation concludes that performance is satisfactory through the achievement of performance levels deemed satisfactory for Results 3. On the other hand, Results 1, 2 and 4 were judged to be of unsatisfactory performance (Appendix A.19). Page | 19 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ _6.2.1.2 Level of achievement of Products by PKU sector_ ❖ _Child Protection Sector_ 71. The evaluation of the effectiveness of the PKU in the area of child protection consisted of the analysis of three (03) components including: (i) protection and respect for the rights of children and adolescents; (ii) registration and availability of a birth certificate for children and (iii) access to quality psychosocial and protection services. 72. The analysis of the indicators for Products 1.1 and 1.2 (Appendix A.20) of the “_Promotion and respect for the rights of children and adolescents_” Component allows us to conclude that there is satisfactory performance for this Component based on the achievement of all the Performance Product indicators. 73. Likewise, for the Component “_Registration and availability of a birth certificate for children_”, a satisfactory level of performance is perceived by the achievement of all the Product indicators of satisfactory performance for Products 2.1 and 2.2. In the same vein, the evaluation concludes that the component _“Psychosocial support and child protection service”_ has performed satisfactorily through the achievement of product indicators 3.1 and 3.2 deemed to be satisfactory. 74. On the other hand, based on the EPEL (Graph 6.2), the evaluation notes that the Component “_Promotion and respect for the rights of children and adolescents_” is the one where the participants believe that the results have been achieved to a large extent (39%) compared to the components relating to birth registration (28%) and access to psychosocial services (21%). Also, the evaluation notes that the SSP Component is the one where participants believe the most (12%) that the results have been achieved to a low extent. ==> picture [515 x 140] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- Figure 6.2: Extent to which planned PKU outcomes were achieved for each component of the child protection sector Access to quality psychosocial and child protection services 19.7 45.9 34.4 childhood Registration and availability of birth certificate for 13.6 43.9 42.4 children Promotion and respect for the rights of children and 1.5 39.4 59.1 adolescent girls 0.0 20.0 40.0 60.0 80.0 100.0 120.0 To a small extent To a medium extent To a large extent ----- End of picture ----- Source: Data from the online survey (EPEL) as of 11/12/2024 75. The results presented above are corroborated by data from interviews with stakeholders on their perception of the achievement of the planned results of the evaluated project. Indeed, the people interviewed demonstrate notable progress in the areas of establishing birth certificates and reporting cases of abuse (GBV and early marriages) as highlighted in Table 1.2 as well as for the Impact criterion of this report. _ “The phenomenon of early marriage was significant but through the intervention of the KOICA project, we can say that the phenomenon of early marriages has declined considerably and is on the verge of disappearing, easy access to the birth certificate is also working wonders[27]”. _ _ “The establishment of birth certificates has been made easier because as soon as a woman gives birth, even at home, we carry out the birth certificate for the child... Thanks to RECOPE's awareness-raising efforts, all women draw up birth certificates for their children upon delivery[28]”. _ Box 6.2: _Contribution to the satisfactory performance of the “Psychosocial support and child protection services” component by_ _the capacity building of 140 social workers by the PKU in collaboration with the MINAS at the regional and central levels_ This capacity building had the general objective of improving the skills of Social Workers at level 2 and level 3 concerning Social Workers of all categories in the Eastern Region and Adamaoua with the aim of providing an effective response to the problems faced by the various vulnerable people, particularly children, in these two Regions. This training gave rise to exchanges of experiences and discussions of practical cases while focusing on respect for ethical and professional standards within the structures providing these services. Considering the pre- and post-test results, it is noted that the training sessions made it possible to maximize the prerequisites acquired in the field by the stakeholders: |Training center|Pre-test of participants (%)|Post-test of participants (%)|Training center|Pre-test of participants (%)|Post-test of participants (%)| |---|---|---|---|---|---| |Batouri|68.9|70.9|Abong Mbang|85.71|96.3| |Garoua-Boulai|60 (Level2)|69.13 (Level2)|Bertoua|78.12|86.4| > 27 FGD, Young girls from the host community of Sandji 1, Commune of Kentzou. > 28 FGD, OBC, RECOPE, Community of Gbiti, Commune of Ketté. Page | 20 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ ❖ _WASH sector_ 76. The evaluation of the achievement of indicators in the field of WASH allows us to conclude that this sector is performing satisfactory (Appendix A.21). This result results from the improvement in coverage in the provision of a safe drinking water source, the establishment of water management mechanisms, the increase in the number of schools with access to WASH services as well as the number of municipal actors trained in WASH services. An observation that can be illustrated as in the following remarks: _“Before our populations drank water from the backwater and this created illnesses for us, today thanks to the good water offered by the KOICA drilling we have improved health[29]”._ 77. However, the evaluation notes that these results contrast with the assessment of stakeholders regarding the achievement of these results. Indeed, a significant proportion of respondents (45%) believe that these results have been achieved to a low extent, compared to only 33% who believe to a large extent that the planned results of the project in the water, hygiene and sanitation sector have been effectively achieved (Figure 6.3). It should be noted that this observation could be explained by the fact that interventions in terms of infrastructure for this sector only affected 39 villages out of 254 (i.e. 15%) of the six communes. Moreover, the evaluation noted a negative perception among certain people interviewed in relation to the achievement of results in the field of WASH to the extent that in certain localities, populations continue to travel “_ long distances to have drinking water and it is really painful. UNICEF agents had promised us to dig a borehole here but have since not done so[30].” _ Figure 6.3: Extent to which planned PKU results are achieved in the WASH sector ==> picture [354 x 136] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- To a large extent 32.8 To an average extent 22.4 To a small extent 44.8 0.0 5.0 10.0 15.0 20.0 25.0 30.0 35.0 40.0 45.0 50.0 Source: Online survey data (EPEL) as of 11/12/2024 ----- End of picture text ----- ❖ _Education sector_ 78. Analysis of data relating to the education sector reveals planned output indicators that have all been largely achieved (Appendix A.22). Thus, the evaluation concluded that this sector was performing satisfactorily, associated with the increase in reception capacity in schools, the provision of school kits and the capacity building of teaching staff. _“With KOICA, our children have school materials which have been distributed since the project was implemented[31]”._ 79. Based on the EPEL results (Figure 6.4), the evaluation notes that almost one in two (47.7%) respondents affirmed that the planned results of the project in the education sector were achieved to a large extent. Figure 6.4: Extent to which PKU planned results are achieved in the education sector ==> picture [355 x 171] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- To a large extent 47.7 To an average extent 47.7 To a small extent 4.6 0.0 10.0 20.0 30.0 40.0 50.0 60.0 Source: Online survey data (EPEL) as of 11/12/2024 ----- End of picture text ----- > 29 FGD, OBC, RECOPE of Mbal village, Garoua-Boulai commune > 30 FGD, men, Host community, Yoko Siré, commune of Garoua-Boulai > 31 FDG, OBC, COSIFE, Community of Mbilé, Commune of Kentzou Page | 21 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 80. The results presented above are corroborated by data from interviews with stakeholders questioned on their perception of the achievement of the planned results of the project. Indeed, the people interviewed testify to notable progress in areas relating to awareness-raising on the schooling of children and the construction/equipment of classrooms. _ “Before, the children were not too interested in school, but there is an increase in student attendance. Cash transfers have made it possible to ensure the education of adolescent children[32].” _ _ “The two classrooms and the 60 benches that were built by UNICEF allow us to be more comfortable and fewer in number in a classroom[33]”. _ 6.2.2 Factors (internal and external to UNICEF) which favored the achievement or failure of the expected results or not 81. Several factors (internal or external) contributed to the achievement of the PKU's objectives. The evaluation listed and categorized these factors in the table in Annex A.23. Some illustrative remarks on some of these factors are highlighted below: _ “The coaching was local: the implementation actors (ADRA) lived with us, they ate what we ate, they were there[34]”. _ _ “The AGR that we received allowed us to produce an agricultural song which allowed us to cover certain additional expenses relating to the schooling of our children[35]”. _ 82. Also, in addition to these factors favoring the achievement of results, the evaluation also identified factors which hindered the achievement of the objectives planned by the project, whether internal or external to UNICEF (Appendix A.24). These illustrative remarks of some of these factors are highlighted below: _ “The means of transport is very limited, which reduces our frequent trips to localities because we have only one motorbike to cover 41 villages each month[36]”. _ _ “We only had 3 motorbikes to cover 41 villages: one in TIMAGOLO, one in GBITI and one for Ketté[37]”. _ - 6.2.3 Reduction of the weaknesses of deconcentrated/decentralized structures in reaching the most vulnerable sectors (refugee population, people living with disabilities and those from deprived social sectors) of the population, especially children 83. The evaluation notes that the implementation of the PKU contributed to reducing the weaknesses of deconcentrated/decentralized structures in reaching the most vulnerable groups. Thus, in compliance with the principle of _“leave no one behind”,_ the implementation of the project through a holistic and multisectoral approach made it possible to effectively combat the deprivations suffered by children in these localities by providing them with access to basic social services to improve their well-being. 84. The evaluation notes that one of the key elements which significantly contributed to reducing the weaknesses of decentralized and deconcentrated structures is the establishment of community networks (RECOPE, COSIFE, COGES) which worked in raising awareness and communicating for behavior change in the project intervention sectors. This mobilization of additional human resources mainly consisted of community visits to inform, train and educate parents, young people and community leaders on the promotion of children's rights, particularly the fight against child marriage, birth registration on the one hand, and good practices in WASH on the other. 85. Specifically, in the education sector for example, the evaluation noted that following the implementation of the project, the improvement in educational results results from the effectiveness of a comprehensive approach which simultaneously addresses various aspects. Indeed, by combining the capacity building of teachers in terms of psychosocial support and conflict/disaster risk reduction, monitoring of enrollment and school attendance, the implementation of a clinical educational supervision model, the provision of school kits, as well as the manufacturing and distribution of benches, significant improvements were observed in school performance indicators. What was lacking in the structures in charge of the education sector in the areas of intervention of the evaluated project. This holistic approach not only improved the learning environment, but also ensured the safety and well-being of students, resulting in increased enrolment, attendance, retention and overall academic performance. 86. Faced with the gaps observed in the area of birth registration, the establishment of advanced civil registration posts in health facilities and the monitoring of births at the community level by RECOPE have considerably increased birth registration rates in underserved areas, as indicated by some stakeholders > 32 FDG, OBC, COSIFE, Community of Mbilé, Commune of Kentzou. > 33 Evaluation H, Male children from the refugee community of Yoko-Siré, Commune of Garoua-Boulai > 34 Individual interview, municipal stakeholder in charge of civil status, Municipality of Ketté > 35 FDG, OBC, COSIFE, Community of Mbal, Municipality of Garoua-Boulai > 36 Individual interview, municipal stakeholder of Boubara in charge of awareness-raising, Municipality of Ketté 37 Individual interview, municipal stakeholder in charge of civil status, Municipality of Ketté Page | 22 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ during interviews with key informants. These words from this interlocutor also illustrate this situation well to the extent that he declares that _ the distance to complete the birth certificates of my 2 children was no longer a problem because thanks to the intervention of the project by providing community leaders this facilitated access to the service in charge of establishing birth certificates[38]. _ Interoperability between health and civil registration systems has made it possible to streamline the issuance of birth certificates, while the commitment of religious and community leaders has facilitated the identification and declaration of unregistered births. Field observations (District Hospital; Protestant Hospital) made it possible to assess the effectiveness of the operation of the advanced civil status posts in Garoua-Boulai. 87. In addition to these observed weaknesses of deconcentrated and decentralized structures in providing quality services to beneficiary populations, the PKU also contributed to filling the gaps in terms of equipment and supplies. The evaluation noted that this contribution of the project in reducing the weaknesses of decentralized structures resulted in: (i) The provision of computer equipment to those responsible for social centers and civil status centers (main and secondary, particularly in the commune of Garoua-Boulai); (ii) Provision of rolling stock (notably motorcycles) to facilitate the movement of stakeholders in communities; (iii) Equipping with supplies, particularly registers in civil status offices and outposts. 6.2.4 Use of project monitoring tools by UNICEF as well as government authorities to monitor results consistently and inform programming 88. Several monitoring tools have been developed and used by both UNICEF and government authorities with the aim of monitoring results consistently and informing decision-making. Among the tools used by UNICEF to monitor project achievements and inform decision-making, we can cite: (i) UNICEF financial management tool; (ii) Monthly monitoring meetings between the actors of the Consortium and UNICEF to monitor the progress of the project and to make possible readjustments and assess bottlenecks; (iii) the reporting framework every 6 months with the aim of having project traceability; (iv) programmatic visits/visits with Consortium actors, municipal and sectoral stakeholders. 89. In addition to these tools, several other tools were developed by certain consortium leaders, notably ADELPA, with the support of UNICEF and the sectoral administrations concerned (MINAS, MINPROFF) and, in the opinion of the stakeholders interviewed, made it possible to strengthen the results resulting from the implementation of the project. These tools include: (i) the trainer's guide on positive parenting; (ii) birth registration forms developed and made available by BUNEC to community structures; (iii) the simplified guide to psychosocial care; (iv) the concept note on Cash Transfer. It should be mentioned that many of the stakeholders had to emphasize the fact that these tools have contributed to a large extent to improving not only the expected results of the implementations but also and above all the reporting of the interventions. _ “The monitoring tools made available by UNICEF as well as those developed by ourselves in agreement with the sectors proved to be very useful in decision-making throughout the implementation of the project[39]” _ Preliminary conclusion 90. Effectiveness (EQ2): Based on the completeness of the information provided in the results framework of the evaluated project, the evaluation resulted in satisfactory performance for Result 3 in relation to the _ number of children and adolescents benefiting from quality psychosocial and child protection services _ as well as for all Products in the child protection, education and WASH sector. This observation of satisfactory performance is corroborated by the EPEL data, which demonstrate to a large extent (21%, 32.8% and 47%) the achievement of the planned results respectively for the “Psychosocial support and child protection services” component, the WASH and Education sectors. Also, this evaluative exercise made it possible to list the main factors, both internal and external to UNICEF, which favored or failed to achieve the results (Para 87-110). With regard to the project's contribution to reducing the weaknesses of decentralized and decentralized structures, the evaluation noted as a priority the establishment of the community networks that made this possible. Indeed, the people interviewed attribute most of the achievements of the evaluated project to the implementation of holistic and multisectoral approaches which enabled local administrations to continue to carry out their sovereign missions relating to the provision of basic social services to affected populations. (Para: 101-105). Finally, the evaluation noted the use of several tools that made it possible to monitor project achievements and inform decision-making. In addition to these tools, several other tools have been developed by the consortium managers with the support of UNICEF and sectoral administrations. (Para: 106-107). > 38 FDG, OBC, COSIFE, Community of Mbal, Municipality of Garoua-Boulai > 39 Individual Interview, ADELPA, implementing NGO, Eastern Region Page | 23 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 6.3 Efficiency Evaluation criterion question EQ3: To what extent did the KOICA project have sufficient resources (human, material and financial) in quality and quantity to achieve the expected results? Sub-question to the evaluation criterion - 3.1 To what extent were financial resources, human resources and supplies sufficient (quantity), adequate (quality) to achieve the results? Are there any uncovered gaps? - 3.2. To what extent is the use of financial resources for each of the components/intervention sectors of the KOICA project in line with the level of performance of the results achieved for each of these said components, taking into account the planned/mobilized budget envelope? - 3.3. To what extent did management factors improve or reduce the efficiency of the KOICA project? - 3.4. To what extent have partnerships and synergies (with municipal and sectoral administrations, NGOs, United Nations Agencies) made it possible to reduce implementation costs and maximize the achievement of the best results? - 4 types of data sources are used for triangulation for analyzes relating to the efficiency criterion, namely: 1. The documentary review covering the documents (Project Document, UNICEF Country Program) and the annual implementation reports of the evaluated UNICEF/NGO project between 2021 and 2024; 2. Semi-structured interviews in the 4 target municipalities with representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated program, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project; 3. The Online Perception Survey (EPEL) among representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated project, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project as well as among indirect rights holders; 4. Focus Group Discussion (FGD) with representatives of CBOs and rights holders (men, women and children aged 12-17) from the communities served by the project (host communities and refugees). Source: Photos from the KOICA project evaluation data collection, November 2024 ==> picture [237 x 146] intentionally omitted <== ==> picture [262 x 145] intentionally omitted <== _ Image 6.6: Latrine block, Mbal Village, Commune of Garoua-Boulai_ _ Image 6.6: Drilling, Commune of Kentzou, Village Lolo_ 6.3.1 Analysis of the provision of financial resources in sufficient quantity and in a timely manner to achieve PKU results 91. The evaluation notes that for the implementation of the PKU, the mobilization of financial resources allowed a contribution of 7,931,830 USD divided into administrative costs (2,927,276 USD) and implementation costs (5,004,554 USD) over the period under review for a budget forecast initially estimated at 7,996,000 USD[40]. The execution of this budget was based on a disbursement plan staggered annually and broken down as follows for each Sector of the project: (i) 3,154,504 USD (63%) for the strategic axis “Child Protection”, (ii) 650,000 USD (13%) for the strategic axis “WASH” and 1,200,050 USD (24%) for the strategic axis “Education”. However, at the end of 2024, budget mobilization was optimal and stood at USD 7,996,000. 92. However, despite this strong budgetary mobilization (100%), it should still be noted that the implementation of the project was characterized by a low level of execution of financial resources. Indeed, the analysis of financial data from the financial report[41] made available to the evaluation team reveals low execution levels of around 46%, 51% and 56% respectively for the first, second and third year of project implementation. Significant delays were recorded in the selection process of NGOs and subcontractors in 2021, justifying the low level of budget execution this year as well as the low level of achievement of the planned results of the project. In 2024, the level of budget execution increased to 90%, highlighting a strong increase in the budgetary implementation of the PKU. > 41 KOICA, Mid-term Financial and Performance Report, December 2023-30 May 2024, UNICEF 40 KOICA project document, UNICEF 2020 Page | 24 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 93. According to the stakeholders met, the financial resources mobilized were not adequate to achieve the results. Indeed, several bottlenecks linked to cumbersome procedures and significant delays in making these resources available were observed during the implementation of the project. These difficulties in the provision of financial resources hindered the implementation of some project interventions, including among others: (i) The finalization of the construction of e-containers in the schools of the “_Connect my school”_ initiative having slowed down the implementation of the complete package of the initiative; (ii) the implementation of WASH interventions linked to insufficient financial resources. _ “Many delays were observed in the disbursement of funds, particularly the 1st FACE. The cumulative delay was established at 6 months, which had a real impact on the implementation of activities. Some funds were made available towards the end of the project, which did not make it possible to truly monitor the project's achievements[42].” _ 6.3.2 Analysis of the provision of human resources in sufficient quantity and in a timely manner to achieve results of the PKU 94. On the other hand, the evaluation notes that the mobilization of human resources was based on a strategic choice with a view to achieving the planned results effectively. These human resources mainly came from decentralized and decentralized government structures concerned by the thematic axes of the KOICA project (2021-2024). These included: (i) heads of decentralized services from MINEDUB, MINAS, MINPROFF, MINEE, MINEPAT; (ii) decentralized administration officials, notably the CTDs; (iii) leaders of NGOs/OBCs (ADRA-ADELPA-ASAD Consortium; IMC); (iv) members of community networks (RECOPE, COSIFE, COGES); (v) municipal volunteers (Community Animators, social workers). 95. While there is no doubt that the human resources mobilized were adequate to provide effective responses to achieve the project results, the stakeholders met agree that they were in sufficient quantity in general and particularly with regard to the actors at the CBO levels. Indeed, the specificity of the PKU, unlike the interventions of other partners, lay in its capacity to cover all the target municipalities using the staff hired under the project. The following comments from this interlocutor are quite illustrative of this situation to the extent that he declares that _ in relation to the availability of human resources, I congratulate the KOICA project because, it is my first time to see a project which covers the entire district with a community facilitator assigned to each of the 41 villages of the commune[43]. _ However, mention was made of the low number of staff at the social center level (CESO) of the DRAS which does not specifically facilitate the processing of case management from the mechanism for reporting violations of children's rights at the level of this entity. Likewise, it is also noted that there is a low representativeness of the Centers for the Promotion of Women and the Family (CPFF) under MINPROFF at the department level in the context of professional training activities for the beneficiaries of the evaluated project. 96. However, the evaluation notes a mixed assessment of the provision of human resources to guarantee the achievement of the expected results. This was reflected in particular in the difficulty for community leaders and communal focal points to cover the vast areas that constituted their work area in the villages of assignment. This situation was noted in certain municipalities such as Ketté which does not have a municipal focal point and in Kentzou where only two municipal focal points were responsible for covering 38 villages, “which restricts the scope and effectiveness of interventions[44]”. _ These words from another speaker clearly summarize this situation: _ “It must be said that each of the organizations only had social workers and community mobilization officers as field staff and of course supported by community leaders and municipal volunteers. So in each municipality and for each organization, social workers and community mobilization officers, regardless of the tasks initially assigned to them, had to distribute the activities to be implemented because the coverage area was enormous and the implementation staff was not sufficient[45].” _ 97. Furthermore, the EPEL corroborates this state of affairs because 60% (3 out of 5 participants) of those questioned affirmed to an average extent that human resources were mobilized sufficiently and adequately to achieve the results. When on the other hand 40% (2 out of 5 participants) affirm that this situation is perceptible to a small extent. 6.3.3 Analysis of the provision of supplies in sufficient quantity and in a timely manner to achieve the results of the PKU 98. With regard to the mobilization of supplies as part of the implementation of the PKU, all stakeholders agree to welcome the efforts of UNICEF in making these resources available. Indeed, the deployment of supplies consisted of: (i) equipping the offices of the civil status centers (office furniture and civil status registers); (ii) The allocation of > 42 Individual interview, Consortium resource person (ADRA-ADELPA-ASAD), Eastern Region 43 Individual interview, municipal stakeholder in charge of civil status, Municipality of Ketté 44 Idem > 45 Individual interview, Consortium resource person (ADRA-ADELPA-ASAD), Eastern Region Page | 25 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ social centers through provision of computer equipment; (iii) Provision of rolling stock, particularly motorcycles, to municipal focal points and municipal facilitators; (iv) Distribution of school kits to students. 99. These various supplies granted by UNICEF to those implementing the project made it possible, on the one hand, to strengthen the supply of services and, on the other hand, to monitor the progress of the project's achievements. However, the stakeholders met testified to the difficulty of these supplies adequately meeting the increased needs of the population. This situation was perceptible in the context of the distribution of school kits or even in the context of the breakdown of civil status registers, itself due to the increase in demand for birth certificates both in the outposts of GarouaBoulai and Ketté and in the main centers. 100. Indeed, the EPEL reveals that only 20% of those questioned, or 1 in 5 participants, attest to a large extent that the provision of supplies was provided in sufficient quantity and adequately. On the other hand, 40% of those questioned, or 2 out of 5 participants, say this to an average or low extent. 6.3.4 Use of financial resources in line with the level of performance of the results achieved, taking into account the budget envelope mobilized and spent 101. The efficiency analysis undertaken here focused on the adequacy between the results obtained, the resources mobilized and used for each Sector/Product of the PKU. This analysis is aimed at knowing whether the costs of the interventions have been adequately optimized according to the achievement of the expected results by Sectors/Products. It was used to understand the cost-efficiency analysis of the activities undertaken by the project in relation to the results achieved by Sectors/Products. 102. It is useful to reiterate that this analysis of efficiency based on 8 scenarios (_Cf._ Chapter 5, paragraph 47), resulted in a favorable/adequate efficiency ratio between the performance achieved, the resources allocated and used with regard to the “WASH” and “Education” Sectors corresponding to _Situation 1_ and _Situation 2_ respectively (Appendix A.25 ) _ . _ 103. Concerning the examination of the Products of the evaluated project (2021-2024), it reveals a favorable/adequate efficiency ratio between the performance achieved, the resources allocated and used for Products 1.1 (_Situation 1)_, 1.2 (_Situation 2)_, 2.1 (_Situation 2)_ and 2.2 (_Situation 3)_ as well as Products 3.1 (_Situation 2)_, 3.2 (_Situation 2)_, 4.1 (_Situation 1)_, 5.1 (_Situation 2)_ and Products 5.2 (_Situation 2)_. Products 1.1 (_Situation 5)_, 1.2 (_Situation 8)_, 2.1 (_Situation 5)_ and finally 2.2 (_Situation 6)_. 6.3.5 Improvement/reduction of PKU efficiency by management factors 104. The governance and management of the PKU benefited from the involvement of several stakeholders with varied and diverse skills coupled with the implementation of innovative strategies. To this end, several management factors were identified as having contributed to improving the efficiency of the project, notably: 1. The intersectorality and interoperability of project implementation strategies: These strategies enabled the production of guides in the protection and education sectors, thus promoting better ownership of the project as well as better monitoring of achievements by stakeholders. These strategies also contributed to strengthening the public-private partnership in terms of professional training to combat early and forced child marriage. 2. The positioning of the project in the civil status coordination platforms at the regional level: which made it possible to bring together actions between the different stakeholders, thanks to the intersectorality implemented as part of the implementation of the project; 3. Detailed planning coupled with effective coordination between stakeholders: monthly meetings or biannual meetings which brought together the heads of the Consortium, UNICEF and the heads of the decentralized sectoral administrations involved in the implementation of the project, made it possible to assess the state of implementation and identify the main bottlenecks. 4. The relaxation of financial management procedures by UNICEF: which contributed to improving the financial governance of the project as well as reducing the time taken to make resources available. 5. The strong involvement of those responsible for the Municipal Technical Services (STC) during monitoring visits to WASH works: This involvement made it possible to identify sustainable management mechanisms for the water service, specific to the municipality and adapted to socio-economic realities; 6. Implementation of community mechanisms within the project: They have proven their effectiveness in birth registration, the promotion of social change and, consequently, community feedback. 105. On the other hand, the absence of a financial management system by certain administrations was perceived as limiting the management of the evaluated project, having reduced its efficiency. Indeed, it was noted that certain decentralized administrations did not have an account and were forced to use the DRAS account. 106. Moreover, the EPEL data are mixed with regard to whether or not project efficiency is improved by management factors. Indeed, while 40% of those questioned (i.e. 2 out of 5 participants) regarding management factors believe that these factors made it possible to a large extent to improve the efficiency of the project, the same proportion affirms that these factors were only to an average extent. On the other hand, 40% (2 out of 5 participants) and 20% (1 out of 5 participants) of stakeholders estimate this to a medium extent and a low extent respectively. Page | 26 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 6.3.6 Extent to which partnerships and synergies (with municipal and sectoral administrations, NGOs, United Nations Agencies) have made it possible to reduce implementation costs and maximize the achievement of the best results 107. As part of this evaluation, the existence of the NGO/CSO consortium (ADRA-ADELPA-ASAD) as well as the implementation approach with the contribution of the CTDs have undoubtedly made it possible to reduce costs execution of the PKU. 108. Thus, the existence of the NGO/CSO consortium (ADRA-ADELPA-ASAD) has enabled this strategy to achieve economies of scale to the extent that the work that could have been entrusted to different entities depending on their sectors of activity was done in the name of the consortium with a single entity per municipality. In doing so, the structural costs per implementing entity were only considered for the consortium only, for example. 109. Also, by involving the staff of the Mayors' STC (which comes from the budget of this structure) in the context of monitoring and supervising the implementation of the project's interventions, the people met like this interlocutor agree in thinking that _ this strategy of implementing the interventions necessarily allowed reductions in the execution costs of the evaluated project although it cannot be formally quantified _ [46]. Preliminary conclusion 110. Efficiency (EQ3): As part of the efficiency analysis, the evaluation concluded that if the reality of the mobilization of resources (human, financial and material) no longer needs to be demonstrated, it was nevertheless not adequate for achieving the expected results throughout the implementation of the PKF. Thus, despite strong budgetary mobilization (99.2%) which made it possible to make available USD 7,931,830 over the period evaluated for a budget forecast of USD 7,996,000, the execution of this budget reveals low execution levels of around 44%, 51% and 56% respectively between 2021 and 2023. Also, the evaluation stakeholders deplore, among other things, delays in the availability of financial resources and the low availability of material resources relating to means of travel to ensure the implementation/coordination of interventions (91-97). The use of financial resources was also assessed through the analysis of the adequacy between the results obtained and the resources mobilized/used for the Sectors/Products of the evaluated project. At the end of this analysis, it is concluded that there is a favorable/adequate efficiency ratio between the performance achieved, the resources allocated and used with regard to the “WASH” and “Education” Sectors corresponding to Situation 1 and Situation 2 respectively. Also, a favorable/adequate efficiency ratio is concluded for all PKU products: Products 1.1 (Situation 1), 1.2 (Situation 2), 2.1 (Situation 2) and 2.2 (Situation 3) as well as Products 3.1 (Situation 2), 3.2 (Situation 2), 4.1 (Situation 1), 5.1 (Situation 2) and Products 5.2 (Situation 2). Products 1.1 (Situation 5), 1.2 (Situation 8), 2.1 (Situation 5) and finally 2.2 (Situation 6) (Para: 98-103). The evaluation highlighted several management factors which were identified as having contributed to improving the efficiency of the project, including: (i) the intersectorality and interoperability of implementation strategies; (ii) the positioning of the project in the civil status coordination platforms at the regional level; (iii) detailed planning coupled with effective coordination between stakeholders; (iv) the strong involvement of STC managers during monitoring visits to WASH works; (v) the implementation of the project’s community mechanisms (Para: 104-106). Finally, the evaluation noted the existence of the NGO/CSO consortium (ADRA-ADELPA-ASAD) and the implementation approach with the contribution of Town Halls as favorable to reducing the costs of implementing the KOICA project (Para: 107-109). 46 Individual interview, Consortium resource person (ADRA-ADELPA-ASAD), Eastern Region Page | 27 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 6.4 Sustainability Evaluation criterion question EQ4: To what extent has the KOICA project generated ownership at the national and community level or is it in the process of being so, so as to (i) ensure the continuity of its effects beyond the duration of its implementation and (ii) influence in the long term policies and programs for access to basic social services (education and WASH) and child protection (fight against child marriage and birth registration)? Sub-question to the evaluation criterion - 4.1. To what extent have the KOICA project interventions made it possible to establish strategic partnerships or are in the process of doing so in order to guarantee the sustainability of the achievements? What established and potential partnerships for the future are most appropriate to achieve the expected results? 4.2. To what extent have the communities in the areas covered by the project and the institutional partners taken ownership of the project's interventions and its achievements? What are the main factors or measures taken that influenced the support of beneficiaries and other stakeholders? ==> picture [300 x 151] intentionally omitted <== Image 6.7: _Photovoltaic drinking water supply point, Commune of Kentzou, Village Mbouye_ Source: Photo from the KOICA project evaluation data collection, November 2024 In order to ensure triangulation in the context of analyzes relating to the accountability criterion, 5 types of data sources are used, namely: 1. The documentary review covering the documents (Project Document, UNICEF Country Program) and the annual implementation reports of the evaluated UNICEF/NGO project between 2021 and 2024; 2. Semi-structured interviews in the 4 target municipalities with representatives of the structures - government/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated program, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project; 3. The Online Perception Survey (EPEL) among representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated project, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project as well as among indirect rights holders; 4. Focus Group Discussion (FGD) with representatives of CBOs and rights holders (men, women and children aged 12-17) from the communities served by the project (host communities and refugees); 5. The H evaluation with children (girls and boys) living in the project’s target communities. 6.4.1 Mixed assessment of the PKU's capacity to create the most appropriate partnerships and to be able to establish potential partnerships with a view to achieving the expected results and guaranteeing the sustainability of the achievements 111. The evaluation highlighted a mixed assessment of the project's capacity to establish partnerships to achieve the expected results and to guarantee the sustainability of the achievements on the basis of EPEL data as well as those coming from individual interviews and FGDs carried out with stakeholders. 112. Thus, the EPEL data reflect this mixed assessment of the project's capacity to establish partnerships to achieve the expected results and to guarantee the sustainability of the achievements. Thus, 50% (2 out of 4 participants) of the stakeholders surveyed believe this state of affairs to a large extent; conversely, 50% (2 out of 4 participants) of the other stakeholders are of this opinion but to a small extent. 113. During the interviews and FGDs carried out with the various stakeholders, the implementation of the KOICA project evaluated was seen as contributing to the creation of the most appropriate partnerships with a view to achieving the expected results and guaranteeing the sustainability of the achievements, to the extent that it allowed (i) the establishment of the NGO Consortium constituted by ADRA-ADELPAASAD; (ii) the establishment of platforms for consultation and coordination of interventions implemented both at the municipal level and centrally in the Eastern region; (iii) the establishment of official partnership agreements between UNICEF and the 6 Town Halls covered by the project through its social policies section as well as public-private partnership partnerships through NGOs (IMC in this case) as part of the professional training of beneficiaries at the local level Page | 28 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ ; and, (iv) the development of a joint work plan in 2023 with the ILO[47] addressing issues of synergy, collaboration and complementarity between the 2 agencies implementing 2 distinct projects under KOICA funding. 114. Also, the people met recognize the PKU's ability: (a) to have brought together synergies and efforts with regard to the 3 sectors of intervention taken into account in order to achieve the expected results; (b) to position actors at the town hall level as essential partners in local development in its implementation and monitoring; (c) the creation of public-private partnership. _ “The PKU enabled the establishment of exchange platforms (consultation and coordination platform) both at the municipal level and at the central level in the Eastern region and promoted the adequacy of the interventions implemented with other stakeholders outside those concerned by the evaluated project (Red Cross, WHO, UNFPA, UNHCR, etc.). Being thus seen as an opportunity to establish possible future partnerships[48].” _ _ “Certain Town Halls as part of this partnership with UNICEF as part of the KOICA project have had to exempt from taxes for periods ranging from 3 to 6 months shops for beneficiaries of professional training _[49] _”. _ _ “Private companies under the supervision of the sectors (MINJEC, MINEPIA, MINEFOP) made discounts on the cost of training, supervised no less than twenty learners in their structures and also facilitated the placement of at least ten learners after the end of their training _[50] _”. _ 115. On the other hand, certain interviews and FGDs noted in the implementation of the project a missed opportunity to establish strategies that could guarantee the achievement of the expected results. Indeed, on the basis of its operating mode based on interventions at the municipal level, the evaluation did not perceive the involvement of actors at the departmental level. This is why some stakeholders met would have liked _ “better involvement of decentralized services at the departmental level in the implementation of the project so that, for example, the Departmental Delegation of Social Affairs (DDAS) plays the role of coordination, monitoring and evaluation. This would lead the Technical Operational Units (UTOs) to be better accountable[51] »_. 116. Finally, the evaluation noted the absence of specific strategic partnerships throughout the cycle of the evaluated project with the Ministries in charge of vocational training, agriculture and livestock and youth. These strategic partnerships are desirable when setting up clubs for listening to radio broadcasts by adolescents and professional training on various skills granted to beneficiaries of professional training. This lack of specific strategic partnerships with these three entities does not allow monitoring of these beneficiaries by the ministries concerned within an institutional framework. A situation which, if it had been taken into account, would have allowed more impact for monitoring of beneficiaries by professionals from these entities with a view to guaranteeing the sustainability of the achievements of the Project evaluated. - 6.4.2 Appropriation of PKU interventions and its achievements both by the communities in the areas covered and by institutional partners 117. For 4 of the 5 participants (80%) in the EPEL, mention is made of the existence to a large extent of appropriation of the project interventions as well as its achievements by the communities and institutional partners when only 1 of the 5 participants (20%) estimate this appropriation to a lesser extent. 118. On the basis of the interviews carried out (individual interviews and FGD), the evaluation also notes that the ownership by the institutional partners involved in the implementation of the project was evident to the extent that numerous activities (training of actors, distribution of kits, construction of WASH/school infrastructures) were carried out under the technical leadership of key actors (at the regional, departmental and municipal level) of the sectoral ministries and the Town Halls of the 3 Sectors of intervention of the project evaluated. Likewise, at the decentralized level, the evaluation noted technical appropriation by the town halls of the intervention areas. Moreover, for many stakeholders, _ the involvement at the technical level of local authorities and actors perceptible at the regional and departmental level was a guarantee of intersectorality during the implementation of interventions and allowed regular monitoring of interventions by these stakeholders themselves _ [52] . Also, this technical appropriation of the actors at the regional/departmental level of the 3 Sectors of the project was revealed through: - 1) The development of documents with a view to strengthening the project’s intervention sectors. Specifically to the child protection system, for example, a practical guide on children's rights has been produced in collaboration with the Ministry of Basic Education and the Ministry of Social Affairs, to help primary school teachers equip children with knowledge about their rights (and how to claim them); - 2) The choice of sites to build classrooms and water points; > 47 The agency is implementing the ILO-KOICA Project: Empowerment of women through capacity building of entrepreneurs in the agropastoral value chains (corn, cassava, chicken), social protection, fight against HIV-AIDS and GBV, in 16 communes in the East and Adamaoua. > 48 Individual interview, IMC, implementing NGO, Eastern Region > 49 Individual interview, IMC, implementing NGO, Eastern Region 50 Idem > 51 Individual interview, Departmental Stakeholder of Social Affairs, Municipality of Batouri. > 52 Individual Interview, ADELPA, implementing NGO, Eastern Region Page | 29 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ - 3) Strengthening the capacities of actors in the project intervention sectors. For example, in 2024[53], 57 teachers, 12 educational facilitators and 6 inspectors were trained in the use of the guide on children's rights in selected schools in the targeted municipalities; 119. For the particular case of Town Halls, _ if it had been noted that there was a wait-and-see attitude on the part of the municipalities in their involvement at the start of the implementation of the project evaluated, this attitude has greatly changed thanks to the adequacy of the interventions with the municipal development plans _ [54]. Indeed, one of the strengths of the PKU was to be in line with the law on decentralization which promotes local development. Thus, thanks to the project interventions and the strategies implemented (institutional partnership, intersectorality), the evaluation noted strong ownership of the project evaluated by the Town Halls. Since then, each of the 6 Town Halls covered by the project _ capitalizes on the benefits generated by the project, ensuring effective coordination of interventions and community involvement in accordance with the Municipal Development Plans (PCD)[55] _. As a result, each municipality runs a coordination platform chaired by the Mayor, which has to date enabled local authorities to be empowered to plan, execute and evaluate local development policies with an emphasis on the integration of children's rights [56]. As a result, by issuing municipal decrees, these Town Halls are increasingly taking ownership of the project's interventions. Thus, for example, the evaluation highlights 2 municipal decrees in Ouli, one on the creation of the Operational Unit for the Supply of the Drinking Water Service (COFSEP) and the other on the appointment of its staff, which were signed in February and April 2024 respectively. In March 2024, a procedures manual for the management of hydraulic infrastructure was adopted. Making the Municipality of Ouli a pioneer in this area because it has taken actions that lay solid foundations to ensure the sustainability of the management of drinking water supply installations in all of its 21 villages. This translates for example as follows: _ “The town hall during our awareness campaigns provided us with the necessary logistics for good awareness on the one hand and on the other hand after recording the acts we submit them to the town hall which in turn finalizes the rest of the steps. As far as the population is concerned, they have taken ownership of the project because we see their implications in the training and awareness raising offered to them[57].” _ 120. At the community level, ownership of the project and its achievements in the areas covered is clearly illustrated through the change in behavior that has begun to take shape in perceptions, practices and habits in the community. In fact, the evaluation noted that changes are observed in terms of community perception. 121. This ownership of the population has significantly contributed to developing a conducive environment and changes to issues of child protection and schooling as well as those related to the use of WASH infrastructure. As such, at the level of the population benefiting from KOICA project interventions over the period evaluated, the interviews carried out with stakeholders strongly highlighted the development of a conducive environment and changes relating to these issues around the following aspects: (i) a clear increase in demand and effective use of services in connection with the 3 Sectors of intervention of the project; (ii) spontaneous denunciation of existing cases of violation of children's rights within communities; (iii) the weak use of community arrangements relating to violations of children's rights; (iv) the increasingly pronounced recourse to institutional authorities through the use of the mechanism for reporting violations of children's rights put in place with a view to taking over for the most appropriate care depending on the case. Thus, the evaluation noted that community protection mechanisms now increasingly enjoy great confidence among the population. Victims of any type of violence and/or abuse are convinced that if they report it, the perpetrators will be punished by the law. In fact, for the evaluation, even if it is still too early to rejoice, the culture of speaking out is gaining ground in communities. For example, the increase in demand for birth registration is illustrated as follows: _ Since 2022, the demand for registers has gradually increased in the six municipalities where the KOICA project is implemented. The number of registers sent to civil status offices has practically doubled to meet the massive demand created by all the awareness raising and the work carried out by community mechanisms[58]. _ _ “Initially, before the launch of the KOICA project, we did not know that marrying our young girls before the age of 18 was a bad practice. This act has been practiced in our communities for a long time, but with the messages we have received over the past 2 years, we recognize that what we have done is not right. As community leaders, we must promote the rights of our young girls, ensure that they go to school and become educated girls.”[59] _ 122. However, although the appropriation of the project's interventions as well as its achievements by the decentralized sectoral administrations and the Town Halls is commendable, there remains the question of the integration of municipal volunteers among the town hall staff through their support within the PCDs. This integration among municipal staff will allow them to continue the support and ensure the sustainability of the achievements. > 53 KOICA project implementation report evaluated, UNICEF, May 2024 > 54 Individual Interview, UNICEF, Country Office, Yaoundé > 55 Individual Interview, ADELPA, implementing NGO, Eastern Region > 56 Individual Interview, UNICEF, Eastern Region > 57 FGD, OBC, RECOPE of Mbal village, Bétaré-Oya. > 58 KOICA final report, May 2024. > 59 FGD, OBC, RECOPE of Mbal village, Bétaré-Oya Page | 30 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 123. Also, the ownership of project interventions at the community level as well as institutional stakeholders was also illustrated within the framework of this evaluation exercise, through the use made of the project's achievements. Indeed, the field visits carried out during the data collection phase made it possible to observe better use of the allocations made by the project. The observations carried out in the 5 communes (Betaré-Oya, Garoua-Boulai, Mandjou, Kette and Kentzou) demonstrate effective but also efficient use of the infrastructure created, notably classrooms, toilets and water supply points in schools as well as advanced civil status posts. 124. However, the evaluation noted a mixed assessment regarding the conditions established which guarantee the sustainability of these achievements. Indeed, if there is no doubt about the establishment of mechanisms relating to the maintenance of latrines and water supply points by the communities to ensure the upkeep and maintenance of the achievements, it remains no less true with regard to the schools built. Indeed, an absence of fences was observed in the schools built by the project evaluated, particularly in Bindia in the commune of Mandjou and in Garoua-Boulai. This poses not only the problem of safety of learners but also that of the installations created in these schools. 6.4.3 Main factors or measures taken that influenced the support of beneficiaries and other stakeholders 125. Several factors led to the support of beneficiaries and other institutional stakeholders (sectoral, NGOs) in the vision advocated by the evaluated project for improved access to child protection, education and WASH services. Beyond their diversity, these membership factors generally include 5 essential points and refer to: (i) the relevance of the response provided by the interventions of the evaluated project to the needs expressed by the population; (ii) support for local authorities in charge of the 3 intervention sectors and town halls as well as community leaders in the intervention areas; (iii) the financial contribution to households receiving money transfers as well as to the OBCs (RECOPE) set up, to community leaders and municipal volunteers through support for the financing of viable IGAs; (iv) the implementation of awareness-raising and mass mobilization campaigns; (v) strengthening and anchoring all project interventions in community structures perceived as essential for the sustainability of the initiatives implemented; and, (vi) the organization of the competition called “child-friendly village competition”. 126. Specifically, the relevance of the needs met by the evaluated project was illustrated for example in the case of: (i) the Education Sector through the distribution of school kits to learners and capacity building through the training provided to teachers, educational facilitators and inspectors[60]; (ii) the Protection Sector by strengthening the capacities of 140 social workers as well as members of RECOPE. Thus, according to these interlocutors, by recognizing the usefulness of the opportunities made available to the populations by the evaluated project, they declare: _ “Through the distribution within schools by UNICEF and ADRA of buckets, soap and toothbrushes, regarding WASH, good hygiene practices have become a reflex for children _ [61] _”. _ _ “The sewing training allowed several young girls who did nothing in the village to learn a trade and today they are a great contribution to the community both in making clothes nearby and in their empowerment _ [62] _”. _ 127. As for the financial contribution[63] to the place the financial contribution to households benefiting from money transfers as well as the OBCs (RECOPE) set up, community facilitators and municipal volunteers through support for the financing of viable IGAs, it will have enabled the sustainability of the interventions within the framework of the 3 Sectors of the project. However, the evaluation highlighted the lack of financial support for the beneficiaries of the interventions, namely girls having received professional training in promising professions for the effective implementation of the capacity building received. Indeed, funding would have enabled them to set up to put the training received into practice and guarantee their autonomy: _ “The funds we received allowed us to carry out an agricultural song as an AGR which allowed us to cover certain additional expenses as part of our activity as a member of RECOPE _ [64] _”. _ _ “During the training for young girls in sewing, we were told that we would receive financial support to launch ourselves independently but to date, we have not received this immediately, which is an obstacle to our empowerment _ [65] _”. _ 128. Regarding the implementation of awareness and mass mobilization campaigns, this was appreciated for the contributing role played in changing community behavior around issues of access to the services induced by the 3 sectors of intervention of the project as well as the change in behavior of the populations. These campaigns were coupled with the organization of the competition called “child-friendly village competition”. A competition which really helped to boost > 60 As part of the use of the guide on the rights of the child in selected schools in targeted municipalities > 61 Evaluation H, Male child from the host community, Commune of Ketté > 62 FGD, Women, Host community, Yoko Siré, Commune of Garoua-Boulai > 63,120,000 FCFA to each of the 150 households receiving cash transfers in three distribution cycles; 200,000 FCFA to each of the 230 community leaders to enable them to remain active beyond the project; 200,000 FCFA incentive bonus for each municipal volunteer, in order to continue to provide support under the supervision of heads of social affairs social centers; 200,000 FCFA for each of the 243 RECOPEs in order to carry out an income-generating activity and remain in business beyond the project. > 64 FDG, OBC, RECOPE, Commune of Bétaré Oya > 65 FGD, Women, Host community, Yoko-Siré, Commune of Garoua-Boulai Page | 31 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ the commitment of communities to systematically register births and protect girls from child marriage. He was instrumental in the change observed in the communities. _“The project helped improve my quality of life as a refugee Because through KOICA’s multiple awareness campaigns, the host populations understood the importance of living together and are able to share their land with foreigners and the chief gave me space to raise my cattle[66].”_ 129. Finally, the evaluation notes the strengthening and anchoring of all interventions in community structures (RECOPE, COSIFE, AVUE, Village Savings and Credit Associations (AVEC)) as contributory to ensuring sustainability to the extent that they made it possible to ensure the inclusion of all components and categories of the population in each of the project implementation sites. This community engagement and the inclusive nature highlighted around the implementation of the evaluated project are seen as essential for the sustainability of the interventions. Indeed, the inclusive nature of the project is strongly linked to the support of the populations to the extent that the design, implementation and monitoring concerned and actively involved the beneficiary populations. Indeed, the establishment of community bodies and networks around issues of education, child protection and WASH has facilitated the management of cases within the beneficiary communities themselves. The communities thus facilitated children's access to protection services and/or schooling through their involvement in the actions undertaken during the implementation of the interventions of the evaluated project. Preliminary conclusion 130. Sustainability (QE4): The evaluation highlighted a mixed assessment of the project's capacity to establish partnerships to achieve the expected results and to guarantee the sustainability of the achievements on the basis of EPEL data as well as those coming from individual interviews and FGDs carried out with stakeholders (Para: 111-116). Also, this analysis revealed the strong ownership of the project interventions and its achievements by the communities and institutional stakeholders. This materializes at the community level through the behavioral change that has begun to take shape in perceptions, practices and habits in the areas served by the project. The appropriation by the institutional partners involved in the implementation of the project was perceptible by the technical leadership (during the training of actors, distribution of kits, construction of WASH/school infrastructures) of the leading actors at the regional and departmental level of the 3 intervention sectors of the evaluated project (Para: 117-124). Finally, the evaluation identifies several factors that led to the support of beneficiaries and other stakeholders - institutional (sectoral, NGOs) in the objectives of the PKU (Para: 125 129). 66 FGD, Men, Refugee community, Commune of Ndokayo Page | 32 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 6.5 Impact Question for evaluation criterion EQ5: To what extent has the KOICA project resulted in a significant change in the lives of the intended beneficiaries? Sub-question to evaluation criterion 5.1. To what extent has the KOICA project created lasting changes in social norms and behavioral determinants – including gender norms – and systems, whether intentional or not? ==> picture [376 x 142] intentionally omitted <== Image 6.8: _Advanced civil status post of the district hospital of the Commune of Garoua-Boulai_ Source: Collection of data from the evaluation of the KOICA project (2021-2024), November 2024. In order to ensure triangulation within the framework of the impact criterion analyses, 5 types of data sources are used: 1. The documentary review covering the documents (Project Document, UNICEF Country Program) and the annual implementation reports of the evaluated UNICEF/NGO project between 2021 and 2024; 2. Semi-structured interviews in the 4 target municipalities with representatives of the structures - government/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated program, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project; 3. The Online Perception Survey (EPEL) among representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated project, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project as well as among indirect rights holders; 4. Focus Group Discussion (FGD) with representatives of CBOs and rights holders (men, women and children aged 12-17) from the communities served by the project (host communities and refugees); 5. The H evaluation among children (girls and boys) under 12 years old living in the project’s target communities. 6.5.1 Strong contribution of PKU to creating lasting changes in social norms (including gender norms), behavioral determinants and institutional systems 131. Based on the data sources mobilized (documentary review, EPEL, individual interviews and FGD), the evaluation concluded that the evaluated project made a strong contribution to creating sustainable changes in all areas of project intervention in social norms (including gender norms), behavioral determinants and institutional systems. Indeed, the evaluation noted the contribution of the PKU to creating lasting changes in social norms (including those of gender) and behavioral determinants through the strengthening of community systems (establishment and strengthening of the technical and financial capacities of CBOs) and institutional (strengthening of technical capacities, establishment of mechanisms and creation of a new service specifically in charge of water management). Thus, these changes perceptible through changes in perceptions and attitudes of beneficiary communities were observed in all areas of intervention in the 3 sectors of the project. We also note that the competition called “_child-friendly village competition_” really helped to boost the commitment of communities to systematically declare births and protect girls from child marriages. He was instrumental in the change observed in the communities. 132. Indeed, the extracts from the life stories of the beneficiaries (Table 7.1) from the interviews and FGDs carried out with the stakeholders, made it possible to highlight their perception of the PKU in connection with the creation of lasting changes in the aspects mentioned above. Page | 33 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ Table 6.1: Excerpts from participants' life stories by sector of PKU interventions highlighting PKU's contribution to creating lasting changes in social norms (including gender norms), behavioral determinants and systems institutional. | Sectors | _Excerpts from the life stories of participants in the evaluation data collection_ | _Characteristics of_ _participants_ | |---|---|---| |Protection of children|_“Awareness raising on the establishment of birth certificates has changed the mentalities of_ _parents with regard to this document. Now, mothers automatically want to make a birth certificate for their child after delivery to guarantee them being protected by the law and being able to have their C.E.P._|Man from the host community of Yoko-Siré, Commune of Garoua-Boulai ||_“I say thank you to KOICA for the opportunity which gave me the opportunity to produce the birth certificates of_ _my children. Before, it was very complicated for me. For each birth, I was unable to establish the birth certificate of my children because the procedure was long given the circumstances I was going through. Now, thanks to KOICA, I have established the birth certificates of my last_ _children without problems and I really say thank you to KOICA.” _|Woman from the host community, Commune of Ndokayo| ||_“Before, we parents were very negligent regarding the establishment of birth certificates and did not do it for our children. After the awareness raising, I became more and more aware and I set myself the goal of issuing birth certificates to all my children who did not yet have them. I also made myself available to the community_ _to attend birth registrations.” _|Man from the community host of Yoko-Siré, Commune of Garoua- Boulai| |Education|_“During the start of the school year my parents did not have the means to pay for my notebooks, pens,_ _bags thanks to KOICA I benefited from these school supplies and I am now really very_ _satisfied because I am now going to school without any problem.”_|Refugee girl from the village of Mbilé, Commune of Kentzou| ||_“I greatly thank UNICEF-KOICA for helping me pay my pension in 2023 because_ _my parents did not have the means to pay. I had to spend a blank year. It’s_ _thanks to UNICEF-KOICA which allowed me to attend in 2023.” _|Girl from the host community, Commune of Ketté| ||_“Before, we saw young girls wandering around the village doing nothing, getting pregnant, or being sent off to marry very early. I didn't know it could harm them. But now I_ _know, and I make sure that my daughters go to school.”_|Man from the refugee community of Yoko-Siré, Commune of Garoua- Boulai| |EHA|_«_The rehabilitation of the water point next to the mosque here in Boubara helps me a lot because my wife draws water from there to wash clothes and wash my children. We drink, we wash our hands for prayers at the mosque. Before we suffered a lot_”._|Man from the host community, Village of Boubara, Commune of Ketté| ||“Before our populations drank water from the backwater and this created illnesses for us today thanks to the good water offered by the KOICA drilling we have improved health.”|Man from the host community, Commune of Bétaré Oya| Source: Elaboration by the evaluation team based on the data collection methods used ❖ Child protection sector 133. Based on the EPEL (Figure 6.5), for the child protection sector, the evaluation notes that respondents largely attest to the contribution of the KOICA project (2021-2024) to bringing about lasting changes in social norms (including gender norms), behavioral determinants and institutional systems. Thus, for stakeholders, the area of ​​promotion and respect for children's rights is the one most impacted by these lasting changes (67%), followed by registration and availability of a birth certificate for children (49%) and access to psychosocial and child protection services (42%). Figure 6.5: Extent to which PKU has created lasting changes in social norms and behavioral determinants specifically across different components of child protection ==> picture [419 x 170] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- Access to psychosocial and child protection services 18.6 33.9 47.5 Registration and availability of a birth certificate 13.6 37.9 48.5 for children Promotion and respect for the rights of children and adolescents 0.0 32.3 67.7 adolescent girls 0.0 20.0 40.0 60.0 80.0 100.0 120.0 To a small extent To a medium extent To a large extent ----- End of picture text ----- Source: Data from the online survey (EPEL) as of 11/12/2024 Page | 34 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 134. Everything made possible by a number of activities implemented that have induced this lasting change in the community, including among others[67]: (i) Strengthening the skills of parents on positive parenting in Ketté (410 parents trained or 248 women) and in Bétaré-Oya (800 parents trained or 268 women); (ii) Supporting community leaders in developing their action plan to combat forced child marriage; (iii) Raising awareness among adolescents about their ability to claim their rights; (iv) Training adolescents on everyday life skills (marriage, menstrual hygiene, reproductive health, practical skills, etc.) in the localities of Bétaré-Oya (1,266 including 605 girls), Garoua-Boulai (242 including 112 girls); Ketté (3,392 including 1,846 girls and Kentzou (334 including 186 girls); (v) Monetary transfers (Cash-Transfer) to families with a teenage girl at high risk of early marriage, with a view to supporting her schooling; (vi) Granting of AGR funding to 253 RECOPE to contribute to strengthening the autonomy of the populations. 135. It should be noted in this regard that all of these activities provided necessary information, knowledge and basic protection services to girls, boys, families and communities Services provided included prevention, identification, community care and referral of cases of violence, exploitation, abuse, discrimination, neglect and social and behavioral change identified. ▪ Birth registration, which is experiencing a significant increase thanks to project interventions. However, the increase in demand thus generated produces a shortage of materials, particularly civil status registers, both at the level of municipalities such as Garoua-Boulai and Kentzou, but also at the national level. Thus, all births occurring in the community are now declared to the civil registry through community mechanisms. For example, until May 2024, 1,139 births (including 506 girls) were recorded by RECOPE using the village notebooks made available to them; ▪ Access to psychosocial and child protection services: 162 children and adolescents identified as victims of various forms of violence benefited from quality psychological support and child protection in the localities of BétaréOya (43 or 18 girls), Garoua-Boulai (30 including 20 girls), Ketté (56 including 28 girls) and Kentzou (33 including 26 girls); ▪ Reporting and handling cases of violation of children's rights: following the establishment of this mechanism, there has been an increase in the number of reporting cases of abuse and violence against children and the handling of these cases compared to previous years. Thus, this mechanism made it possible to report a total of 543[68] cases (474 girls and 69 boys) of violations of children's rights and appropriate measures were taken to resolve these violations. 136. Also, lasting changes in perceptions and behaviors induced by the evaluated project were understood through the action of community networks (RECOPE). Indeed, the evaluation noted that one of the greatest successes of the PKU concerns raising awareness among populations about early child marriage through this body set up by the project. The populations of the communities met now fully and consciously perceive the fact that early child marriage is a harmful practice and are themselves taking action to stem this practice. A perceptible observation in these terms stating that the phenomenon of early marriage was significant but through the intervention of the KOICA project, we can say that the phenomenon of early marriages has decreased considerably[69]. _ ❖ Education sector 137. Based on the EPEL (Figure 6.6), for the education sector, the evaluation notes that respondents attest to a large extent (56%), the contribution of the PKU to inducing lasting changes in access to quality education in social norms, behavioral determinants and institutional systems. Figure 6.6: Extent to which the project created lasting changes in social norms and behavioral determinants specifically related to access to quality education ==> picture [343 x 155] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- To a large extent 56.1 To an average extent 36.4 To a small extent 7.6 0.0 10.0 20.0 30.0 40.0 50.0 60.0 Source: Online survey data (EPEL) as of 11/12/2024 ----- End of picture text ----- > 67 KOICA project implementation report evaluated, UNICEF, May 2024 68 Idem > 69 FGD, Young girls from the host community of Sandji 1, Commune of Kentzou. Page | 35 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 138. The evaluation noted that by strengthening synergies between the child protection and education sectors, community mechanisms (RECOPE, COSIFE) through the actions (awareness raising, capacity building and monitoring) put in place have ensured that children regularly attend school and are cared for by their family and community. Also, strengthening the capacities of teachers to: (i) eradicate violence against children in schools; (ii) identification of cases of distress among children; and (iii) the provision of first-aid psychosocial support contributed to strengthening the protective environment for all children. Likewise, parents and teachers were trained in the various modules of the positive parenting program in order to effectively contribute to the protection of their children at home and at school and to eradicate harmful cultural practices such as early and forced marriages, etc. 139. These COSIFE actions highlighted above which contributed to _the increase in the schooling rates of children in the 6 municipalities of interest of the project[70],_ sufficiently demonstrate the lasting changes in perceptions and behavior induced by the project evaluated. In addition, PKU's interventions in this education sector have contributed to a lasting change in living conditions by making school infrastructure available for children from host and refugee communities (girls and boys). This has contributed to positively influencing local perceptions on the importance of children's education in general and girls in particular, but also on the empowerment of young girls [71]. Thus, specifically with regard to the empowerment of women and girls, the significant improvement in the education and economic power of young girls was made possible by the PKU through the creation of 117 WITH. ❖ WASH sector 140. Based on the EPEL (Figure 6.7), for this sector, the evaluation notes that 26% of respondents attest to a large extent the contribution of the evaluated project to inducing sustainable changes in access to drinking water and adequate sanitation facilities in social norms (including gender norms), behavioral determinants and institutional systems. 141. Also, the lasting changes in perceptions and behaviors induced by the project are understood in this sector mainly in the Municipality of Ouli through the establishment in 5 villages (Bengue Tiko, Mbelebina, Ounssounou, Zongabona and Toctoyo) of local water point management structures, called "Village Water User Associations (AVUE) the action of community networks (AVUE) as well as the creation of an institution in this case the _ Operational Unit for the Supply of Drinking Water Service (COFSEP) _ Also, these lasting changes are perceptible even among very young children under 12 years of age, as this verbatim extract from an H evaluation sufficiently demonstrates. _ “From now on, I wash my hands before eating and when I go to shit because ADRA came here to teach us how to wash our hands with clean water and soap[72]”. _ Figure 6.7: Extent to which the project created lasting changes in social norms and behavioral determinants specifically in access to safe drinking water and adequate sanitation facilities ==> picture [356 x 110] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- To a large extent 26.2 To an average extent 36.1 To a small extent 37.7 0.0 5.0 10.0 15.0 20.0 25.0 30.0 35.0 40.0 Source: Online survey data (EPEL) as of 11/12/2024 ----- End of picture text ----- 142. However, the evaluation highlights obstacles that persist and do little to facilitate sustainable changes in social norms (including gender norms), behavioral determinants and institutional systems for the project intervention sectors. Such obstacles not only pose a risk to children's rights but also hardly facilitate the expected lasting changes in social norms (including gender norms) and behavioral determinants, and therefore therefore the achievement of the expected results of the PKU. These extracts from the interviews reveal this resistance to lasting change: _ “Women have always depended on men for everything in the community[73]”. _ _ “Several women do not know how to read and this is a problem because they are unable to read the educational documents that were made available to the community during the interventions[74]”. _ > 70 Individual Interview, UNICEF, Eastern Region > 71 Individual Interview, ADRA, implementing NGO, Eastern Region > 72 Evaluation H, male children under 12 years old from the host community of Yoko-Siré, Commune of Garoua-Boulai. > 73 FGD, OBC, COSIFE, Yoko-Siré, Commune of Garoua-Boulai. > 74 FGD, OBC, COSIFE, Yoko-Siré, Commune of Garoua-Boulai. Page | 36 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ _ “Particularly socio-economic challenges make it difficult for parents to provide children with the necessities for school[75]”. _ _ “In our community, women did not have the right to speak, they were not considered and girls did not have the right to education[76]”. _ 6.5.2 Most significant lasting changes made by the PKU relating to the services induced in the 3 sectors of intervention 143. Referring to the EPEL data (Graph 6.8), it appears that _“promotion and respect for the rights of children and adolescents”_ in the child protection sector is the area of intervention where lasting changes have been observed to a large extent (59.4%). the most significant at the end of the implementation of the evaluated project. Then follow in decreasing order of importance the areas relating to _“access to quality education for children and adolescents”_ (47%), _“registration and availability of a birth certificate for children”_ (46%) and _“access to psychosocial and child protection services”_ (45%). Access to drinking water and adequate sanitation facilities in the WASH sector is the area for which stakeholders believe to a small extent that the lasting changes of the evaluated project are the most significant, to the extent that the related interventions have not had to impact the populations served in sufficient proportion. Figure 6.8: Extent to which the change that occurred was most significant specifically in the PKU intervention sectors ==> picture [439 x 128] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- Access to psychosocial and child protection services 15.0 40.0 45.0 Childhood Registration and availability of a birth certificate for 10.8 43.1 46.2 children Promotion and respect for the rights of children and 4.7 35.9 59.4 adolescent girls 0.0 20.0 40.0 60.0 80.0 100.0 120.0 To a small extent To a medium extent To a large extent ----- End of picture text ----- Source: Data from the online survey (EPEL) as of 11/12/2024 144. Referring to the results of the final PKU survey (Table 6.2), this trend provided by the results of the evaluation is confirmed to the extent that the protection sector records the greatest improvements in indicators (15% for the decline in girls aged 15-19 married before the age of 15 and 44% for registration of refugee children under 5 years of age registered in the civil registry). This follows for the education sector, a 33% improvement in the (adjusted) net secondary school attendance rate. Also, the WASH sector is the one where one of the lowest (4.8%) improvements is recorded in relation to the use of an improved source of drinking water by the populations. Table 6.2: Evolution of PKU basic indicators between the baseline survey (May 2021) and the final survey (December 2024) | Sectors | Indicators | Value of base (2021) | Value Achieved (2024) | Evolution rate(%) | |---|---|---|---|---| |Education|Net primary school attendance rate (adjusted)|59|67.7|14.7| ||Net secondary school attendance rate (adjusted)|21.3|28.3|32.9| |WASH|Percentage of population using an improved source of drinking water|75.7|79.3|4.8| ||Percentage of population using improved sanitation facility|47.8|55.6|16.3| |Protection|Percentage of children under 5 years old registered in civil status|50.8|55.4|9.1| ||Percentage of children aged 0-11 months registered in the civil registry|45.1|49.3|9.3| ||Percentage of refugee children under the age of 5 registered in the civil registry|35.6|51.4|44.4| ||Percentage of refugee children aged 0-11 months registered in civil register|45.1|42.9|-4.9| ||Percentage of non-refugee children under 5 years old registered in the civil registry|53.9|55.8|3.5| ||Percentage of non-refugee children aged 0-11 months registered in civil register|45.1|50|10.9| ||Percentage of girls aged 15-19 married before age 15|7.7|6.5|-15.6| ||Percentage of girls aged 20-24 married before age 18|39|42.6|9.2| Source: Elaboration by the evaluation team based on data from the baseline and final surveys > 75 Individual interview, DREB resource person, Municipality of Bertoua. > 76 FGD, Male beneficiaries from the host community, Municipality of Betaré Oya. Page | 37 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 145. The use of secondary data from the baseline and final surveys reflects the evolution of other PKU indicators (Table 6.3) apart from those relating to access to the 3 PKU intervention sectors. In the protection sector, clear improvements were recorded in the fight against child labor, which resulted in the subsequent decline in indicator levels of around -24%, -14% and -15% respectively for own-account work, work in the family business and the sale/production of articles. However, efforts remain necessary to stem the phenomenon of child labor in artisanal mining sites, which experienced a remarkable resurgence during the period of implementation of the PKU (rising from 2% in 2021 to 5% in 2024). In the same vein, the evaluation noted an improvement in the WASH sector through an increase in the percentage of household members with a handwashing facility (36%). Table 6.3: Evolution of PKU indicators between the baseline survey (May 2021) and the final survey (December 2024) | Sector | Indicator | Basic value | Value Achievement | Evolution rate (%) | |---|---|---|---|---| |Protection|Percentage of children aged 5 to 17 who worked on their own land/farm/vegetable garden or in the household or took care of animals|9.7|7.4|-23.7| ||Percentage of children aged 5 to 17 who helped in the family business or that of other parents with or without payment or worked in their own business|13.9|12|-13.7| ||Percentage of children aged 5 to 17 who produced or sold articles, crafts, clothing, food or agricultural products|8|6.8|-15.0| ||Percentage of children aged 5 to 17 who worked in an artisanal mining site (GOLD mine, sand quarry, stone quarry)|2.1|5.2|147.6| |WASH|Percentage of household members with a handwashing facility where soap and water are available|17.3|23.5|35.8| Source: Elaboration by the evaluation team based on data from the baseline and final surveys Preliminary conclusion 146. Impact (EQ5): The PKU impact analysis reveals a strong contribution of the project to creating lasting changes in social norms (including gender norms) and behavioral determinants through the strengthening of community systems (establishment and strengthening of technical and financial capacities of CBOs) and institutional systems (strengthening of technical capacities and establishment of mechanisms). Thus, these perceptible changes through changes in perceptions and attitudes of the beneficiary communities were observed in all areas of intervention of the 3 sectors of the project. However, the assessment highlights persistent obstacles to consider that do little to facilitate lasting changes in social norms. These obstacles, which constitute avenues for possible actions to guarantee progress towards the desired changes, mainly concern the persistence of socio-cultural barriers relating to the economic power of parents, education and the status of women in these communities (Para: 131-142). Finally, the evaluation notes that among the most significant sustainable changes made by the KOICA project, the promotion and respect of the rights of children and adolescents in the child protection sector is the area of ​​intervention where the most significant lasting changes were observed to a large extent (59.4%). Conversely, access to drinking water and sanitation facilities is the area where stakeholders estimate to a small extent that the lasting changes of the evaluated project are the most significant. These changes noted during the evaluation are directly in line with those highlighted from the results of the final quantitative PKU survey. However, efforts remain necessary to stem the phenomenon of child labor in artisanal mining sites, which experienced a remarkable resurgence during the period of implementation of the PKU, going from 2% in 2021 to 5% in 2024 (Para: 143-145). Page | 38 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 6.6 Equality, Gender and Human Rights and Equity Evaluation criterion question QE6: To what extent does the PKU integrate the gender and equity approach into the different aspects of programming and implementation? Sub-question to the evaluation criterion - 6.1. To what extent have gender, human rights and equity principles been duly integrated into the design, implementation, monitoring and reporting of the project? - 6.2. To what extent have other targeted vulnerable groups been affected, in particular refugees and people living with disabilities? - 6.3. To what extent has the project identified and eliminated the barriers that prevent children (girls and boys) and women from accessing the services it makes available to them in the targeted communities? - 6.4. To what extent measures to prevent and respond to sexual exploitation have been integrated into the different phases of the project - ? Do beneficiaries, including children and adolescents, know how to access gender-based violence service providers in the intervention area? ==> picture [513 x 100] intentionally omitted <== Image 6.9: _Information plaque for the KOICA project (2021-2024) and some related illustrative achievements_ Source: Photo taken from the KOICA donor report, May 2024 In order to ensure triangulation in the context of analyzes relating to the criteria of gender, human rights and accountability, 5 types of data sources are used, namely: 1. The documentary review covering the documents (Project Document, UNICEF Country Program) and the annual implementation reports of the evaluated UNICEF/NGO project between 2021 and 2024; 2. Semi-structured interviews in the 4 target municipalities with representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated program, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project; 3. The Online Perception Survey (EPEL) among representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated project, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project as well as among indirect rights holders; 4. Focus Group Discussion (FGD) with representatives of CBOs and rights holders (men, women and children aged 12-17) from the communities served by the project (host communities and refugees); 5. The H evaluation among children (girls and boys) under 12 years old living in the project’s target communities. 6.6.1 Integration of gender, human rights and equity principles in all stages of the PKU 147. All sources of data used, in particular the documentary review (Appendix A.26), the EPEL, the individual interviews, the FGDs and the H Evaluation carried out reveal a strong integration and implementation of the principles of gender equality, human rights and equity [77] in the design, implementation, monitoring and reporting of the KOICA project evaluated. 148. Thus, specifically according to the EPEL, in the design, implementation, monitoring and reporting of the KOICA project, the evaluation results in the observation of a more pronounced integration to a large extent of the principle of gender equality compared to the principles of human rights and equity (67.2%; 60.9% and 57.8% respectively). On the other hand, for the EPEL stakeholders, these principles were to a small extent the least perceptible (9.4%) in terms of integration at the time of the design of the KOICA project (Appendix A.27). 149. Also, this analysis of the integration of the principle of gender, human rights and equity, both in the design and in the implementation, monitoring and reporting of the PKU is based on findings from the table in Annex A.28 and Table 6.4, with regard to the following principles: ▪ Gender: although taken into account in all aspects (design, implementation, monitoring and reporting) of the evaluated project, this principle appeared to be the one most weakly integrated during design. Indeed, relative to the enunciation/formulation > 77 Refers to reaching the most vulnerable/hardest to reach in order to take into account the principle of the SDGs in connection with the fact of _“Leaving no one behind_”. This is to ensure that all forms of discrimination and other root causes of inequalities are identified and taken into account. This involves knowing who they are (their profile), where they are and what are the factors of their vulnerability and which is only possible through national survey data. Page | 39 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ elements of the results framework, gender was only taken into account 6 times out of 9 (67%) at the Product level, 4 times out of 6 (67%) at the level of the Results indicators and 11 times out of 26 (42%) at the level of the Product indicators. ▪ Human Rights: represents the principle most taken into account in all aspects (design, implementation, monitoring and reporting) of the evaluated project. Indeed, at design, this is perceptible in relation to the statement/formulation of the elements of the results framework in relation to the Products, the Results indicators and the Product indicators as well as in relation to the activities of the evaluated project. Indeed, in their statement/formulation the “human rights” dimension is taken into account for 8 (89%) of the 9 Products, 5 of the 6 (83%) Results indicators and 21 out of 26 (81%) of the Product indicators. ▪ Equity represents the second principle most strongly integrated during the design following the analysis of its statement/formulation based on the elements of the results framework. Thus, the statement/formulation of the equity dimension is taken into account for 8 (89%) of the 9 Products, 5 of the 6 (64%) Results indicators and 12 out of 26 (46%) of the Product indicators. Table 6.4: Representation of women within PKU OBCs in the 6 target municipalities | MCPE Type | Number of members all genders | Number of female members | Proportion (%) of female members | |---|---|---|---| |RECOPE|1,655|558|34| |COSIFE|1,779|500|28| |Committing leaders|208|40|19| |Teen clubs|2,488|1,048|42| Source: Elaboration by the KOICA project evaluation team (2021-2024) based on data made available by UNICEF on 08/11/2024 6.6.2 Consideration of targeted vulnerable groups, in particular refugees and people living with disabilities 150. The evaluation established that to a large extent (56.5%), targeted vulnerable groups, including refugees and people living with disabilities were taken into account under the PKU based on EPEL data (Figure 6.9). The explanation provided to justify this proportion observed here again remains the poor consideration of populations living with a disability explicitly through project interventions targeting them in particular. Figure 6.9: Extent to which other targeted vulnerable groups were affected by the PKU ==> picture [366 x 119] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- To a large extent 56.5 To an average extent 30.6 To a small extent 12.9 0.0 10.0 20.0 30.0 40.0 50.0 60.0 ----- End of picture text ----- Source: Data from the online survey as of 11/12/2024 151. This consideration of both refugees and people living with disabilities in the implementation of the interventions of the evaluated project is also reflected by the documentary review which allowed the highlighting of _ 4,879[78] out-of-school children (2,415 girls, including 1,035 refugees) who have been able to integrate the school system since 2021_ just as it is perceived through a large number of testimonies from stakeholders including the following: _ “Non-Cameroonians (referring to refugees) also receive bags and books like us Cameroonians when distributions are made in our school here[79]”. _ _ “We refugees benefit from professional training in sewing and embroidery; there are 8 girls from the community there and we are 7 refugees[80].” _ _ “During the awareness raising, everyone was gathered, namely women, men, young people and children from the host and refugee communities. Even some disabled people were included in this framework[81].” _ _ “The project management team implemented several strategies to ensure transparency and accountability throughout the project, community members, including refugees and children with disabilities, were consulted to make joint decisions regarding project activities and implementation… The WASH facilities at MBAL Primary School are illustrative of the consideration of the needs of children with disabilities for this project[82].” _ > 78 KOICA project implementation report evaluated, UNICEF, May 2024 > 79 Evaluation H, Children under 12 years old, Host Community, Municipality of Mandjou. > 80 FGD, Refugee community, Women of Gbiti, Commune of Ketté. > 81 FGD, Beneficiaries, Adolescent girls from the refugee community of Yoko-Siré, Municipality of Garoua-Boulai > 82 Individual Interview, ADELPA, implementing NGO, Eastern Region Page | 40 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 152. However, the evaluation notes that the consideration of the disability situation under the principle of human rights deserves to be strengthened for the next cycle of the evaluated project. Indeed, during the design, no mention was made regarding this aspect for the indicators. Also, in no way did this aspect of the disability situation emerge through the reports resulting from the implementation of the evaluated project. This situation is expressed and summarized by this interlocutor through his thoughts: _ “People living with disabilities have not benefited from specific interventions concerning them, which has the consequence of limiting the impact of the project on this vulnerable population group[83]”. _ 6.6.3 Identification and elimination by the PKU of barriers that prevent children (girls and boys) and women from accessing the services available to them in targeted communities 153. The collection of data from stakeholders made it possible to better understand the communication activities for social and behavioral change undertaken by the evaluated project with a view to identifying obstacles to education as well as protection services for vulnerable groups in the communities served (children and women). Thus, the evaluation made it possible to understand five (5) types of predominant barriers of cultural, social and economic type. 154. These barriers are linked on the one hand to the early marriage of girls, causing those concerned (here the girls) to leave the school system. On the other hand, the second barrier is linked to the participation of household actors (girls and boys regardless of age) as economic agents (particularly on gold sites) with a view to strengthening the financial resources of households in response to the economic needs that households must face. The evaluation also noted that pregnancies in schools still remain a barrier to access to school while the preference of boys in terms of schooling or birth registration constitutes reasons which still affect access to these services. Finally, to this is added the lack of awareness of respect for rights in general and those of the child in particular. _ “Before in our community, girls did not have the right to education but today thanks to the various awareness-raising activities of the KOICA project my daughter goes to school like her brothers[84]”. _ _ “Before, I didn’t know that sending girls off in marriage before the age of 18 was dangerous and that it affected their growth. But after the awareness series on early marriage, I understood all this and I took it upon myself to fight for my daughters to go to school[85].” _ _ “The existence of gold sites constitutes a real obstacle to the deployment and reaching of certain segments of the population expected for the implementation of the KOICA project interventions[86]”. _ _ “Before here, women did not have the right to speak, they were not considered, but today thanks to the various awareness-raising activities of the KOICA project, I take the time to listen to my wife in decision-making[87].” _ 155. To overcome these barriers and act to remove the obstacles that prevent vulnerable groups in the communities served (children and women) from accessing services related to schooling and protection, the evaluated project made it possible to put in place at least six (6) types of mechanisms in the communities served. These mechanisms thus made it possible to understand the effect of communication activities for social and behavioral change carried out within the communities served to remove the obstacles highlighted above (Appendix A.30). 156. All the same, the evaluation noted with satisfaction that the costs relating to the declaration of births with a view to issuing the certificate as well as the distance from civil status centers are less and less perceived by the populations of the areas encountered as barriers to access to this service. This was made possible by the establishment of outposts at health facilities and community-based birth monitoring that significantly increased birth registration rates in underserved areas. Indeed, interoperability between health and civil registration systems has helped streamline the issuance of birth certificates, while the engagement of religious and community leaders has facilitated the identification and reporting of unregistered births[88] _ . 157. The evaluation notes that to a large extent more than half (62.5%) of EPEL stakeholders believe that the project succeeded in identifying and eliminating the cultural, social and economic barriers that prevented children and women from accessing these services (Annex A.29). Beyond these statistics which reflect notable progress in terms of social change, it remains true that these barriers persist within the targeted communities and require the strengthening not only of the five (5) types of mechanisms deployed in order to remove the obstacles relating to access to the services provided by the KOICA project (2021-2024) but also the development of certain other mechanisms. _ “After the awareness-raising that has been done and despite the fact that the number of forced marriages has decreased, some girls still marry very young of their own free will, and sometimes still by force[89]”. _ > 83 FGD, Beneficiaries, Adolescent girls from the refugee community of Yoko-Siré, Municipality of Garoua-Boulai > 84 FGD, Beneficiaries, Men from the host community, Municipality of Bétaré Oya > 85 FGD, Beneficiaries, Men from the refugee community of Yoko-Siré, Municipality of Garoua-Boulai > 86 Individual Interview, ADELPA, implementing NGO, Eastern Region > 87 FGD, Beneficiaries, Men from the host community, Commune of Ndokayo > 88 Individual Interview, BUNEC, Eastern Region > 89 FGD, Beneficiaries, Adolescent girls from the refugee community of Yoko-Siré, Municipality of Garoua-Boulai Page | 41 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ _ “Birth certificates are only established as a priority here for children under 90 days old and this is insufficient because there are plenty of children in the community over 90 days old who do not have a birth certificate... most of the parents themselves have neither a birth certificate nor an identity card. Consequently, they do not themselves meet the conditions to establish a birth certificate for their children[90].” _ 6.6.4 Integration of prevention and response measures to sexual exploitation within the framework of the PKU 158. Nearly 3 out of 5 stakeholders (60%) believe that the KOICA project integrates measures to prevent and respond to sexual exploitation in its implementation, while 40% (2 out of 5 participants) of the stakeholders interviewed estimate this state of affairs to an average extent. 159. Based on the six (6) types of mechanisms deployed (Appendix A.30) with a view to removing obstacles relating to access to the services provided by the KOICA project (2021-2024), the evaluation establishes that all of these mechanisms integrate prevention and response measures to sexual exploitation within the framework of the evaluated project. This set of mechanisms which to date has enabled 17,836[91] children and adolescents to be made aware of their rights, the mechanism for reporting cases of violence and to be protected against violence. This in a context of project implementation where the actors of community and formal protection mechanisms now benefit from the trust of users and where the level of denunciation is increasing because the culture of speaking out or denouncing abuse linked to sexual exploitation is gaining ground in the communities[92]. 160. The evaluation establishes that to a large extent 49.2% of people met, including children and adolescents, know how to access gender-based violence service providers in the intervention areas (Figure 6.10). However, it remains true that awareness raising around the mechanisms of prevention measures and responses to sexual exploitation is increasingly carried out by community radios or during mass events (16 days of activism, Children's Day among others). Indeed, some of the stakeholders met had to make the following comments: _ “We were made aware of GBV, what it means and how we can stop practicing it and also how to avoid making other victims. On the other hand, we do not know what to do or who to turn to if we are victims or if we want to report an act of GBV[93].” _ 161. Also, beyond these mechanisms put in place, the evaluation assesses the implementation of other types of lasting measures for prevention and response to sexual exploitation, namely: (i) the availability of an SOP for case management and referral to the competent services in matters of violence against children; (ii) the development and validation of a practical guide on children's rights was produced in collaboration with the Ministry of Basic Education and the Ministry of Social Affairs, in order to help primary school teachers give children knowledge about their rights (and how to assert them). Figure 6.10: Extent to which beneficiaries, including children and adolescents, know how to access GBV service providers ==> picture [362 x 184] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- To a large extent 49.2 To an average extent 46.0 To a small extent 4.8 0.0 10.0 20.0 30.0 40.0 50.0 60.0 Source: Online survey data as of 11/12/2024 ----- End of picture text ----- > 90 FGD, OBC, RECOPE of Yoko-Sire, Municipality of Garoua-Boulai > 91 KOICA project implementation report evaluated, UNICEF, May 2024 > 92 Individual Interview, ADRA, implementing NGO, Eastern Region > 93 FGD, Beneficiaries, Adolescent girls from the refugee community of Yoko-Siré, Municipality of Garoua-Boulai Page | 42 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ Preliminary Conclusion 162. Gender Equality, Human Rights and Equity (QE6): The evaluation resulted, according to the EPEL, in the finding of a more pronounced integration to a large extent of the principle of gender equality compared to the principles of human rights and equity (67.2%; 60.9% and 57.8% respectively) both in the design and in the implementation, monitoring and reporting of the PKU. This perception of the taking into account of these principles by the EPEL contrasts with their statement in the results framework. Specifically at the design phase of the PKU, the principle of human rights was perceived as the one having been most strongly integrated in relation to the statement/formulation of the elements of the results framework; on the contrary, the principle of gender appeared as the one having been the most weakly integrated during the design of the evaluated project. Also, significant challenges remain in taking into account the most vulnerable populations such as people with disabilities both during planning and during the implementation and reporting of interventions (Par.147-149). Also, the evaluation establishes that to a large extent (56.5%), targeted vulnerable groups, including refugees and people living with disabilities were taken into account under the PKU) based on EPEL data. An observation which is reflected in the documentary review which also allows us to discuss information on interventions implemented with refugees as well as people living with disabilities, as well as through a large number of testimonies from stakeholders. However, the evaluation notes that the consideration of the disability situation under the principle of human rights deserves to be strengthened for the KOICA project both during the design, no mention was made concerning this aspect for the indicators as well as the fact that no aspect of the disability situation emerged through the reports resulting from the implementation of the evaluated project (Par.150-152). Furthermore, the evaluation made it possible to understand five (5) types of predominant cultural, social and economic barriers which prevent vulnerable groups in the communities served (children and women) from accessing services related to schooling and protection. Barriers for which six (6) types of mechanisms have been put in place in connection with communication activities for social and behavioral change within the communities served. As a result, the evaluation establishes that to a large extent more than half (62.5%) of EPEL stakeholders believe that the project succeeded in identifying and eliminating the cultural, social and economic barriers that would prevent children/women from accessing said services (Par.153-157). Finally, the evaluation establishes that all of these mechanisms within the communities served integrate prevention and response measures to sexual exploitation within the framework of the evaluated project. However, awareness raising around these mechanisms of prevention measures and responses to sexual exploitation should increasingly be carried by community radio stations or during mass events (16 days of activism, Children's Day). Finally, beyond these established mechanisms, the evaluation assesses the implementation of other types of lasting measures for prevention and response to sexual exploitation, namely: (i) the availability of an SOP for case management and referral to the competent services in matters of violence against children; (ii) the development and validation of a practical guide on children's rights was produced in collaboration with the Ministry of Basic Education and the Ministry of Social Affairs, in order to help primary school teachers give children knowledge about their rights (and how to assert them) (Par.158-161). Page | 43 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ 6.7 Accountability and community engagement Evaluation criterion question EQ7: To what extent does the KOICA project guarantee accountability towards the populations benefiting from the interventions? What mechanisms are in place to collect and respond to people’s concerns? Sub-question to evaluation criterion 7.1. To what extent did the implementation of the project facilitate the participation of all community members in the planning, implementation and monitoring of interventions? 7.2. To what extent do communities have access to feedback mechanisms and do they receive adequate responses to their concerns? ==> picture [248 x 126] intentionally omitted <== ==> picture [252 x 122] intentionally omitted <== Image 6.10 _: Strong involvement of community leaders during_ Image 6.11: _Information plaque of school infrastructure and awareness session in Mbilé its equipment in Sabongari, Commune of Garoua Boulai_ Source: Photos taken from the KOICA donor report, May 2023 5 types of data sources are used to ensure the triangulation of the analysis for this accountability criterion: 1. The documentary review covering the documents and implementation reports of the UNICEF/NGO PKU for 2021 and 2024; 2. Semi-structured interviews in the 4 target municipalities with representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated program, UNICEF and NGOs/CBOs implementing the project; 3. EPEL; 4. Focus Group Discussion (FGD) with representatives of OBCs and rights holders; 5. The H evaluation among children (girls and boys) under 12 years old living in the project’s target communities. 6.7.1 Facilitating the participation of all community members in the planning, implementation and monitoring of PKU interventions 163. To a large extent (63.1%), EPEL stakeholders perceived the implementation of the evaluated project as contributing to the facilitation of the participation of all members of the community during all phases (Figure 6.11). Figure 6.11: Extent to which the implementation of the PKU facilitated the participation of all community members ==> picture [362 x 148] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- To a large extent 63.1 To an average extent 32.3 To a small extent 4.6 0.0 10.0 20.0 30.0 40.0 50.0 60.0 70.0 Source: Online survey data (EPEL) as of 11/12/2024 ----- End of picture text ----- 164. Thus, the evaluation noted that from the project design phase, population participation was one of the guiding principles. Thus, it was noted that _leaders and representatives of the host and refugee communities were consulted to approve_ Page | 44 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ _jointly project priorities and results frameworks before finalizing the project during consultation meetings organized in each department (Lom and Djerem and Kadey)[94]_. 165. During the implementation and monitoring of the project, the CBOs set up within the communities demonstrate the involvement of the populations in matters of protection, education and WASH. This ensured everyone's participation in the project and contributed to making these CBOs appropriate frameworks for community consultative mechanisms and responses to all collective challenges and issues. Indeed, _ the inclusion of all categories of the population (men, women, young people from host or refugee communities) in these local committees was truly ensured in order to silence any potential allegation of bias of exclusion of a type of population or a particular segment of the population [95]_. 166. Finally, the discussions with the communities revealed that the consultative missions carried out by the institutional implementation stakeholders with the beneficiary communities constituted a framework for exchange during which the populations expressed their expectations and opinions regarding the project evaluated in a transparent manner. This involvement of the population was also observed during the planning of construction works (WASH, classrooms), as well as during their reception and at the time of the inauguration of the infrastructure built. Furthermore, the evaluation noted that information panels were always displayed on the infrastructures from the construction stage until the inauguration and even beyond in order to allow the populations of the communities to have perfect information about the infrastructures of the KOICA project. 6.7.2 Existence and accessibility of communities to feedback mechanisms/reception of responses to their concerns 167. The evaluation found that feedback mechanisms have been put in place in communities to encourage community members to provide feedback and raise complaints or concerns. As part of the accountability to affected populations (_Accountability to Affected Populations - AAP_) concerning the implementation of the cash transfer, a complaints management and feedback mechanism _ was put in place to ensure that the financial resources allocated to target households were used effectively to (i) fight against early marriages, (ii) keep girls in school and (iii) enable them to develop an Income Generating Activity (IGA) _[96] . Analysis of the relevant statistics reveals that 66[97] complaints were collected in the field via the committees set up (RECOPE, COSIFE) and the toll-free line (long number[98]), and all of these complaints were processed. Around 83% of the complaints recorded were non-sensitive and related to distribution planning. 168. In the protection sector, thanks to capacity building and awareness-raising on Protection against Sexual Exploitation and Sexual Abuse (PSEA) as well as the reporting channels for these SEAs, feedback channels have been put in place. They included an emergency telephone line[99] that community members could also use to report any Sexual Exploitation and Abuse (SEA) situation either directly to UNICEF or to the municipal or gendarmerie authorities. In total, 97 complaints and comments were recorded until May 2024, the majority of which (58%) came from women. All of these complaints were all handled in collaboration with the social services of each municipality. 169. However, the accessibility of feedback mechanisms and the receipt of responses to concerns raised is judged to an average extent (50.8%) by the evaluation stakeholders (Appendix 6.31). 170. This moderate accessibility would be justified on three points, namely: - (i) the lack of awareness of this mechanism by the populations: _ To date, many are still unaware of the existence of this feedback mechanism and the receipt of responses to the concerns raised although awareness raising is ongoing [100]_. - (ii) the channel used _:_ _ it is not within everyone's reach to use the feedback mechanism in the context of CASH transfer to the extent that you must first have a telephone and then have the telephone network in order to make calls[101] _; - (iii) responsiveness time: _ the response to concerns raised is still often long. Often feedback on concerns is provided when the solution has already been found on site based on the advice/mediation received/made[102]. _ 171. Also, the evaluation established that formally/informally the CBOs set up (RECOPE, COSIFE specifically) constituted both a formal and informal mechanism for feedback and receiving responses through the collection/processing/retransmission of community concerns for the 3 sectors of the KOICA project. Thus: > 94 Individual Interview, UNICEF, Eastern Region, Bertoua Municipality > 95 Individual Interview, ADRA, implementing NGO, Eastern Region > 96 Individual Interview, ADELPA, implementing NGO, Eastern Region > 97 KOICA project implementation report evaluated, UNICEF, May 2024 > 98 Number of the ADELPA Protection coordinator > 99 Same > 100 FGD, OBC, RECOPE from Yoko-Siré, Municipality of Garoua-Boulai > 101 FGD, OBC, RECOPE of Mbal, Commune of Garoua-Boulai > 102 FGD, OBC, COSIFE of Mbal, Municipality of Garoua-Boulai Page | 45 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ - ❖ In the protection sector, the technical support provided to RECOPEs enabled them to better coordinate their community capacity building and social mobilization, and to put in place precise and functional referral systems for adequate case management in the three areas covered by the child protection sector (birth registration, child marriage, psychosocial support). Thus, specifically through the establishment of the mechanism for reporting violations of children's rights, the RECOPEs also served as a framework for collecting/processing/retransmission of information on the one hand, and as key players in the feedback mechanism and receiving responses to community concerns on the other hand. This information focused on prevention, identification, community management and referral of identified cases of violence, exploitation, abuse, discrimination, neglect and social and behavioral change. _ “We have expressed the concerns received at our level to the NGO coordinating the project regarding the geographical limits to the establishment of birth certificates, the lack of financial support for young girls trained in sewing and the lack of intervention at the WASH level in the communities. The responses provided were transmitted to the communities [103]_ _”. _ - ❖ In the education sector, COSIFEs were responsible for identifying out-of-school children and raising awareness among parents in order to encourage them to take the necessary measures so that their children enjoy their right to education. Also, COSIFE ensured that the vulnerability criteria used to select children receiving school kits were well known to community members as well as the type and number of items to be given to each child before distribution. As such, specifically through the establishment of the mechanism for reporting violations of children's rights, COSIFE also served as a framework for collecting/processing/retransmission of information on the one hand, and as key players in the feedback mechanism and receiving responses to community concerns for education on the other hand. As part of post-distribution surveys of school materials, they also served as a channel for transmitting people's concerns. _ Specifically in 2023, complaints through COSIFE had been received regarding the quality of the chalk distributed and, to remedy this, UNICEF had therefore purchased locally produced chalk during purchases. This means that purchased items were adapted to meet the needs of the community following concerns expressed by them before purchasing school items _[104] . - ❖ Finally, in the WASH sector, the framework of local water point management structures called "Village Water User Associations (AVUE)" specifically set up in 5 villages (Bengue Tiko, Mbelebina, Ounssounou, Zongabona and Toctoyo) in the commune of Ouli, ensure the daily management of all water points present in each village. Since they act to facilitate the upkeep and maintenance of water points in these villages, _ these AVUEs ensure on a daily basis the role of mechanisms for collecting the concerns of the populations for whom, with the contribution of the Operational Unit for the Supply of the Drinking Water Service (COFSEP) of the commune of Ouli, responses are provided to requests made by the communities [105]_ _. _ Preliminary conclusion 172. Accountability to the population (EQ7): The evaluation highlighted the perception to a large extent (63.1%) of EPEL stakeholders of the participation of all members of the community during all phases of the PKU (Par.163-166). Also, the evaluation made it possible to note the existence and effectiveness of community feedback mechanisms specifically with regard to cash transfers and child protection. In the first, a complaints management and feedback mechanism was put in place to ensure that the financial resources allocated to girls in their respective homes were used effectively to combat early marriages, keep girls in school and enable them to develop AGR. In the case of child protection, specifically through the establishment of the mechanism for reporting violations of children's rights, CBOs have constituted a complaint management and feedback mechanism. Finally, the evaluation notes the reasons given for the accessibility of feedback mechanisms and the reception of responses judged to an average extent (50.8%), namely: (i) lack of awareness of this mechanism by the populations; (ii) the communication channel relating to the use of the telephone through a number to use this feedback and response mechanism; (iii) the response time considered long enough to receive responses to the concerns raised (Par.167-171). > 103 FGD, OBC, RECOPE, Commune of Kentzou > 104 Individual Interview, UNICEF, Eastern Region, Bertoua Municipality > 105 Individual Interview, Regional Delegation of Water and Energy, Eastern Region, Municipality of Bertoua Page | 46 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ CHAPTER 7 LESSONS LEARNED 173. Lessons learned are lessons learned from the evaluation that could potentially be applied to other similar projects and programs. They are located at the strategic (04) as well as operational (04) levels and are presented as follows 7.1 Strategic level ▪ Lesson learned No. 1: Strong contribution of the strategies implemented (intersectorality, interoperability between stakeholders coupled with the use of community networks) to achieving the expected results. LA1. The adoption of a holistic, multi-sectoral and integrated approach translated through the strategies implemented made it possible to contribute to effectively combating the deprivations suffered by children and consequently made it possible to reduce the weaknesses of decentralized structures in providing quality social services to affected populations. ▪ Lesson learned no. 2: Strong involvement of institutional actors and town halls in design, training, supervision, monitoring-evaluation and coordination activities which made it possible to obtain the expected results of the PKU. LA2. The effective and regular involvement of institutional actors as well as those of the 6 Town Halls covered by the evaluated project allowed them to be in control and to coordinate all the activities implemented with a view to contributing positively to obtaining the results obtained and linked to the expected results of the implementation of the PKU interventions. ▪ Lesson learned No. 3: Strong enthusiasm for population access to services in the sectors of intervention induced by the implementation of innovations by the PKU. LA3. The adoption of innovations such as the integration of Tech Clubs into education, the prioritization of solar energy for water pumping and the promotion of “Child-Friendly” villages, is a key success factor which has made it possible to increase the access of populations to the services of the sectors of intervention in the areas served and, in turn, the achievement of the expected results of the PKU. This is part of a holistic transformative approach that strengthens overall outcomes in terms of improved access to protection, education and WASH services. ▪ Lesson learned No. 4: Strong contribution of the UNICEF-Ube strategic partnership to the PKU evaluation process which can also serve as a channel for disseminating the results obtained to communities in the East and the university network. ▪ LA4. The implementation of the UNICEF-Ube strategic partnership not only allowed the involvement of Ube students in data collection but also their training in the principles of evaluation and PSEA. Also, in order to influence change in programs and practices, UNICEF could capitalize on this partnership to further involve the institution through its students in the dissemination of conclusions and recommendations to communities in the East while constituting a regional/national university network for the promotion of children's rights. 7.2 Operational level ▪ Lesson learned No. 5: Establishment of functional community bodies as part of multi-sectoral interventions, a guarantee of social and behavioral change sought within communities and the sustainability of the PKU's achievements. LA5. The establishment and operation of community bodies (RECOPE, COSIFE, Avec and AVUE) for multisectoral interventions have been levers for effective implementation to the extent that they have strongly contributed to the social and behavioral change sought within the communities served as well as to the sustainability of achievements. ▪ Lesson learned No. 6: Need to streamline financial procedures depending on the context of implementation of interventions in order to guarantee timely provision of financial resources. LA6. Provision of financial resources in a timely manner on the basis of financial procedures taking into account the context of implementation, would ensure effective use of these resources because it would make it possible to avoid discontinuity in the achievement of the expected objectives of the PKU. ▪ Lesson learned No. 7: Need to extend decentralized financial management to all decentralized administrations LA7. It appears necessary to extend decentralized financial management to all decentralized sectoral administrations involved in the implementation of the PKU. This initiative will have the effect of reducing the delays and procedures for making financial resources available on the one hand, and would undoubtedly increase the accountability of financial governance and contribute to the effectiveness of interventions on the other hand. ▪ Lesson learned no. 8: Strong capitalization of the interventions of other partners (ASNU, Town Halls and Private Companies) in the areas served by the PKU through the establishment of strategic partnerships LA8. The establishment of strategic partnerships between actors operating in the areas served by the PKU (ASNU; Town Halls and Private Companies) has contributed to better implementation and effective monitoring of interventions. All things having contributed to guaranteeing ownership of the interventions and therefore aiming to increase the sustainability of the achievements of the evaluated project. Page | 47 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ CHAPTER 8 FINAL CONCLUSIONS 174. The conclusions of this evaluation of the KOICA project (2021-2024) are provided by evaluation question and by recommendation made. | _Criterion_ | 1. CONSISTENCY | |---|---| | _Question_ _evaluation_ | _QE1: To what extent did the KOICA project prove to be adequate in its implementation mechanisms and did it allow_ _to create synergies between implementation parties?_ | | _Conclusion No. 1_ _(C1)_ |The evaluation established a perceptible coherence and complementarity of the KOICA project interventions with the other components of the country program and those of other partners including the ASNU. A situation which made it possible to avoid duplication by setting up a consultation/coordination platform/framework at the level of the 6 Town Halls served. However, there is a need to strengthen the provision of financial resources to this coordination body with a view to optimal functioning. Because their operation, although regular, has experienced implementation difficulties with the costs of holding meetings. Finally, the evaluation resulted in a strong assessment of the project's capacity to contribute through its strategies (intersectorality, community engagement, partnerships) implemented to: (i) synergy, collaboration and complementarities between its different components; (ii) and the offer of service packages to different target groups| | _Recommendation_ _associated_ | R1 | | _Criterion_ | 2. EFFICIENCY | | _Question_ _evaluation_ | _EQ2: To what extent were the expected results of the KOICA project achieved and did they contribute to progress_ _towards the strategic results sought by its implementation?_ | | _Conclusion no. 2_ _(C2)_ |A satisfactory performance of Result 3 and all Product indicators in the child protection, Education and WASH sector was noted. The capacity building of social workers by the project through MINAS (regional and central) having been seen as contributing to the performance of the SSP Component, it would be appropriate to strengthen the production of evidence around these activities. However, the evaluation made it possible to highlight five (5) types of predominant barriers of a cultural, social and economic type which constitute possible avenues of action for more effectiveness of SBC actions for possible future KOICA project interventions, namely: (i) early marriage of girls; (ii) poverty requiring the participation of girls/boys as economic agents (particularly on gold sites); (iii) pregnancies in schools; (iv) the preference of boys in matters of schooling or birth registration by parents/guardians; and, (v) lack of awareness of respect for rights in general and those of the child in particular. The evaluation highlighted the existence of possible avenues of action for greater effectiveness for possible future KOICA project interventions in connection with: (i) gaps or challenges in the timely provision of financial and/or material resources; (ii) the weakness of the results framework of the evaluated project and the reporting of results indicators; (iii) missed opportunities to establish strategic partnerships with certain key stakeholders (MINEPIA; MINFOP; MINJEC) in the context of monitoring beneficiaries and sustaining the achievements of the evaluated project; (iv) dysfunctions in the monitoring-evaluation framework for project implementation; (v) the lack of decentralization of financial management in certain administrations (DREE); (vi) the absence of financial support for municipal volunteers by Town Halls despite a commitment previously made to do so; and, (vii) the existence of cultural, social and economic barriers which constitute obstacles to the expected change in social behavior.| | _Recommendation_ _associated_ | R2; R3; R4; R5; R6; R8 and R9 | | _Criterion_ | 3. EFFICIENCY | | _Question_ _evaluation_ | _EQ3: To what extent did the KOICA project have sufficient resources (human, material and financial)_ _in quality and quantity to achieve the expected results?_ | | _Conclusion no. 3_ _(C3)_ |The evaluation reveals low levels of budgetary execution of the project of around 44%, 51% and 56% respectively for the years 2021, 2022 and 2023 despite strong budgetary mobilization (99.2%) of 7,931,830 USD for a budget forecast of 7,996 000 USD. Budget execution justified by: (i) the slowness of the contractualization procedures of the NGOs in the consortium; (ii) the late provision of financial resources for IGAs to OBCs (RECOPE) and to households benefiting from Cash transfer in order to combat early marriages and keep girls in school; (iii) fairly long delays in processing partners' financial documents. Also, it was deplored by the stakeholders in the evaluation that the low number of staff at the social center level (CESO) of the DRAS did not specifically facilitate the processing of the management of cases arising from the mechanism for reporting violations of children's rights at the level of this entity. Finally, material resources were marked by a low provision of rolling stock (motorcycles) in order to ensure the implementation/coordination of interventions in very large areas served. Also, their supply was made within deadlines that were often largely exceeded in the Education sector.| | _Recommendations_ _associated_ | R8 | | _Criterion_ | 4. SUSTAINABILITY | Page | 48 || _Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024)_ | |---|---| | _Question_ _evaluation_ | _EQ4: To what extent has the KOICA project generated ownership at the national and community level or is it_ _it is in the process of being so, so as to (i) ensure the continuity of its effects beyond the duration of its implementation and (ii) to_ _influence in the long term policies and programs for access to basic social services (education and WASH) and_ _child protection (fight against child marriage and birth registration)?_ | | _Conclusion no. 4_ _(C4)_ |For several reasons, the implementation of the KOICA project evaluated was seen as contributing to the creation of the most appropriate partnerships with a view to achieving the expected results and guaranteeing the sustainability of the achievements. However, on the basis of its operating mode based on interventions at the municipal level, the evaluation did not perceive the involvement of actors at the departmental level in certain implementation zones on the one hand, as well as it was not possible to highlight strategic partnerships with certain key actors (MINEPIA; MINEFOP; MINJEC) which would have been useful for the sustainability of the project's achievements and the monitoring of beneficiaries of (i) professional training, (ii) radio broadcast listening clubs by adolescents. In addition, it is highlighted by the analysis of EPEL data the existence to a large extent (80%) of the appropriation of the project interventions as well as its achievements by the communities and institutional partners. However, although the appropriation of the project interventions as well as its achievements by the administrations decentralized sectoral and town halls is laudable, there remains the question of the integration of municipal volunteers among the town hall staff through their financial support in the development of PCDs. This integration among the municipal staff would allow them to continue the support and ensure the sustainability of the achievements.| | _Recommendations_ _associated_ | R4 and R5 | | _Criterion_ | 5. IMPACT | | _Question_ | _EQ5: To what extent has the KOICA project brought about a significant change in the lives of the targeted beneficiaries?_ | | _Conclusion no. 5_ _(C5)_ |The WASH sector is the area where the lowest significant sustainable change is reported (37.7% to a small extent), a result linked to the fact that this sector only covered 15% of the target localities, i.e. 39 localities (including 9 for new works and 30 for rehabilitation) in terms of infrastructure. The evaluation noted the project's contribution to creating sustainable changes in social norms (including gender norms) and behavioral determinants through the strengthening of community systems (establishment and strengthening of the technical and financial capacities of CBOs) and institutional systems (strengthening technical capacities, establishment of mechanisms and creation of new services). However, the evaluation highlights obstacles in terms of the effectiveness criterion which persist and which hardly facilitate sustainable changes in social norms (including gender norms), behavioral determinants and institutional systems for the project intervention sectors.| | _Recommendations_ _associated_ | R3, R6 and R9 | | _Criterion_ | 6. GENDER EQUALITY, HUMAN RIGHTS AND EQUITY | | _Question_ _evaluation_ | _QE6: To what extent does the PKU integrate the gender and equity approach in the different aspects of programming_ _and implementation?_ | | _Conclusion no. 6_ _(C6)_ |The evaluation resulted, according to the EPEL, in the observation of a more pronounced integration to a large extent of the principle of gender equality compared to the principles of human rights and equity (67.2%; 60.9% and 57.8% respectively) in all phases of the KOICA project. However, the evaluation notes that the consideration of the disability situation under the principle of human rights deserves to be strengthened for the KOICA project. Indeed, during the design, no mention was made concerning this aspect for the indicators just like the fact that this aspect on the disability situation was not emerged through the reports resulting from the implementation of the evaluated project. Through the implementation of six (6) types of mechanisms linked to communication activities for social and behavioral change within the communities served, the evaluated project managed to identify and eliminate to a certain extent the cultural, social and economic barriers preventing children (girls and boys) and women from accessing the services induced by the evaluated Project. However, awareness raising around these mechanisms should increasingly be carried out by community radios or during mass events (16 days of activism, children's day).| | _Recommendations_ _associated_ | R3 and R9 | | _Criterion_ | 7. ACCOUNTABILITY TOWARDS POPULATIONS | | _Question_ _evaluation_ | _EQ7: To what extent does the evaluated program integrate the approach to gender equality, equity and human rights_ _in its various aspects of programming and implementation?_ | | _Conclusion no. 7_ _(C7_ |The evaluation noted the existence and effectiveness of community feedback mechanisms concerning cash transfers and child protection. However, accessibility to an average extent (50.8%) to feedback mechanisms and the receipt of responses to the concerns raised would be justified for three main reasons: (i) lack of knowledge of this mechanism by the populations coupled with the lack of awareness of respect for rights in general and those of the child in particular; (ii) the communication channel relating to the use of the telephone through a number to use this feedback and response mechanism and (iii) the response time considered to be long enough to receive responses to the concerns raised.| | _Recommendations_ _associated_ | R7 and R10 | Page | 49 _ Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024) _ CHAPTER 9 RECOMMENDATIONS 175. This evaluation made it possible to identify ten (10) strategic (7) and operational (3) recommendations. These call for direct action by the Government through central and decentralized sectoral administrations, UNICEF, KOICA, implementing NGOs, the project's target municipalities and the communities served with a view to improving possible future interventions. These recommendations are classified according to their level of priority (High/Medium/Low) and the level of resources (financial, human, material) necessary for their implementation (High/Medium/Low). 9.1 Strategic recommendations | Evaluation criterion || Priority level | Origin | Resource needs || Recipients | |---|---|---|---|---|---|---| | COHERENCE ||_Observation:A low level of funding was noted and did not_||||_facilitate the holding of meetings of the coordination body in_| ||| _municipal level of project interventions._||||| ||| High | C1 | High || Recipients: Town halls; UNICEF, KOICA and NGOs. | ||| R1. Plan during the validation of PCDs, an allocation of substantial financial resources to institutionalize the holding of meetings to coordinate project interventions (including the PKU) at the municipal level with partners in view of the role and importance of this type of body to guarantee the appropriation of acquired knowledge on the one hand, and with the aim of guaranteeing its optimal functioning on the other hand. ||||| | EFFICIENCY: || High | C2 | High || Recipients: MINAS; DRAS; UNICEF and NGOs. | |||_Observation: Robust evidence could not be produced in relation to the capacity building of social workers_ _thanks to the project by MINAS (regional and central). This is with the aim of understanding in its entirety the contribution of this_ _reinforcement provided to the “psychosocial support and child protection services” component. _||||| ||| R2. Strengthen knowledge management and the production of robust evidence, specifically to strengthen the capacities of social workers carried out thanks to the KOICA project evaluated through MINAS (regional and central) with a view to understanding in its entirety the contribution of this strengthening provided to the “psychosocial support and child protection services” component. ||||| | EFFECTIVENESS & IMPACT & GENDER EQUALITY, HUMAN RIGHTS AND EQUITY: .|| High | C2 & C5 & C6 | Bottom || Recipients: MINAS/DRAS; MINEDUB/DREB; MINEE/DREE; UNICEF; KOICA, NGOs and communities served. | |||_Finding:_ _The evaluation highlights obstacles that persist and which hardly facilitate sustainable changes in_ _social norms (including gender norms), behavioral determinants and institutional systems for_ _the project intervention sectors. Such obstacles not only constitute a risk for children's rights but also hardly facilitate the expected lasting changes, and therefore the achievement of the expected results of the PKU. Also, although the PKU has effectively contributed to the reduction of gender and equity gaps, it has contributed very little to improving the situation of people with disabilities both in the NGO/UNICEF implementation reports and in the monitoring/evaluation of the evaluated project._||||| ||| R3. Further strengthen the capacities of all PKU stakeholders on community engagement and behavioral change to guarantee integrated protection of children in connection with the fight against child marriage, early pregnancies, and other forms of violence against children, while integrating into these actions the principle of human rights in relation to the consideration of children with disabilities.||||| | EFFECTIVENESS & IMPACT .|| High | C2 & C5 & C6 | Bottom || Recipients: MINAS/DRAS; MINEDUB/DREB; MINEE/DREE; UNICEF; KOICA, NGOs and communities served. | |||_Finding:_ _The evaluation highlights obstacles that persist and which hardly facilitate sustainable changes in_ _social norms (including gender norms), behavioral determinants and institutional systems for_ _the project intervention sectors. Such obstacles not only constitute a risk for children's rights but also hardly facilitate the expected lasting changes, and therefore the achievement of the expected results of the PKU. Also, although the PKU has effectively contributed to the reduction of gender and equity gaps, it has contributed very little to improving the situation of people with disabilities both in the NGO/UNICEF implementation reports and in the monitoring/evaluation of the evaluated project._||||| ||| R4. Further strengthen the capacities of all PKU stakeholders on community engagement and behavioral change to guarantee integrated protection of children in connection with the fight against child marriage, early pregnancies, and other forms of violence against children, while integrating into these actions the principle of human rights in relation to the consideration of children with disabilities.||||| | EFFICIENCY & || Average | C3 & C4 | Bottom || Recipients: MINEFOP; MINJEC; MINEPIA; UNICEF; KOICA and NGOs. | Page | 50 || _Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024)_ | _Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024)_ | _Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024)_ | _Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024)_ | _Final report of the formative and summative evaluation of the UNICEF-KOICA project (2021-2024)_ | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | SUSTAINABILITY: |_Observation:_ _The absence, within the framework of the multisectorality driven by the PKU, of strategic partnerships established throughout the_ _cycle of the project as part of the monitoring of the beneficiaries of the professional training carried out and the sustainability of the achievements_ _of the project evaluated by a better contribution from state structures._||||| || R5. Expand for greater efficiency and sustainability of project interventions as well as perpetuation of achievements through better contribution from state structures, strategic partnerships and/or strengthening coordination with other Ministries such as those in charge of vocational training, agriculture and livestock and youth as part of local monitoring and support for beneficiaries in these various areas. ||||| | EFFECTIVENESS & IMPACT: | Average | C3 & C5 | Bottom || Recipients: MINEE; UNICEF; KOICA and NGOs. | ||_Observation:_ _The low coverage (15% of the 254 targeted localities) in terms of infrastructure in the WASH sector by the PKU_ _compared to other sectors has induced smaller significant lasting changes for this sector._||||| || R6. Strengthen the coverage of the PKU in terms of infrastructure in the WASH sectors with a view to promoting their access to related interventions and consequently increasing the desired impact in terms of social and behavioral change within the communities served.||||| | ACCOUNTABILITY .| High | C7 | Bottom || Recipients: MINAS/DRAS; MINEDUB/DREB; MINEE/DREE; UNICEF; KOICA, NGOs and communities served. | ||_Observation:_ _The lack of awareness of respect for rights in general and those of the child in particular still remains_ _despite interventions in the area of ​​SBC undertaken by the PKU._||||| || R7. Strengthen knowledge on children's rights and the merits of promoting these rights through training activities, mass awareness raising, community dialogue, engagement of local authorities and use U-reports and the various other platforms that exist and are accessible for this purpose. ||||| 9.2 Operational recommendations | Evaluation criterion || Priority level | Origin | Needs in resources | Recipients | Recipients | |---|---|---|---|---|---|---| | EFFECTIVENESS & EFFICIENCY: ||_Observation:_ _Malfunctions which hamper the effectiveness and efficiency of the KOICA project's interventions are observed: (i)_ _the slowness in the contractualization procedures of the NGOs in the consortium; (ii) the late provision of_ _financial resources for IGAs to OBCs (RECOPE) and to households benefiting from Cash transfer in order to fight_ _against early marriages and keep girls in school; (iii) fairly long delays in processing partners' financial documents._||||| |||_financial partners ._||||| ||| High | C2 & C3 | Medium || Recipients: MINAS; DRAS; UNICEF and NGOs. | ||| R8. Remedy the dysfunctions observed which hinder the effectiveness and efficiency of the KOICA project's interventions by implementing 3 types of corrective actions linked to: (i) The gaps or challenges in terms of the timely provision of financial and/or material resources (means of travel for NGOs and municipal volunteers); (ii) Quite long delays in processing documents financial partners or taking into account non-compliance with deadlines for liquidating the funds made available to them. (iii) The low number of staff at the social centers (CESO) of the DRAS does not facilitate the processing of the management of cases arising from the mechanism for reporting violations of children's rights at the level of this entity.||||| | IMPACT & GENDER EQUALITY, HUMAN RIGHTS AND EQUITY: ||_Finding:_Challenges were met regarding social and behavioral change in the communities served.||||| ||| High | C5 & C6 | Medium || Recipients: UNICEF; NGOs and communities served. | ||| R9. Highlight on community radios or during mass events (16 days of activism, Children's Day) actions in the communities served regarding social and behavioral change with the aim of their acceptance of the positive perception of the improvement of child protection systems, (including issues related to early child marriage, early pregnancies in schools, child labor, boys' preference in schooling or declaration of births by parents/guardians, lack of awareness of respect for the rights of the child). ||||| | ACCOUNTABILITY TOWARDS THE POPULATIONS: ||_Observation:_ _The poor accessibility of populations to feedback mechanisms and the reception of responses to_ _concerns is underlined_.||||| ||| High | C5 & C6 | Medium | UNICEF; NGOs and communities served. || ||| R10. Carry out communication actions towards the populations of the areas served with a view to overcoming the lack of their accessibility to feedback mechanisms and responses to concerns related to access to the interventions implemented. These actions will be linked to awareness raising on: (i) the promotion of knowledge of this mechanism by the populations; (ii) the existence and use of communication channels other than the telephone to make use of them; (iii) the need for communities to be patient in order to receive adequate responses to the concerns raised.||||| Page | 51
