AB  - The purpose of this evaluation will be focused on supporting UNICEF and NORAD's quest for accountability and learning for key stakeholders in the response in the three countries. As such, this evaluation therefore has a formative element and a summative element: • Formative (learning): it aims to generate information that can inform the design and implementation of future UNICEF initiatives aimed at strengthening the public education system for the effective delivery of quality educational outcomes. The evaluation aims to inform future implementation strategies and the project's theory of change. It aims to highlight potential corrective actions and identify good practices and effective strategies to explore further in the future. The assessment also aims to provide advice and guidance on future strategies related to education and multi-sectoral issues such as child protection, child marriage, gender-based violence and programs focused on adolescent girls; • Summative (accountability): It aims to hold implementing agencies, development partners and duty bearers accountable for the performance of the intervention and the results achieved in terms of strengthening systems for the effective delivery of quality education. The evaluation aims to provide solid evidence to the donor, relevant development partners and population groups, including boys and girls served by UNICEF, on the extent to which the intervention achieved its objectives in each of the countries involved. This includes both vertical accountability to the donor and horizontal accountability to the populations served. -----Full Evaluation text----- EXECUTIVE SUMMARY 1. INTRODUCTION Following the implementation of the “Program to improve access to quality education for children in the Sahel in Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger (2017-2023), this independent and formative evaluation was commissioned and carried out thanks to the technical and financial support of UNICEF. This evaluation, conducted from January 2024 to October 2024, is undertaken to promote accountability and learning about the implementation of NORAD interventions in the three countries. It aims first of all to assess the achievements of the program, draw lessons learned and make recommendations to inform strategic and operational decision-making for similar future interventions. 2. PURPOSE, USE AND USERS OF THE EVALUATION This evaluation had two main purposes. The first is formative (learning) to generate information that can inform the design and implementation of future UNICEF initiatives aimed at strengthening the effectiveness of public education systems in producing quality outcomes. The second is summative (accountability) and aims to inform donors and the community of the results obtained in terms of strengthening systems for the effective delivery of quality education. The main users of this evaluation are UNICEF, the Governments of the 3 countries (through the Ministry of Education and other sectoral ministries) and development partners in the field of education/child protection as well as the local communities/bodies served by the program evaluated. 3. OBJECTIVES OF THE EVALUATION The general objective of this evaluation is to assess the achievements of the said program, to draw lessons learned and to make recommendations which will better inform strategic and operational decision-making with a view to improving the future implementation of similar interventions. Specifically, the evaluation aims for four (4) objectives, namely: (i) Determine the relevance, coherence, efficiency and effectiveness, impact, sustainability and gender, human rights and equity dimensions of the project; (ii) Identify good practices as well as lessons learned on what worked and what did not work in the project, including unexpected results (positive and negative); (iii) Provide advice and guidance on future strategies to be implemented/strengthened in relation to the regional education strategy, as well as multi-sectoral issues such as child protection, child marriage, GBV or convergence programs around adolescent girls; and, (iv) Formulate strategic and operational recommendations. 4. SCOPE OF THE EVALUATION The thematic scope of this evaluation concerned four (4) components of the program: (i) Expanding access to education for the most marginalized (KRC3); (ii) Improve the quality of basic education (KRC4), (iii) Strengthen research and monitoring of the education system and (iv) Ensure communication and visibility of the project. The geographical scope of primary data collection covered the regional level through UNICEF WCARO and the central and decentralized levels of the 3 countries concerned. The evaluation. covered the period from 2017 to 2023 for Mali and Niger, taking into account addendums since 2019, and from 2017 to 2021 for Burkina Faso. 5. EVALUATION CRITERIA AND QUESTIONS The evaluation focused on six (6) main criteria of the United Nations Evaluation Group (UNEG) and the Development Assistance Committee of the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (DAC/OECD) namely: (i) relevance, (ii) coherence, (iii) effectiveness, (iv) efficiency, (iv) impact and (vi) sustainability. In addition to these criteria, the cross-cutting criterion of gender equality, human rights and equity was also taken into account. All of these 7 criteria retained gave rise to 7 evaluation questions and 24 evaluation sub-questions developed through an evaluation matrix. 6. EVALUATION METHODOLOGY The evaluation methodology followed a human rights and gender-based approach with a focus on equity, in line with UNICEF ethical standards for research, evaluation, data collection and analysis. Thus, the evaluation adopted the approach of analysis of contributions as well as that of the so-called "_Most Significant Change_ (CPS)" aimed at capturing the change and evaluating the effects of the program. In addition, a quasi-experimental impact analysis based on the method of Propensity Score Matching (ASP) was used to compare the "treated" and "non-treated" groups (transversal) between schools which benefited from the program's interventions and those who have not benefited from it. The evaluation used six (6) main data collection methods: _documentary review_ with 42 documents consulted; _semi-structured individual interviews_ carried out in person or virtually at the regional level through UNICEF WCARO as well as at the central (capital) and decentralized level in the 3 countries concerned with 98 key informants (including 24 women) out of the 110 interviews Page | xi planned (i.e. 89% participation rate); 30[1] _Focus Group Discussion (FGD)_ carried out in Mali and Burkina out of all 40 planned (i.e. 75% completed); 12 _Frame H_ sessions out of the 16 planned (75%) with groups of 6 to 8 children (girls and boys separately) under 12 years old in Burkina Faso and Mali; _an online questionnaire_ for which a total of 128[2] people for the 3 countries out of the 180 targeted respondents (i.e. 71% participation) responded favorably and completely regarding the Online Perception Survey (EPEL); _a Household Opinion Survey_ (EOAM) which reached 649[3] households in total out of the 650 targeted. Also, it should be noted that the participation of stakeholders in these focus groups and in the Cadre H sessions was done on the basis of purposive sampling which made it possible to ensure the consideration of women, vulnerable categories, including people living with disabilities, particularly 5 people in total took part in the focus groups and Cadre H sessions. Two analysis approaches were applied: the qualitative approach (content and comparative analysis using the Excel software package), the quantitative approach (descriptive statistical analyzes at the 5% threshold with SPSS software and impact analysis with propensity score matching to measure the impact of the different interventions of the Program evaluated with STATA software). Finally, the overall analysis of the results was based on a triangulation of data comparing at least three sources (documentary review, individual interviews/FGD/Cadre H/EPEL and EOAM). 7. LIMITS OF THE EVALUATION AND MITIGATION MEASURES IMPLEMENTED Several limitations were identified in this evaluation. One of them concerns the reduced sample size with only 4 schools per country (2 in the treatment group and 2 in the control group) for a total of schools numbering 526 and 2,292 respectively in Mali and Burkina Faso. The use of mixed research methods and triangulation with qualitative data to complement quantitative findings helped provide depth and richer context, especially regarding the implementation process and stakeholder perceptions. The Online Perception Survey (EPEL), which informed several analyses, is based on a sample of 128 very diverse and non-representative stakeholders. Also, a limitation should be noted, the absence of basic data necessary to accurately measure the change attributed to the intervention. Therefore, the use of a quasi-experimental research method consisting of a combination of Propensity Score Matching (PSM) complemented by a variety of qualitative perspectives (KII, FGD and Frame H) and a review of program documentation allowed the identification of proxy indicators to measure the change attributed to the intervention evaluated. Finally, the difficulty of finding the implementation actors and beneficiaries of the program at all levels given the time elapsed between the implementation period and that of the evaluation and the unavailability of focal points and people identified for carrying out interviews during the period allocated to data collection should be noted. Strategies have been put in place to obtain up-to-date lists of actors and beneficiaries and to reschedule appointments, including by telephone, in virtual mode when necessary _._ 8. MAIN FINDINGS AND CONCLUSIONS BY EVALUATION CRITERION The conclusions are based on the responses to the 7 evaluation criteria assigned to this evaluation, including: 1. Concerning the relevance of the evaluated program, first of all, it emerges from the evaluation, the existence of a clear distinction between the needs identified during the design of the program and those emerged during its implementation relating to contextual factors linked to population movements, the COVID-19 pandemic and the change in socio-political and security context in the three countries. The interviews carried out make it possible to affirm that the activities implemented by the program evaluated were deemed adequate and in relation to the initial needs or those which emerged during the implementation of all the stakeholders, whether institutional or community type. However, this result remains to be put into perspective if we stick to the results from the EPEL with participants at regional, national and decentralized levels as well as with secondary obligation bearers (School management staff, Teachers) and holders of indirect rights (Parents and family members of students, Members of the community adjacent to the schools visited). Finally, based on two (2) main elements, the evaluation notes a strong adaptation during the implementation of the program in connection with the changing contextual factors relating to population movements, the COVID-19 pandemic and the change in socio-political and security context in the three countries studied. These 2 elements being: (i) the development and implementation of adaptive strategies (education in emergencies; strengthening the resilience of school system actors in affected areas), and (ii) the implementation of innovative and alternative educational offers in order to ensure the continuity of educational services for vulnerable groups during disruptive events with a view to taking into account the needs induced by changes occurring in the national context of the countries. > 1 Including 11 FGDs carried out with children aged 12-17 whether or not in formal schools or not; 6 FGDs with teachers and school principals; 6 FGDs carried out with members of the school management committee, 3 FGDs with the parents’ association and 4 FGDs with the association of mother-educators. > 2 This survey was carried out among 44 representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, 7 actors from the implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated program (2017-2023) from NGOs/CSOs/national associations specializing in the protection of children and 6 actors from ASNU as well as with 70 bearers of secondary obligations and holders of indirect rights. > 3 That is, 329 households in Burkina Faso were affected (including 166 intervention-beneficiary households and 163 non-beneficiary households) while 320 households in Mali were also affected (161 intervention-beneficiary households and 159 non-beneficiary households). Page | xi 2. Regarding the coherence of the evaluated program, the evaluation highlights a fairly perceptible complementarity between UNICEF's interventions and those implemented by the Governments in the 3 countries to reach the most vulnerable people in the areas of education and child protection. This is manifested through Components 1 to 3 of the program and the following national programs: the Strategic Development Program for Basic Education (PDSED, 2012-2021) in Burkina Faso, the Ten-year Program for the Development of Second Generation Education and Vocational Training (PRODEC 2, 2019-2028) in Mali and the Sector Program for Education and Training (PTSE 2014-2024) in Niger. Furthermore, the evaluation also noted that the 4 fundamental humanitarian principles guided the work of all stakeholders. The design and implementation of interventions aimed to save lives, alleviate suffering, maintain human dignity and protect the rights of affected populations. 3. The analysis of the effectiveness of the evaluated Program was carried out on the basis of the program results framework which highlights contrasting results depending on the countries and program components. Two satisfactory performance indicators were noted in Niger, compared to only one in Mali and Burkina Faso. Analysis of program products shows that Component 1: Expanding access to education for the most marginalized achieved satisfactory performance for 2 out of 3 products in Mali and all 3 products in Burkina Faso. For Component 2: Improving the quality of basic education, the three products from Burkina Faso as well as products 2.1: Capacity building of members of school management committees and 2.3: Establishment and functionality of a database on target schools in Mali were judged satisfactory, while those from Niger presented insufficient performance. On the other hand, Component 3: Strengthening research and monitoring of the education system recorded unsatisfactory performance in Niger, while Burkina Faso obtained a satisfactory result for Product 3.1: Carrying out the surveys planned as part of Edu-Track. Finally, Component 5: Supporting country offices in improving access to quality education by developing effective strategic tools at the regional level was deemed satisfactory, with the establishment of a public good by UNICEF WCARO. The evaluation identified several internal and external factors that influenced the effectiveness of the program. Among the positive factors, we note the existence of financial and material resources, the adaptation capacity of stakeholders, the deployment of appropriate strategic and technical approaches, as well as the strong involvement of local communities. However, significant challenges have hampered the implementation of the program, including security constraints, the COVID-19 pandemic, insufficient human and financial resources, as well as lack of coordination with some key sector ministries. The program generated several unexpected results, both positive and negative. Among the positive effects, we observe an increased commitment of local communities in favor of education, particularly for girls, as well as the empowerment of teachers thanks to the training received. Also, the evaluation noted that in its implementation, the Program evaluated resulted in a challenge linked to the overcrowding/overload of classes and the demand for early childhood schooling (3 to 5 years) by the communities following the implementation of interventions at the level of Component 1 of the program. And this in a context where one of the challenges of the Program lay in the fact that the construction/rehabilitation of school infrastructure was not planned during its implementation. The involvement of strategic and operational partners was judged generally positive, although a lack of collaboration with key ministries limited certain initiatives, particularly in supporting professional training for young girls. Concerning the impact of COVID-19, the evaluation shows a contrasting situation: some stakeholders believe that the health crisis has had little disruption to the program, while others note a drop in the enrollment rate and a deterioration in the quality of education. Nevertheless, the program has significantly strengthened the resilience of schools in the face of the pandemic, in particular through the implementation of distance learning solutions and the distribution of health materials. Finally, the program's monitoring and evaluation system was generally effective, making it possible to adapt implementation strategies. However, certain gaps persist, particularly in Niger where the Edu-Track tool has not yet been fully implemented. In conclusion, the program has achieved several objectives despite major constraints, but improvements are needed to strengthen its effectiveness and future sustainability. 4. As part of the analysis of the efficiency of the Program evaluated, the evaluation reveals that the main stakeholders (whether governmental or NGO type) are unanimous in the provision of sufficient financial resources, but with delays considered inappropriate for the implementation of the Program. These opinions are also supported by resource expenditures estimated at $17,642,147 USD for resource mobilization beyond the expected level ($18,470,196 USD) compared to the programmable resources at the start of implementation which were $15,540,305 USD for all 3 countries. Consequently, the evaluation noted budget execution considered satisfactory (96%) despite the impacts of the COVID-19 health crisis and the persistent security instability in the three countries. Stakeholders from the 3 countries are unanimous regarding the good quality and quantity of human resources deployed in a timely manner for the implementation of interventions. However, delays and staff shortages were noted for NGO partners due to insufficient planning. Page | xiii Regarding the supply of material resources, particularly school and educational kits for students and teachers, was judged to be of impeccable quality, but in insufficient quantity and often delivered with significant delays. The delivery of these material resources (school/educational kits, construction materials for local colleges specifically in Niger) encountered great difficulties due to the security situation. In addition, many suppliers have not respected their commitments due to ECOWAS sanctions on financial transactions linked to the political-military situation in the three countries. 5. The analysis of the impact of the evaluated Program, based on the implementation of the interventions reveals a positive development in Niger in the perception of EPEL participants, at the regional, national and decentralized levels, concerning the gross admission rate to the last year of primary education. In Mali, this level remained constant between the start and end of program implementation. This observation is also relayed by all stakeholders in the evaluation in Mali and Burkina Faso, whether they are Governments or NGOs. Finally, the evaluation establishes a strong contribution of the program to promoting a positive perception of girls' education and to improving child protection systems in the communities served. Indeed, despite the persistence of harmful cultural practices, qualitative data has made it possible to establish a notable change in behavior and perception. These changes are linked not only to the schooling of all children in general and in particular that of girls and children with disabilities but also to questions of marriage and GBV. 6. As part of the sustainability of the Program evaluated, the evaluation highlights a varied reproduction of program activities in Mali and Niger which do not require budgets for their implementation by the Government of the countries served and by other partners. A situation which thus weakens the institutionalization of the activities of the program evaluated within local and national structures and which does not ensure maintenance and long-term impact with a view to guaranteeing the sustainability of the implemented initiatives. However, in Burkina Faso, the reproduction of activities was perceptible with the institutionalization of the “_Safe school_” approach and the adoption by MENAPLN of the _Edu-Track_ platform for the monthly monitoring of closed and open schools throughout the territory until 2021. However, this platform is no longer functional despite its usefulness due to the lack of financial resources. in order to pay the fees to the local telephone agencies with which at the time UNICEF had benefited from preferential costs for implementation by SMS. As for Niger, the reproduction of bridging classes by the Government and by NGOs (NRC and World Vision) was also highlighted by the evaluation. In addition, significant changes brought by the evaluated Program at the level of the communities served are perceptible through the EOAM in Burkina Faso and Mali through three main elements: (i) the evolution of the availability and sufficiency of functioning educational institutions; (ii) the evolution of the primary school enrollment rate for children of school age; and (iii) the evolution of the number of teachers in sufficient numbers at the primary school level. 7. The evaluation demonstrated strong integration and implementation of the principles of gender equality, human rights (including disability) and equity in the design, implementation and monitoring of the Program evaluated. Thus, in general, it should be noted that taking into account the disability situation remains of interest for all countries. Furthermore, the evaluation highlights a differentiated consideration of these principles depending on the countries and phases of the program. If the principle of human rights was largely integrated into the design in Niger (75%) and monitoring/reporting in Mali (57%), gender equality experienced weaker integration, particularly during implementation and monitoring in Burkina Faso. Equity has been partially taken into account, with varying efforts depending on national contexts. The inclusion of children with disabilities has been more targeted in Niger, while Burkina Faso and Mali have adopted a more comprehensive approach, making it difficult to specifically measure progress for this population. The program enabled expanded access to education and protective services for the most vulnerable populations. In Burkina Faso, 25,852 children accessed education thanks to “Back to school campaigns” and more than 5,409 out-of-school children were reintegrated into the formal education system. In Niger, 265,000 children received educational kits and 3,262 out-of-school children were integrated via bridging classes. In Mali, 463,800 students benefited from educational activities, and more than 7,800 members of school communities saw their capacities strengthened, in particular through better involvement of women in school management. The assessment highlights several socio-cultural and economic barriers to access to quality education for vulnerable children. Early marriage, children's participation in family economic activities, pregnancies in schools and the preference for boys' schooling remain major challenges. Communication activities for social change have helped improve community attitudes, particularly in favor of the schooling of girls and children with disabilities. However, this progress remains uneven depending on national contexts and must be strengthened to ensure lasting impact. In conclusion, if the program has succeeded in partially integrating the principles of gender equality, human rights and equity, additional efforts are necessary to guarantee more homogeneous consideration of these principles at all stages of implementation. Page | xi work. In particular, the integration of children with disabilities deserves increased attention, as does the elimination of socio-cultural barriers to the schooling of girls and vulnerable children. 9. LESSONS LEARNED The evaluation identified nine lessons learned, including four at the strategic level and five at the operational level, as well as several good practices implemented in the three countries concerned. Strategic level 1. Strengthening community resilience: The security and political crisis has a negative impact on the education sector, requiring increased accountability of communities through active local structures (AME, COGES). 2. Strengthening the results framework: A robust results framework is essential to ensure effective program evaluability, which has been lacking in this initiative. 3. Strengthened communication: An effective communication strategy promoted the support of institutional and community stakeholders, thus strengthening the synergy between stakeholders. 4. Institutional involvement: The participation of the ministries in charge of education facilitated the coordination and implementation of activities, contributing to the achievement of the expected results. Operational level 1. Improved monitoring-evaluation and reporting: Strengthening these systems allows for better transparency and more effective knowledge management. 2. Establishment of functional community bodies: The AMEs and COGES played an essential role in the implementation of the program and the social and behavioral change sought. 3. Impact of COGES on school retention (Burkina Faso): A direct correlation was observed between the functionality of COGES and improved student performance. 4. Link between teacher training and academic performance (Mali): Continuous training of teachers and monitoring of academic inspectors have proven crucial to improve student learning. 5. Decentralization of implementation (Niger): Strengthened involvement of partners at the decentralized level is essential for effective implementation of interventions. Best practices: Burkina Faso - Adaptation of program activities to safer areas to guarantee their continuity. - Designation of academic focal points for data collection via Edu-track, thus facilitating the availability of data despite the crisis. Mali - Training on local education development plans to better adapt interventions to local needs. - Strengthening the capacities of AMEs to promote access to education for the most vulnerable, particularly girls. - Financial support from AMEs to schools via Income Generating Activities (AGR), making it possible to improve school infrastructure. Niger - High-level participation, including that of the First Lady, in education awareness campaigns. - Decentralization of public services through consultations with mayors and municipal councils, thus strengthening local involvement in education. 10. RECOMMENDATIONS The evaluation of the “Program to improve access to quality education for children in the Sahel (2017-2023)” made it possible to identify strategic and operational recommendations essential for improving future interventions. These recommendations, resulting from a participatory process involving stakeholders (governments, UNICEF, NORAD), are classified according to their priority and the resources required for their implementation. ❖ At the strategic level R1. Integration of early childhood schooling (Burkina Faso, Niger) Integrate interventions specific to early childhood education (3 to 5 years) within the framework of education sector programs. This is to reduce the demand induced by the schooling of children of all ages in response to the positive effect of communication activities carried out within the communities served for social and behavioral change in favor of children's education. _Recipients: Governments of Mali and Niger and the Technical and Financial Partners (PTF) (UNICEF; NORAD) through_ Page | xvi _stakeholders in the development and implementation of education sector programs _under the leadership of the Ministries of National Education (MEN)._ R2. Strengthening the results framework and the monitoring-evaluation system (Burkina Faso, Mali, Niger) Design a robust results framework with SMART indicators (Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Realistic and Timely defined) which integrates the gender dimension, equity, rights of vulnerable people and those living with disabilities when formulating education sector programs in order to ensure their evaluability. Except in Niger, the results frameworks have not sufficiently collected data on children living with disabilities. _Recipients: Governments of the 3 countries and the TFPs (UNICEF;_ _NORAD) through stakeholders in the development and implementation of education sector programs under the leadership of MEN._ R3. Construction and rehabilitation of school infrastructure as well as awareness raising among teachers on the specific needs of children with disabilities (Burkina Faso, Mali, Niger) Include in national priorities and those of international organizations as part of education sector program interventions, those relating to the construction/rehabilitation of school infrastructure within a reasonable time frame in order to remedy the flow of new arrivals driven by the implementation of alternative/accelerated learning (SSA/P) offerings aimed at access to education for the most marginalized. Above all, this implies a greater focus on the specific needs of children living with disabilities with tailor-made activities which include not only the improvement of classroom infrastructure, but also an overall strengthening of training and awareness-raising for teachers and school management staff to improve the access of children living with disabilities to dignified and quality education. _Recipients: Governments of the 3 countries and the TFPs (UNICEF; NORAD) through the stakeholders in the development and implementation of education sector programs under the leadership of the MEN._ R4. Establishment of strategic partnerships (Burkina Faso, Mali, Niger) Expand for greater efficiency and sustainability of education sector programs, strategic partnerships and/or strengthen coordination with other Ministries such as those in charge of vocational training, agriculture and livestock and youth which deal with related issues of schooling/training and protection of children/adolescents. _Recipients: Governments of the 3 countries through the sectoral ministries (Ministry of Vocational Training; Ministry of Agriculture and Livestock; Ministry of Youth) and the TFPs (UNICEF; NORAD) through the stakeholders involved in the development and implementation of education sector programs under the leadership of the MEN._ R5. Institutionalization of program activities (Mali, Niger) Promote replicability or even institutionalization of the interventions implemented as a whole by carrying out advocacy with relevant stakeholders with a view to their budgetary consideration in national/decentralized development plans. This is so that the sustainability of implemented interventions now concerns interventions requiring implementation budgets. _Recipients: Governments of Mali and Niger through the Ministries of Finance and Budget as well as decentralized communities_, _PTF (UNICEF; NORAD) through stakeholders in the development and implementation of education sector programs under the leadership of the MEN._ ❖ At the operational level R6. Improvement in the deployment of human resources (Mali) Decide and enforce deadlines for timely transmission of resources by implementing partners in accordance with the need for implementation of interventions based on concluded partnership agreements. _Recipients: Government of Mali and the TFPs (UNICEF; NORAD; NGO/CSO) through stakeholders in the development and implementation of education sector programs under the leadership of the MEN._ R7. Optimization of the distribution of material resources (Burkina Faso, Mali, Niger) Address dysfunctions in the supply system for material resources in general and particularly those in school kits for students and educational kits for teachers by improving their availability if possible through actions: (i) prepositioning in a timely manner if funds allow; (ii) prioritizing local purchases if conditions permit; (iii) taking into account the CASH modality in the implementation of interventions if possible. Action required to address the discontinuity in the implementation of interventions. _Recipients: Governments of the 3 countries and the TFPs (UNICEF; NORAD; NGO/CSO) through the stakeholders in the development and implementation of education sector programs under the leadership of the MEN._ R8. Strengthening actions for social and behavioral change (Burkina Faso, Mali) Accentuate actions in the communities served regarding social and behavioral change with the aim of their acceptance of the positive perception of girls' education and the improvement of child protection systems (including issues related to child marriage, GBV and disability). These actions, reinforced by the provision of study grants or the construction of local secondary establishments limiting the movement/entrustment of children, would aim at the accountability of communities and would primarily seek: (i) the education of girls so as to ensure that no discrimination exists in the financing of education between girls and boys; (ii) the continuity of schooling for children after the primary cycle regardless of their sex. (iii) The Page | xvi children with disabilities who should benefit from a more tailored approach to their specific needs. _Recipients: Government of Mali and Burkina, the populations of the areas served, the established community bodies and the TFPs (UNICEF; NORAD; NGO/CSO) through the stakeholders in the development and implementation of education sector programs under the leadership of the MEN._ R9. Institutional stabilization for continuity of interventions (Burkina Faso, Mali, Niger) Organize capacity building for stakeholders reporting to the Ministry of National Education on a large scale while ensuring the creation of a pool of beneficiaries (extended to community populations) established over the long term in their deployment area. This is with the aim of remedying their unpredictable transfer outside the implementation areas after having benefited from capacity building. _Recipients: Governments of the 3 countries and the TFPs (UNICEF; NORAD; NGO/CSO) through the stakeholders in the development and implementation of education sector programs under the leadership of the MEN._ Page | xvii CHAPTER 2. PURPOSE OF THE EVALUATION 40. Table 2.1 below provides a brief overview of the purpose of the evaluation: Table 2.1: Overview of the purpose of the evaluation ||| Table 2.1: Overview of the purpose of the evaluation| Table 2.1: Overview of the purpose of the evaluation| Table 2.1: Overview of the purpose of the evaluation| |---|---|---|---|---| | Program Title ||Improving access to quality education for children in the Sahel||| | Countries ||BurkinaFaso, Mali and Niger || Maliet Niger 46 | | Funding sources ||SC170576, reference Norway RAF 16/0040, Norwegian Development Cooperation Agency (NORAD)||SC170576, reference Norway RAF 19/0053, Norwegian Development Cooperation Agency| | Budget ||150,013,266 NOK (18,436,630.39 USD in 11/2017)||120,000,000 NOK (13,140,000. - USD in 12/2019)| | Duration ||01/11/2017-30/10/2020||05/12/2019-05/31/2024| | Overall objective ||By 2020,47children aged 6 to 16 will have access to quality basic education to improve their ability to cope with internal and external shocks with particular attention to schoolchildren, including children living with disabilities, talibés and out-of-school adolescents||| | Components ||Component 1. Expand access to education for the most marginalized Component 2. Improve the quality of basic education Component 3. Strengthening research and (pedagogical) monitoring of the education system Component 4. Ensure communication and visibility of the Program evaluated Component 5. Help country offices improve access to quality education by developing effective strategies and tools at the regional level||| | Partners | Execution | Burkina Faso: Ministry of Education and Literacy, international and national NGOs; Mali: Decentralized structures of the Ministry of Education, NGOs; Niger: Ministry of Primary Education, Ministry of Secondary Education, Ministry of Vocational Training.||| || Strategic | Burkina Faso: Ministry of Education, NGO, JICA, sectoral group of partners, Communities; Mali: Communities; Niger: Ministry of Education, NGO, Communities.||| || ASNU | Burkina Faso: PAM and FAO at the interinstitutional level; Niger: PAM; UNHCR||| || Financial |Norwegian Development Cooperation Agency (NORAD)||| | Right Holders48 || Burkina Faso: (i) Children enrolled in formal and non-formal schools as well as children not in school, including children with disabilities; (ii) Teachers as part of initial and continuing training for the implementation of the EQAmE approach; (iii) Members of school management committees, parent associations and mother-educators in targeted schools within the framework of school governance; Mali: (i) Children in school and out of school within the framework of alternative and accelerated education programs; (ii) Members of school management committees; (iii) Teachers and school principals as part of continuing teacher training initiatives; Niger: (i) Children of preschool, primary and lower secondary school age, in the 35 convergence communes; (ii) Teachers and directors of nursery, primary and lower secondary schools in the 35 convergence municipalities; (iii) Members of the management committees of the schools of the 35 convergence municipalities.||| |||| Source: Elaboration by the evaluation team|| > 46 In the implementation of the Addenda for Mali and Niger, the initial Program was renamed to Project “Improving the quality of education for girls and adolescents aged 7 to 18” > 47 Initially the project “_Improving access to quality education for children in the Sahel_”, targeting Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger from November 2017 to October 2020, was extended in December 2019 for Mali and Niger until 2023 > 48 Information provided by the document “_UNICEF WCARO Multi-Country Initiative Proposal October 2017, Submitted to the Government of Norway_” Page | 9 2.1. Presentation of the evaluated program 41. To respond to the challenges of the education sector in the targeted countries, the aim of this UNICEF multi-country program was to collaborate with the governments of Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger to improve access to quality education, focusing on the most marginalized groups. This effort was aligned with global, regional and national priorities such as Sustainable Development Goal 4 (SDG4[49]) relating to education primarily, the Convention on the Rights of the Child (CRC) specifically in Articles 28 and 29 declaring quality education as an enshrined right, the Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (CRPD), the UNICEF strategic plans (2018-2021 and 2022-2025 specifically linked to Objective Group 2 on education for each of these plans) as well as other regional charters and national strategies. Also, the alignment of this evaluated program in relation to national policies and strategies for each of the 3 countries is perceptible through the Strategic Development Program for Basic Education (PDSED, 2012-2021)[50], the Ten-Year Development Program for Education and second generation vocational training (PRODEC II, 2019-2028)[51] and the evaluated Program is well aligned with the Education Sector Program and Training (PTSE 2014-2024) respectively in Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger. 42. The program also aimed to innovate and experiment with new approaches to continuing education and completing the education cycle, modeling “safe school” strategies for resilient educational environments, and promoting peer learning through school clubs. These components were essential for all three countries and expanded the regular programs by introducing new and effective methods for the delivery and improvement of education. 43. Additionally, the project aimed to enable comparisons and shared learning between countries, improving understanding of the impacts and variables of different actions in various contexts. The regional office guided and assisted country offices to improve access, retention, achievement and learning outcomes, in line with the regional priorities of improving learning outcomes for all children and protecting and empowering adolescent boys and girls. 44. Particular attention was paid to supporting inclusive education for which a joint regional workshop was planned on the subject when developing the interventions to take place during the implementation of the program as well as interventions related to knowledge management (studies, documentaries) and the development of materials and tools as well as technical support to country offices. Additionally, efforts to bridge the gap between Quranic and formal education were underway, with a regional study to further inform UNICEF's engagement in this area.[52] The various key stakeholders such as the implementing agency, development partners, duty bearers, academic researchers as well as rights holders are described in detail in section 2.6. 2.2. Results framework of the program evaluated 45. The final results framework[53] (Appendix A.2) at the end of the implementation of the “Program to improve access to quality education for children in the Sahel (2017-2023)” taking into account the various addenda is summarized in the table below: > 49 The targets of this SDG4 are: 4.1 and 4.7 for Burkina Faso; 4.1, 4.5 and 4.7 for Burkina Faso and Mali. these targets are stated as follows: _4.1 By 2030, ensure that all girls and boys follow, on an equal footing, a full cycle of free, quality primary and secondary education, providing them with truly useful skills_; _4.5 By 2030, eliminate gender inequalities in education and ensure equal access for vulnerable people, including people with disabilities, indigenous people and children in vulnerable situations, to all levels of education and vocational training; 4.7 By 2030, ensure that all students acquire the knowledge and skills necessary to promote sustainable development, including through education for sustainable development and lifestyles, human rights, gender equality, the promotion of a culture of peace and non-violence, global citizenship and appreciation of cultural diversity and the contribution of culture to sustainable development._ > 50 PDSEB_final-version-signed_WORD_format (unesco.org) > 51 mali-prodec2_2019-2028.pdf (unesco.org) > 52 Improving access to a quality education for children of the Sahel, Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger; Multi-Country Initiative Proposal; UNICEF WCARO; October 2017 > 53 Country information provided from the report dated September 30, 2023 relating to the “UNICEF progress report for Mali and Niger, UNICEF ref. SC170576 / Norway ref RAF 16/0040 RAF 19/0053” and the report “_Approval of the mid-term report for “Improving access to a quality education system for children in the Sahel in Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger” (RAF-16/0040) and confirmation of the disbursement of the final payment_” dated 10/24/2019 Page | 10 Table 2.2: Summary of the Program results framework evaluated according to each of the 3 countries concerned | Components | Country | Country | Country | Country | Country | Country | |---|---|---|---|---|---|---| || Burkina ||| Mali | Niger || || Number | Number | Number | Number || Number | |||||| Number of || || of | of indicators of | of | of indicators || of indicators | |||||| Products || || Products | Products | Products | Products || of Products | |||||||| |||||||| |Component 1. Expand access to education for the most marginalized|3|5|3|5|4|5| |Component 2. Improving the quality of basic education|3|4|3|4|4|6| |Component 3. Strengthening research and (pedagogical) monitoring of the educational system|2|2|-|-|3|3| |Component 4. Ensure communication and visibility of the project|1|1|1|1|-|-| |Component 5. Support country offices to improve access to quality education by developing effective strategies and tools at the regional level|1|1|-|-|-|-| | Total | 10 | 13 | 7 | 10 | 11 | 14 | Source: Developed by the evaluation team 46. Thus, for the 5 components of the Program evaluated, Niger presents the greatest number of Products and Product indicators (11 and 14 respectively), followed by Burkina Faso (10 and 13 respectively) and Mali (7 and 10 respectively). 2.3. Implementation strategy for the evaluated program 47. While emphasizing inclusive education, quality education and early childhood development, the 3 countries were supported in the implementation of national activities linked to the regular UNICEF program over the period evaluated through strategies relating to technical assistance, capacity development, production of evidence, knowledge management, monitoring and evaluation, communication and advocacy. 48. Thus, it should be noted that each of the 5 components of the Program evaluated is perceived to have significant value in the three countries and is based on the regular UNICEF cooperation program by contributing, among other things, to: 1. Testing new and innovative approaches to support continuing education and the completion of the educational cycle, particularly in the context of development; 2. Modeling “safe school” approaches for more resilient schools; 3. Peer learning through school clubs. 49. The strategies and key activities by component of the implementation of the Programs for each of the countries concerned are presented in detail in Annex A.3. It particularly emerges that the key strategies and activities by program component are based on: - (i) A renewal of the commitment of UNICEF and other stakeholders to the right of every child to quality education with particular emphasis on the needs of the most vulnerable as well as the reduction of pockets of disparity and the consideration of equity in the education system in general; - (ii) An intensification of approaches that improve the quality of educational provision by building capacity to ensure adequate decentralized school management and localized initial/in-service teacher training initiatives that will strengthen their skills, as country Governments develop and implement strategies to improve the qualifications of all unqualified teachers; - (iii) Sector planning and strengthening of education systems through the dissemination and promotion of validated knowledge of national norms and standards for quality education identified and developed with the technical and financial support of UNICEF. 2.4. Theory of change of the program evaluated 50. A Theory of Change (ToC) was established for each of the countries concerned by the implementation of the Program evaluated following the documentary review and preliminary interviews carried out with stakeholders during the inception report. Moreover, the logic of these ToCs for each country and presented in Appendix A.4 highlight the following findings for all 3 countries concerned: Page | 11 - The strategic results and expected effects, the expected products and the interventions planned for each of the 5 components of the program are clearly established; - The coherence of the logic of the ToCs developed for each of the countries concerned on the basis of the problems of the education system makes it possible to deduce its robustness. This therefore guided the evaluation team to use the approach based on ToC and contribution analysis, in order to establish to what extent the program interventions have (or have not) made a contribution to the expected strategic results. In fact, on this basis, the assessment consisted of seeking consolidated evidence relating to: - ✓ The activities of the interventions implemented within the framework of the Program evaluated; - ✓ In the chain of Strategic Results achieved for each of the 5 Components of the Program evaluated; - ✓ The cause and effect links between the activities of the interventions implemented and the Strategic Results achieved for each of the 5 Components of the Program evaluated; - ✓ External factors that may have interacted to influence the level of achievement of the Strategic Results by the Program evaluated for each of these 5 Components. 51. During the implementation of this evaluation, taking into account the implementation of the interventions of the evaluated Program (20172023) and the inputs of the UNICEF country teams, the evaluation team set out to compare each of these ToCs, with a view to guaranteeing compliance and achieving a final ToC per country (Appendix A.5: Figures 5.1; 5.2 and 5.3 respectively for Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger) taking into account all contributions from evaluation stakeholders. 2.5. Geographic coverage of the evaluated program (2017-2023) 52. As illustrated in Figure 2.1, the evaluated Program was implemented in Mali in 2 regions, namely Kayes and Sikasso. In Burkina Faso, 4 regions were affected by the implementation of the Program, notably the Sahel, East, Central Plateau and North Center regions. Finally in Niger, a total of 4 regions were affected by the implementation of the Program evaluated, namely the regions of Agadez, Diffa, Maradi and Zinder. ==> picture [430 x 269] intentionally omitted <== ==> picture [439 x 28] intentionally omitted <== ----- Start of picture text ----- Legend: Area covered in Burkina Faso Area covered in Mali Area covered in Niger ----- End of picture text ----- Figure 2.1 _:_ Geographic coverage of the program evaluated for each of the 3 countries Page | 12 2.6. Roles and responsibilities of stakeholders in the evaluated program 52. The stakeholders (Appendix A.6) in the implementation, monitoring-evaluation and coordination of the Program evaluated are spread across the global, national, subnational and community levels in all three countries of implementation. At most of these levels a wide range of stakeholder types were involved: the donor (NORAD), the implementing agency (UNICEF) as well as a wide variety of development partners, primary and secondary duty bearers who were required to serve the targeted rights holders. 53. The rights holders of the “Program to improve access to quality education for children in the Sahel (2017-2023)” are generally located in the communities of the schools benefiting from the Program. They are made up of three different categories: (i) school-age children; (ii) school teachers; (iii) and school management staff. All of these program rights holders are summarized in the table below[54]: Table 2.3: Rights holders of the Program evaluated for each of the 3 countries concerned || Table 2.3: Rights holders of the Program evaluated for each of the 3 countries concerned| |---|---| | Burkina Faso |▪ Children of school age: 104,500 children enrolled in formal and non-formal schools have access to quality education according to the standards of Child-Friendly Schools (EQAmE), including 4,500 out-of-school children including 1,500 children living with a disability (EVH) ; 1,500 Koranic students (talibés) and 1,500 adolescents benefit from adapted alternative programs such as the accelerated schooling strategy and pre-vocational training.| | Mali |▪ Children of school age: Approximately 121,000 children spread across 605 schools will benefit from better quality education. 7,020 out-of-school children benefit from alternative and accelerated education programs adapted to their context. 10,500 vulnerable children at risk of dropping out of school receive educational materials.| | Niger |▪ Children of school age: 350,000 children of preschool, primary and lower secondary school age, including 18,000 children and young people out of school, and 10,500 children with disabilities in the 35 convergence communes.| 2.7. Implementation schedule for the evaluated program 54. The key stages of implementation of the Program evaluated are listed in each of the 3 implementation countries in Table 2.4. However, Annex A.7 shows in more detail the implementation of the Program evaluated by type of Component. Table 2.4: Key stages of the Program evaluated according to the evolution of the context in the 3 countries of intervention |Burkina Faso|Mali|Niger| |---|---|---| | November 2017 - June 2018: | November 2017 - June 2018: | November 2017 - June 2018: | |• Start of the intervention in November| • Planning of the project “Improving access to a| • Planning of the project “Improving access to a| |2017. Project planning phase.|quality education for the children of the Sahel.”|quality education for the children of the Sahel| |• Launch of project implementation| • Start of first strengthening activities| ”.| |with partners from the year| of capacities.|• Start of the first strengthening activities| |school 2018-2019.| June 2018 - August 2019: |capacities.| | June 2018 - August 2019: